DELETE FROM unit_detail WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart';
DELETE FROM chapter WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>And know that the love of the world that is blameworthy is love motivated solely by the benefit of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> — not the kind of love that keeps you safe, or keeps others safe from you. In other words, it does not include loving the world in order to spare others from dependence on you, or to free yourself from dependence on Allah''s servants. This is why the Islamic tradition teaches that the world is a bridge: cross it toward the next life, and do not try to build on it as though you intend to stay forever.</p>
<p>He also points out that blameworthy love of this world is not a single, undifferentiated thing — in fact, love of the world falls under the five categories of legal rulings, and its judgment depends entirely on what it leads one to achieve. So love of worldly things that help one attain the next life — love of the Kaaba, love of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, love of the people of Allah, love of books of knowledge, love of the means that draw one closer to God, love of fellow Muslims who support one in one''s religion — all of that is obligatory, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاجِب</span>. There is also love that is recommended, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْدُوبٌ</span>; love that is permissible, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبَاح</span>, such as love of one''s children or one''s spouse; love that is disliked, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكْرُوهٌ</span>; and love that is outright forbidden, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>.</p>
<p>He states: to love anything that assists one in committing something forbidden is itself prohibited. And censuring the world applies only to those aspects of it that do not assist toward salvation. Its reproach is restricted to love of it for those kinds of reasons. Indeed, the best of creation <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> forbade cursing the world. There is a hadith in which the Prophet said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَسُبُّوا الدُّنْيَا</span> — do not curse the world — because Allah created it, and it is a means of attaining knowledge of Allah, Glorified and Exalted.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> itself is worth reflecting on. Scholars differ: some say it derives from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَنَاءَ</span>, others from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَنُو</span>. Either way, it is distinct from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span>, which refers to the world in the sense of the cosmos, nature, all of existence. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> refers specifically to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَاةُ الدُّنْيَا</span> — the ephemeral dimension of life, the hustle and preoccupation that people get swept up in. That is what Allah warns us about: being deceived by this transient thing. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ</span> that Allah created, by contrast, derives from a root meaning <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَامَة</span> — a sign. The cosmos is the greatest sign of Allah, which is precisely why we do not despise or look down on it.</p>
<p>This sets Islam apart from traditional Christian doctrine — though Christianity has been moving away from this position over the past century. The traditional Christian view condemned the world as evil. Islam does not say that. Allah says He created everything in the world for human beings: some things to reflect upon, as signs leading to Allah; others to benefit from as stewards and custodians of the earth. All of that is affirmed.</p>
<p>Things are praised or blamed according to what they produce — like a remedy or a poison. If something leads to disease, it is blameworthy; if it leads to a cure, it is praiseworthy. That is the lens through which one should view worldly things. Whatever of the material necessities of earthly life one obtains — food, housing, shelter — these are not considered <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> in the blameworthy sense. Even one''s first wife, according to the scholars of Islamic law, is not considered <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. And if a man marries to care for an orphan or a woman in need, that too falls outside the blameworthy category. If he takes a second, third, or fourth wife for purely worldly reasons, it falls under a different ruling — though it remains permissible, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبَاح</span>, it does not carry the same merit.</p>
<p>Similarly with wealth and reputation — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَهْ</span>, meaning standing or position. Scholars have said that having a good name can be valuable insofar as it enables one to help others, not as an end in itself. The scholars have generally regarded the accumulation of excessive wealth as disliked, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكْرُوهٌ</span>, because the more wealth one acquires, the greater the probability of falling into something displeasing to Allah. Becoming absorbed in amassing and competing for wealth is a dangerous occupation. People become addicted to the stock market, to the chase of accumulation — it becomes a compulsion.</p>
<p>As for earning wealth in order to show off — to say, look at me, look how clever I am, look how much I have — the scholars counted this among the people who have acquired enormities. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُكْتَسِبُ الْكَبَائِرِ</span>: it is a major sin, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, to do this.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 2">page 2</div>
<p>He then says: love of praise is another disease. Love of being praised for what one has not done or accomplished is caused by craving something other than the Exalted. Those who love to be praised for things Allah has not praised them for — this is considered forbidden, a disease of the heart. One should only desire to be praised for what one has genuinely done, though some scholars go further and say one should not really desire praise except from Allah, Glorified and Exalted — while stopping short of prohibiting it entirely. It is not necessarily a bad thing when someone says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحْسَنْتَ</span>, you did well. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> did say this to people.</p>
<p>There is a distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْحٌ</span> that is forbidden and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْحٌ</span> that is permitted. The forbidden kind is flattery. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: throw dirt in the faces of flatterers — meaning those who tell you how wonderful you are when it is not true, or when they do not mean it. The poets who would compose elaborate panegyrics for the caliphs exemplify this: al-Mutanabbi wrote poem after poem for Sayf al-Dawla, all of it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْحٌ</span>, telling him how magnificent he was. That is the blameworthy kind. Whereas sincerely telling someone <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحْسَنْتَ</span>, or that they did good work, is not prohibited — especially when there is no risk that it will make them conceited. It functions as encouragement, as a form of gratitude.</p>
<p>He goes on to say that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> — covetousness, greediness of expectation — is the very reason people flatter others and also why they love to be praised themselves. They want something in return. This, too, is a disease. To enjoy being told you did a great job for something you did not do, while knowing full well you did not do it, is a deeply contemptible thing. You smile and say, thank you so much, I''m glad you appreciate the effort — when you put in none. This happens in universities, where graduate students do all the work and the professor takes the credit. It happens in corporations, where a manager assigns tasks, the team does the work, and the manager claims the recognition. It is a very common human tendency — which is precisely why scholars recognized it centuries ago, and why the Quran addresses it. Allah says that there are those who love to be praised for what they have not done. It is, in that sense, a Quranic reality.</p>
<p>He then turns to envy — and here I will pause, because envy is a lengthy topic and a major illness. We will cover envy and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span> next week, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>. We are continuing with this text now in our third meeting, and we should reach the diseases of fear and desire of other than Allah, though whether we get that far depends on how much ground remains.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'love of the world';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>We ended on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> last time. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> is probably one of the most serious diseases. There is a difference of opinion among scholars about which disease is the root of all others: some say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> is the root disease. I would argue it always traces back to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمَا</span> — desire — because within <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> there is always an underlying desire. So ultimately it goes back to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمَا</span>. Or to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الْجَاهِ</span>, as Ibn Ashraf suggests — though even Ibn Ashraf admits that the source of all wrong is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الْعَاجِلَةِ</span>, love of the immediate world, which again connects to desire.</p>
<p>According to the scholars, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> is the first disease, the first manifestation of disobedience in the heavens. The first act of disobedience toward Allah in the heavenly realm was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> — because nothing prevented Iblis from prostrating except <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span>. The particular form is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَزُزّ</span>: he could not bear that Adam, peace be upon him, would hold a higher station than him. He could not endure it. It is also connected to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> — he was filled with arrogance and pride. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> and the arrogance were intertwined.</p>
<p>There is a hadith in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> devours good deeds the way fire devours dry wood. If you have ever watched fire consume dry wood, you should let that image tell you what happens to your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَنَاتٌ</span>. The person who harbors envy is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَسِدُ</span>, and the object of his envy is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المَحْسُودُ</span>. In the verse of the Quran that people should commit to memory: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمِن شَرِّ حَاسِدٍ إِذَا حَسَدَ</span> ‎ — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَاسِد</span> referred to is shaytan, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَسِدُ</span>. To harbor envy is to embody one of the attributes of shaytan.</p>
<p>There is also a hadith in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُلُّ ذِي نِعْمَةٍ مَحْسُودٌ</span> — every possessor of a blessing, any blessing at all, is envied. There will always be someone who envies you for whatever you have. Consider two street sweepers: one has only his brooms to carry; the other has a donkey pulling his cart. To you, this is entirely trivial. But the one without the donkey looks at the one with the donkey and envies him. Whatever the level, however small the difference, envy operates. It is a strange and pervasive force that runs through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمَا</span>n culture and society.</p>
<p>It is also understood that envy carries real harm — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَضَرَّة</span>. But what the scholars say is that the envy of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَاسِد</span> harms him before it harms the object of his envy. He is damaged by it faster than he can damage the one he envies.</p>
<p>The evil eye is generally connected to envy, though it does not require envy. Some people simply have a powerful gaze — a kind of psychic force. It does not necessarily mean they envy; it can operate independently, though it is still something to take seriously. Across many Muslim cultures, traditionally, a firstborn male child would have his ears pierced and be dressed as a girl for the first several years of life, precisely out of fear of the evil eye. This was widespread practice. I knew an older man who had holes in his ears, and when I asked him, that was the explanation.</p>
<p>In contemporary Western culture, much of this is dismissed as superstition. But it is striking that every culture in the world has some concept of the evil eye. Every one. The Latin root of the English word <em>invidious</em> — meaning to envy — literally means to look with evil. The evil eye is an evil glance. The Chinese have a concept of it as well and employ various ritual protections against it; you will often see mirrors on Chinese houses, placed there to deflect the evil eye. This is a universal <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمَا</span>n belief. And when a belief is universal, it usually has some foundation — it is not simply a shared fantasy.</p>
<p>The anthropologist might say: things happen to people, and when they cannot explain why, they reach for explanations like the evil eye. It becomes a convenient way of accounting for the inexplicable. Someone falls ill for no discernible reason, no one can figure it out, and people say it might be the eye. So that dynamic exists too.</p>
<p>But the hadith <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَيْنُ حَقٌّ</span> — the evil eye is real — is a sound, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحِيحٌ</span> hadith. And it is worth considering its source. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was someone who actively worked to dismantle superstition. When the moon eclipsed on the day his son Ibrahim died, the Arabs believed a lunar eclipse meant a great person had died. It was a perfect moment for a charlatan to exploit — and in fact, people became Muslim that day precisely because they thought the moon had eclipsed for his son. He went out and announced to the people: the sun and the moon are signs of Allah; they do not eclipse for anyone''s death or life. He dispelled that superstition on the spot.</p>
<p>So when a man of that character said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَيْنُ حَقٌّ</span> in a sound hadith, he was telling us that there is something genuinely real in the world called the evil eye, and it deserves to be taken seriously. In English, people say, &quot;if looks could kill&quot; — there is a folk intuition that a gaze carries force, that something passes between people through the eyes. So: the hadith establishes that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> devours good deeds the way fire devours dry wood. And the reason for that becomes clear when he explains it: if you desire that someone lose a blessing they possess, you are envious of them. It can be anything — a house, a car, a job. Someone received a promotion over you and you wish they would fail, get fired, so that you might take their position. Someone has a spouse you envy, and you think they do not deserve that person and you wish the marriage would fall apart. The variations are endless, but at the core of every one of them is the desire for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span> — the blessing — to be lost. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span>, from the Arabic root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span>, relates to softness, ease — it is the opposite of hardship.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span> are things that ease life — that is what a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span> is. Now, obviously, a blessing can be hidden inside what looks like a hardship, and a hardship can be hidden inside what looks like a blessing. In English we say &quot;a blessing in disguise&quot; or &quot;every cloud has a silver lining&quot; — expressions that remind us to be careful about judging what is and is not a blessing. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span> is something Allah gives, and Allah''s name is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُنْعِم</span>: the Giver of blessings. If Allah gives a person a blessing and you wish that person would lose it, you are essentially saying Allah was wrong to place that blessing with them. You are passing judgment on Allah''s wisdom.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 22">page 22</div>
<p>One of my favorite stories — and some people have heard this — concerns <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأَصْمَعِيُّ</span>, who used to travel the desert encampments collecting words and lore. He was once at a Bedouin''s tent, and, as is part of Bedouin culture then and now, the women came in, served, and sat. This man had a remarkably beautiful wife, and he himself was remarkably ugly. When the man stepped out to slaughter a sheep in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأَصْمَعِيُّ</span>s honor, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأَصْمَعِيُّ</span> could not resist asking the woman: &quot;How did you end up with him? How did a woman as beautiful as you come to be with a man who looks like that?&quot; The woman said — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأَصْمَعِيُّ</span> is the one relating this, which tells you something about the kind of man he was — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِتَّقِ اللَّهَ</span>,&quot; fear Allah. Then she said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَعَلَّهُ أَحْسَنَ إِلَى رَبِّهِ وَأَنَا جَزَاؤُهُ</span>&quot; — perhaps he did good things with his Lord, and I am his reward. Or perhaps I did some bad things, and he is my punishment.</p>
<p>The point <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأَصْمَعِيُّ</span> was making is that Allah has a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَة</span>, and you do not know what it is. So you must be very careful before asking, &quot;Why is that person with that person?&quot; or &quot;What is that doing there?&quot; Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُنْعِم</span>, the Giver of blessings, and when you question the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span>, you are questioning the One who gave it. That is why Allah hates <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> — and it is precisely why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَانٌ</span> fell into disobedience.</p>
<p>The difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَانٌ</span> and Adam, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, is that when Adam made his mistake, he made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَاسِدٌ</span> does not make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> — cannot make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> — because part of his problem is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>. He believes he is more worthy and that the other person does not deserve what they have. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>: &quot;I am better than him; he does not deserve what he has.&quot; A person in that state finds it nearly impossible to admit he is wrong, so he never turns away from his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span>. He simply does not turn away from it.</p>
<p>So when the author says that the true meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> is that if you could, through some scheme, eliminate that blessing yourself, you would — that is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> definition he is pointing toward. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا يَسْلَمُ الْمُؤْمِنُ مِنْ ثَلَاثٍ</span>&quot; — the believer will never be entirely free of three things — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> is one of them. The other two are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span>, which he will address later. Since no believer is ever fully safe from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span>, the cure prescribed is: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَبْرِي</span> — do not act on it. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> says: if you feel envy, do not harm the person, do not oppress them. As long as you do not actually do something to cause that person to lose their blessing, your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> does not fall into the category that destroys your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَنَاتٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> that devours your good deeds is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> where, if you were able, you would actually cause the person to lose that blessing — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَرَأَى الِابْتِلَاءَ</span>&quot; — and then act on it.</p>
<p>Then the author says: if your fear of Allah is what holds you back from harming the person, then that is not the kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> that will damage you. But if you would act on it given the chance — that is dangerous. He then attributes this distinction to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ الْغَزَالِيِّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللّٰهُ</span>, and that is the basis of his judgment here.</p>
<p>Then he says that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ الْغَزَالِيِّ</span> also said: if you hate the envy you feel — if you despise that part of yourself that is generating it — then that is a sign that you are not truly an envious person. Hating the experience of envy within yourself is evidence that you are not an envious person at your core. This again points to the recognition that you have a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُوح</span> — which are, in a real sense, the same thing, except that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُوح</span> that still carries the tarnish of this world; it has not been purified. Recognizing that these stirrings in the heart are part of that taint — part of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَانَ</span> that accumulates over the heart through wrong actions and through being immersed in this world — and then detesting those stirrings: that is a sign that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُوح</span>, the pure aspect of yourself, is the stronger force within you.</p>
<p>Then he turns to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَوَاءٌ</span> — the treatment — and as always he gives us two. The rectification of the heart requires two things: knowledge and action. He has already given the knowledge base: you must understand that the path out of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> is to refuse to harm the person, to have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> of Allah, and to recognize just how destructive <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> is. That is your foundation of knowledge.</p>
<p>Now he gives the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَمَل</span> — the practical work. The first thing is not to follow your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْهَوَى</span> is caprice, whim, passion. The word comes from a root meaning &quot;to fall,&quot; and it is related to the word for wind — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوَاء</span>. The nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span> is exactly like the nature of wind: it sweeps in, shakes everything, does its damage, and then dies down and moves on. Sometimes it rises in the self; sometimes it subsides. If you follow the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span>, you are following something unstable, something with no fixed foundation — unlike the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقْل</span>, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَثْبِيت</span>, fixed and firm. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقٌّ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَثْبِيت</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَاطِل</span> is something that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَزُولُ</span> — something that passes away. Look at history and you can see it clearly: falsehoods rise and fall, rise and fall, rise and fall. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقٌّ</span>, by contrast, is always recognized for what it is by the people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقٌّ</span>, no matter where or when. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَاطِل</span> shifts from people to people; it has no foundation. So he is saying: do not follow your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span>. And it is worth noting that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَاوِيَة</span> — one of the names of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَهَنَّم</span> — is from the same root, because your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span> is what pulls you down into it. &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمُّهُ هَاوِيَةٌ</span>&quot; — its mother is the abyss. So when he says to do the opposite of what your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span> demands, he then gives you a concrete example.</p>
<p>&quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَنَظِيرِهِ إِنْ كَانَ ضَرَّاً</span>&quot; — like benefiting the person when your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span> is urging you to harm him. When your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> wants to cause harm, that is exactly the moment for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهَدَة</span>: you do the opposite and actively benefit the person you feel envy toward. This is a crucial point. If you feel envy toward someone and you want to harm them, you go out and give them a gift. You do the very opposite of what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> is screaming at you to do. That goes against the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, earns reward with Allah, and simultaneously begins to dissolve the disease.</p>
<p>Or — another thing he mentions — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ حَيْثُ لَكَانَ ذَمًّا زَيَّنًا</span>&quot;: praise him when your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> is itching to say something bad about him. If someone you feel <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> toward is mentioned in a gathering and you feel the urge to say, &quot;Oh, you know what he did?&quot; — that is the moment to say something good about him instead. And there is always something good you can say about someone. Even about the worst of people, you can find something. So you choose. When you want to say something bad, say something good instead.</p>
<p>One effect of doing this is that it tends to soften that person''s heart toward you. As their heart inclines toward you — and now obviously this is not guaranteed, especially if they carry their own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> toward you and have not done this work on themselves — but generally, good people are inclined to love those who treat them well. If you do good toward that person, they will tend to respond in kind. And when they do, it changes something in your own heart as well. What <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيدِي أَحْمَد زَرُّوق</span> is saying is: if you actually apply this, you will witness a transformation — in their heart and in yours.</p>
<p>Then he adds another dimension of knowledge: you should know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> harms the one who envies, not the one who is envied. In this world, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَاسِدٌ</span> is perpetually anxious and agitated. Why did he get that promotion? He didn''t deserve it. He goes home and spends two hours complaining to his wife about how so-and-so got the position he should have had. He becomes consumed by the object of his envy. It is a disease of the heart that produces nothing but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَمٌّ</span> — a persistent sadness — and it is completely pointless. There is absolutely no meaning in it. Envying another person is, at its core, an irrational act. The Arabs have a powerful saying: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَاسِدُ لَا يَسُودُ</span>&quot; — the envious person never attains leadership. The very fact of the resentment prevents the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَاسِدٌ</span> from ever accomplishing anything. A person free of envy, when he sees someone else doing something impressive, may be motivated to go out and do it himself. He does not sit there stewing over why that is happening for someone else and not for him. He simply acts. The one who envies, by contrast, will never achieve <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيَادَة</span>.</p>
<p>One of the striking things about the Muslim world today is how much envy saturates it. When many Muslims look at Europeans and Americans — even those who dress it up in Islamic rhetoric about the evils of the West — the underlying reality for a great many of them is simply: they have the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> and we do not. That is the root of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span>. Or consider the case of those who got the oil wealth: you have people across the Arab world and beyond perpetually denouncing the Gulf Arabs — they are wasting the Muslims'' money, it all belongs to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, and so on. But the honest question to put to those critics is: if oil had been discovered in their own country, would they have been distributing it as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَطَاءٌ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ</span> across the Muslim world? That is the question they need to sit with. It is easy to condemn the wealthy when you have nothing. The real test is what you do when the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> suddenly lands in your lap.</p>
<p>And in fairness, there is a strong case that the Gulf Arabs give far more than most other nations would in their position, because generosity is deeply woven into traditional desert Arab culture, and there are genuinely many extraordinarily generous people there. So the question worth asking yourself is: is your anger coming from resentment and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span>, or is it genuinely driven by an altruistic concern for wealth to be used properly? That is a real question, and it deserves an honest answer.</p>
<p>You can also look at the communist revolution. Communism is essentially institutionalized <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span>. Marx''s writing is saturated with resentment — it is entirely about taking back what rightfully belongs to &quot;us&quot; from the rich. Now, the wealthy love to hear this framing because it lets them dismiss any critique as mere envy. But from the perspective of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, both sides carry an obligation. The poor have an obligation not to envy the rich. The rich have an obligation not to belittle the poor. It goes both ways. The fact that one side is not fulfilling its obligation does not justify becoming a communist and trying to overturn the entire order. And nowhere in Islam does it say you must remain impoverished. You can go out and earn. But wealth does tend, historically, to concentrate in families — and that pattern persists even today. America is held up as the land of opportunity, where you can come, work eighty-hour weeks, open your grocery store or whatever it may be, grind hard enough, and acquire the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. We already covered in an earlier section why that path has its own pitfalls — because often the moment you finally acquire it all, you drop dead. But even so, the concentration of wealth is remarkable: in this country, well over ninety percent of the wealth sits in the hands of less than three percent of the population. What most people think of as wealthy — a millionaire with two, three, four million dollars — is not real wealth by that standard. And even Bill Gates, for all the attention he receives, was at one point worth only about thirty percent of what Rockefeller was worth in real terms. He was, in that sense, an upstart.</p>
<p>The point is that money is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَوَاءٌ</span> — it circulates among people — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَيْنَ ٱلْأَغْنِيَآءِ</span>&quot; — and one of the most historically radical things Allah did in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> was to legislate inheritance laws specifically designed to break monopolies. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> inheritance system is built to disperse wealth. In traditional cultures, the firstborn son — the progenitor — inherited everything. In English culture, women could literally lose their homes if they lacked a male protector. That is one of Jane Austen''s recurring themes: women watching everything slip away the moment their father died, because some uncle or distant male cousin inherited it all under a system that gave women nothing. Austen saw it as an injustice — even though she was primarily a comic writer, she recognized this as a real wrong done to women. In Islam, Allah broke that pattern entirely. A woman suddenly had her own inheritance. And what did that mean? When a woman married outside the family, wealth moved outside the family — which is precisely why wealthy families so often preferred to marry within the family, trying to keep the money consolidated.</p>
<p>But one can see that Islam genuinely attempts to break down the monopoly of wealth. Once that monopoly takes hold, you get what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> calls a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَوَاءٌ</span> — wealth circulating only among the rich — and that inevitably breeds resentment. In classical Islamic civilization, there was traditionally a very strong middle class. Where a strong middle class exists, social pressure is far less severe than in a society where, say, ten percent of the population holds all the wealth while ninety percent lives in poverty.</p>
<p>That kind of extreme gap creates massive tension. When forty to sixty percent of people occupy a middle ground, it remains possible for those at the bottom to move upward, because the distance is not insurmountable. But when the gap is enormous, it breeds resentment and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span>.</p>
<p>So poor people begin to envy the rich, and at that point a society becomes ripe for something like the Communist Manifesto — and we know how many millions died in communist revolutions, all of it fueled by resentment. What is also striking about Marxism is that nowhere does Marx criticize industrialization or mass production itself. He never questions the technological society and the direction it was heading. Baudrillard wrote a fascinating book addressing precisely this blind spot in communist critique: the factories themselves are never challenged. Marx''s answer was simply to hand the factories over to the workers who run them — but whether factory life is something we genuinely want for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمَا</span>n beings is a question he never asked.</p>
<p>That is an important thing to reflect on. Much of what people call righteous indignation is, in reality, nothing more than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> — envy and resentment dressed up in moral language. This has played out visibly in the Muslim world: many communists of the 1970s and 80s became Islamists, simply swapping the vocabulary. The language of communist social justice was replaced with the language of Islamic social justice, but the underlying impulse was the same. Islam was being used as a vehicle for anger rooted not in sincerity toward Allah, but in dissatisfaction with one''s worldly position. People must be genuinely wary of that — it is a subtle and treacherous thing.</p>
<p>He then makes an additional point: envy does not benefit you in the slightest, nor does it harm the one you envy. The person you resent is not going to lose his blessing because you feel envious of it. Allah gave it to him, and Allah will take it away — not you. So envy is, in the end, a complete waste of time and energy.</p>
<p>He then moves into the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْبَاب</span> — the causes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> — which is particularly important, because if you understand the causes, you can recognize whether you are afflicted and know how to rid yourself of it.</p>
<p>The first cause he mentions is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدَاوَة</span> — enmity. When someone is your enemy, their good fortune infuriates you. Consider how many people who are loudly anti-American are simultaneously obsessed with American culture — the would-be militant wearing Nike shoes, a Mickey Mouse T-shirt, ordering hamburgers and milkshakes. American culture has spread globally with remarkable speed. People in Beijing line up at McDonald''s. One has to ask honestly: is the anti-American sentiment rooted in a genuine perception of injustice and oppression, or is it simply envy of the fact that America happens to be the most powerful country in the world by worldly measures? People must be very honest with themselves about where their motivations actually lie. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدَاوَة</span>, then, is a cause of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span>. You may dislike a colleague at work, and so when good things happen to him, anger rises in you — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> born of enmity.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 23">page 23</div>
<p>The next cause is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخَايُل</span> — a kind of rivalrous competition. There is almost a darkly comic dimension to it, though it can turn vicious. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخَايُل</span> involves two or more people competing for the same object of desire. The clearest example is siblings competing for a parent''s love and attention. A mother has five children but only so many hours in a day, so the children vie for her notice. There is a book — I believe by Frank Sulloway — called <em>Born to Rebel</em>, which is an extensive statistical study of birth order. Sulloway argues that personality is shaped significantly by this competition for parental attention. With sweeping theories one must always be cautious, but there is clearly something to it. First-borns tend to be less rebellious; later-borns, seeing the first-born enjoying all the advantages, are more inclined to challenge the status quo. Most of the historically rebellious figures in social history, he argues, were later-borns. It is an interesting study.</p>
<p>That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخَايُل</span>. Another manifestation is students competing for a teacher''s favour — the classic teacher''s-pet dynamic. Another is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَاءُ الدَّرَا</span> — the disease of second wives. In a polygamous household, the husband''s time is finite, and so the wives compete. This is precisely why Islamic law is so insistent on fairness between wives: whatever you give one, you must give the other, and the same applies to how one divides one''s nights. Significantly, the Arabic word for a second wife, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داراء</span>, literally means harm. Whether or not it was the first wife who coined the term, the Arabs clearly understood something about the psychology of plural marriage — that the second wife introduces a kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داراء</span> into the life of the first. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخَايُل</span>.</p>
<p>The next cause is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> — arrogance. When someone arrogant sees another person getting ahead, he thinks: <em>he doesn''t deserve that; I''m better than him.</em> This was precisely the attitude of figures like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَلِيدُ بْنُ الْمُغِيرَةِ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو جَهْل</span> toward the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. They called him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَتِيمُ بَنِي هَاشِمٍ</span> — the orphan of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي هَاشِمٍ</span> — or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ أَبِي كَبْشَةَ</span>, referring to his foster father''s lineage. Their position was: he doesn''t deserve the blessing of revelation from the heavens. <em>We</em> are more deserving. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>, which is fairly straightforward.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَزُّز</span> is subtler. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَزُّز</span> is not about wanting the other person to fall — it is about a person being comfortable as long as things are equal. As long as we are on the same level, it is fine. But then you receive a promotion and I do not, and I begin to think: now he is going to look down on me. The equilibrium has shifted. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَزُّز</span> is an attempt to preserve one''s sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِزَّة</span>, one''s dignity and self-worth. It is, at root, a manifestation of poor self-esteem. The person feels that their worth has been diminished by another''s gain, and envy arises as an attempt to restore balance.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 24">page 24</div>
<p>You can see this in the words of those early opponents of Islam who said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَوْلَا نُزِّلَ هَذَا ٱلْقُرْآنُ عَلَىٰ رَجُلٍۢ مِّنَ ٱلْقَرْيَتَيْنِ عَظِيمٍ</span> — &quot;Why was this Quran not revealed to a great man from one of the two cities?&quot; In other words, had it been revealed to al-Walid ibn Mughira, they could have accepted it — bowing to him would not have disturbed them because he was already their superior. But this is the orphan of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي هَاشِمٍ</span>. How can he be above us? They wanted the equilibrium preserved. And similarly those who said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَٰذَا بَشَرٌ مِّثْلُنَا</span> — &quot;This is just a man like us.&quot; How can he be a prophet while we are not? If it were a king, they could accept it — a king is already above them. But their next-door neighbour? He should have nothing that they do not have. And Pharaoh''s contempt for Moses carries a similar logic — how can a prophet come from a people who are our slaves?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَجُّب</span> is different. It is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> and not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَزُّز</span> — it is more a kind of stunned disbelief. <em>I cannot believe this is happening.</em> This person won the jackpot? I have known him my whole life. It does not make sense. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> here is rooted in a kind of incredulity — an inability to accept that this particular person could have received such a blessing.</p>
<p>Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الرِّيَاسَةِ</span> — love of leadership and the desire to maintain one''s position of authority. A person may not want another to receive a blessing precisely because it would disturb the hierarchy, shifting the balance of authority between them. This is similar to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> but distinct: the person either already holds a position of authority over the other or desires to, and another''s gain threatens that arrangement.</p>
<p>And finally, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُحُّ النَّفْسِ</span> — covetousness. This too is a cause of envy. You see that someone has something and a craving seizes you — you simply want it. Allah speaks to this when He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَن يُوقَ شُحَّ نَفْسِهِۦ فَأُو۟لَٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلْمُفْلِحُونَ</span> — those who are saved from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُحٌّ</span> of their own souls, those are the ones who truly succeed.</p>
<p>Now, one of the concepts Imam al-Ghazali returns to repeatedly is that these diseases are woven into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمَا</span>n nature, and so the task is not simply to eliminate them but to transform them — to redirect them into something beneficial. He draws on a parallel: certain Native American tribes would give their children names associated with physical disability, like Lame Deer. The reasoning was that animals with disabilities tend to overcompensate, becoming more alert and more resourceful than their peers. The disability becomes an advantage. In worldly terms, this is what genuinely successful people do with their weaknesses. And what Imam al-Ghazali is saying is that this is precisely what those seeking the next life must do with the darker aspects of their character — turn liabilities into assets.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 25">page 25</div>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا حَسَدَ إِلَّا فِي اثْنَتَيْنِ</span> — &quot;There is no envy except in two cases&quot;: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجُلٍ آتَاهُ اللَّهُ مَالًا</span> — a man whom Allah has given wealth — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَسَلَّطَهُ عَلَى هَلَكَتِهِ فِي الْحَقِّ</span> — and he deploys it entirely in the service of what is right. One narration adds that he gives it out by night and by day. That is a worthy object of envy — not envy that you want him to lose his blessing, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِيبَة</span>, the positive longing to be like him, to have wealth so that you too can give. This is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِيبَة</span> in Arabic, and it is distinct from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span>. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِيبَة</span> you want the other person to keep what they have; you simply wish you had the same. The second case is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجُلٍ آتَاهُ اللَّهُ حِكْمَةً</span> — a man given wisdom — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَهُوَ يَقْضِي بِهَا وَيُعَلِّمُهَا</span> — and he judges by it and teaches it to others. The message is clear: do not envy people over trivial worldly things — it is not worth it. Reserve your envy for what is truly high, what belongs to the next life. And when you do, that envy becomes a spur to action, driving you to achieve what they have achieved.</p>
<p>He then turns to the question of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> directed at a disbeliever or a sinful Muslim — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاجِر</span>, someone openly engaged in grave sins. In that case, he says, a form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> resembling <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَاءُ الدَّرَا</span> is actually permissible. The crucial distinction, however, is the reason.</p>
<p>Earlier the point was made that envying non-Muslims simply because they enjoy worldly prosperity you do not have is not a sound basis for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span>. What makes it permissible here is that they possess worldly power or resources they are actively using for harm and oppression. When that is the case, the feeling is justified.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'envy';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>He then addresses <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَيَاءُ الْمَذْمُومُ</span> — blameworthy modesty. Modesty itself is a virtue; the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَيَاءُ مِنَ الْإِيمَانِ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَاء</span> is from faith. But blameworthy modesty is a different thing entirely: it is the shyness that prevents you from speaking against wrongdoing when you witness it. You see a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْكَرٌ</span> and you should say something, but you hold back out of embarrassment. A grown child, for instance, may feel that the modesty owed to parents prevents them from addressing a wrong the parents are committing — but a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْكَرٌ</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْكَرٌ</span> regardless of who is committing it.</p>
<p>A few important conditions attach to the obligation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْكَارُ الْمُنْكَرِ</span>. First, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْكَرٌ</span> must be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجْمَعٌ عَلَيْهِ</span> — agreed upon by scholars — before you can speak against it. You cannot make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْكَار</span> of something that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْتَلَفٌ فِيهِ</span>, a matter of scholarly disagreement. Wherever difference of opinion exists, you must proceed with great caution, because what one scholar considers a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْكَرٌ</span> another may consider permissible. It is said of Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَازِرِيُّ</span> that his knowledge was so vast he rarely made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْكَار</span> of anyone, because he could almost always find a valid proof for their action somewhere in the tradition. This is something Muslims desperately need to hear today, given how freely they condemn one another.</p>
<p>Blameworthy modesty also includes the shyness that prevents a person from asking questions about the religion. This is why — as Aisha <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهَا</span> remarked — the best women were those of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمْصَار</span>, the great cities, because modesty did not stop them from seeking knowledge of the deen. She was praising them: despite the sensitivity of certain topics, it did not deter them. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> himself once had a woman come to him asking about menstruation; she kept pressing with questions until eventually he asked Aisha to show her what to do, because the subject had become difficult for him to address directly.</p>
<p>And Aisha could answer the question because she was a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَقِيهًا</span>. But the point is that the woman wanted to know. Some women would even send, in a small box, a cloth from their menstrual period to ask Aisha about its ruling — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُكْم</span> — regarding color, to make sure they understood when the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيْض</span> began and ended.</p>
<p>Obviously, most women would not feel comfortable doing that at all, given their modesty. But those women were so firmly committed to doing what Allah commanded that their modesty did not prevent them. This tendency runs stronger in women than in men, but it applies to both — there are men who are so modest that they refrain from asking questions they ought to ask.</p>
<p>Then he said — and this is from the line of poetry — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَّا حَيَاءُ أُخْرَى مِنْ كَمَا جَرَى، لِرَمْسِ صَفَا إِذْ زَيْنَبًا تَكَمْ مَرَى</span> — as for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَاءٌ</span> of generosity, an example of it is what occurred with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُصْطَفَى ﷺ</span> when he married Zaynab. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَمْ مَرَا</span> refers to spending the night — that is, the first night of their marriage.</p>
<p>What happened was that the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> had married Zaynab <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهَا</span>, and he invited people over for a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيمَة</span>, a wedding celebration. The guests came, ate, and then some of them simply did not leave. Three men stayed behind, and the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> — generous as he was — sat with them. But he had just married, and it was his wedding night. He was waiting for them to leave, while they, for their part, were savoring his company and did not want to go.</p>
<p>So he got up and left the room, hoping to signal to them that it was time to leave. He returned, and they were still there. He sat down again, then eventually left once more. It was at that point that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span> was revealed — instructing believers to leave when they had finished their business, not to linger, to move on. The verse essentially said: when you finish eating, go.</p>
<p>That kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَاءٌ</span> is not blameworthy, because there was no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرْعِيٌّ</span> obligation being neglected — no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُكْمٌ شَرْعِيٌّ</span> at stake. It was purely out of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَمٌ</span>, his noble generosity, that he did not simply say, &quot;It is my wedding night; please go now.&quot; Some people would have had no trouble saying that at all. But it was not in his nature to do so, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, and so the point is that this is not a blameworthy form of modesty.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَشْوَاقُ الْقَلَمِ الْوَلِيمَةِ</span> — he had fed them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَخَرَجُوا عَنِ الْحِسِّ وَثَرَثَتِهِ</span> — and they left, except for three. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا بُوَّ وَلَا مُؤْتَمَرَ بِالْبَلَاخِ، فَهُوَ مِنْ مَحَاسِنِ الْأَخْلَاقِ</span> — they stayed, and he did not tell them to leave, and that is from good character. It is not blameworthy.</p>
<p>Then he said — referring to a line of poetry — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَانَ رَجُلًا صَرِيحًا</span>, and it echoes a hadith: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَوْ كَانَ الْحَيَاءُ رَجُلًا، لَكَانَ رَجُلًا صَالِحًا</span> — if modesty were a man, he would be a righteous man. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> used this expression to convey that modesty is among the highest of qualities.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 23">page 23</div>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'blameworthy modesty';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>Now the text turns to the heart''s engagement in matters that do not concern it — the subject of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَلْد</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْدُ الْقَلْبِ</span> is the movement or stirring of the heart. He will go into considerable detail later about the various types of thoughts, and we will get to that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>. What he establishes here is that if the heart moves and dwells upon something prohibited, then that dwelling itself is prohibited. This is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخَيُّل</span> — not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَكُّر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَكُّر</span> is reflection on intelligible, rational matters, while <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخَيُّل</span> is mental imaging of sensory things, things perceived through the senses.</p>
<p>He says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَلْدُ، حَيْثُ مَا كَانَ، إِنَّمَا يَحْرُمُ وَحَيْثُ كَانَ فِيمَا حَرُمَ</span> — wherever the heart''s movement lands, it becomes prohibited when it lands on what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. He gives an example: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَالْفِكْرِ فِي مَحَاسِنِ الْأَجَانِبِ</span> — reflecting on or fantasizing about the attractive qualities of those to whom one is not married, whether male or female. Allowing the mind to dwell on what it should not is itself considered prohibited.</p>
<p>Also included in this is dwelling on the weaknesses and faults of Muslims, whether they are absent or present. Simply sitting and mentally cataloguing the faults of other Muslims is something one should not do. There is a saying: there is a tree in paradise for the one who is so preoccupied with his own faults that he forgets the faults of others. His own self-examination keeps him too busy to scrutinize everyone else.</p>
<p>The older you get, the more seriously you should take that. Life is simply too short. Everyone has faults. If you spend your time thinking about other people''s faults, you are a fool — the whole point is to rid yourself of your own.</p>
<p>Also worth noting — because it appears in the commentary — is that reflecting on the nature of Allah''s Essence, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span> of Allah, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَات</span> of Allah is beyond the reach of human reflection, and one cannot think about it in any meaningful or permissible sense.</p>
<p>I will go until 2:30 <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, and then we can pray.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'fantasizing';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next topic: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَصْلُ الْخَوْفِ مِنَ الْفَقْرِ سُوءُ الظَّنِّ بِهِ تَعَالَى وَدَوَافِرُ حُسْنِهِ وَأَنْعُمِهِ أَنَّمَا دَرَّهُ يَعْقِلُ وَأَنَّمَا تُرْسَكَهُ لَكَ يَصُولُ</span> — the root of the fear of poverty is a bad opinion of Allah.</p>
<p>Fear of poverty is one of the diseases of the heart. In the Quran, Allah tells us that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> promises you poverty: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَعِدُكُمُ ٱلْفَقْرَ</span> — he promises you that you will lose your wealth. That is one of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> tools.</p>
<p>This fear is particularly alive right now. We are living through a time of real <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَوْفُ الْفَقْرِ</span>, with uncertainty spreading across the world — collapses in various places, Americans growing anxious about markets, portfolios shrinking, and so on. But the truth is, the people worrying about this have little idea what real poverty looks like. If you want to see poverty, go to parts of India, West Africa, or places like Kosovo — there you will see it plainly. Most people speaking of poverty have never encountered it.</p>
<p>Fear of poverty is, at its root, a bad opinion of Allah — because Allah has promised to provide for you. In Arabic, this is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُمْلَةٌ شَرْطِيَّةٌ</span>, a conditional sentence: if you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, I will provide for you. That is a direct promise from Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. If you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, He will provide, and He will do it from sources you would never expect — not necessarily your job or your monthly salary, but from wherever He wills. The condition, however, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>.</p>
<p>Without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, Allah is not obligated to care for you. If you are not fulfilling what He has asked of you as a condition of walking this earth with dignity, you will walk it in humiliation. This is especially true for Muslims, who should always remember that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> is the paradise of the non-believer. Whatever good they have is all they are getting. Let them enjoy it — seriously, reflect on that. This is all they are getting.</p>
<p>There is a story whose meaning is sound, even if scholars differ on exactly who it is attributed to. There was a Jewish man in Cairo who sold oil — he also heated oil and sold it hot. He was covered in oil, his clothes were dirty, and he was poor. One day a famous and wealthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالِمٌ</span> rode out on a white horse, well-dressed and impressive. This Jewish man approached the horse and said: do you narrate the hadith that says this world is the paradise of the disbeliever and the prison of the believer? The scholar said yes. The man said: then I have a question — what kind of paradise am I in, and what kind of prison are you in?</p>
<p>The scholar looked up, then looked down. Then he said to him: when I contemplate what awaits you in the next world because of your disbelief, I can only conclude that you are in paradise right now. And when I contemplate what Allah has prepared for me in the next world because of my belief, I can only see this life as a prison. According to the story, the man then said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ</span>. But the point, regardless, is that everything is relative.</p>
<p>Poverty is a relative condition. And the fear of poverty is also a foolish thing, because what benefit does it bring? The American humorist Mark Twain said that most of the problems he worried about never actually came to pass. You build up all these concerns in your mind, and they never materialize — they exist only in your head. That is part of what makes fear of poverty so corrosive. And if you are already impoverished, what are you afraid of? That is simply your state. But if you have money and live in constant fear of losing it, what is the point of having it? The anxiety it generates may be worse than having nothing.</p>
<p>There is a hadith: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْهَمُّ نِصْفُ الْهَرَمِ</span> — anxiety is half of aging. And another: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبِرُّ يَزِيدُ الْعُمْرَ</span> — righteousness lengthens your life. One interpretation of this that I find particularly striking is that righteous people do not carry anxiety. People who are irreligious live in a constant inner turmoil; their hearts are never at ease, so they age faster. They wear out sooner than others. Their lives dissipate more quickly.</p>
<p>So fear of poverty is a waste of time and energy.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 26">page 26</div>
<p>The cure for it is to have a good opinion of Allah. Allah says — and this is the meaning — I do not want them to feed Me or provide for Me. Allah says that. He expects nothing of that kind from you. He then describes Himself as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّزَّاقُ ذُو ٱلْقُوَّةِ ٱلْمَتِينُ</span> — the Provider, the Possessor of enduring strength. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَزَّاق</span> is a form of intensive exaggeration: always providing, endlessly providing. So Allah is the Provider — have a good opinion of Him.</p>
<p>Then the text says: know that giving does not diminish Allah''s dominion. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُلْك</span> is not reduced by what He distributes. If He makes someone wealthy, that does not take anything from His kingdom. This is also why you should not harbor <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span> toward someone who has been given wealth — just as Allah made that person wealthy, He can make you wealthy. But the foundation should be contentment. That is why the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القَنَاعَةُ كَنزٌ لَا يَفْنَى</span> — contentment is a treasure that is never exhausted.</p>
<p>Know also that what has been allotted for you will reach you. There is a difference of opinion among the scholars about the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>. Some hold that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> written for you — which was decreed in your mother''s womb — consists only of what you will actually consume and use in this life. Others say it is everything you possess. The dominant opinion, however, is that it is only what you use. If you die leaving behind a million dollars you never touched, that wealth was not your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> — it was the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> of those who inherited it. If you owned a house you never once visited, it was not your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>, even though you held legal title to it. You derived no benefit from it, so it was not decreed for you.</p>
<p>I came across a book recently titled along the lines of <em>How to Live Well</em> — or more bluntly, the idea that you should live wealthy and die broke. Just spend it all. That is, of course, a problematic notion from an Islamic standpoint, because Islam encourages thinking about your children and those who come after you. It is praiseworthy to leave something behind. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> himself did not leave behind worldly goods — but that is because prophets are not permitted to leave the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> as inheritance. That is their particular station.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'fear of poverty';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next disease is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاهَنَة</span>. This is an important one.</p>
<p>A person who uses his religion as a means of securing worldly benefit is a sycophantic hypocrite — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاهِن</span>. In his transaction, he has been cheated in the end. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاهَنَة</span> is when you leverage your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span> to fix your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>.</p>
<p>Ibn Khaldun put it plainly: we try to stitch our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> together by tearing our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span> apart. The result, he said, is that we end up with neither — our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span> is gone, and our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> was never actually repaired. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> will not rectify your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> through the destruction of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>. He will not grant <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> in that transaction. You lose both.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاهَنَة</span>, practically speaking, is when you fail to speak against something wrong because it involves your business, your boss, your livelihood — some worldly stake. You perform a kind of religious hypocrisy out of fear of losing a worldly benefit. That is the essence of it. Now, if something is genuinely none of your business, or it is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاجِب</span> upon you to act — and in many cases it is not — then you have a choice, and staying silent may be perfectly acceptable. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاهَنَة</span> is when you actually sacrifice your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span> because of your love of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. That is where the line is crossed.</p>
<p>There is a related concept worth distinguishing: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَارَاةٌ</span>. The root of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاهَنَة</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُهْن</span> — oil, like the oil used in cosmetics — something that covers and conceals. It implies a cosmetic covering of something rotten, and scholars have described it as a form of spiritual deception.</p>
<p>Shaykh Ali al-Adawi says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَاتِمَة</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاهَنَة</span> is when a man surrenders his religion in order to protect his wealth or his life — sitting with people of disobedience, keeping their company, praising their actions, displaying approval of their condition, and offering no condemnation, all for some worldly gain. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَارَاة</span>, by contrast, is expen<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g something of your worldly life — your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> — for the sake of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>, or for some legitimate worldly benefit, or for both. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُدَارِي</span> them, keep the peace, as long as you do not compromise your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>. The saying goes: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَارِهِمْ مَا دُمْتَ فِي دَارِهِمْ، وَحَيِّهِمْ مَا دُمْتَ فِي حَيِّهِمْ، وَرَاعِهِمْ مَا دُمْتَ فِي أَرْضِهِمْ</span> — greet them and get along with them as long as you are among them, and do not make unnecessary enemies. That is permissible. It is not obligatory, but it is permissible.</p>
<p>If it costs your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>, it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاهَنَة</span>. If it does not cost your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>, it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَارَاةٌ</span>, and there is nothing wrong with it.</p>
<p>A small example: my brother-in-law had just arrived from Morocco and drove forty-five minutes to reach an office, only to be told he needed an appointment he did not have. He asked if anything could be done, and the clerk said no. He said something along the lines of, &quot;That''s America, I suppose,&quot; and I told him: do not say that. The way people will hear that is &quot;go back where you came from,&quot; and most Americans will take it exactly that way. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَارَاة</span> would have been to stay calm, be gracious, and make an appointment — because you might very well need to return.</p>
<p>Now she remembers that you said that, so she doesn''t write it down in the appointment book. When you show up, there''s no record of your visit. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَارَاة</span>, then, covers situations like that — where it is permissible, even though you would rather say something else, to say something pleasant as a means of securing a worldly benefit, or of avoi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g a worldly harm, or both.</p>
<p>It can also apply to someone who wants to help another person, and it is not considered <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِفَاق</span>. This is not hypocrisy. Islam recognizes that there are things in the world one simply has to navigate, and this is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُخْصَة</span> — a dispensation — for people.</p>
<p>Another example is using whatever influence you have on someone else''s behalf. If you are close to a minister and someone has been wronged, and you are in a position to get in and help that person, that is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَارَاة</span>. Your relationship with the minister becomes a means of obtaining justice for someone else.</p>
<p>All of these are praiseworthy in Islam. By contrast, the kind of culture that prevails in some circles today — the &quot;I''m okay, you''re okay&quot; attitude — is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاهَنَة</span>. People do wrong, and everyone says it is fine, there is nothing wrong with it.</p>
<p>A conspiracy of silence takes hold. In Islam, by contrast, a believer is the mirror of his brother or sister — he wants to see them improve, and so he acts accor<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>gly.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 46">page 46</div>
<p>The sheikh now turns to the root causes of the next disease. He says its origin is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span> — base desire — and doing works of worship in order to earn a place in the hearts of others. That is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> is performing an action that is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْبَة</span> — something that draws one near to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — but doing it for other than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s sake. Whoever does that is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَاءٍ</span>. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> called it a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, specifically <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشِّرْكُ الْأَصْغَرُ</span> — the lesser polytheism.</p>
<p>In a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that he does not fear for his community that they will worship the sun, the stars, or the moon, but that he fears for them that they will worship other than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. And in another <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>: &quot;The thing I fear most for my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّهْ</span> is doing things for other than the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.&quot; That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>.</p>
<p>Now, if someone is a skilled archer, for instance, and people are watching, and someone else arrives and that person suddenly performs with extra effort to impress them — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَذْمُومٌ مُرُوءَةً، لَا شَرْعًا</span>. In other words, it is blameworthy in terms of human dignity and comportment, but we must distinguish between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرُوءَةٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرُوءَةٌ</span> refers to the human dignity a person is expected to preserve. Showing off in a non-religious context is blameworthy, but it is not the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> that religious <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> constitutes.</p>
<p>So the source of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> is desire — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span>. You want people to think well of you; you want a place in their hearts. The sheikh says his cure for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span> is the same as his cure for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. &quot;Roll up your sleeves,&quot; he says — get to work and set out on the cure for the root of all these diseases.</p>
<p>Tama and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> go together, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> is itself a form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span>: you are desiring something from people rather than from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.</p>
<p>He says that what he means by this — what he calls the head of the matter, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقْدُ الْوِيَاءِ</span> — is showing off in good works, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. The Arabs use the expression <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقْدُ الْوِيَاءِ</span> for the one who begins something, the one who raises the banner at the outset.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 47">page 47</div>
<p>Now comes something important. He identifies three signs of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>.</p>
<p>The first is laziness and a diminishment of action when one is alone with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. You have no energy to read the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> in private, but when you are in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> surrounded by people, the energy appears from nowhere. The second is the opposite — enthusiastic effort and abundant action when people are present. The third is increasing one''s acts of worship when praised and diminishing them when praise is absent. A person wants to hear others say, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, that man is always praying — it is incredible.&quot; And inside, he thinks: <em>What about my fasting? Do they know about that too?</em></p>
<p>There is something worth noting here. Even though a heightened sense of energy in the company of others can be a sign of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>, it is not always so. There is something to be said for the effect of good company — seeing another person worship can stir you to worship as well, and that impulse may not come from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> but simply from a kind of esprit de corps, a collective energy that lifts everyone. So that is not necessarily negative. The danger is when the driving force is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> but the audience.</p>
<p>On the matter of praise: behavioral psychology has built entire systems on exactly this — rewards and incentives to motivate people. In the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, there is certainly room for encouragement. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, when he saw people do good, would affirm them. And there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> that says when a believer hears someone praising him, it increases him in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَانٌ</span> — it makes him want to do more for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. So praise produces the opposite effect in a sound heart: it redirects the person toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> rather than away from Him.</p>
<p>The distinction the sheikh is drawing is this: the dangerous thing is to perform acts of worship <em>for</em> the praise. That is what he is warning against.</p>
<p>There is also a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْح</span> — flattery, saying what is not true — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثَنَاء</span> — genuine acknowledgment of someone''s good. Affirming people for what they actually do is not prohibited; in fact, it is encouraged. The danger lies inside the heart of the one who <em>craves</em> the praise. That is what a person must work on, and a cure is coming.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 48">page 48</div>
<p>He now lists what must be removed from the heart in order to treat and purify it from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. There are four things: love of praise, fear of blame, desire for worldly benefit from people, and fear of harm from people.</p>
<p>The first means of accomplishing this is having <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقِين</span> — certainty — that only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> can benefit or harm you. This begins at the level of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقِيدَة</span>, because it is a premise built into the belief system we are commanded to hold. In the Islamic understan<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g of reality, there is no harm and no benefit except from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّافِعُ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الضَّرُّ</span> are two of His names. Once a person genuinely believes this, he recognizes that no one else can truly benefit or harm him. It is an illusion. And once that illusion is seen for what it is, the craving for praise and the dread of blame begin to lose their grip. What you ultimately want is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> to be pleased with you, not people, because the approval of people — divorced from the approval of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — is simply a waste of time. If the whole world is praising you and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is not pleased with you, that is a disaster, and the disaster becomes fully visible at the moment of death, which is when the reality of this life finally comes into focus.</p>
<p>Do not be deluded by praise and blame. Muhammad Ali, to take one example, was once the most praised man in the world. And then the time came when he was no longer the man who could defeat others in the ring, and the calls stopped coming. That is the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. Praise and blame follow worldly position; when you lose the position, the praise evaporates. Some of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> noted that one of the clearest proofs of this is how quickly people find it difficult to maintain friendships when they have nothing, and how suddenly everyone wants to be your friend the moment things start going well. That is simply the way of the world.</p>
<p>This is part of why, in the traditional Muslim world — unlike today''s wealth-oriented culture — knowledge was what people sought to be associated with, not wealth. Scholars achieved extraor<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>ary stan<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g simply on account of their learning. That is largely no longer the case, but it was genuinely true.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 49">page 49</div>
<p>I was rea<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g Helen Keller''s autobiography recently. It is very short and remarkably powerful — I would genuinely recommend it. She was deaf, blind, and unable to speak, and the book traces the awakening of her consciousness. One thing she wrote struck me as particularly beautiful. She became deaf, blind, and mute after a bout of scarlet fever in childhood — she was not born that way. She lost her sight and hearing at nineteen months old, yet she said that even that brief glimpse of the world — having once seen a tree, just once, before the age of two — was enough. When she later felt a tree with her hands, the memory of having seen one was still there, and she said that single blessing was sufficient for her. The power of the human eye is just extraor<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>ary.</p>
<p>She also wrote something that has stayed with me: there is no slave in this world who does not have a king somewhere in his ancestry, and there is no king who does not have a slave somewhere in his. The vicissitudes of life. Everyone''s lineage, somewhere down the line, probably passes through both a throne and a servant''s quarters. And that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> — nothing in it is permanent.</p>
<p>When you read the history of the Arabs, the rise and fall of rulers is astonishing. Take <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْمَنْصُور</span> of Andalusia. He was not a caliph but a minister — a mediocre student at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْرَسَة</span>, by some accounts, but extraor<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>arily ambitious. He worked his way into the palace, cultivated the favor of one of the powerful women there — a mother of the caliph — and through her patronage was elevated to positions of authority. There was one minister before him who was the most feared man in the court, one of the wealthiest people in Cordoba. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَنْصُور</span> eventually had him imprisoned, and that man ended his life as a pauper, dying in rags in his cell. Imagine the arc of that life. That is the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 50">page 50</div>
<p>So: firm certainty that no harm and no benefit comes except from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, and that if the entire creation wished to harm you, they could not do so without His will. If someone believes that with genuine conviction, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> ceases to arise in them — because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> exists precisely to obtain some worldly benefit, and when you know that worldly benefit flows only from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, the performance for others loses its purpose.</p>
<p>In the traditional Muslim world, because religious learning and piety were so honored, there was a particular danger of showing off in order to gain stan<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g among people. That danger has not disappeared — Muslims still respect religious comportment, so the temptation remains.</p>
<p>He now gives his definition: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> means performing an act of devotion for other than the Creator''s sake, in reality seeking worldly benefit, or praise from creation, or protection from its opposite — harm. Some people do it purely to hear how wonderful they are, and the praise itself is what they are after.</p>
<p>He then goes on to discuss <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>s various forms. The worst of them is displaying religiosity in order to be entrusted with an orphan''s wealth — and then misusing that trust. This is religious hypocrisy deployed as an instrument of outright fraud, using the appearance of piety to steal what belongs to the most vulnerable. That is a particularly grave form of it.</p>
<p>I once met a Moroccan man who had a beautiful voice and could have been paid to sing praises of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> at wed<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>gs and gatherings — something that does happen in Morocco. But he refused to do it for money. He said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has made countless ways for a person to earn provision in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. Do not seek it through something that was created to draw you near to Him.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 51">page 51</div>
<p>There are indeed countless ways to earn a living, especially in a place like America — my father used to say he realized this early on, in the early 1960s, when he sent in a dollar for a barbecue starter kit and received back a match and a small piece of flint. People find every conceivable means. There are the psychic hotlines, for instance — I have never called one, thank God, but people do. The caller gets on the line and hears: &quot;You''re looking for a better job.&quot; Well, of course — everyone is looking for a better job. &quot;You''re looking for a better relationship.&quot; They also know that anyone calling them is someone whose life is not working out, so they know precisely what to play on. It is all a performance.</p>
<p>The next category he discusses, after the other-worldly misuse just described, is the worldly misuse: a religious endowment — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقْف</span>, or funds collected for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — that is then redirected for worldly purposes. The money was gathered in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s name, and the people entrusted with it use it for themselves. That is another of the graver forms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>.</p>
<p>That is the worst type of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span> — to display religiosity and make people think you are trustworthy, so they put you on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> board, make you treasurer, and then you misuse the funds. You read about people who deceive the elderly and the vulnerable, and it is stunning what human beings will do. There are degrees of despicable behavior — not all wrongdoing is equally despicable — and this ranks among the worst.</p>
<p>He then says that the least form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span> is what is done out of fear of others'' scorn — you act a certain way simply because you do not want people to think badly of you. That is the mildest form of it.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 46">page 46</div>
<p>The cure, he says, lies in knowing that if all of creation joined forces to oppose you or support you, they could not do so without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s permission. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> alone holds the reward for your actions in both abodes — this world and the next. The disease is also cured by maintaining awareness of the harm <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span> causes, which leads to a genuine hatred of it and allows it to pass. That is the intellectual treatment.</p>
<p>The practical treatment is accomplished by veiling one''s actions from the eyes of others and by reciting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْإِخْلَاصِ</span> frequently — along with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِخَارَة</span>. This is a vitally important treatment, because this disease is serious and we all want to be free of it.</p>
<p>Veiling your actions does not mean you should never act in front of people. It means that out of fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span>, when you are able to act privately, that is preferable. If you are going to donate money, it is better not to do it publicly — though the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> say that if your genuine intention is to encourage others, it is permissible. The key is to look into your own heart and determine your real motive. If you want to be seen as the one giving the large donation, then give secretly. If you truly feel no such pull and genuinely wish to inspire others, then it is allowed. You must watch and guard your own heart. This is a very subtle matter.</p>
<p>Avoi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g public displays extends to night prayers, recitation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, and similar acts of worship — doing them where no one will see you. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> do note, however, that for scholars it is sometimes better to perform such acts visibly, to encourage others. But people of knowledge are equally susceptible to this disease — often more so, given the nature of their position.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 47">page 47</div>
<p>The other practice is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْإِخْلَاصِ</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلُصَ</span>, meaning to be pure. It relates to the image of pure honey — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَاص</span> — or pure milk, fresh from the udder, uncontaminated by anything. That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدِّينُ الْخَالِصُ لِلَّهِ</span> means: worship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> untainted by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span>, by hidden <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, by any motive other than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْلِصِينَ</span> are the people who attain this.</p>
<p>In the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, two related words appear: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْلِص</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْلَص</span>. The first is the active participle — one who makes himself sincere. The second is the passive — one who has been made sincere by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. The passive form points to something essential: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is required. Your own sincerity is not sufficient on its own; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s enablement must accompany it. That is the secret of success. This is why Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْ سَيِّئَاتِي سَيِّئَاتِ الْمَحْبُوبِ إِلَيْكَ، وَلَا تَجْعَلْ حَسَنَاتِي حَسَنَاتِ الْمَرْدُودِ عَلَيْكَ</span>&quot; — O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, make my bad actions the bad actions of those You love, and do not make my good actions the good actions of those You reject. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> must be present.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> promises this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> when you fulfill certain conditions, so the two go together. This is why being <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْلَص</span> but being a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفْتَدِيكَ</span> means you are not truly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْلَص</span>. A Christian can be absolutely sincere — genuinely so. A Buddhist, a Jew, a person in any tradition can possess sincerity. But that person is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْلَص</span>. Why? Because the action is not in accordance with what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has prescribed. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْلَص</span> who is also <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْلِص</span> is one whose action is sincerely for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> alone and simultaneously aligned with what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> loves — not what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has prohibited. That distinction is critical.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 48">page 48</div>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has commanded us and given us a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> to follow. If we are not in accordance with it, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> does not come — no matter how sincere we are. And I understand this is a difficult point for many Christians, whose tradition tends to say the law is secondary and the spirit is what matters. We say both are essential. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> comes from following both the letter and the spirit of the law together.</p>
<p>You can be following the law outwardly — praying five times a day as commanded, paying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاةٌ</span>, performing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span> — and yet be doing all of it for other than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span>, and there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> in it. Conversely, you can be inwardly sincere, acting for God, doing charitable works in secret — yet not be acting in accordance with what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has prescribed. So you must have both: the spirit of the law and the letter of the law. Our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> is the middle way. The tendency of one tradition has been to focus on the letter and neglect the spirit; another has tended toward the spirit and neglected the letter. Both are incomplete. The active form, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْلِص</span>, is what comes from you. The passive form, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْلَص</span>, is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> does in you.</p>
<p>Reciting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْإِخْلَاصِ</span> is therefore one of the core practices for purifying from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span> also derives from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصًا</span>, meaning to render something pure. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span> is literally the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span> that makes you pure — it purifies through its very recitation. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَابِطُ الْحَاجِّ</span>, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> preserve him, prescribes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْإِخْلَاصِ</span> to most people who come to him with diseases of the heart, inclu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسْوَسَة</span> — obsessive intrusive thought. Do not underestimate this prescription. It is a real cure.</p>
<p>You should recite it at minimum three times in the morning and three times in the evening — that is the least. Since it equals one-third of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> in weight and virtue, it is a tremendous <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span> with a special stan<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 49">page 49</div>
<p>The second practice for treating <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span> is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدُ الِاسْتِغْفَارِ</span>. It comes from a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> narrated on the authority of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُرَيْدَة</span>, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be pleased with him, recorded by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ أَحْمَدَ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو دَاوُدَ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ مَهْدِيٍّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ خُزَيْمَةَ</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَكِيمُ</span> — its appearance in so many collections signals its importance. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that whoever recites it in the morning and in the evening, and then dies that day or that evening, will enter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>. You should recite it three times in the morning and three times in the evening, and ideally after every prayer — even in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَجْدَة</span> — as a regular <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ أَنْتَ رَبِّي، لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا أَنْتَ، خَلَقْتَنِي وَأَنَا عَبْدُكَ، وَأَنَا عَلَى عَهْدِكَ وَوَعْدِكَ مَا اسْتَطَعْتُ</span></p>
<p>This is a genuine cure. Try it on yourself — this is a science, not conjecture. Do what is being prescribed and you will see results. Recite the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> every evening. Recite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْإِخْلَاصِ</span>. Do it with the intention of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللهُ</span>, grant me <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span>. Say it to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> alone in the night. If you do it with sincerity, He will respond. But if you move through it casually — ticking boxes and moving on to the next disease — the effect will not come. This is serious. This is our lives.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 50">page 50</div>
<p>I read <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ الْغَزَالِيِّ</span>s signs of the insincere preacher versus the sincere preacher, and I recognized far more of myself in the insincere column. It is unnerving. When you read this material, you start to see yourself in it — and you should be unsettled by that, because we will be held to account for all of it. This is what life is about. Either we grasp the point or we do not. But the guidance has been given to us; we are being told.</p>
<p>There is another important <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> not mentioned in the text here, but cited in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصِيحَة</span>. It is from the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, so it must be great. Recite it three times in the morning and three times in the evening:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ إِنِّي أَعُوذُ بِكَ أَنْ أُشْرِكَ بِكَ شَيْئًا وَأَنَا أَعْلَمُ، وَأَسْتَغْفِرُكَ لِمَا لَا أَعْلَمُ</span></p>
<p>This is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِعَاذَةٌ مِنَ الرِّيَاءِ</span> — seeking refuge from hidden <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> — and the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> himself recited it. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِفَاءٌ</span> for what is in the hearts. It is a healing, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> states: it cures the hearts of the believers. And what the Prophet gave you, take it — so this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> is a sound daily practice.</p>
<p>He then adds: as for the chronically diseased heart that has long been given to showing off through good works, even it will find a cure in this — and what a cure. He is telling you this is a serious and effective treatment. Believe it, practice it, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> you will see the results.</p>
<p>He then turns to the question of hypocrisy. Here, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span> is closer to a type of hypocrisy that involves veiling one''s wrong actions — concealing one''s faults. This kind of concealment is, accor<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g to Ibn Zukri, actually <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاجِب</span>. If someone asks you whether you ever drank alcohol, and you have made sincere <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, you say no. If someone asks whether you ever stole, and you have repented, you say no. That is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span>; it is an obligation.</p>
<p>My note here is that concealment does become <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span> when the person is hi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g the misdeed because they want others to think better of them than they really are — that motive makes it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span>. But the obligation to conceal one''s wrongdoing exists for a different reason entirely: so that misdeeds are not taken lightly by the community. If I ask you whether you have ever drunk alcohol and you freely say yes, I unconsciously think: well, I am not so bad then. The admission softens the enormity. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَبِيرَةٌ</span> means enormity — and enormities should remain enormous in the communal consciousness. You do not want society to become comfortable with them.</p>
<p>I would add that when people freely admit their transgressions, it leads to complacency and the erosion of shame. Although there is an apparent hypocrisy in concealment, it is in reality a kind of homage that vice pays to virtue — and that homage is what allows virtue to function as a deterrent in the social arena. In a healthy society, virtue is the norm — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْرُوف</span> — and vice is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْكَرٌ</span>. In an unhealthy society, the reverse is true. When virtue is still the norm, vice must pay tribute to it by hi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g. That is not hypocrisy; it is a form of respect for virtue, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> honors it.</p>
<p>Freud argued the opposite: that all of this is mere hypocrisy, that we are all seething with suppressed desires, and that letting it all out is the path to health. The consequences of that idea are now visible. There is no longer shame — none. Vice no longer hides itself or pays homage to virtue. And the argument has even been made that the real problem is not the act itself but the lie about it — as if concealment of wrongdoing were the greater offense. That inversion is a sign of how far things have gone.</p>
<p>It was not always this way. In the 1960s, in most American cities, if an unmarried young woman became pregnant, she quietly disappeared from the community. Several months later she would reappear, and people would say she had gone to stay with relatives. What actually happened was that her family sent her away to give birth, the baby was placed for adoption, and she returned with the community none the wiser — because there was shame. That is how recently this was true. In 1968, a woman was expelled from Radcliffe for living in the same apartment as a man. Radcliffe was considered a relatively progressive institution at the time, and even there, that boundary held. Now there are coeducational dormitories and no one thinks twice about it.</p>
<p>So how did we get from there to a shrug — a collective &quot;who cares?&quot; Islam says no: it is actually better to maintain that sense of shame, because what it really represents is an honoring of virtue. That is the essential point.</p>
<p>The hypocrisy itself is seen as less harmful than what happens when everything is treated as acceptable, when nothing is a big deal. That permissiveness is the true danger, not the hypocrisy. And this, again, reflects the principle in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> of choosing the lesser of two evils. Both options involve something wrong — no one wants to be a hypocrite, no one wants to deny something they actually did. But the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> holds that the greater evil lies in open admission, because it diminishes the weight of the action in the eyes of others. People begin to say, &quot;Oh, he does it too,&quot; or &quot;Well, maybe it''s not that serious.&quot; Rather than that, the instinct is to recoil — &quot;Of course not, I would never do that&quot; — because the act genuinely is serious. This is how Muslims have understood the world. There was shame. The person who denies isn''t trying to look virtuous for their own sake; they are refusing to let others reduce their fear and revulsion toward the wrong action. That is what matters.</p>
<p>Even now, Imam Junaid — and I find this a genuinely healthy view — said: if you commit a wrong action and then make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, let it go. Do not dwell on it. Maintain a fear of returning to it, yes, but do not dwell on it, because dwelling produces guilt, a kind of despair, and self-loathing. None of those are healthy responses. If you say &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرُ اللَّهَ</span>,&quot; make a sincere <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, resolve not to return, and then leave it behind as though it never happened — that is the right approach.</p>
<p>Now, to address the broader picture: if someone is caught up in a major sin — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِبَا</span>, for instance — that is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْر</span>. It is a grave enormity, and it must not be taken lightly. But it does not remove a person from the fold of Islam. If you have gotten yourself into that situation, and it is not easy to get out, you must make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> and take whatever steps are necessary to extricate yourself. Begin that process, make the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, and work your way out. Once you are out, you perform what is required. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> must be sincere — the signs of sincerity are genuine remorse, immediate cessation of the act, and a firm intention never to return. If you do that, then that is a valid <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>.</p>
<p>And when you make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, you should have a good opinion of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. To say &quot;I am so horrible, I am so wretched, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> will never forgive me&quot; — no. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> tells us not to think that way. That kind of despair is, frankly, a Christian disease. Many Christians fall into it. Though to be fair, Christianity is complex on this point, because alongside that tendency toward despair there exists the opposite extreme — particularly in Baptist Christianity, where a person can do the most outrageous things and be entirely convinced they are safe. That''s why the phenomenon of the fallen Baptist minister — the Jimmy Swaggart syndrome — doesn''t actually shock Baptist congregations. The attitude is: the devil got you that time, you''ll get him the next. That''s not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>.</p>
<p>In Islam, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> is real. And when a non-Muslim accepts Islam, that is itself the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> of a non-Muslim from his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْر</span> — and by the consensus of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّهْ</span>, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> is accepted. When a person enters Islam, all their previous wrong actions are forgiven, by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِجْمَاع</span>. It is as though they were just born. Whereas the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> of a Muslim is a matter of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافٌ</span>: if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> wills, He accepts it; if He does not, He may decline. But you should hope that He accepts it, because we have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاء</span> — hope. Do not be absolutely certain either way; the uncertainty keeps the fear alive and guards against returning to the sin.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 73">page 73</div>
<p>Someone asked: &quot;I have heard people say that when you take <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَة</span>, all your bad deeds become good deeds — is that correct?&quot;</p>
<p>No, but your good deeds do come with you into Islam. Your bad deeds are wiped out; your good deeds remain. So you lose nothing and only gain. That is a really important point.</p>
<p>On the question of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and evil: we do not say that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> brings evil. We say that within the decree of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, good and evil both exist — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has measured them out, and they exist in the world. But the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>: &quot;All good comes from You, and evil does not return to You.&quot; So while <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has created a world in which evil is permitted to exist, we do not attribute evil to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, even though we believe it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> who has measured it out.</p>
<p>Evil, interestingly, can only exist where there is moral agency — and that means human beings. It cannot exist in the animal kingdom. If a lion kills a baby, that is not an evil act; the lion has no moral obligation, no compunction. You cannot call the lion evil. You cannot call a cobra evil for biting someone — it is a snake, doing what it was created to do. But if a human being takes a gun, robs someone of their hard-earned money, and then shoots them — the robbery is evil, and the shooting on top of it is a heinous evil.</p>
<p>What we say is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> created the human being, gave him the capacity to choose, and then measured out what he would choose. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷻ</span> has decreed that at that moment, that person would make that choice. The person is fully responsible for it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> permitted it — empowered the person to carry it out through His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُدْرَة</span> — and it was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَشِيئَة</span>, His will and providence, that allowed that person to will what he willed.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا تَشَآءُونَ إِلَّآ أَن يَشَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span></p>
<p>You do not will anything except that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> wills it at the same time. If I intend to go and harm someone and begin walking toward them, my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَشِيئَة</span> has started. But if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> does not will harm to come to that person — say the person suffers a heart attack and dies before I arrive — then I willed something that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> did not will. So anything you actually carry out can only be carried out if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> wills it as well.</p>
<p>And this is the distinction: when what you will is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> loves, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span>. When what you will is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> hates, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِذْلَان</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَفِّق</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَاذِل</span>. But you are ultimately the one performing the evil. We do not attribute the evil to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, yet we acknowledge that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has measured it out in the world. It is part of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدَر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has permitted evil to exist — it is part of His creation. And we are fully responsible for the consequences of our actions, whether good or evil.</p>
<p>Ultimately, had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> not willed it, no evil could exist. But it does not return to Him — we do not attribute it to Him.</p>
<p>Someone asked: &quot;How do you know, in the moment before an action, whether what you will aligns with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s will?&quot;</p>
<p>If it accords with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'ostentation';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'ar">تَوْفِيق</span>. If it does not accord with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span>. If what you are doing is done with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span> — sincerity — and in accordance with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s laws, then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span>. That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَفَّق</span> means: a person whose will is aligned with what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> loves for them to do.</p>
<p>You should never engage in anything without first knowing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُكْم</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> regar<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g it. It is impermissible to act without knowing what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has decreed about that action. And, thankfully, the vast majority of things are permissible; only a small category is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, and those can be learned. It is not that complex.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> did not make the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> difficult. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> is not hard. The problem is that human beings simply resist certain things. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is straightforward — it has four elements: doing what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> commanded, inwardly and outwardly; inwardly with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span>, outwardly in accordance with His laws. And not doing what He prohibited, inwardly and outwardly — refraining not out of fear that someone might see you walking into a bar, but out of fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. That is the inward dimension. And outwardly, not doing it whether you are alone or in company. That is all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is.</p>
<p>And if you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> promises you every good in this world: He promises you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَخْرَج</span> — a way out. He promises to rectify your children. He promises to forgive your wrong actions. He promises to open the gates of heaven and send rain and bless your agriculture. He promises you a good life — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَاةٌ طَيِّبَةٌ</span>. Those are the worldly promises. And then the otherworldly ones: He promises to forgive you, to enter you into paradise, to place you in the company of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَبِيِّينَ</span> — the prophets — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيقِينَ</span>, the righteous, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُهَدَاءُ</span>, the martyrs, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحِينَ</span>. What a deal. What a bargain.</p>
<p>On the matter of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْرَاج</span> — publicly exposing someone''s wrong actions — the Arabs use that word to describe putting someone in an embarrassing position, particularly regar<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g sins. It is not a good thing to do. Again, this is a matter of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرُوءَةٌ</span>, of personal dignity and honor. And the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: &quot;From the beauty of a person''s Islam is that he leaves what does not concern him.&quot; What happens between another person and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is no concern of yours. And it is better not to know, because knowing often leads to thinking ill of others.</p>
<p>Someone asked about disclosing one''s age — the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was reportedly asked about his age and responded: &quot;Is it any concern of yours?&quot; He explained that if you tell someone your age, either they say, &quot;Oh, I thought you were older&quot; — and they diminish you — or they say, &quot;I thought you were younger&quot; — and you still can''t win.</p>
<p>But that ruling is different when there is a legitimate concern, such as marriage. Age is a valid consideration in marriage. It is entirely reasonable for a man to be concerned about a woman''s age, and vice versa, because age bears directly on marriage and its circumstances. That is why it is impermissible, for instance, to dye gray hairs and present yourself to a prospective spouse as a young man, and then have her discover the truth once married. That is a serious problem — a form of deception.</p>
<p>In traditional cultures, people generally knew good families from bad families — it was common knowledge. A good family meant people who were not engaged in wrongdoing. Unfortunately, that is no longer reliably true. Good families, bad families — the lines have blurred. There are many people who have committed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِينَة</span>, consumed alcohol, taken drugs. And the stakes are especially high now because of diseases and other serious consequences tied to these behaviors.</p>
<p>I think, therefore, there is a valid concern. In the context of marriage and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِينَة</span> specifically — people do have a right to know what they are entering into. Now, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>ic verse stating that a fornicator can only marry a fornicator or a polytheist applies to the person''s condition before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرِيَّة</span> was a woman who sold herself openly in the marketplace; it was prohibited to marry someone in that active condition. But if they made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, there is no prohibition. Still, given the situation we are in today, I think people have a legitimate right to know. Does that make sense?</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 75">page 75</div>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, let us return to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَضَارَّ</span>. Where did we get to last week?</p>
<p>&quot;The middle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span> — what a terrible place to be.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> give us all a cure.</p>
<p>Did we reach the section on Imam Malik, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حين لم يرعب حين بأس</span></p>
<p>Yes, all right. So what the text says here is that the scholars hold two opinions concerning those who seek some worldly benefit through worship rather than a benefit in the next world. For instance, if a person recites <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْوَاقِعَةِ</span> for the sole reason of protecting themselves from financial hardship — because there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> is reported to have said that whoever recites <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْوَاقِعَةِ</span> every day will not be afflicted with poverty or serious financial calamity in this life. This is well known. Even Ibn Masud, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>, when asked what inheritance he left his daughters, said: &quot;I left them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْوَاقِعَةِ</span>.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْوَاقِعَةِ</span> is understood to protect from poverty when recited daily.</p>
<p>The question the scholars discuss is: what if that protection from poverty is the only reason a person recites it? The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> indicates the benefit is there for whoever does it daily, but what is the intention behind it? Obviously, there is always some degree of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَانٌ</span> involved when a person does anything the Messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> has encouraged. Rea<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْوَاقِعَةِ</span> every day is not like, say, investing in the stock market, where the benefit is tangible and worldly in a straightforward sense. The benefit here comes from the unseen world — it is grounded in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَانٌ</span>.</p>
<p>He also raises the case of someone who worships in order to attain the next world — seeking paradise as the purpose of their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. Imam al-Ubi al-Maliki, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>, said that worshipping for the sake of the next world is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَائِزٌ</span> — permissible — but that it should not be the sole reason driving a person''s worship. So it falls under the category of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبَاح</span> — permitted but not the ideal. That is one opinion.</p>
<p>The people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span>, however, tend to be extremely critical of doing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> for any worldly or otherworldly benefit. Their position is that you should perform <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> solely because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷻ</span> is worthy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> and worthy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْفِرَة</span> — full stop. No reward expected, no paradise sought, nothing in return. That is the stance of the greatest Sufis. Qadi Abu Bakr ibn al-Arabi, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span> — who was both a Sufi and a jurist —</p>
<p>He argues that if you read the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> carefully, using your intellect, you will see that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is actively encouraging you to strive for His garden and to fear His fire. There is a famous saying attributed to Rabia al-Adawiyya: &quot;Go after the Gardener, not the garden.&quot; She also said, &quot;O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, if I worship You for the garden, then put me in the fire.&quot; That is a remarkably strong statement — I would not say it myself — but it reflected her state, her <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span>, her spiritual station. Qadi Abu Bakr ibn al-Arabi, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be pleased with him, along with Sidi Ahmed Zarruk, both maintain that the worship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> in hope of His garden should in no way be belittled, because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> itself does not belittle it.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 57">page 57</div>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ الْغَزَالِيِّ</span> identifies three types of worshippers. The first worships <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> as a free person — purely for His sake, desiring nothing else. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> — though I believe it is either weak or has been commented upon by scholars — whose meaning is that there are people who would not disobey <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> even if there were no fire, simply because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is not to be disobeyed. He is worthy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> in and of Himself. The second type, as Qadi Abu Bakr describes it, resembles the merchant — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تُجَارُ</span> — asking, &quot;What is in it for me?&quot; This person tallies rewards: if I pray so many <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكْعَتَانِ</span>, I receive such-and-such; if I recite so much <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, I earn such-and-such. He desires the garden, and that desire drives his worship. The third type worships like a slave fearing the whip — he acts out of terror of the fire. So you have three categories: those who worship purely for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s sake, those who worship desiring the garden, and those who worship fearing the fire.</p>
<p>If you look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, there is clearly both <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْغِيب</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْهِيب</span> — both encouragement and warning. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says: race toward forgiveness from your Lord and a garden — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَابِقُوٓا۟ إِلَىٰ مَغْفِرَةٍۢ مِّن رَّبِّكُمْ وَجَنَّةٍ</span> — and elsewhere: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَفِرُّوٓا۟ إِلَى ٱللَّهِ</span> — flee toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. And: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَفِى ذَلِكَ فَلْيَتَنَافَسِ ٱلْمُتَنَفِسُونَ</span> — let those who compete, compete for that. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> contains many verses warning about the fire and many more encouraging good deeds by promising reward. What Qadi Abu Bakr, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be pleased with him, is saying is that having <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span> — a longing — for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>s garden, and fear of His fire, is itself <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span>. It is sincerity, and there is no taint of impurity in it.</p>
<p>So all he is telling us in this passage is that there is a genuine difference of opinion here. If you perform worship for the sake of the next world, some will take issue with that — and those tend to be the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُوفِيَّة</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُقَهَاء</span>, scholars like Qadi Abu Bakr, Imam Ruyani, and others, consider that position extreme. They hold that worshipping <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> with hope for His garden is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span>, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> Himself affirms it in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. That is, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, the stronger opinion.</p>
<p>The next point he raises is equally important: some scholars also consider seeking the pleasure of worship itself to be a concern. And this is something that people who worship seriously over a sustained period genuinely discover — that there is a pleasure in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> that surpasses other pleasures people or<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>arily chase. One of the great Hanafi scholars said: &quot;If kings knew the pleasure we are in, they would send their armies with swords to take it from us.&quot; He was saying that the pleasure of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْم</span>, of worshipping <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is so profound that a king, with all his palaces and all his worldly wealth, would fight to possess it if he knew what we had. So some scholars — especially the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَارِفٍ</span> and the people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span> — warn that this pleasure can become so intense that a person begins performing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> in order to obtain that feeling. He prays the night prayer because of the pleasure it brings.</p>
<p>Some scholars caution against this. But Ahmad ibn al-Hawari — or one of the great early scholars, I am working to recall exactly — said that were it not for these gifts, most people would struggle to sustain their practice consistently. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has granted that pleasure as a reward for regular, committed worship, and it is a blessing that should not be diminished. He is simply laying out the range of scholarly opinion on this question.</p>
<p>Then he says: as for the one who likes people to see him doing good deeds — meaning he performs the deed for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, but enjoys the fact that others happen to learn of it — that is considered <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> accor<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g to some scholars, and not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> accor<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g to others. The basis for the second view is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>ic reference to those who love to be praised for what they have not done. Some scholars drew from that verse the conclusion that it is natural — part of human nature itself — for a person to enjoy recognition for genuinely good deeds. The problem arises when that recognition becomes the reason for doing them. There is a distinction: if you did the deed for that reason, that is unambiguously <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. But if that was not your reason, yet you find yourself pleased that people acknowledge what you did, some scholars say that is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>.</p>
<p>One scholar said: whoever loves to do something and to be known and remembered for it has committed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, Exalted and Glorified. Imam al-Jazuli — a great Maliki <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَقِيهٌ</span> from the Maghrib and the teacher of Sidi Ahmed Zarruk — added that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> can occur even when no one is present. For example: you are rea<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>g and come across a rare, obscure point that few people know, and you think, &quot;This would make a strong impression in a gathering&quot; — so you write it down and memorize it to produce it later. He says that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. Full stop. It is a serious problem.</p>
<p>If your intention was to benefit others, of course, that is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. But what Imam al-Jazuli was describing is the person who thinks, &quot;This will really get them,&quot; or &quot;They will be impressed by this&quot; — which is an obvious trap for people of scholarship in particular. When scholars gather, there is a kind of expectation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُذَاكَرَة</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُشَارَكَة</span> — mutual discussion and exchange — and that can quietly slide into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. Sakhr ibn Abdillah, a great early scholar and muhaddith among other things, said: &quot;Whoever desires people to know what is between him and his Lord is in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>.&quot; In other words, it is no one''s business but yours and your Lord''s. You should not want others to know — unless he sees that people have abandoned good deeds and performs them publicly as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصِيحَة</span>, as sincere counsel and example.</p>
<p>So, for instance, Ibn Umar, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be pleased with him, and Ibn Masud were known to go to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> to perform <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهَجُّد</span>. They could have done this at home, but they did it there so that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَابِعِين</span> would see them and follow their example. Their intention was not self-promotion but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصِيحَة</span> to the Muslims.</p>
<p>Then he says — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّجْم</span> here refers to Imam Malik, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be pleased with him, who is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّجْم</span> because his student, later the independent mujtahid Imam al-Shafii, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be pleased with him, said: &quot;When the scholars are mentioned, Malik is the star&quot; — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا ذُكِرَ الْعُلَمَاءُ فَمَالِكٌ النَّجْمُ</span>.. From that, Imam Malik became known as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّجْم</span>. It is one of his titles, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلْقَابٌ</span>. He also carries the title <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالِمُ الْمَدِينَةِ</span>, just as Imam al-Shafii is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالِمُ قُرَيْشٍ</span> and Abu Hanifa, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be pleased with him, is often called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِمَامُ الأَعْظَمُ</span> — the greatest imam. These are titles bestowed upon them by later generations.</p>
<p>Imam Malik did not consider it harmful, as long as the original intention was grounded in sincerity. This position traces back to a question once put to Imam Malik about a man who set out for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — his intention was clear: to pray in congregation. But on the way, a thought crept in: &quot;I hope someone sees me going to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span>.&quot; Imam Malik said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا أَرَى بَأْسًا</span>&quot; — I see no harm in that — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا كَانَ السَّرَائِرُ إِخْلَاصٌ</span>.&quot; He does not see it as harmful if the man began with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span> and does not follow through on that inner whisper — if he repels it, says &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ</span>,&quot; and does not entertain it. That is an important distinction to hold.</p>
<p>Then he says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَمَرٌ عَلَى الرِّيَاءِ أَنْ أَفْعَلَهُ مِنْ تَرْكِهِ لِخَوْفِهِ وَأَفْضَلُ ذِكْرُ اللِّسَانِ فَارِغُ الْجَنَانِ أَرَاقِبُ الْقَلْبِ وَاللِّسَانِ</span>.. This is a crucial point. He is saying that to abandon an action out of fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> is actually worse than performing it with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. Imagine you walk into the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> and want to pray a few <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكْعَتَانِ</span>, but then you think: &quot;Those people might think I am showing off,&quot; or &quot;Maybe I am only doing this so they will think well of me.&quot; That inner concern is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَوْفُ الرِّيَاءِ</span> — fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. If that fear causes you to leave the action, it is itself <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. Why? Because the reason you abandoned the act was the people. You were preoccupied with them, not with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Leaving an act out of concern for what people think is as much a form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> as performing it for their eyes — in both cases, the people are at the centre, not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. That is why the scholars say it is better to perform the action even when you sense a trace of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> in yourself.</p>
<p>This does not mean, however, that you should be careless. If the act of worship is something you can perform privately, it is always better to do so — precisely because of the danger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>. But abandoning it out of fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> is worse than doing it despite that fear. The same principle applies to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرٌ</span>. Scholars say it is better for your tongue to make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرٌ</span> while your heart is heedless than for both your tongue and your heart to be heedless together. If you keep the tongue moving, it may reach the heart — because, as they say, a man''s heart lies beneath his tongue. And interestingly, in Chinese medicine, their physiology affirms a direct connection between the tongue and the heart: what the tongue does actually affects the heart, and even the physical movement of the tongue has an impact on it.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 59">page 59</div>
<p>One more thing worth knowing: the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> is derived from the root of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُؤْيَا</span> — seeing. And it is not about others seeing you. It is about you seeing them. The fault lies in your gaze toward the creation rather than toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, Glorified and Exalted. You are the one looking at them; that is where the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> lives.</p>
<p>One of the many profound things about the Day of Resurrection is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَائِشَة</span>, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be pleased with her, when told by the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, that everyone would be raised naked, asked whether people would be looking at one another. He replied: &quot;It will be far greater than that, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَائِشَة</span>.&quot; People will be in such a state of awe that they will not even be aware of those around them. And this is the very point of worshipping for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> alone. When worship is truly for Him, you carry a knowledge of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> that renders everyone else insignificant by comparison. When you truly contemplate what you are worshipping — consider that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, once saw Jibril in his true form, and Jibril filled the entire horizon. One angel. The entire horizon. Imagine looking out across that vast expanse and seeing a single being filling all of it. And that is Jibril. When you begin to grasp that what you worship is infinite, the thought of other human beings — equally helpless, equally needy, in the same predicament as yourself — loses its grip. They can do nothing for you. No benefit, no harm, nothing. That is the bottom line.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'ostentation';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>That is why he says: fear of other than Allah and desire for other than Allah are incompatible with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَكُّل</span>. Allah says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَعَلَى ٱللَّهِ فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ ٱلْمُتَوَكِّلُونَ</span> — upon Allah let those who trust, place their trust. If you are preoccupied with other people, you do not have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَكُّل</span>. And if you lack <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَكُّل</span>, the reason is an absence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقِين</span> — certainty. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقِين</span> you are missing is this: that all good comes from Allah, and that no harm can befall you except by His will. That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> is teaching us — to be utterly certain that only Allah can harm you or benefit you, and for that reason, fear of human beings dissolves.</p>
<p>This does not mean becoming reckless or impetuous — driving like a madman and saying, &quot;It is all written by Allah.&quot; That is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَكُّل</span>. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, told the Bedouin: &quot;Tie your camel, then put your trust in Allah.&quot;</p>
<p>Then he says: the root of every disease is a lack of certainty. What is prohibited of these two — fear and desire directed at the creation — is specifically that which prevents an obligation from being fulfilled. If it leads instead to neglecting something recommended, it is considered reprehensible. In either case, flee to your Lord from both. Do not desire the creation and do not fear the creation. And the cure for both, he reminds us, is to know with certainty that no one harms or benefits except Allah.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'relying on other than god';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>He then moves to the next disease, which is displeasure with the divine decree — and this will sound very familiar to many people. This disease occurs when a person resists what Allah, Majestic and Exalted, has decreed — for instance, saying, &quot;I did not warrant this happening to me,&quot; or &quot;I do not deserve this.&quot; And that is a remarkable thing to hear, because you hear it from people all the time.</p>
<p>I don''t deserve this. Why is this happening to me? Or, as he puts it next: what did I do to deserve this suffering? What wrong did I do to deserve this? The point is that everything comes from Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. This is why Imam Abu al-Hasan said that people possess a quality they are entirely unaware of, and it destroys all of their good deeds. Very few people actually recognize it: being displeased with the divine decree. They do not realize they are showing displeasure toward what Allah has ordained. Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says that they were displeased with what Allah had given them, and so Allah removed all of their good deeds. By contrast, the people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, when asked what their Lord has sent down, say: &quot;Good — it is all good.&quot; That is their answer. Everything is good. Nothing is evil. There is a reason, a lesson, a wisdom in it. This is the difference between the people of Allah and everyone else.</p>
<p>The people of Allah recognize that this world is a place of purification — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَبَرَّة</span> in itself. We were sent here to purify ourselves. The very purpose of our existence is to purify ourselves, to draw near to Allah, and to worship Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. That is it. Once you recognize that, everything becomes an opportunity to learn, to grow, and to draw closer to Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> — everything.</p>
<p>Because there are only four possible states a human being can be in. Only four — no fifth. If you can name a fifth, that would be remarkable. According to Abu al-Abbas al-Mursi, you are either in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>, or in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلِيَّة</span>, or in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَاعَةٌ</span>, or in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْصِيَةٌ</span>. That is it. You are either in a blessing from Allah, or a tribulation from Allah, in obedience to Allah, or in disobedience. That is the full scope.</p>
<p>Each one of these states calls for a specific response. Allah wants something from you in each case. If Allah gives you a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> — what response does He desire? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>. Gratitude. That is the response. And what is gratitude according to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>? It is to use the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> in what is acceptable to Allah — whether <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبَاح</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْدُوبٌ</span>, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاجِب</span>. To use it in something <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكْرُوهٌ</span> does not quite constitute gratitude, but neither is it ingratitude, since it is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. Look at those five categories: only one carries punishment. The other four either earn reward or nothing. So: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>.</p>
<p>He says later in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَتْن</span> that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> is to utilize what Allah has given you in what pleases Allah. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>. And so you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْرُ اللِّسَانِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْرُ الْجَنَانِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْرُ اللِّسَانِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْرُ الْبَنَانِ</span> — gratitude of the tongue, the heart, and the limbs. Part of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> is to thank people, to tell others what someone did for you. That is actually considered part of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> — to say, so-and-so benefited me in this or that. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِظْهَارُ النِّعْمَةِ</span>, showing the blessing openly, is part of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>. Allah loves that. To feel the blessing in your heart, and to act with those blessings in a way that pleases Allah.</p>
<p>The next state is tribulation — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلِيَّة</span>. What does Allah expect from us? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span>. If He gives you a tribulation, He expects <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْرٌ جَمِيلٌ</span>. Though in truth, you are not even required to have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْرٌ جَمِيلٌ</span> in its fullest sense. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْرٌ جَمِيلٌ</span> is when you do not complain to anyone at all. You can complain — and we will get into that in a moment — but complaining to others is not ideal; it just gives you a degree of release.</p>
<p>Then the next state: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَاعَةٌ</span> — obedience. What is the response? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُهُودُ الْمِنَّةِ</span> — recognizing the gift. The danger is not to become arrogant. If you find yourself in obedience, you must recognize that it is from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> of Allah that you are there. Do not start thinking you are better than others because you are in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَاعَةٌ</span> while they are in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْصِيَةٌ</span>. That is the danger of obedience — and in a deeper sense, the inherent danger of religion itself. The religious person begins to see himself as better than the irreligious person. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>''s opening. Once he has you there, it is rope-a-dope — like Muhammad Ali used to do. He has you until the end of the fight, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> is his game. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. Once he has you in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّرٌ عَلَى النَّاسِ</span>, he has you.</p>
<p>So that is the danger of obedience. What Allah expects when you are obedient is that you recognize it as a gift from Him. Recognize that just as He made you obedient, He could just as easily have given the situation of the person who is disobeying Him to you instead, and given them the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span> said: do not find fault in others, because if you seek out their faults, Allah will remove those faults from them and place them in you. So there should be a sense of fear — a fear when you see someone in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلِيَّة</span>. That person is in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلِيَّة</span> and he needs to change. And if someone is in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْصِيَةٌ</span>, that too is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلِيَّة</span>, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَاعَةٌ</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>, and they go together. He is in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلِيَّة</span>.</p>
<p>This is why Imam Malik relates in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَطَّأ</span> that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِيسَى عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span> said — and Imam Malik frames it as something he heard reported — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَغَنِي أَنَّ عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ قَالَ</span>,&quot; meaning: I have just heard, so it is a narration, though he evidently thought it sound enough to include in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَطَّأ</span>. He said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِيسَى عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span> was reported to have said that there are only two kinds of people: those in tribulation and those in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>. And he said: do not look upon the people of tribulation as though you are lords over them. Look upon them with compassion, as servants would look upon them.</p>
<p>That is the point. When you see people in tribulation, do not say to yourself, &quot;I am better than these people.&quot; Say rather: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, they are in tribulation, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي عَافَانِي</span>. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> of the Prophet. When he saw people in tribulation, he said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي عَافَانِي مِمَّا ابْتَلَاهُ بِهِ وَفَضَّلَنِي عَلَى كَثِيرٍ مِمَّنْ خَلَقَ تَفْضِيلًا</span> — praise be to the One who granted me well-being in a way that spared me from the tribulation He gave to these people, and who favored me over so much of His creation. That is where you should stand when it comes to obedience.</p>
<p>Now in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْصِيَةٌ</span> — what is expected of you? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. You must make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. And if you look at this and realize: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, every single thing that happens to me will be one of these four things, and for each one there is a response that will please Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> — then if I please Allah, the thing that happened to me was in fact a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>, because it brought me closer to Allah, and that is the whole point. Perhaps you dislike a thing and there is much good in it for you. Perhaps you love a thing and there is much harm in it for you. Allah knows and you do not. So just admit you do not know — and then comes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. Ibn Abbas said: if a tribulation befalls you, it is itself a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> in that it could have been worse. So you must thank Allah. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> — it is not worse than it could be.</p>
<p>And the best model is to look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيرَة</span> of the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span> — to see how he lived, and to recognize that no one endured greater tribulation. He buried all of his children except Fatimah. How many people will ever face that? Out of six children, to bury five? He lost the people closest to him. His most beloved uncle was slaughtered on the battlefield. The beloved wife with whom he had shared years of a blissful marriage died in his most difficult year. Abu Talib, the uncle who had raised him — he had been raised an orphan. There came a time when he had no supporters. The very people who had honored him were calling him a madman, saying he was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَجْنُونٌ</span>. How many people experience that — where suddenly your friends, your community, everyone around you calls you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَجْنُونٌ</span> and spits on you? He endured sanctions that most people could not bear to even imagine, and that was just one stretch of his life. All of those tribulations — and yet we have no record, not a single word, of complaint. Twenty-three years. No complaints. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. That is all he ever said. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>So that is the situation. To be angry at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدَرٌ</span> is to be in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> — in heedlessness, pure and simple.</p>
<p>Now, here is another important point. Imam al-Qarafi, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>, wrote a book dedicated entirely to distinguishing things in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> that appear similar but are not. One of the distinctions he draws is between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرِّضَا بِالْقَضَاءِ وَالْمَقْضِيِّ بِهِ</span> — being pleased with the divine decree itself versus being pleased with the thing that is decreed. When looking at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَضَاءٌ</span>, you have two components. There is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَضَاءٌ</span> itself, and separately the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدَرٌ</span> — and there is some difference of opinion around these two terms. The Maturidis and the Asharis actually hold opposite positions on which is which, but the point stands regardless of which label you assign. In one reading, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَضَاءٌ</span> means that Allah has decided to bring something about, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدَرٌ</span> means that He has measured and enabled the means by which it comes to be. The maqdi bihi is the thing that actually occurs.</p>
<p>Now — should we be pleased with everything that happens, the maqdi bihi? Why not? Because bad things happen, and do not forget that within Allah''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدَرٌ</span> is both <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَيْر</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرٌّ</span>. You should not be pleased with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرٌّ</span>, because Allah Himself is not pleased with it. Allah has decreed that there will be evil in the world He created, but He is not pleased with it — meaning He does not reward it; that is what His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِضْوَان</span> is. He does not reward it. So when you see <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْصِيَةٌ</span>, you should not like it or be pleased with it. Allah does not want you to be pleased with it. But you should not be displeased with the fact that Allah created a world in which such things exist. Do you see the distinction? One thing is refusing to be displeased with Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> and the world He created — because it is His world and He made it. The other is recognizing that within this world, Allah has placed things as a test and challenge for believers, and those are the things you should not be pleased with, because He does not want you to be pleased with them. If you were pleased with them, you would do nothing to change them. Your displeasure with them is precisely what compels you to go out and transform what is wrong in society.</p>
<p>That is the distinction. Imam al-Qarafi offers an analogy — just for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْرِيبُ الْمَعَانِي</span>, to bring the concept closer. He says: imagine you have a doctor, and you go to the doctor, and the doctor says you have gangrene — he has to cut your arm off. So he does. One day that doctor is walking past and overhears you talking to a friend, your arm gone, saying: &quot;I went to this incompetent doctor. I should have gotten a second opinion. He had no business doing this to me.&quot; The doctor hearing that is going to be hurt and upset — because he did not want to cause you pain; he wanted to help you, and the amputation was how he helped you. Now, if instead the doctor walks past and hears you say to someone: &quot;I went to this remarkable doctor. He cut off my arm, and it has caused me enormous pain — I cannot sleep at night. It has been the most severe treatment I have ever been through&quot; — when the doctor hears that, he feels compassion, not offense. Because you said nothing against him. All you said is that the treatment was grueling.</p>
<p>And this is the analogy for how we speak about what Allah does to us in this world. If you say, &quot;Allah is really testing me hard right now — it is difficult, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, there is wisdom in it&quot; — that will not make Allah angry. It is better not to broadcast your troubles to others, and doing so does diminish your patience. But there is a world of difference between saying &quot;I don''t deserve this — why is this happening to me?&quot; and saying &quot;this is so hard to bear.&quot; The first is a complaint against what Allah has done. The second is an expression of the difficulty you are having in enduring it. That distinction matters enormously.</p>
<p>He also makes this point: we were never commanded by Allah to enjoy hardship and tribulation. Nowhere in the Quran does Allah tell you to take pleasure in horrible things that happen to you. That is not how He made us, and there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكْلِيف</span> in that.</p>
<p>But Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> does censure certain people in the Quran when He says that He sent them a punishment, yet it did not humble them and did not make them call out to Him to lift it from them. You see this today — things worsen year after year, and people simply throw up their hands. Muslim countries — look at what happened to Kuwait. You would think they would all be rising every night for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهَجُّدٌ</span>, beseeching Allah to never let that happen again. But things return to how they were. There is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِكَانَة</span>, no genuine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> before Allah. Tribulations come, and if they do not change people, that is a deeply troubling state to be in.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'displeasure with the divine decree';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>Then he moves to the next disease: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُمْعَة</span>. Sumah is one of the diseases of the heart. The clearest way to understand it is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُمْعَة</span> is identical to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>, except that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> pertains to what the eyes perceive, while <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُمْعَة</span> pertains to what the ears hear. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُمْعَة</span> is wanting other people to hear about you — needing to tell them what you have done. &quot;You won''t believe it — I gave ten thousand dollars to such-and-such charity.&quot; You want to tell people about your good deeds. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُمْعَة</span>. And the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ سَمَّعَ سَمَّعَ اللَّهُ بِهِ يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ</span> — whoever does this, whoever broadcasts their deeds to people, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will broadcast to all people on the Day of Judgment what they really did. Because everyone has their skeletons. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُلُّكُمْ خَطَّاؤُونَ</span>. All of you sin. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَخَيْرُ الْخَطَّائِينَ التَّوَّابُونَ</span>. Everyone falls short. So if you go around advertising your good deeds, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says He will advertise your bad deeds on the Day of Judgment — and you do not want that. So say nothing.</p>
<p>He says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسُمْعَةُ أَكْبَرِ الطَّاعَاتِ بَعْدَ الْخُلُوصِ مِنْهُ مِنَ الْآفَاتِ</span> — you performed the action for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and then you went and told people about it. And so: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْبَتَ الْعَمَلُ</span> — you ruined it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لِبَاسُ الْعَرَضِيِّ الرِّيَائِيُّ وَالْعَمَلُ تُفْسِدُهُ، وَلَٰكِنْ إِنْ تُبْتُمْ ضَمَادٌ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> taints the action and ruins it, but if you make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, it can be rectified. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> is available to someone who fell into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُمْعَة</span>. And he also addresses the one who performs a deed intending from the outset to tell others about it.</p>
<p>So he didn''t act with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span>. He actually did it in the first place intending to tell other people about it — and that, too, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُمْعَة</span>.</p>
<p>The note here clarifies that the difference between seeking reputation by telling others of one''s deeds and showing off is that the former operates through hearing and the latter through sight. In other words, they are one and the same disease, but they travel different routes to affect the hearts of others. Ibn Abd al-Salam says that if the reason for disclosure is a desire to encourage others or to acknowledge <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s blessings upon you, then it is a different matter.</p>
<p>The hadith states that whoever informs others of his good deeds out of a desire for their benefit — and that is the explanation, not the literal text — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will inform them of his foul deeds on the Day of Judgment. And whoever displays his good deeds to others, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will display his bad deeds to them on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الْقِيَامَةِ</span>. It is related by Ahmad and Imam Muslim.</p>
<p>It is a well-established practice among the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَدِّثِينَ</span> to cite Imam Ahmad before Imam Muslim, even though Sahih Muslim is the more rigorously authenticated collection. The reason is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَلَاءً</span> — the exalted standing — of Imam Ahmad''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>. You will see this ordering frequently in classical texts.</p>
<p>Now he moves on to discuss what lies ahead, and this is a fascinating passage. There was a brigand from Bani Ababa, and the Arabs used him as a proverb — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَقْتَمُ مِنْ شِلَابٍ</span>, &quot;more of a brigand than Shilab&quot; — meaning if you want to describe someone as a truly ruthless robber, you invoke his name. The author uses this image deliberately: just as a highway robber severs the road and robs travelers of what they carry, the disease of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> — covetousness — severs the road to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and robs the seeker of his rewards. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span> is that brigand lying in wait on the path, and so we return once again to the root disease of disordered desire.</p>
<p>Abu al-Abbas al-Mursi relates that he was once in the marketplace intending to buy something when he spotted someone he recognized — a man well known for his scholarship and spiritual standing. The thought crossed his mind that if he approached this person, the man might offer him a favorable price out of deference to a fellow man of religion. At that moment, he says, he heard a call — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُودِيَ لَهُ</span>, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَاتِفٌ</span>, an inner voice — telling him: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامَةُ الدِّينِ</span> is to have no desire toward created things.&quot; That is the meaning of a sound <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>: to strip the heart of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> toward other people, entirely and without exception.</p>
<p>This is precisely the warning being sounded, because if you look back over the previous diseases discussed, they all share a common thread — the illusion that something can be gained from them. You imagine you are going to get something out of them. And the root cause of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span>, according to Sidi Ahmad Zarruq, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللهُ</span>, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> — heedlessness. You forget that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> alone is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّزَّاقُ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الضَّارِي</span>. You forget that human beings have nothing to give you that was not first given to them.</p>
<p>This <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> that descends on the heart is something <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> warns against repeatedly in the Quran. One verse reminds us: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ هُوَ ٱلرَّزَّاقُ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, He is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَزَّاق</span>, the sole Provider. Another verse states: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِن يَمْسَسْكَ ٱللَّهُ بِضُرٍّۢ فَلَا كَاشِفَ لَهُۥٓ إِلَّا هُوَ</span> — &quot;If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> causes you harm, there is none who can remove it except Him.&quot; And in the famous hadith, when the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> addressed Ibn Abbas, he said, &quot;O young man, I will teach you some words.&quot; Among what he taught him was this: if all of creation were to gather together to harm you, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> had not decreed it for you, they could not harm you in the slightest. And if all of creation were to gather to prevent some harm from reaching you, they could not prevent what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> had decreed. The pens have been lifted and the ink is dry. It is settled.</p>
<p>Everything that will happen to you is in the hands of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. So your desire should be directed toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> — not toward what other people possess.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 74">page 74</div>
<p>And what about people who wish you ill — who pray against you? Well, if you are standing upright with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, their prayers will gain them nothing. If you are oppressing them, then you have genuine cause for fear, because the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: &quot;Fear the prayer of the oppressed.&quot; But if you are not oppressing anyone, you have nothing to fear. A prayer made against someone without just cause does not reach its target — it returns to the one who made it. That is their affair, not yours.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> did not command us to make prayers against people except in the case of oppressors: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا عُدْوَانَ إِلَّا عَلَى الظَّالِمِينَ</span> — &quot;There is no transgression except against the wrongdoers.&quot; And it is said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> is the weapon of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span>. In many situations, the believers have nothing else at their disposal — no army, no political power — only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>. And to say &quot;only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>&quot; is to understate it, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> is immense. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> answers the prayers of those who call on Him sincerely.</p>
<p>So there is no need to worry about people''s prayers against you unless they have a justifiable grievance, in which case the appropriate response is to seek their forgiveness.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is heedlessness. And what Sidi Ahmad Zarruq said is that the cause of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> — you have become heedless of how creation actually works. If you genuinely believe that another person can benefit you independently of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>. It is like someone who recovers from an illness and says, &quot;I''m doing great — I took this remarkable medicine and it really worked,&quot; attributing the cure to the medicine itself and forgetting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> entirely. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>. A believer says: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> cured me. I took some medicine, and may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> have placed healing in it — He cured me through it.&quot; When you remind someone of this, they usually say, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, of course, of course&quot; — and they mean it. But the point is, human beings slip into this forgetfulness constantly. They forget how things truly work. And among the most consequential forms of this forgetfulness is the belief that people can harm or benefit you independently of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s will. They cannot. Not in any way, unless <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> wills it.</p>
<p>Now, in matters of armies and military strategy, one must obviously remain in the realm of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْبَاب</span> and deal with things realistically. But when it comes to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>, to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>, to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>, to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span>, to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُمْعَة</span> — to all these spiritual diseases — the belief that people hold power over your outcomes is pure <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَهْم</span>, an illusion with no foundation.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is a disease of the heart, and a serious one. Some scholars went so far as to say it is the only disease. Imam al-Junayd reportedly said he never witnessed a single wrong action that was not ultimately a consequence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>. If the heart is genuinely awake and aware of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, wrongdoing becomes impossible. He considered it the source disease, the root of all others.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 75">page 75</div>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِل</span> — heedless — is related to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُغَفَّل</span>, which means a simpleton, someone easy to deceive. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, then, is a state of being fooled, but the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِل</span> is fooling himself — he has simply stopped seeing things as they are. It is a kind of careless stupidity, though not a permanent one. It can be cured.</p>
<p>Then the text turns to ask: why would you speak ill of someone? What is the point? There is some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> lurking there — some desire. Why would you want to speak badly about a person to someone else unless you wanted that listener to think poorly of them? That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span>. You want something out of it. There is no other reason to do it. Lying, too — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنَاتُ الْغُرُورِ</span>, the deceits of the ego — falls into the same category. People lie because they want something. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span> will also occupy and distract the heart during prayer. And it produces insincere flattery of others — saying things you do not believe, like the salesman who tells every customer they look wonderful in what they are trying on, because he wants to make the sale. He may privately think the item looks terrible. But he will not say so. He says, &quot;That looks really good on you&quot; — because there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> in his heart.</p>
<p>There is a well-known hadith that the truthful, honest merchant will be with the martyrs on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الْقِيَامَةِ</span>. Consider what that means: to be a merchant without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> in the heart. How is that possible? It is possible when the merchant sees everything as coming from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. If a customer arrives, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has brought his provision to him. If the customer leaves without buying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> did not decree it. The merchant can simply be a human being. He does not need to lie. He does not need to manufacture enthusiasm he does not feel.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 76">page 76</div>
<p>In traditional Muslim societies, the marketplace was organized in a way that defies modern marketing logic entirely. All the merchants selling the same goods would be clustered together — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُكُّ الْعَطَّارِينَ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُكُّ الإِسْكَافِيِّينَ</span>, each trade in its own row. In Damascus you still know exactly where to find the clothes merchants and where to find the cobblers. They all sat side by side. Not only that, but traditionally they often prayed together, belonged to the same guilds, trained the same apprentices, attended the same circles of knowledge, and shared the same sheikhs. They were, genuinely, close friends.</p>
<p>What would happen — and this is not an idealized fiction — is that if a merchant felt he had sold enough for the day, he would close his stall, go home or to the mosque, and let his fellow merchants serve the remaining customers. There are still a few people like this alive today, though they tend to be quite old, and their sons — back from business school with their MBAs — often think they are being foolish.</p>
<p>Compare that with a culture whose business literature is full of titles like <em>How to Swim with the Sharks</em>, and whose advertisements picture a young man in a hotel mirror declaring he is going to &quot;eat the competition for breakfast&quot; — followed by a line about taking his children to an amusement park afterward. That image captures something true about the dominant culture: competitive capitalism in its purest form. Eliminate the competition. Whoever stands in your way, consume them. This mentality scales — from the individual merchant to the geopolitical stage — and the same logic that drives one businessman to undercut a neighbor drives nations to bomb other nations into ruin. It is, at its root, the same disease, just magnified to catastrophic proportions.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 77">page 77</div>
<p>This is precisely what the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> warned of when he said that what he feared most for this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التَّنَفُّس</span> — the frenzied competition over <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. He described it as the disease of civilizations, the thing that ultimately destroys them. Because if your philosophy is &quot;us against them,&quot; the problem is that everyone else''s philosophy is also &quot;us against them&quot; — and you are their &quot;them,&quot; just as they are your &quot;them.&quot; In the end, the logic of mutual destruction runs its course.</p>
<p>Some scholars observed that the vessels of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> are all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَوَّفَة</span> — hollow, open-ended, impossible to fill. You always want more. The desire is never satisfied. The Greeks had a word for this kind of longing: Eros — not in its later reduced sense, but as an insatiable yearning, always reaching toward something just beyond grasp. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> captured this perfectly when he said that nothing will fill the mouth of the son of Adam except the soil of his grave. If he had one mountain of gold, he would desire a second. Nothing suffices.</p>
<p>He also said that two types of people will never be quenched: the seeker of knowledge and the seeker of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. So you choose. Those are the two insatiable drives in this world — and you are either one or the other. You are either seeking knowledge or seeking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>.</p>
<p>Now, it must be said: if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> is directed toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, it becomes something praiseworthy. Desire itself is a human faculty — it is built into us. The disease is when desire is directed toward other than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, when you imagine that benefit lies in created things. The same principle applies to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَدٌ</span>: envy in its blameworthy form is resenting someone for what they have. But if you see someone surpassing you and it moves you to emulate them, to do what they are doing so that you might reach their level — that is a constructive use of the same impulse. These faculties can be turned toward your benefit or your destruction. It depends entirely on where they are pointed.</p>
<p>Now, listen to what comes next — it is a truly remarkable passage.</p>
<p>He says: if you ask desire what its trade is, it will answer, &quot;Earning humiliation.&quot; That is its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِرْفَة</span>. If you ask about its father, it will say, &quot;Doubt concerning the divine measuring out — what is not coming to me.&quot; And if you ask about its goal, it will say, &quot;The deprivation of the very thing it desires.&quot;</p>
<p>Now, another matter.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 78">page 78</div>
<p>In the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَاتِمَةٌ</span> — and this is genuinely important, so I want everyone to listen closely — what he is saying is that your desiring something relates directly to what has been allotted to you in this life, which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>. There are some vital concepts here. One is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> rarely pairs two names with the same attribute. You find it occasionally — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَفِيعُ الدَّرَجَاتِ</span>, things like that — but He is both <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّزَّاقُ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّازِقُ</span>, and we are called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَرْزُوقٌ</span>: the recipients, the passive form. What we receive is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>, and what gives it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>I mentioned last week that scholars differ on the definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>: some say it is only what you actually benefit from; others say it is everything you possess. The dominant opinion is that it is what you benefit from. There is nothing on earth except that its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> — He has given every creature its provision. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has divided the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْزَاق</span> of people, their provisions, and there are two kinds: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْقُ بُوپَانِي</span> — inward provision — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْقُ لَاهِرِي</span> — outward provision. The outward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> is your food, your health, the things we can observe. The inward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> is your company, your knowledge, what you genuinely learn, the character you develop — all of that.</p>
<p>Now, one of the striking things about life is that the same people keep appearing in it. It is a remarkable phenomenon. There are five billion people on this planet, yet when you think carefully about the people who truly matter in your life, the number is actually very small — and they keep returning, again and again, because they are part of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>. They are written into it. So your company will largely consist of the same people cycling back through your life. And within that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> there is both trial and blessing, hardship and good. Some people hold a pessimistic view of this; others hold an optimistic one — especially once you understand that all of it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one bringing it about.</p>
<p>Here is what is truly important to grasp. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has given the human being wealth as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> and placed it within him, and in His divine decree He has distributed the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْزَاق</span> among people. But some of one person''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> He has placed with another person, and there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَبٌ</span> — a means — through which you must go and retrieve it. So here is this person, and he holds your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>. It is not his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>; it is yours. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has placed it in his possession. How you receive it depends on the circumstances. If it is food he has that was written for you, he will invite you to dinner — he may not even particularly like you, but if it was written for you, you will end up at that table. Perhaps you were not even invited; you arrived with someone else. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> — it is there, it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقْسٌ</span> for you, and he cannot consume it, because it is not for him.</p>
<p>That is why the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, loved welcoming guests more than almost anything. The guest arrives with his own provision. You lose nothing by feeding a guest — that provision was not written for you; it was written for him. And he departs carrying the wrong actions of the household with him: when you are generous and courteous to a guest, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> removes wrong action as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَفَّارَةٌ</span>. The guest, then, is a tremendous blessing from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>Now, because of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْبَاب</span> — these means are so mysterious — people begin to think, &quot;My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> comes from him.&quot; That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, the heedlessness. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has arranged things so that your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> travels through someone else, people begin to reason: &quot;He benefits those people; perhaps I can get something from him too.&quot; And so the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> enters — you see the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَبٌ</span>, the means, and lose sight of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسَبِّب</span>, the true originator, the one who causes those means to exist. This is precisely where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعْ</span> becomes dangerous, and the clearest sign of it is when you grow angry at not receiving what you want. That is the surest indicator. When an expectation goes unfulfilled and you become angry, that is the sign.</p>
<p>This is obviously not about legal rights — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُقُوق</span>. If someone owes you something, has it, and is withholding it, that is injustice, and your anger at injustice is legitimate. What we are speaking about here is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>: what is coming to you, what is yours by divine decree.</p>
<p>This is why the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said in the hadith — conveying what Jibril told him — that no soul will die until it has completed every portion of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> allotted to it. It must complete it.</p>
<p>I was once on a plane in Mauritania, flying from Nema to Nouakchott. A man boarded at the front in a wheelchair. The moment we landed in Nouakchott — just as we touched down — the seats went back and the crew began performing CPR. He had died. And then, in the way that is not so extraordinary in Mauritania, the man who had come to meet us at the plane turned out to be a close friend of the deceased. So we learned the man''s whole story. He had been a minister, well respected, around forty-four years old. He had fallen ill and had gone out into the desert to recuperate. Someone administered an injection; he went into a coma. They brought him anyway — they were transporting him to Nouakchott. The point is that he had an appointment with the Angel of Death in that place. He had been out in the middle of the Mauritanian desert in a tent, but he was destined to die in Nouakchott. So all the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْبَاب</span> were arranged so that he would reach the place where his death had been decreed, because there was no other place he could die — it was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقْسٌ</span> for him to be in that place at that time.</p>
<p>And so it is: if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> intends for someone to reach a certain place, He will furnish the reasons to go there. The same holds true for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>. This is why the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said — Jibril conveying it to us — that all your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> is written for you. Then he said: have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Do not do anything <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to obtain your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span>, because it is coming to you regardless. Do not go about begging or soliciting from people. In fact, among the pledges the Companions took with the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه ﷺ</span> — one item of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَيْعَة</span> — was that they would not ask people for money. That was explicitly part of the covenant. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> should free a person from all such anxieties.</p>
<p>And then he said something further: do not let the delay of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِزْق</span> make you disobedient to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. If it is not arriving as quickly as you wish, do not let that delay become the cause of your falling into disobedience. That is a vital principle.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'seeking reputation';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>Now, everyone in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاح</span> — the next disease is extended hope. We will cover that next week. Last week we covered covetous desire, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span>. And in speaking of it, he says the Arabs have what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَمَّ سَاعَةً</span> — a fast-acting, immediately lethal poison. The immediately acting poison of this love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span> and all the diseases of the heart, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَطْوِيلُ الْأَمَلِ</span>. He defines it as habituating yourself to the assurance that death is a long way off.</p>
<p>Now, on one level this is a necessary human condition, and there is actually a remarkable wisdom in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَطْوِيلُ الْأَمَلِ</span>. Among the wisdoms the scholars have noted is this: if people did not have extended hope, they would not bother planting trees. If you believed you might die at any moment, you would not farm, you would not plan, you would not raise children with any thought for their future. The result would be that each generation would have to deal only with immediate circumstances, with no infrastructure to pass on. But because human beings do carry hopes of living on, they build, they farm, they create the infrastructure that the next generation inherits.</p>
<p>The famous story — from one of the Persian tales — is of a Persian <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَاهْ</span> who passed an old man planting an olive tree. The king asked: &quot;Will this tree be of any benefit to you? Surely you will die before it bears fruit&quot; — because a good olive tree can take several decades before it yields well. And the old man replied with what became a proverb among the Arabs: <em>&quot;They planted before us, and we ate from what they planted. So we plant, that those who come after us may eat.&quot;</em> The king gave him a reward. Then the man said, &quot;Already the tree has benefited me.&quot; So the king gave him more.</p>
<p>The point is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَطْوِيلُ الْأَمَلِ</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> from Allah — He has placed it in human nature, because without it people would not go to school, would not plan, would not build anything lasting.</p>
<p>One thing worth noting about modern society is that because of the apocalyptic character of the age we are living in, people are beginning to lose hope in the future. Many young people are becoming nihilistic — dressing in black, adopting a morbid view of the world. The fast-food culture — the idea of getting it now, not waiting — and the credit culture are genuinely ero<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g people''s sense that there is a future worth buil<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g toward. In fact, this may be the first American generation that genuinely expects to live worse than their parents. Prior generations of Americans were noted for their buoyant optimism about the future. That optimism is fa<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g — and its loss is itself a bad sign.</p>
<p>On the other hand, what he is saying is that too much hope is equally a deadly poison. It becomes a false hope, an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَانُ الْأَمَانِ</span>. The result it generates is hard-heartedness and indolence — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُلْقِحُ قَسْوَةَ الْقَلْبِ وَالْكَسَلَ</span>. Through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طُولُ الْأَمَلِ</span>, the heart becomes hard because it stops reflecting on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>, stops reflecting on its own mortality and dependence. And so it hardens.</p>
<p>Al-Fudayl ibn Iyad, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span> — one of the great early scholars and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهَّاد</span>, the people of asceticism — said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَمْسٌ مِنْ عَلَامَاتِ الشَّقَاوَةِ</span> There are five signs of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقَاوَةٌ</span> — wretchedness. The world is divided into exactly two kinds of people: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَعِيدٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقِيٌّ</span>. There is no third category. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَعِيد</span> is felicitous; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقِيٌّ</span> is wretched.</p>
<p>Now, if you look at Aristotle — foundational to so much of Western civilization — he wrote a book called <em>The Ethics</em> in which he essentially argues that the goal of life is leisure and happiness. This is echoed in the Declaration of Independence, written by Thomas Jefferson, which states that people have certain inalienable rights — and among them are life (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيٌّ</span>), liberty (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُرِّيَّة</span>) — rights recognized in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> as well — and then &quot;the pursuit of happiness.&quot; Jefferson''s original phrasing was actually &quot;the right to be free of debt when you come into the world,&quot; because what happens now is that national debt means children are born already in debt — approximately fifty thousand dollars at birth, based on previous generations'' overspen<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g. But he changed it to the pursuit of happiness. And what was happiness to a middle-aged white landowner in 1776? More land. In essence, the pursuit of stuff, of goods. This is woven into the fabric of the modern American dream: happiness understood as the pursuit of things.</p>
<p>What Islam did was radically reorient human beings'' understan<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g of happiness. Happiness is not necessarily what we might call a &quot;good life&quot; in the worldly sense. For Muslims, happiness — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَعَادَة</span> — is in the next world. If you are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَعِيدٌ</span> in the next world, it does not matter how your life in this world appeared. We looked at this in the previous class, in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِلَاء</span>, when we examined the Prophet''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> supplication: &quot;If You are not angry with me, then I do not care what You do with me&quot; — meaning: if my life in this world is difficult but I have not incurred Your wrath, I am content with that. Because he knew that ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَلْآخِرَةُ خَيْرٌۭ لَّكَ مِنَ ٱلْأُولَىٰ</span> ‎ — the afterlife is better for you than the first life.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 82">page 82</div>
<p>The opposite of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَعَادَة</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقَاوَةٌ</span> — wretchedness — the condition of the person who has no portion in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>. That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقِيٌّ</span> means. So al-Fudayl ibn Iyad said there are five signs of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقَاوَةٌ</span>.</p>
<p>The first is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَسْوَةُ الْقَلْبِ</span> — a hard heart. That is a sign of wretchedness. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> once said to a man who had just watched him kiss a child: &quot;I kiss my children — I have ten and I have never kissed a single one.&quot; He was boasting of it as a kind of tough-Arab pride. And the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: &quot;I cannot do anything for a man from whom Allah has removed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> from his heart.&quot; A hard heart means you have lost your portion of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>. <em>&quot;Have mercy on those on the earth, and He in the heavens will have mercy on you.&quot;</em></p>
<p>The second sign is an eye that does not weep. The scholars say: if you want to know whether your heart is hard, look at your eye. A dry eye indicates a hard heart. The person with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِقَّةٌ</span> — tenderness — in the heart has a moist eye; the person without it has a dry one.</p>
<p>The third is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِلَّةُ الْحَيَاءِ</span> — a deficiency in modesty and shame. People who have no shame. As the saying goes: if you have no shame, do as you please. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِلَّةُ الْحَيَاءِ</span> is a sign of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقَاوَةٌ</span>.</p>
<p>The fourth is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّغْبَةُ فِي الدُّنْيَا</span> — desire for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> — which is likewise a sign of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَقَاوَةٌ</span>. The scholars say that no one adds to his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> except that it diminishes something of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, and no one adds to his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> except that it diminishes something of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> — because the two are opposites by nature. An increase in one is a decrease in the other.</p>
<p>This does not mean you automatically assume that a wealthy person has sacrificed his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>. There is a well-known teaching story — a Moroccan tale — about a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span>, an ascetic, who had heard of a great <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ</span> of Allah and set out to visit him. He came down from his mountain ri<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g his lion. He arrived to find the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخ</span> living in a large palace. He was astonished — he himself lived in a cave. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخ</span> came out to greet him, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> asked where he could stable his lion. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخ</span> said, &quot;Put him in my barn with the cow.&quot; He did not realize the lion would eat the cow. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخ</span> said, &quot;No, no — just put him in the barn with the cow.&quot; So in he went.</p>
<p>Then they sat down to a large <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>ner. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخ</span> ate a few bites and said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرُ اللَّهَ</span> — how luxurious this is.&quot; The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> thought: &quot;I live on water and dates.&quot; That night the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخ</span> went to sleep, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> stayed up all night in prayer — only to hear the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخ</span> snoring in the next room. He was utterly scandalized. He decided that after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> he was leaving. He went to retrieve his lion — and found that the cow had eaten the lion.</p>
<p>So he asks the man, &quot;Where''s my lion?&quot; The man replies, &quot;Your lion is just like you — all outward show.&quot; And then he adds, &quot;And you thought my cow was harmless.&quot;</p>
<p>But she was a lion on the inside. The point of that story is that you have to be very careful, because outward appearances can fool people. Sidi Ahmad Zarruk has something remarkable to say about this — though we''ll return to that when we reach the discussion of having a bad opinion of others.</p>
<p>Those five things, then, are signs of hardness of the heart. What this science is ultimately about is bringing life back to the heart.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 75">page 75</div>
<p>One of the interesting observations in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُورُ الْيَقِينِ</span> — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَّةٌ</span> by an Egyptian scholar — concerns how many of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> lost everything they possessed of this world. He notes that their concern was not for material comfort, and he speaks of the first masjid built at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُبَاء</span>, describing it as so simple that a Muslim today would not recognize it as a masjid. He argues that we have become obsessed with adorning outward appearances while our interiors have been left desolate. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span>, he says, were the opposite: they had no concern for beautifying the outward and were concerned solely with purifying their hearts. That difference in emphasis is enormous.</p>
<p>What the text is saying, then, is that hardness of heart will generate indolence toward obligations, which in turn opens inroads into the prohibited. Indolence here means a lack of energy, a failure of will. Interestingly, there are people who have tremendous energy for worldly pursuits yet none whatsoever for the Hereafter. People genuinely say things like, &quot;I really can''t pray — I work so hard, I get home exhausted, I fall asleep, I''d like to pray but I just don''t have time.&quot; That is a sign of a dead heart, because when someone has time for the things of this world but not for the Hereafter, it reveals that they do not truly believe in the Hereafter.</p>
<p>Hasan al-Basri said: &quot;I saw seventy men of Badr. If you saw them, you would say they were madmen&quot; — he was speaking to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَابِعِينَ</span> — &quot;you would call them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَجَانٍ</span>. And if they saw the best of you, they would say these people have no character. And if they saw the worst of you, they would say these people do not believe in the Day of Judgment.&quot; Belief in the Hereafter manifests in a person''s behavior and concerns. The stronger the belief, the more one is preoccupied with protecting one''s stan<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g in the Hereafter.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 76">page 76</div>
<p>Looking at the footnote: loss of energy even for acts of obedience leads a person to convince himself that he is simply unable to fulfill his obligations — due to his state — and this is precisely what opens the door to the forbidden. He tells himself, &quot;I just can''t do it,&quot; and that very attitude leads him toward the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طُولُ الْأَمَلِ</span> is genuinely dangerous.</p>
<p>There is a saying — not a hadith, though it is often quoted as one — that goes: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْمَلْ لِدُنْيَاكَ كَأَنَّكَ تَعِيشُ أَبَدًا، وَاعْمَلْ لِآخِرَتِكَ كَأَنَّكَ تَمُوتُ غَدًا</span> — &quot;Act for your worldly life as if you will live forever, and act for your Hereafter as if you will die tomorrow.&quot;</p>
<p>Shaykh Bashir — Usman Bashir, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ ٱللَّٰهُ</span> — a dear friend of mine who spent most of his life in Me<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>a and was from Eritrea — once told me that people completely misunderstand that saying. It is almost always used as a justification for working hard at worldly pursuits. People love to quote it: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَخِي</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْمَلْ لِدُنْيَاكَ كَأَنَّكَ تَعِيشُ أَبَدًا</span>!&quot; But he said: if you read it with both lenses at once — your worldly life, for which you have forever; and your Hereafter, for which you die tomorrow — you will naturally begin pouring your energy into the Hereafter and say to yourself, &quot;I have plenty of time to take care of my worldly affairs.&quot; The understan<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g most people carry is exactly backwards, and his rea<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g is really the correct one.</p>
<p>Related to this is the Quranic verse: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا تَنسَ نَصِيبَكَ مِنَ ٱلدُّنْيَا</span> — &quot;Do not forget your share of this world&quot; (28:77). This has been interpreted in two ways. One rea<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g is: do not forget what Allah has given you, so that you may expend it for the Hereafter. The other is: the world is here, Allah gave it to you, so do not forget that it exists — but the essential thing remains the Hereafter. Both interpretations are valid positions.</p>
<p>We are not anti-worldly in the way that, say, traditional Christianity tended to be. We are not against the world. The world is a place Allah created for us to enjoy as well — but not to enjoy to the point where we forget what we are truly here for, and that it is a bridge.</p>
<p>Then the text cites a verse in which Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says, &quot;A long time passed and their hearts became hard.&quot; The idea is that if you are not reflecting on the Hereafter, the longer you remain on this earth, the harder the heart grows.</p>
<p>If you observe children, you notice something striking: they can cry at the drop of a hat. Part of this is that their hearts are very supple — they can move through extreme joy and extreme sadness within a single day. Their hearts are like this because they are very pure, and so everything affects them in profound ways. As a person grows older, that softness becomes increasingly difficult to recover.</p>
<p>This is one reason the scholars say the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> loved women among the things made beloved to him in this world — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لِرِقَّةِ قُلُوبِهِنَّ</span>, because their hearts are more tender than those of men. Women, generally speaking, have more compassion and more openness of heart. For men, it is very easy for the heart to harden. And so the danger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طُولُ الْأَمَلِ</span> is precisely that it produces hardness of heart.</p>
<p>Then the text says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَكِنْ نُحُوفِ حَقْرِي مِنْ نِرَادٍ أَنْعَبْ، أَوْ كَانَ إِفِي تَرْسِنِيكِ الْإِرِنْمَانِ لَمْ يُعَبْ</span> — regar<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g the one who is engaged in preparing for the next life, or in producing works of knowledge. The author — himself a scholar — notes that writing books that benefit people is among the highest things one can do: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ جَارِيَةٌ</span>, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْمٌ يُنْتَفَعُ بِهِ النَّاسُ</span>, ongoing charity or knowledge from which people continue to benefit. For such a person, desiring a long life is not blameworthy.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَتَمَنَّ الْمَوْتَ</span> — do not wish for death. For if you did wrong in the past, you want more life in order to make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span> and set things right. And if you did good, you want more time to do more good. Either way — whether your past has been poor or praiseworthy — each new day is an opportunity to turn back to Allah and change. So one should always desire a long life when engaged in good things. If one is not engaged in good things, however, then a long life is a disaster.</p>
<p>For the person who does not waste time, who recognizes the urgency of the human condition and their own impen<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g mortality, and who sets out doing useful deeds — sprea<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g knowledge or anything else that benefits people — Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَمْكُثُ فِي الْأَرْضِ</span> — what benefits people remains and continues in the earth.</p>
<p>Mutamir ibn Sulayman once fell gravely ill, and someone asked him whether he feared dying from it. He said, &quot;No, no, no — I have a verse in the Quran that tells me I am not going to die yet.&quot; The person asked, &quot;Which one?&quot; He said, &quot;Allah says that what benefits people stays for a long time&quot; — and he was a great scholar, enormously beneficial to others.</p>
<p>Hard-heartedness, then, manifests in several ways: eyes that do not weep, an absence of shame and modesty, love of this world, and extended, false hope. There is also a very beautiful tradition that says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأَمَلُ رَحْمَةٌ مِنَ اللهِ، وَلَوْلَاهُ لَمَا زَرَعَ زَارِعٌ وَلَا وَلَدَ وَالِدٌ</span> — extended hope is a mercy from Allah, for had it not been for hope, no farmer would plant seeds and no mother would bear a child. It is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span> from Allah — that is the important thing to understand.</p>
<p>It is part of our nature. We genuinely believe, at some deep level, that we are going on. Without that sense, we could not function in the world — we would simply be paralyzed. So the point is not to fall into paralysis, but to reflect on death. The poet says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَصِيدَة</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَكَّرْ أَخِيَ فِي الْمَوْتِ، إِنَّهُ نَازِلٌ، وَقَصِّرِ الْأَمَلَ، فَيَنْكَسِرَ الْقَلْبُ</span> — reflect on death, for it is coming, and shorten your hopes, for that is what breaks open the hardened heart.</p>
<p>One of the beneficial exercises the early Muslims practiced was to meditate on death and on the stages that follow it — to reflect on the stations of the journey itself, inclu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطٌ</span>, and what it means to cross it. It is interesting that athletes in this country are trained to use visualization: a boxer will mentally run through a fight in advance as psychological preparation. This is really the same idea. You are preparing yourself psychologically for a journey you will actually make. Marat al-Raji has a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَصِيدَة</span> about death in which he says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَسَتُسَافِرُ، رِضًا لَا يُسَافِرُ</span> — &quot;You will travel tomorrow, and what a journey.&quot; Meaning death. What a journey indeed.</p>
<p>The moment you die, there is a severance — and yet you remain conscious, in a state of heightened awareness so acute that this present life comes to seem like a dream by comparison. This is why people at the moment of death often feel as though they are waking up. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّاسُ نِيَامٌ، فَإِذَا مَاتُوا انْتَبَهُوا</span> — &quot;People are asleep; when they die, they awaken.&quot;</p>
<p>The exercise many of the early Muslims practiced was to actually lie down and imagine being lowered into the grave. This is not a morbid exercise — it is training. And it is genuinely effective. Some of the scholars said that for a person overcome by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span> — appetites and desires — particularly young people, this kind of reflection is one of the best ways to bring sobriety to the heart.</p>
<p>Now, there is an important distinction that Imam al-Qarafi, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ ٱللَّٰهُ</span>, draws: the difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاءٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طُولُ الْأَمَلِ</span>. <em>Raja</em> means hope, and it is something altogether different from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَمَلٌ</span>. There are many verses in the Quran affirming this: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَّقَدْ كَانَ لَكُمْ فِى رَسُولِ ٱللَّهِ أُسْوَةٌ حَسَنَةٌۭ لِّمَن كَانَ يَرْجُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَٱلْيَوْمَ ٱلْآخِرَ وَذَكَرَ ٱللَّهَ كَثِيرًۭا</span> ‎ — whoever hopes in Allah has a beautiful example in the Messenger of Allah. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاءٌ</span> directed toward Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, and toward the Last Day.</p>
<p>There is a famous hadith in which Aisha, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهَا</span>, recounts that the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رضي الله عنه من أحب الله أحب لقاءه</span> — whoever loves to meet Allah, Allah loves to meet him. Aisha said, &quot;O Messenger of Allah, but what about disliking death?&quot; He said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُلُّكُمْ تَكْرَهُ الْمَوْتَ</span> — that natural aversion to death itself is something everyone shares. But not wanting to meet one''s Lord is a different matter — the one whose heart is at peace (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُطْمَئِنّ</span>) looks forward to that meeting.</p>
<p>The scholars emphasize the relationship between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَوْف</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاءٌ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاعْبُدُوهُمْ بَيْنَ الْخَوْفِ وَالرَّجَاءِ</span> — you walk with the two sandals of fear and hope. If you are overwhelmed by fear, it pushes you into a psychological state of terror that produces <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَأْس</span> — despair. Too much fear leads to despair of Allah''s mercy; a person begins to think they will never be saved. This is a well-known religious illness. It is less common today, since people are generally less religiously intense, but it remains real for certain individuals. Some Christians, particularly in fire-and-brimstone evangelical traditions that dwell heavily on hellfire and damnation, can easily fall into this state. The early Puritan settlers actually believed that only 144,000 people would be saved — the elect. Since there were already more than 144,000 Puritans, the odds in that theology were not favorable. The Jehovah''s Witnesses hold a similar belief, drawn from the Book of Revelation. That is excessive fear.</p>
<p>Too much hope — excessive <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاءٌ</span> — is the opposite disease, producing complacency: &quot;I have no problem, I am saved,&quot; and so action stops entirely. There is an interesting observation one could make about certain Baptist theology, which holds that once you accept Jesus as your Savior, you are saved regardless of what you subsequently do. The emphasis falls overwhelmingly on salvation through faith, with very little weight placed on fear of hellfire. This is why someone like Jimmy Swaggart can fall publicly, and then return to the pulpit to preach — and the congregation is still there listening. If the same thing happened to an imam, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا طَهَّرَ اللَّهُ</span> — that would simply be the end. No one would retain any respect for that person, because Muslims hold far higher expectations of those they accept as examples and leaders in their communities.</p>
<p>This is, incidentally, one reason why it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكْرُوهٌ</span> for the child of an illegitimate union to lead the prayer — not because of any sin on the child''s part, but because such a person is not the most fitting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُدْوَة</span>, or example, for the community. There is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِثْم</span> — no blame — on such a child. None whatsoever. What the parents did is entirely between them and their Lord. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَّا تَزِرُ وَازِرَةٌۭ وِزْرَ أُخْرَىٰ</span> ‎ — no soul bears the burden of another''s wrongdoing. There were people in the Arabian Peninsula at the time of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> who were born outside of marriage, who became Muslim, and who were counted among the finest human beings. The least of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span>, in terms of their deeds, surpasses the best of us — simply because they were given the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُؤْيَة</span>, the vision of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> in waking life, and believed in him. That alone is a bounty from Allah beyond reckoning.</p>
<p>So: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَمَلٌ</span> is a desire for long life, for wealth, for all the things we generally associate with happiness in this world. It is a kind of hope — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاءٌ</span>. But there is something else called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمْنِيَّةٌ</span>, and it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمْنِيَّةٌ</span> that is blameworthy in Islam. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمْنِيَّة</span> is a hope —</p>
<p>The difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاح</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمْنِيَة</span> is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاح</span> always involves taking the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَبَبٌ</span> — in other words, actually pursuing the means to obtain what you want. With <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمْنِيَة</span>, by contrast, it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِلَا سَبَبٍ</span>: no means are taken at all.</p>
<p>Say you want to get healthy. You start watching your diet, you start exercising — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاح</span>. But someone who says, &quot;I really want to get healthy,&quot; while continuing to eat junk food and sit in front of the television, doing nothing to change his situation — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمْنِيَة</span>. And that, Allah says, is exactly what the Jews and Christians have toward the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>: they have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمْنِيَة</span>. They are doing nothing to earn the next life; in fact, they are corrupting in the earth. That is why Muslims must be on guard, because — as my friend Abd al-Hakim Murad once told a story about a Pakistani man who overheard two or three racists in London: one said he was moving to Wales because Muslims were only one or three percent of the population there, another said he preferred Scotland at two percent, and a third was going to Ireland at one percent. The Pakistani man said, &quot;Why don''t you go to hell? There are no Muslims there.&quot; What Abd al-Hakim observed about that is that the assumption, of course, is that Muslims don''t go to hell. Unfortunately, there is going to be a great shock on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الْقِيَامَةِ</span> — because if all you have is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمْنِيَة</span>, and all your Islam amounts to is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَةُ تَيْمٍ</span>, what they call the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَة</span> of birth and death — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَةُ تَوْلِيدَتِكَ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَةُ تَوَفَّاتِكَ</span> — your testimony recorded at birth on your birth certificate and at death on your death certificate, if that is the sum of it, then you have a serious problem.</p>
<p>I was once in Santa Clara; Farif was with me. A family came — their grandfather had died, and they came to the masjid to bury him. None of them were practicing Muslims, and neither had their grandfather been, but when he died they realized, &quot;We are Muslims — how do we bury him? Let''s take him to the mosque.&quot; So they brought him, and we washed the body and performed the rites. But it is deeply sad when people''s entire experience of Islam is reduced to the moments of marriage and death — they need an imam to perform the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِكَاح</span>, or they need someone who knows how to wash the body and pray over it. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمْنِيَة</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاء</span>, then — hoping in Allah — means you are actually doing what is required to be pleasing to Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. The scholars also say that hope should be the dominant state throughout one''s life, but when you reach your deathbed, you should let go of fear entirely and rest in absolute hope in Allah. At that moment there should be no fear; you should be completely immersed in a sense of Allah''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, trusting that He will forgive you and accept you, and hol<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>g the best opinion of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>Now, Imam al-Haythami makes another point that Shaykh al-Khadim mentions in his commentary, and it is important: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُعْدُ الأَمَلِ</span> — this extended hope, this drawn-out expectation — is, at its root, a heedlessness about the reality of death. But he says that in and of itself it is not a wrong action. There is no obligatory command (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">واجِبٌ</span>) to remember death. When the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said to remember death, and also said, &quot;I used to tell you not to visit graves, but now I tell you: go visit them, because it reminds you of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>&quot; — the point is that remembering death is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْدُوبٌ</span>, recommended. In the same way, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> beyond what is prescribed in the prayer is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْدُوبٌ</span>:</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اُذْكُرِ اللَّهَ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اُذْكُرُوا</span> — there is the imperative, the command, but it is for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَدْب</span>, not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُجُوب</span>. You are not obligated to remember Allah as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْضٌ</span>; it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْدُوبٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَحَبٌّ</span>, strongly encouraged, because it prepares you. So heedlessness of death is not a wrong action in itself.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 85">page 85</div>
<p>Similarly, making money, being engaged in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, being active in worldly affairs — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبَاحٌ</span>. Allah did not prohibit it. You may trade on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span>; there is nothing wrong with it. You can combine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span>. You do not have to go in a state of pure spiritual detachment. You can take goods, bring books, sell things — within whatever is permitted. People who shave pilgrims, bringing razors and charging for the service — they are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَّاج</span> and they are earning money, and there is nothing wrong with that. Our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span> does not even frown upon it. You should not say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرُ اللهَ</span> and think they are not spiritual people; that attitude is not from our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>.</p>
<p>So he says: that is permissible — <em>unless</em> it was done for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَاخُر</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَاثُر</span>, or for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَسْوِيفُ التَّوْبَةِ</span>, in which case it becomes a grave sin (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَبِيرَةٌ</span>). If you are acquiring wealth in order to compete with others out of arrogance, or to hoard it in a contest of accumulation, or — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَسْوِيفُ التَّوْبَةِ</span> — to keep putting off repentance, then it is blameworthy. As the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span> says:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلْ يُرِيدُ الْإِنْسَانُ لِيَفْجُرَ أَمَامَهُ، يَسْأَلُ أَيَّانَ يَوْمُ الْقِيَامَةِ</span></p>
<p>Man wants to keep sinning in whatever remains of his life and asks, &quot;When is the Day of Judgment?&quot; — as though it is far off, as though he need not think about it. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> say in their commentaries on this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span> that one of its meanings is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُسَوِّفُ التَّوْبَةَ</span> — he keeps postponing his repentance. This is what is called in this culture &quot;sowing your wild oats&quot; — the idea that when you are young and have the energy for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعَاصِي</span>, you should get them out of your system, because you won''t have the energy when you are older. This is a concept in American and European culture. And this is precisely why Allah loves the young man who is in obedience — because when you grow old, you no longer have the energy for disobedience.</p>
<p>Some people live badly for decades, then around forty-five or fifty, death begins to feel real, they make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, and then they become self-righteous, pointing fingers at everyone else, having completely forgotten who they themselves were. This is a real problem — fifty years old, having done every terrible thing: drinking, cheating, you name it — and then at fifty realizing mortality, going to the masjid, becoming outwardly devout, and immediately beginning to judge others. That is a disease of its own.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'false hopes';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>Now, the next disease is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span>, and it is a somewhat nuanced concept because it has a related term in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> vocabulary, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span>. The Arabs, before Islam, when they wanted to make a decision, would go to a flock of birds and startle them — a form of divination. If the flock flew to the left, they took it as a bad omen; if it flew to the right, they took it as a positive omen. The right-flying birds they called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُمْنٌ</span> — from which <em>Yemen</em>, being to the right when one faces the rising sun — and the left-flying birds they called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُؤْم</span>, which is why the Levant (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشَّامُ</span>) carries that sense of the left. In the Arabian Peninsula, facing the sun in the morning, Yemen is on the right (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْيَمِينُ</span>) and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشَّامُ</span> is to the left. You also get <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَمَلَ</span> — north, the left side — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُنُب</span>, south, from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنْب</span>, meaning side or flank, because you recline on your right side; so the south is on the right and the north is on the left.</p>
<p>This is also reflected in traditional Muslim cartography. Muslims always oriented their maps with the south at the top and the north at the bottom. I can show you maps from the classical books of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْقِيتٌ</span> — the science of timekeeping, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْمُ الْفَلَكِ</span> of the Muslims — and all of them show the south at the top. This is why I have long believed that Muslims should make their own maps.</p>
<p>I first came to this realization years ago in New Mexico, when I was at a flea market with Hakim Archuleta. I found an old antique compass, and it was cheap. The vendor said, &quot;There''s only one problem — instead of pointing north, the arrow points south.&quot; I said, &quot;That''s fantastic.&quot; As we walked away I asked my friend Hakim, who knows a great deal about science, why it would make any difference. He said it''s simply a matter of polarization — the paint is facing south because that is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُطْب</span>, the pole. That started me thinking about the question of south and north, and why we have made north dominant.</p>
<p>Then I came across a map by al-Idrisi — the first great cartographer of the world, a Moroccan who drew a world map with the south at the top and the north at the bottom. The European mapmakers drew directly from his work, but they recognized the significance of putting the south at the top: it placed Africa above Europe. So they flipped it, and from that point on their maps had north at the top. Marshall Hodgson makes exactly this observation in <em>The Venture of Islam</em> — he says it is unfortunate that Muslims do not recognize that they produced the first world map and oriented it with the south at the top, and that they should return to that orientation, because the north-dominant map reflects a purely Eurocentric worldview that has no historical foundation. A south-oriented map would put Africa at the top and Europe at the bottom; we would say, &quot;I''m going down to Europe and up to Africa.&quot;</p>
<p>We would also have to say &quot;up to Los Angeles and down to San Francisco.&quot; And in Arabic, this is in fact how it works — the south is up. Southern Egypt is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَعِيد</span>, Upper Egypt, while the north is Lower Egypt. In Libya as well, people say, &quot;We are going up south&quot; and &quot;down to the sea,&quot; which is in the north. That is traditional cosmology, and do not think it is insignificant — the effect of spending a lifetime looking at maps that place Africa at the bottom is real. On a south-oriented map, moreover, nothing looms over Arabia. Look at the map the Australians produced when they decided they were tired of being &quot;down under&quot; — they simply turned it right-side up. I have a map from New Zealand, sent to me by a friend after I told her about this. It is a genuinely revealing perspective: you see how close Ireland is to Morocco, how close Germany is to England, and you begin to understand the depth of the Germanic-English connection in a way the north-dominant map never quite conveys. But that is a digression from the diseases of the heart — a worthwhile one, I think, but a digression nonetheless.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span> — the birds flying right or left — is at its root superstition, but it is also more than that, because in Arabic the word for a pessimist derives from the same concept: the pessimist is the one who always sees the worst in any situation, who will say about any plan, &quot;It will never work.&quot; The root of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span> is the ignorance that all affairs belong to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> alone. For the Muslim, this means having a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُسْنُ الظَّنِّ</span> — a good opinion — of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. The non-Muslim has a bad opinion of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>; the Muslim has a good one. That is the essential difference. So you should always cultivate this good opinion of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>He now moves toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُوءُ الظَّنِّ</span> — bad opinion — but before doing so I want to say one more thing about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span>. There are really two types. The first — valid for people of sound intellect and normative experience — is when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحُكْمُ الْعَادِي</span>, empirical probability, establishes that a thing consistently causes harm. If you put your hand near a cobra, it bites you and you die. In that case, you must use your intellect; avoiding it is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span>, it is rational. If something is harmful by established norm, you must avoid it, and the scholars say that if you do not, you are not considered a person of sound reason — you are acting foolishly. There are, of course, exceptional cases: one of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوْلِيَاء</span> was walking with a student, they came upon a lion on the path, the student was terrified, the shaykh told him to take the high road and continued walking, and the lion did not touch him. But that is not a station available to everyone.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 88">page 88</div>
<p>Imam al-Qarafi, a great Maliki scholar, says that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span> is a bad opinion and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span> as an <em>action</em> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَمَلٌ</span> — is what results from holding that bad opinion, as long as the normative experience in which harm results does not apply. Walking under a ladder, for instance. In this culture: step on a crack, you''ll break your mother''s back. Children grow up avoiding the cracks in the pavement. A black cat crossing your path — thirteen years of bad luck. Breaking a mirror. Knocking on wood: &quot;I''ve been healthy for years&quot; — then you knock wood. These are all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span> and are in fact <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to act upon. If the Sharia gives us specific <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْكَار</span> and practices, those are different — they come from revelation, not superstition. But knocking on wood goes back to pagan tree-worship; those who venerated sacred trees would knock on them. That is the origin. Among the Arabs in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاهِلِيَّة</span>, things like walking between two sheep were considered bad luck. Seeing an owl was an ill omen. In some cultures, a shooting star was a bad omen. All of these are forms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span>.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> also established that no one will ever be entirely free of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span> — it is part of human nature. There is a book by the skeptic Michael Shermer called <em>Why People Believe Weird Things</em>, and much of it addresses exactly this: how deeply embedded superstition is in human psychology. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> points to the same reality. People check their horoscope before leaving the house. People will not buy or sell stocks without first consulting their astrologer — a completely absurd premise, yet they believe in it and it gives them a sense of control. People consult the I Ching before making any significant decision.</p>
<p>There''s people that do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَلٌ</span> in the Sahara Desert, and there are those who flip coins or toss them into wishing wells. All of these things are really part of human nature.</p>
<p>So when the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> mentioned that one of three things will inevitably come to you in life, one of them was ill omens — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span>. And he said the way to cure it is this: if you feel that bad omen rising in you, just keep going. So if, in this culture, you''re about to walk under a ladder and something stirs in your heart, you just keep walking — that''s how you break it. And if a plant falls on your head — no, I''m just kidding. But that really is the way you break it: you ignore it and keep moving.</p>
<p>A friend of mine who has strong <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> told me about someone who claimed to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ</span> — a claim my friend didn''t think was warranted — and he said something about it. The followers of this man turned to my friend and said, &quot;Don''t you know? If he makes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> against you, he can destroy you.&quot; And my friend replied, &quot;Go ahead — make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> against me right now. I want you to.&quot; What he was trying to show them was that this is not how Muslims behave. Real Muslims don''t make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> against one another, and a true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ</span> certainly doesn''t. And if he truly is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> can bring you difficulty without him even intending it.</p>
<p>There is a Sheikh I know in Mauritania, Muhammad Zayn — whom some of you have met — who is from the family of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. He is a deeply righteous man who would never willingly harm anyone. But it is well known that people who speak ill of him or bear bad attitudes toward him tend to find trouble. So you don''t want to be careless with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوْلِيَاء</span>. At the same time, as the Moroccans say: don''t belittle any Muslim, because he might be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. In other words, be generally mindful of harming any Muslim — it is simply not a good thing to do.</p>
<p>So that is the matter of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طِيَرَة</span>. Now the next disease is having a bad opinion of others — what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 93">page 93</div>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span> is suspicion, conjecture, opinion formed without any supporting facts. The worst form of bad opinion is harboring it toward someone whose outward appearance and conduct are plainly righteous. Outward appearance was something the early Muslims actually considered significant. While they also cautioned against dismissing those who may look unimpressive — because sometimes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> veils His friends behind an ordinary exterior — they recognized what the Arabs call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسْمَة</span>: the quality of being <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسِيمٌ</span>, someone whose face carries a kind of nobility. When an Arab delegate came to the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> and then departed, the companions asked what he thought of him. He said, &quot;His face is not the face of a liar.&quot; This is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرَاسَةٌ</span> — the capacity to discern goodness or corruption in a person''s countenance. So forming a bad opinion of someone who possesses this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسْمَة</span>, who outwardly presents as a righteous person, is a particularly serious form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says that some forms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span> are wrong action. This is important because ordinarily we are not held accountable for what merely passes through the heart — but there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِبَة</span> of the heart, a form of backbiting that occurs entirely within the chest: entertaining a bad and unfounded opinion of someone, and it is written against you. This is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِبَةُ الْقَلْبِ</span>. It is unlike the passing thoughts that cross the mind without being invited — you are not accountable for those. What does incur accountability is what the scholars call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَزِيمَة</span>: the thought that settles and takes root. We will go into these levels in more detail when we reach the section on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَوَاطِرُ</span>, because there are degrees — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثُ النَّفْسِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَزِيمَة</span> — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَزِيمَة</span> is the highest degree, the one written against you. When it runs deep in your heart such that it changes how you treat someone, it becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِبَةُ الْقَلْبِ</span> — a form of backbiting in which no second person is even present. It is entirely between you and your own heart. And that is not permissible. It is written against you.</p>
<p>So the condition for blameworthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span> is that your heart has reached a verdict — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَزِيمَة</span> — judging a person based purely on suspicion, without a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَلِيل</span>, without any evidence that actually warrants that judgment. If someone asks you, &quot;Why don''t you like that person?&quot; and you say, &quot;I just feel it in my heart — something is wrong,&quot; that is a different matter we will come to shortly. But consider the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكُمْ وَالظَّنَّ، فَإِنَّ الظَّنَّ أَكْذَبُ الْحَدِيثِ</span> — &quot;Beware of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span>, for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span> is the most deceptive of speech.&quot; If you tell someone, &quot;So-and-so is a really bad person,&quot; and they ask, &quot;What''s your proof?&quot; and you say, &quot;I just know&quot; — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَكْذَبُ الْحَدِيثِ</span>. That is a lie, because you have no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَلِيل</span>. Our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> is built on the principle: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمِرْتُ أَنْ أَحْكُمَ بِالظَّوَاهِرِ</span> — &quot;I was commanded to judge by outward matters.&quot; So it is essential to look at behavior, at what is manifest.</p>
<p>Then he says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا إِثْمَ فِي الشَّكِّ وَعَدَمِ الِاسْتِنَادِ لِسَبَبٍ فَعَدَمٌ يَكُونُ مُجَرَّدًا</span> — there is nothing wrong with having doubts about someone, or even a bad opinion of him, if that opinion is grounded in sound reason and not merely baseless. This is a critical distinction.</p>
<p>The difference between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكٌّ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span> is this: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span> is when your internal scales have tipped — you have made a judgment. Shakk is when both pans are level. Is he good? Unknown. Is he bad? Unknown. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكٌّ</span>: &quot;I simply don''t know, and I must be cautious.&quot; That is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span> is when you come to a conclusion — without proof — that the person is bad. Everyone understand the difference? This distinction will genuinely serve you in life.</p>
<p>So how do we deal with people about whom we feel genuine doubt? First, if the person is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاسِق</span> — and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاسِق</span> is someone who commits major sins openly, or who is habitually careless about minor ones, consistently and without remorse — then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says: if a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاسِق</span> comes to you with a report, verify it before you act on it. You cannot take what such a person says at face value.</p>
<p>Now, if what you have is mere <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكٌّ</span> — not certainty, just unresolved doubt — what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيدِي أَحْمَد زَرُّوق</span> advises is excellent counsel, and though it comes from the ninth century it is even more pertinent today: do not entrust your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>, your family, or your wealth to anyone until you have tested them a thousand thousand times. That is of course <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبَالَغَةٌ</span> — hyperbole — but his point is plain: test people. Observe whether they are genuine and sincere. If you don''t yet know, don''t trust.</p>
<p>One of the poets said: &quot;A good opinion in these days is foolishness — think the worst of people and stay on guard.&quot; And truly, this is what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> say: when the times are full of corruption, the sound position is to be cautious and distrustful of people — not from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَنٌّ</span>, not from having made a judgment, but simply from prudence. You don''t extend trust until it has been earned, because so many people lack <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. They will smile at your face, speak beautifully of their own faith and virtue, and then cheat you. It happens all the time.</p>
<p>That is also why the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> say that in corrupt times, it becomes obligatory for those distributing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاةٌ</span> to investigate the circumstances of cla<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>ts. They cannot simply hand it over. When people are generally righteous, you take them at their word. But when the times are corrupt and someone comes saying, &quot;Brother, I have five children and no money,&quot; you bear a responsibility to verify the claim. The cla<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>ts cannot be upset or indignant about this — they must accept it. It is not about distrusting them personally. It is, as they say in this culture, that the bad ones have ruined it for the good ones. The response has to be: &quot;Unfortunately, because this is an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَانَة</span> from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, we have to verify. It isn''t that we doubt you — this is a blanket policy, for that reason. And we are sorry that the reality has come to this.&quot;</p>
<p>So that is the matter of bad opinion of others. Having doubts about someone — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكٌّ</span> — is fine. Having a bad opinion of someone when there is ample evidence he is truly a bad person is simply common sense. On the other hand: when people make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, do not hold their past over them. One of the worst things you can do to a person is remind them of the evil they have left behind. Let people move forward. Do not cling to their past, because it is deeply unfair.</p>
<p>And there is another dimension here. A good opinion of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and a good opinion of the servants of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> — no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span> was ever given anything better than these two. Generally, it is praiseworthy to have a good opinion: certainly of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and generally of His servants. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَمَّارٍ</span> said that no servant was ever harmed by a good opinion, whereas a bad opinion can injure you. And in our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> the principle is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البَرَاءَةُ الأَصْلِيَّةُ</span> — people are presumed innocent until there is real cause to conclude otherwise.</p>
<p>The other point of supreme importance is the opinion one holds of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> reported by al-Tabarani: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا عِنْدَ ظَنِّ عَبْدِي بِي، فَلْيَظُنَّ عَبْدِي بِي مَا شَاءَ</span> — &quot;I am as My servant thinks of Me, so let My servant think of Me whatever he will.&quot; Another <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> clarifies it further: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَمَنْ ظَنَّ بِي خَيْرًا وَجَدَ خَيْرًا، وَمَنْ ظَنَّ بِي شَرًّا وَجَدَ شَرًّا</span> — &quot;I am as My servant thinks of Me. Whoever thinks well of Me finds good; whoever thinks ill of Me finds ill.&quot; This <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> is the key to understanding another, found in the Forty Hadith of Imam al-Nawawi, in which it is stated that every servant receives what he deserves, and then: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَمَنْ وَجَدَ خَيْرًا فَلْيَحْمَدِ اللهَ</span> — &quot;If he finds good, let him thank <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>&quot; — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَنْ وَجَدَ غَيْرَ ذَٰلِكَ فَلَا يَلُومَنَّ إِلَّا نَفْسَهُ</span> — &quot;and if he finds otherwise, let him blame no one but his own soul.&quot;</p>
<p>The underlying idea is that if you are a true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span>, everything ultimately works in your favor. The affair of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span> is all good. Even the bad that befalls you — the true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span> believes it carries a reason, and that there is good within it. This is a fundamentally different worldview from that of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَأَيِّس</span>, the pessimist, who falls into what might be called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَخْطَةُ الْقَدَرِ</span> — railing against destiny, saying, &quot;Why is this happening to me? I don''t deserve this.&quot; The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> himself said something to this effect: &quot;If You are not angry with me, I do not care&quot; — and he acknowledged that ease would naturally be more comfortable, but that the difficulty of a hard moment is itself a good thing for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span>. That is what it means to have a good opinion of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and a good opinion of others.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'negative thoughts';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next disease is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span>.</p>
<p>Imam al-Ghazali says the distinction between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> requires two people. Kibr is arrogance — it needs the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّر</span>, the one who is arrogant, and the one toward whom the arrogance is directed. If Zayd comes to Amr and says, &quot;You insignificant fool — why didn''t you do what I told you?&quot; Zayd is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّر</span> and Amr is the one being looked down upon. Ujb, by contrast, is like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>, except that it requires no second person. The person afflicted with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span> is simply impressed with himself. If he has a mirror, he is content — he looks and is genuinely dazzled by how wonderful and magnificent he is.</p>
<p>He defines <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span> as follows: you magnify and exalt the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span> — the blessing — while forgetting the one who gave it. In this culture, if someone is told, &quot;You''re so beautiful,&quot; and they respond, &quot;Thank you,&quot; as though they had done something to bring it about — that is a strange thing, if you think about it carefully. Perhaps they spent a great deal on reconstructive surgery, but even then they should be thanking the surgeon, not congratulating themselves. The point is: if Allah made someone beautiful, they should be grateful to Allah. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْعِمٌ</span> — the one who bestowed the gift — is Allah, not the person themselves. They did nothing to bring it about.</p>
<p>And even when real human effort is involved — when someone has a genuine talent that took years to develop — that talent, in its raw form, was still a gift from Allah. The labor it took to develop it was itself made possible by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> from Allah. You see this clearly within families.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 95">page 95</div>
<p>Consider beautiful calligraphy. It looks effortless to the observer. I once sat with Sidi Muhammad Zakariyya — the well-known American calligrapher — and watched him work. He made it look so natural that I thought, &quot;I could do that.&quot; They say the mark of a master is precisely this: he makes it look so easy that you believe you could do it yourself — but you couldn''t. Imam al-Jurjani said much the same of the master of rhetoric: when he speaks, he speaks with such ease and fluency that the listeners think, &quot;I could do that.&quot; They could not.</p>
<p>What you have to bear in mind when you see Sidi Muhammad Zakariyya execute his calligraphy is the years of patient, painstaking repetition that preceded it — practicing waws and ya''s and ba''s, writing out passages again and again. You see the finished work and say, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.&quot; And that is a gift from Allah. Now if someone given such a gift were to say, &quot;Look at all my accomplishments — I did this myself, I pulled myself up by my own effort&quot; — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span> of the worst kind. And there are countless people in the world who think exactly this: that whatever they have achieved, they achieved alone. &quot;I built myself. I earned this.&quot; That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْبُ النَّفْسِ</span>.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span>, by contrast, understands: if Allah gave you the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> to be disciplined, that too was from Allah.</p>
<p>One person grows up with discipline; another, in the same household with the same environmental factors, does not. The disciplined one goes on to master their studies, graduates with honors — and all of it is from Allah. As Imam al-Razi says, Allah is the maker of every maker and every made thing. Allah created the one who built the airplane and the airplane itself. Allah created the one who designed the computer and the computer itself. Once you truly grasp that, the urge to be impressed with yourself dissolves.</p>
<p>And then there is this: above everyone who knows something, there is always someone who knows more. This is the lesson Musa, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, had to learn. When he was asked whether anyone on earth was more knowledgeable than he was, he said he did not think so. So Allah sent him to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَضِر</span> — who was not a prophet — and made him a student. Even then, he did not prove to be as patient a student as he could have been. The Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, said that had Musa not asked those questions, he would have learned far more from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَضِر</span>. He did not absorb everything he might have, yet he was still taught. And the greatest lesson he took away was this: there is always someone who knows more than you, so never forget that.</p>
<p>We must choose our words carefully when speaking of prophets. I am not saying Musa was a poor student — he was, I am sure, extraordinary. But he had a lesson to learn, and Allah taught it to him through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَضِر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>.</p>
<p>Now, regarding vanity — the reason I translated <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span> as vanity is that, in the dictionary, vanity is defined as excessive pride in one''s appearance or accomplishments, which maps closely onto the Arabic. The root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَجِيبَة</span> means to wonder or marvel at something; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span> is to marvel at oneself or one''s own achievements. The word <em>vanity</em> itself derives from the Latin <em>vanus</em>, meaning empty — and so the very source of our vanity is empty and will one day vanish.</p>
<p>The Romans understood this. When a general like Caesar held a victory parade through Rome, riding through the city on a chariot while crowds cheered, a slave would walk alongside him holding a laurel wreath over his head and repeating: <em>all is vanity</em>. That slave was there to remind the triumphant general — it is all vanity. This is an ancient wisdom, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَة</span>, a reminder that all these accomplishments will eventually be forgotten. Charles Lindbergh is a case in point: he received an enormous ticker-tape parade in New York City, and within ten years the American government considered him a criminal for his Nazi sympathies. The hero had fallen.</p>
<p>There is a story — I believe it was written by James Thurber, who wrote <em>The Secret Life of Walter Mitty</em> — about a man who saves a child, and the reporters rush in to make him a hero. It turns out he is actually a disreputable person who simply wants to profit from the incident. As he stands at the window watching the adoring crowds below, one of the reporters pushes him out. The point is that there is always another side. No matter how much good a person does, there will always be detractors — or the glory itself will simply prove hollow.</p>
<p>Think of the Yankees parading through New York, celebrated as the greatest team in the world, while the city itself is in moral, social, and ethical disarray. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span> at a collective scale — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُعْجَبَةُ بِالنَّفْسِ</span> — and it is a deeply troubling condition.</p>
<p>He then notes: knowing that you are incapable, that you can neither produce any benefit nor ward off any harm on your own, should be enough to strip away all vanity. Vanity originates from nothing other than ignorance of two truths: that Allah alone is the fashioner and giver of blessings, and that you are incapable of anything by yourself. Forgetting those two things is how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span> takes hold.</p>
<p>This is a disease one must always be on guard against — especially when one does something well. And it is particularly relevant for those whom Allah has blessed with outward, physical beauty. Becoming vain about one''s appearance is a dangerous trap, one that both men and women can fall into, though it is an especially acute danger for women.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'vanity';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next disease he addresses is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِشٌّ</span> — fraud. He defines it as concealing some fault or harm, whether religious or worldly, even when the other party is a member of a protected minority or someone who has a treaty with the Muslims. Others have interpreted it as adorning something that has no real benefit, or making an evil appear to be good.</p>
<p>There is a hadith in which the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, said: <em>Whoever tricks us is not from us.</em> A Muslim is not someone who engages in fraud. And fraud, he notes, can occur in worldly affairs as well as religious ones. In Islamic law, it is prohibited to sell something while concealing a known defect. If you sell a car and genuinely do not know that the engine is in poor condition, that is one matter — but if you know it, you are obligated to disclose it. Failing to do so is a disease of the heart; that is precisely where it originates.</p>
<p>I have noted in the commentary that this disease is conspicuous in modern advertising, which to my mind suggests that only a diseased heart could produce that kind of work comfortably. It also has a historical precedent in the sophists of ancient Greece. The sophists worshipped rhetoric and were, in effect, the first historical relativists. Relativism holds that there is no right and wrong — might makes right, and the most persuasive argument wins, regardless of its truth. That is a thoroughly modern disease as well, but the sophists were its original practitioners. They mastered rhetoric and believed the only thing that truly mattered was being convincing. Truth or falsehood was beside the point; what mattered was getting what you wanted through the art of persuasion.</p>
<p>For the Muslim intellectual tradition, by contrast, rhetoric is the art of presenting truth in the most compelling way — not of dressing up falsehood. It is a fundamentally different worldview. The sophists, by their own admission, set out to make the worse cause appear to be the better one. They believed that men are either wolves or sheep: smart wolves eat the dumb sheep. This is, unfortunately, a widely held view in modern corporate culture — that competition justifies whatever means are available to you. The word <em>sophisticated</em> itself traces back to the Greek root that gave us <em>sophist</em> — people who thought themselves clever and wise.</p>
<p>Next week, he is going to address what I think is one of the most consequential diseases in the entire book — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعُجْبُ الْكَبِيرُ</span>, or rather <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرِّيَاءُ</span> — and he spends nearly as much time on it as he does on some of the other major diseases. We will take that up next session.</p>
<hr />
<p>Are there any questions?</p>
<p><em>On <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَضِرُ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span>:</em> Yes, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> met <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَضِرُ</span> before he was sent to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي إِسْرَائِيلَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَضِرُ</span> was a prophet — that is a separate matter.</p>
<p><em>On bad opinion versus hatred:</em> Having a bad opinion of someone means thinking ill of them without evidence. Hatred is something different — it is an actual emotion, a feeling. We will get into that in more detail when we reach that chapter. It becomes truly dangerous when you act on it. The Arabic terms are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُوءُ الظَّنِّ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُغْضٌ</span>.</p>
<p><em>On fraud toward non-Muslims:</em> What he is saying is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِشٌّ</span> — fraud — applies even in dealings with a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِمِّي</span>, a non-Muslim living under the protection of the Muslim state. You cannot cheat a non-Muslim simply because they are not Muslim. You are obliged to be upright in your transactions with everyone.</p>
<p><em>On reminding people of past wrongs:</em> Generally speaking, if something happened in the past and a person has moved on from it, continually reminding them of it is not fair, because it closes the door to change. If they are repeating the behavior, that is a different matter — but for past mistakes they have left behind, it is better to let it go.</p>
<p><em>On the word</em> <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُسْرَى</span> <em>and the grammatical point about</em> <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُسْر</span>: There is a tradition of saying that one difficulty will not overcome two eases, and the grammatical basis for this is in the Quranic ayah. Allah says: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ مَعَ ٱلْعُسْرِ يُسْرًۭا</span> ‎ — and then repeats it. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعُسْر</span> carries the definite article <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْـ</span>, which in the second instance is what grammarians call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> — it refers back to the same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُسْر</span> mentioned before. So it is the same singular difficulty appearing both times. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُسْرَى</span>, the ease, is indefinite — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَقِّرَه</span> — in both instances. Because it is a new indefinite noun each time, it counts as two separate eases. The lesson is that with any one difficulty come two eases.</p>
<p><em>On energy and vibrations between people:</em> I think there is something real to the experience of feeling uplifted or dragged down by the people around you. We would express it in our tradition as someone having a good or bad soul, or being a good or bad person. The Arabic <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنْسَجِم</span> literally means to be in the flow, in harmony — and you can see the analogy in music: a cacophonous sound creates a vibration that hurts the ear, while something melodious has a positive effect. So the sense that certain people carry a certain energy is not entirely foreign to Islamic thought, even if we frame it differently.</p>
<p>All of us have had the experience of being around someone who is deeply depressed and finding it difficult not to start feeling depressed ourselves. People do seem to have fields — auras, if you want to use that word — that are either uplifting, draining, or simply neutral.</p>
<p>I would also say this: feeling uncomfortable around someone is not the same as judging them to be a bad person. One of my teachers, Sheikh Hamid, told me a story from his student days. There was a fellow student who liked him very much and always wanted to be near him, but he simply did not feel a connection with that person. He went to his teacher feeling guilty about it, and his teacher said: souls are arranged in ranks. Some souls are simply not harmonious with one another. It does not mean either party is bad — it is just that some people you do not feel a natural affinity with, while with others you feel an immediate and powerful connection.</p>
<p>Some of the scholars say this has to do with our pre-worldly existence — the gathering of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْوَاح</span>, the spirits, before they entered this world. The closer your soul was to another soul on that day, the more affinity you will feel toward that person in this life. The further away, the more distant you will feel. This would explain why you can meet someone for the first time and feel as though you have known them all your life. I felt that immediately with Sheikh Abdallah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْـ</span>Qadi — the moment I met him, it was as though I had always known him. Other connections develop slowly, over time. There is wisdom in both kinds.</p>
<p><em>On distrust as distinct from bad opinion:</em> Having a bad opinion of a specific person without evidence is one thing. Being generally cautious and guarded as a disposition is something else. The scholars say that in difficult times, a degree of wariness is actually wisdom — it is being on your guard, not maligning anyone in particular.</p>
<p>Don''t become paranoid — that''s a psychological condition to be avoided. Seriously. You don''t want to be the person who says, &quot;Do you hear what he said? Did you see how he looked at me?&quot; That kind of thinking is an affliction.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'fraud';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next section covers <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span>, which means anger. He opens it by describing anger as the swelling ocean of all these diseases of the heart. Just coming to its shore — not even venturing into it — you will see things that astonish you: waves overflowing, an expanse beyond reckoning. Say of it what you will, without constraint, because it is a truly remarkable thing.</p>
<p>There is a hadith — one with some weakness in its chain, though its meaning is important — in which a man came to the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> from the front and asked, &quot;O Messenger of Allah, tell me: what is the best thing for drawing near to Allah?&quot; The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> replied, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُسْنُ الْخُلُقِ</span>&quot; — good character. The man then came from the right side and asked the same question, and received the same answer. He came from the left and asked again, and again the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُسْنُ الْخُلُقِ</span>.&quot; Finally, the man came from behind him and asked once more. This time the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوَلَمْ تَفْقَهْ؟ لَا تَغْضَبْ</span>&quot; — &quot;Have you not understood? Do not get angry.&quot;</p>
<p>In another hadith, a man asked the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> what the worst thing was that could occur with respect to Allah. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, &quot;His anger.&quot; The man asked how they could avoid it, and the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> replied: do not get angry yourself. If you do not want Allah to be angry with you, do not give in to anger. &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَيْثَمَا تَدِينُ تُدَانُ</span>&quot; — as you conduct yourself, so will it be shown to you.</p>
<p>Then there is the famous hadith in which a man said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, give me some advice.&quot; The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> replied, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَغْضَبْ</span>&quot; — do not get angry. The man said he wanted more advice. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> again said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَغْضَبْ</span>.&quot; The man asked a third time, and three times the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> repeated, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَغْضَبْ</span>.&quot; This hadith, the scholars say, is the controlling hadith — the key to good character.</p>
<p>Now, when the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَغْضَبْ</span>,&quot; the scholars of Allah — our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span>, including Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّوَوِيُّ</span> — explained that he did not mean: do not feel anger. He did not mean: suppress the emotion entirely. Because the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> himself said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّمَا أَنَا بَشَرٌ أَغْضَبُ مِثْلَكُمْ</span>&quot; — I am a human being, and I get angry just as you do. His anger was visible in his face; a vein in his forehead would appear when he was angry.</p>
<p>So the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was not instructing us to eliminate the feeling of anger, because anger is not inherently negative. It is, in fact, essential to the human character. Many of the world''s injustices would never be opposed if people lacked the capacity for anger. What the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> meant, according to the scholars, was: do not let your anger take control of you. Remain in command of your anger.</p>
<p>Some of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> have likened anger to a hunting dog: if you do not train it, it will not bring you what you need. It must be disciplined. Without anger, injustice would go unchallenged — people could act with impunity and no one would object. That is not what Allah wants. He wants us to become angry, but not for our own sake. He wants our anger to be for His sake. This is the same transformation that applies to the other diseases of the heart: you redirect them so they work for you rather than against you.</p>
<p>The reason the author calls anger the ocean is that it drowns many people. They sink into the sea of wrath, and it carries them toward destruction and despair.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 99">page 99</div>
<p>Now, before moving into the detailed discussion, I want to make a detour — though it is not really a detour, because it is directly related to all of this.</p>
<p>Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span> identified four essential qualities of the human soul. This framework also appears in earlier traditions and is not unique to Islam, but Muslim scholars recognized its truth and adopted it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَخْرُ الدِّينِ الرَّازِيُّ</span> adopted it. Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَاغِب الأَصْفَهَانِي</span> adopted it in his book on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span> adopted it as well.</p>
<p>The model examines the human soul and recognizes four fundamental powers. The first is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّةُ الْعَقْلِ</span> — what the Western tradition calls the rational soul. It is the power within the human being that is related to what a person knows and understands. The second is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّةُ الْغَضَبِ</span> — the irascible soul — the element that relates to emotions, whose foundation is anger. Imam Jalal al-Din al-Rumi once observed that the wisdom behind the prohibition of wine is that most people carry anger at their core, and when they drink, that root is exposed. People become belligerent; their inherent anger surfaces. He said it was as though Allah, in His mercy, veiled people from exposing what lies at their core. And indeed, for most people, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّةُ الْغَضَبِ</span> sits at the very root of the soul.</p>
<p>The third is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّةُ الشَّهْوَةِ</span> — the appetitive soul, the soul related to desire and appetite, to one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَانِيٌّ</span> nature. The fourth is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّةُ الْعَدْلِ</span>, which, according to Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span>, is the power that harmonizes the other three so that the human being functions in a healthy and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced way — none of them thrown out of equilibrium.</p>
<p>These four powers correspond to what both the Western and Islamic traditions call the cardinal virtues. The Muslims call them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّهَاتُ الْفَضَائِلِ</span>: wisdom, courage, temperance, and justice — or in Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ، شَجَاعَةٌ، عِفَّةٌ، وَعَدْلٌ</span>.</p>
<p>When the rational soul is in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce, the result is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span>. Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷻ</span> says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَن يُؤْتَ ٱلْحِكْمَةَ فَقَدْ أُوتِىَ خَيْرًۭا كَثِيرًۭا</span> — &quot;Whoever is given wisdom has been given much good.&quot; Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span> says that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span> is the mean between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَدَة</span> — stupidity — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِدَاعٌ</span> — cunning. If the rational soul is deficient, the result is foolishness: people do stupid things. If it is excessive, the result is manipulation — the intellect is turned toward trickery and exploitation of others. This excess, incidentally, tends to be an essential qualification for becoming a CEO: that surplus of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّةُ الْعِلْمِ</span> directed toward manipulation.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span> arises when the irascible soul — one''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span> — is under proper control. It, too, is a mean between two extremes: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهَوُّر</span> on one side, which is impetuousness, irrational behavior, total loss of self-control in which a person acts in rage and then regrets it; and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُبْن</span> on the other side, which is cowardice — insufficient <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span>, too much fear to act. One extreme is deficiency (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَقْص</span>), the other excess (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِيَادَة</span>). The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced state between them is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span>. This connects to the hadith: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَيْرُ الْأُمُورِ أَوْسَطُهَا</span>,&quot; the best of matters is the middle course, and to the Quranic description: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَكَذَلِكَ جَعَلْنَكُمْ أُمَّةًۭ وَسَطًۭا</span>&quot; — We made you a middle community, a people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span>, a people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span>, possessing these virtues in their proper <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِفَّة</span> is the middle way of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّةُ الشَّهْوَةِ</span>. It lies between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُمُودٌ</span> — a kind of rigidity or indifference, where a person has no appetite, no energy, no interest in the opposite sex, no engagement with their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span> — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِسْق</span>, where a person plunges entirely into indulging their appetites and becomes enslaved to the appetitive soul. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُمُودٌ</span> is itself unhealthy in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>; the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> disapproved of it. The excess, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِسْق</span>, is the opposite danger. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce between them is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِفَّة</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِفَّة</span> is explicitly affirmed in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>. Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷻ</span> says that ignorant people, observing poor Muslims who carry themselves with this quality, assume they are wealthy — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَحْسَبُهُمُ ٱلْجَاهِلُ أَغْنِيَآءَ مِنَ ٱلتَّعَفُّفِ</span>&quot; — because their dignity and self-restraint make them appear self-sufficient. They are not out scrambling after money through trickery and deception, because their inner state is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced.</p>
<p>As for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span> — justice — it does not occupy a mean between two things. It has an opposite: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظُلْم</span>. Justice is simply the opposite of oppression. When the other three powers are in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce, you are just. When they are out of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce, you become a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَالِم</span> — oppressing both yourself and others. If you completely indulge the appetitive soul, you have become <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَالِمٌ لِنَفْسِهِ</span>, oppressing your own soul by subjecting it to what it was not created to do. Allah says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُلُواْ وَٱشۡرَبُواْ وَلَا تُسۡرِفُوٓاْ</span> — &quot;Eat and drink, but do not be excessive.&quot; The middle. Eat what you need. It is now, in fact, a science: based on your body size, you can calculate almost to the gram how many calories you require each day. Eating significantly more or less than that is a form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظُلْم</span>.</p>
<p>And Allah, in His mercy, has placed a natural indicator within the human being: when you are in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِطْرَة</span> and health, you will feel satiated at the right point. To go beyond that point is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظُلْم</span> against your own soul, because your stomach has a right — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقٌّ</span>. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَيْرُ وِعَاءٍ مَلَأَهُ ابْنُ آدَمَ، بَعْدَهُ</span>&quot; — the worst vessel the son of Adam fills is his stomach. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقٌّ</span> of the stomach is that a person leave one-third for food, one-third for water, and one-third for air. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدَالَة</span> of eating. To exceed that is to go beyond its proper limit.</p>
<p>The same principle applies to the sexual appetite. If it is expressed naturally and within its proper limits, the human being remains healthy. If he transgresses those limits and goes beyond them — and this is part of what might be called the Viagra culture of our time — the result is excess. As a person grows older, that dimension of the self naturally diminishes, and the soul should be turning toward the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>. But for someone who does not hold the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> as a concern, he continues pursuing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَوَات</span> that he should be moving away from. What was once a physical <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span> has now become a psychological <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span> — the body no longer produces it, but the mind insists on it. That is the im<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce.</p>
<p>These, then, are the elements of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced human being.</p>
<p>By the grace of Allah, Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span> says, the only human being who ever perfected the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce of all four was the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. He alone achieved complete equilibrium in all three of the foundational powers, which resulted in his being the most just of all men. His character is essentially <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدَالَة</span>. His very nature is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span>. This is why the more you emulate him in his behavior, the closer you come to being a truly just human being.</p>
<p>For this reason, the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> did not fast every day — that would have been a deficiency in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِفَّة</span>, a withholding from himself of what was due. Nor did he go to the other extreme. He said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَعْضَ أَيَّامٍ أَصُومُ وَبَعْضَهَا أَفْطُرُ</span>&quot; — some days I fast and some days I eat. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> did not become angry when it was inappropriate. He became angry when it was appropriate, and that was his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span>. He became angry when a situation demanded a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْقِف</span>, a stand. Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span> says that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span> is to be angry at the right time, in the right place, toward the right person, and with the right measure. How does one learn to do that? By studying the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> — because he never let his anger get the better of him to the point of praying against people. He never allowed it to lead him to strike anyone unjustly. He controlled it. And that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span>.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> also possessed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْم</span> — knowledge — beyond any human being, and it was perfectly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced knowledge. There is nothing foolish in his conduct, nothing imprudent, nothing that suggests any deficiency of wisdom. Nor did he deploy his intellectual gifts for cleverness, trickery, or accumulation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. He used them for the sake of Allah, where and as it was appropriate.</p>
<p>Now, one of the things Imam al-Suyuti says in one of his books — and he notes, &quot;I have not seen anyone make this observation before me&quot; — is that, reflecting on what had been said about Abu Bakr being the most courageous of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> after the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, he began to think about that quality. This reflection led him to give a talk he called &quot;Abu Bakr and Courage,&quot; exploring this virtue. But then something else struck him: each of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخُلَفَاءُ الرَّاشِدُونَ</span> embodies one of these four virtues as his dominant characteristic.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span> is most clearly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> Ali, because he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَابُ الْعِلْمِ</span> — the gate of knowledge. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span> is Abu Bakr. And everyone knows <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمَر</span> as the embodiment of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span>. The only one that might give one pause is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> Uthman — but when you look up the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِفَّة</span> in al-Fayruzabadi''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَامُوس</span>, immediately after its definition he writes: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْعَفَافُ هُوَ الْعَفِيفُ مُغَالَاةً</span>&quot; — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَفَنُ</span> is the one who is intensely <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَفِيف</span>. And his name is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُثْمَانُ بْنُ عَفَّانَ</span>. That was what he was known for. Consider what he was doing on the day he was killed: fasting. His entire state was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِفَّة</span> — mastery over the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>. Even the angels were shy in his presence, because of his profound <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِفَّة</span>.</p>
<p>So each one of them embodies one of these four virtues as his most prominent quality — while possessing all four. Ali had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span>; Ali had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span>; Ali had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span>; Ali had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِفَّة</span>. But his dominant characteristic was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span> — that was his gift. Abu Bakr had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْم</span>; he had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُجَّاعَة</span>; he had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِفَّة</span>; he had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span>. But what stands out in his mawaqif is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span>, because courage is defined precisely as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثَبَتَ</span> — remaining steadfast when hearts around you waver. Look across the events of the Seerah, and it was Abu Bakr who remained <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثَبَتَ</span> when everyone else wavered. That is why the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: &quot;I presented Islam to everyone, and the only one who did not waver was Abu Bakr.&quot; His heart was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span>. It was his nature.</p>
<p>Now there is another remarkable observation about all of this. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطُ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span> — the straight path — literally means the upright path. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِقَامَةٌ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْتِدَال</span>. In the prayer, your prayer is only valid when you are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُعْتَدِل</span> — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُعْتَدِل</span> in Arabic means both upright and perfectly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطُ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span> is the perfectly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced path. And there are two ways to deviate from it.</p>
<p>You either deviate through deficiency or excess — those are the only two ways to stray from the path. You are either falling short of what is obligatory, or you are going too far.</p>
<p>These two deviations correspond to the Jews and the Christians. The Christians went to excess. They went beyond what they were commanded.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 122">page 122</div>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَهْبَانِيَّةٌ</span> — monastic renunciation — they invented it; We did not prescribe it for them. They sought it as a means of drawing closer to Allah''s pleasure, yet they could not even fulfill what they themselves had initiated. That is the very nature of excess: it cannot be sustained. By its own definition, it is unsustainable. That is why excess is so dangerous. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> warned: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكُمْ وَالْغُلُوَّ</span> — beware of extremism — for Allah does not grow weary until you grow weary. When you push beyond the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce, you exhaust yourself. It is not sustainable.</p>
<p>So the Christian, archetypally speaking, pursues an extreme of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِفَّة</span> — continence — that tips over into something other than its opposite, but something distorted: an excess that has lost its equilibrium. And what always follows an extreme? The pendulum swings to the other side. Drive something far enough in one direction and it snaps back to its polar opposite. The Christian ascetic tradition pushed all the way to one extreme of renunciation, and then flipped entirely — landing in modern American society, where the culture is all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span>, all appetite and indulgence. They arrived on these shores as Puritans. That was the original impulse of the Pilgrims: to escape a European society they saw as given over to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span>, to build a pure religious community. And they ended up producing the opposite — a civilization defined by extreme appetite. This is precisely what happens with these polarities.</p>
<p>You can see the same dynamic with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُجَاعٌ</span> — courage. The Christian ideal says: turn the other cheek. Yet, historically, Christians have killed more human beings than any other religious or secular civilization. Now they possess weapons of mass destruction. The technologies of industrial warfare — rockets, tanks, missiles — emerged largely from this same civilization. This is the fruit of going to extremes: the ideal inverts into its opposite.</p>
<p>The same pattern holds with justice. Push justice to an extreme and it collapses into manipulation. The law becomes a vehicle for trickery — lawyers finding every loophole so that those with resources get whatever they want. Justice becomes its opposite: oppression. Knowledge, which was originally claimed as the servant of human flourishing — read the original Enlightenment thinkers; Bacon wrote explicitly about how Christian morality would keep technology in check and make it a force for good — that knowledge becomes a tool for getting what one wants at others'' expense. This is the Roman disease: justice for us, oppression for everyone else.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 123">page 123</div>
<p>That is one form of im<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce. The other is the way of the Jews — the way of deficiency. This is the very complaint Moses, peace be upon him, raised with the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> on the night of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْرَاء</span>: his people could not even manage two prayers a day. That was all they were asked, and they could not do it. They said to Moses: <em>You and your Lord go and fight — we will sit here.</em> So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَقْص</span> — deficiency — runs through that entire mode.</p>
<p>Now, these are archetypes. I want to be clear: do not take these as absolute, rigid categories. They are lenses for understanding patterns in the human soul. Within the Jewish fold you will find the Christian phenomenon, and within the Christian fold you will find the Jewish phenomenon. These archetypes can appear in a Hindu, in a Buddhist, in anyone. They are ways of reading the human condition.</p>
<p>This is why the one described in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span> — those who have incurred wrath — is the one who possesses knowledge but goes astray, becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَالّ</span>. And it is worth noting that the Arabic root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَالٌ</span> also carries the meaning of falling in love — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span> means he fell in love — because it is precisely this excessive, consuming love that leads people astray, the love of Qays, of Majnun.</p>
<p>The Muslim is meant to stand in the middle between these two poles. We can think of those poles as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَيْبَة</span> — awe, majesty, the quality of justice — which is the root of the Mosaic tradition. Moses, peace be upon him, is the lawgiver. His tradition is built on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span>, on justice. And on the other side is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span> — mercy — which is the root impulse of the Christian tradition. The Christian understanding is that everyone is forgiven through God''s mercy regardless of what they do, because all they perceive is mercy; they have lost sight of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَيْبَة</span>. Archetypally, you will find Christians who become fixated on wrath — but the dominant Christian emphasis, if you look at what they teach, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَيْبَةٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span> must be held in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce. You need both to walk the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاطُ الْمُسْتَقِيمِ</span>. You need <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَةٌ</span> — the law — and you need <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقِيقَةٌ</span> — the spirit of the law. The law is rules; the spirit of the law is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span>. Allah sent the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَةٌ</span> as a mercy. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا أَرْسَلْنَاكَ إِلَّا رَحْمَةً لِلْعَالَمِينَ</span> — We sent you as nothing other than a mercy to all the worlds. What the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> brought was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span>. When inward and outward are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced — inwardly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, outwardly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْلَام</span> — they produce the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحْسِن</span>, the person of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>, someone whose inner and outer states have become one integrated whole.</p>
<p>This framework is a genuine model for understanding what the straight path actually means. Muslims, according to Ibn Taymiyyah, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللَّهُ</span> — and this ultimately goes back to a hadith — deviate in one of two ways: they deviate like Jews or like Christians, toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَيْبَة</span> or toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span>. They either go in the direction of the hair-splitting, rabbinical, tyrannical scholar — the type the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> warned against: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكُمْ وَجَبَرَاتِ الْعُلَمَاءِ</span>, beware of tyrannical scholars — or they go in the direction of the ignorant worshipper, embodied in the Christian model, or in the extreme of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span>: a state where everything is one, everyone is going to heaven, nothing needs to be worried about, pure peace and harmony. When you hear certain people who claim to represent <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span> and they sound more like a New Age seminar than anything connected to Islam, that is this other extreme.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce is to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُوفِيٌّ فَقِيهِيٌّ</span> — inwardly animated by the spirit of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span>, outwardly disciplined by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْه</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَةٌ</span>. This is why Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشَّافِعِيُّ</span> said: do not be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَقِيهٌ</span> without being a Sufi, and do not be a Sufi without being a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَقِيهٌ</span>, because one without the other either hardens the heart or leads one astray. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce is the joining of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span> — the spirit of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span> — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span> — the spirit of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَةٌ</span>.</p>
<p>Now, our tradition is actually closer to the Christian tradition in one respect: the Christians emphasize <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span>, and when Muslims deal with one another, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span> should predominate over <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَيْبَة</span>. You should be more inclined to forgive your brother than to condemn him. Forgiveness is the higher station. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَن تَعْفُوٓا۟ أَقْرَبُ لِلتَّقْوَىٰ</span> — that you pardon is closer to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. That is a loftier station in Allah''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span> than demanding justice. This is why it has been said that where love truly exists, justice becomes unnecessary — because when the love between people is so profound, no one wants retribution; no one wants to call the other to account.</p>
<p>The first <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِتْنَةٌ</span> among the Muslims arose precisely from this departure: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span> overrode <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا مُعَاوِيَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>, demanded justice. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> Ali, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>, possessed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span> — he counseled forgiveness, recognizing that insisting on strict justice at that moment would tear the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> apart. The Shia position aside, we, as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَهْلُ السُّنَّةِ</span>, hold a good opinion of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُعَاوِيَة</span>. We believe, as with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَائِشَة</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُبَيْر</span>, that his intention was genuinely to seek justice. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> Ali was reaching for the higher expression of Islam — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةٌ</span> — and that is why we consider him to have been given <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِجْتِهَاد</span>, while <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا مُعَاوِيَة</span> was not given <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> in his. That is why his group is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفِئَةُ الْبَاغِيَةُ</span> — the transgressing party. The danger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span> taken to its extreme is precisely that it becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَغْي</span>, oppression. They were unjust toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> Ali, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>. We must be careful when speaking of all of this — we are speaking of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَّضِىَ ٱللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ</span>, and we love them all, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا مُعَاوِيَة</span> included. He was a scribe of revelation; he served Islam greatly. But he made a mistake. He was a human being. He was not infallible.</p>
<p>Now — any questions about that?</p>
<p>The question is about the distinction between wisdom and cleverness, between someone who appears wise and someone who genuinely is. For instance, there are people who are clever but whose cleverness does not lead them to recognize the truth. In that case, by the standard of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَةٌ</span>, they are considered <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاهِل</span> — ignorant. They only appear wise.</p>
<p>Take the example we mentioned earlier: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُتْبَةُ بْنُ رَبِيعَةَ</span>, who was renowned for his sound judgment, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو الْحَكَمِ</span> — &quot;the man of considered opinion&quot; — as he was called before Islam. These were men recognized for their discernment. Even <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو حُذَيْفَة</span> — when his father was killed at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَدْر</span> and thrown into the pit, the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> noticed him weeping and asked whether he was troubled by what had been done with his father. He said: <em>No. I am thinking of how intelligent my father was, how sound his counsel was when I was young, how I always hoped he would embrace Islam — and now I am looking at where he has ended up.</em> That is the example.</p>
<p>And I think those of us who came to Islam from outside — whose parents are not Muslim — can recognize this. My own parents have given me good counsel, and I can see in them a kind of wisdom. I can see that quality. For someone born into a Muslim family, with Muslim parents, that recognition may not come as naturally. But there are non-Muslims who genuinely possess forms of wisdom and insight. The difference is that the fully realized wisdom — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced and complete — sees everything true as true and everything false as false. The only person who achieved that absolutely was the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. But the closer one draws to him, the closer one comes to the kind of wisdom that allows one to see things as they truly are.</p>
<p>Does that make sense? Any other questions?</p>
<p>Right — and others have used these qualities too. The Hellenistic tradition used them. And even modern frameworks reflect them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحِكْمَةُ ضَالَّةُ الْمُؤْمِنِ</span> — wisdom is the lost property of the believer; wherever he finds it, he takes it. And you can see these qualities throughout the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span>. They are called cardinal virtues because all other virtues flow from them. Every virtue can be traced back to one of these four.</p>
<p>Let me give an example. Take generosity. What is generosity, really? What is it that a generous person can do that others cannot? Why can''t other people simply give? Because they are afraid. They fear losing what they have. So the truly generous person is, at root, a courageous person. It is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span> — courage — that enables giving, where others are held back by fear. That is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو بَكْرٍ</span> was the most generous of all the Companions: when the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> asked what people had brought, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو بَكْرٍ</span> brought everything he had. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمَر</span> brought half. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو بَكْرٍ</span>s generosity flowed from his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span>, his magnanimity.</p>
<p>Take another virtue. These four are called the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّهَات</span> — the matrices — of virtue. All virtues emerge from these four. Take mercy. What is mercy? Where does it really reside among the four? Mercy ultimately belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span>, wisdom. Because genuine compassion — like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو بَكْرٍ</span>s counsel to the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> to show mercy to the people — is grounded in the wisdom that Allah gives through mercy what He does not give through wrath. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span> in showing mercy, a knowledge that mercy is itself the more effective and more elevated path. Work through any virtue and you will find it rooted in one of these four.</p>
<p>Imam al-Ghazali goes into extraordinary depth in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْيَاء</span> demonstrating how all the virtues derive from these four matrices. The greatest minds in human history have exhausted themselves on this question — and he truly exhausted it.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 127">page 127</div>
<p>So how does all of this relate to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَب</span> — anger? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَب</span>, because it is one of these core <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّاتٌ</span> — faculties — is rooted in something essential to human nature. If you look at the brain as it has been studied, what they call the triune brain — and this is not purely a modern observation; Imam al-Ghazali touches on something similar — there are three dominant centers. The lowest, sometimes called the R-complex or reptilian brain in evolutionary frameworks, is the appetitive center: the seat of drives and urges — hunger, sexuality, the basic impulses. This would correspond to the appetitive faculty. The midbrain is where emotions are centered, and in this model the root emotion is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَب</span> — anger. This is why the first emotions children display are forms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَب</span>: they get angry, they cry, they throw tantrums as they grow older. They learn <em>no</em> before they learn <em>yes</em>. You can see <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَب</span> rooted right there in the earliest stages of human development.</p>
<p>Then there is the higher brain — the cortex — which is where reason resides.</p>
<p>There is a beautiful hadith in Imam al-Tirmidhi in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: love the one you love up to a measure, for one day he may become someone you dislike. The wisdom here is this: if you have shared every secret with someone and that person later turns against you — a very human experience, and one that devastates people — you are left completely exposed.</p>
<p>And also don''t hate people to extremes, because one day those you hate might become people you love. So be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced in both love and hate. This, again, points to the importance of emotional equilibrium.</p>
<p>When emotions fall out of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nce, problems follow. One of the Arabs put it memorably: don''t be so sweet that people eat you alive, and don''t be so bitter that no one can stand you. Be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span>nced — that is the idea.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 135">page 135</div>
<p>Finally, what researchers call the new brain is the neocortex — the seat of rational thought. When all three systems function properly together, the human being is truly Adam, truly human. And this is part of the human condition: some people are excessively cerebral, living entirely in their heads, where everything is theoretical and nothing practical. Others live at the opposite extreme, like reptiles — purely reactive, with no trace of higher humanity. I once explained this in a prison, and some of the Muslim inmates responded to it powerfully. One man came up to me afterward and said, &quot;You know that thing about the reptilian brain? There are a lot of reptiles here. These guys are like snakes, just waiting to strike.&quot; It is a striking image — people locked in a purely reactionary mode, with no humanity present. And then, of course, there are those who are overwhelmed by emotion, most commonly by anger.</p>
<p>I brought all of that in to put what follows in perspective. Anger is one of the great excesses of the human condition, which is why the author calls it the ocean — it really is the big one. And there are essentially three reasons people get angry.</p>
<p>The first relates to primal needs: food, shelter, survival, and the like. If a person does not get angry when these are threatened, he is considered <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحْمَق</span> — foolish — because this is a matter of survival. If someone threatens your life, you must respond. If someone threatens your family, you must respond. That is a courage Allah loves. Allah loves <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلْ قَالُوا هُوَ شَجَاعٌ</span> — and the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ مَاتَ دُونَ مَالِهِ مَاتَ شَهِيدًا</span>: whoever dies defending his wealth dies a martyr. That courage, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْبَة</span>, reflects the principle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَمْرُ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالنَّهْيُ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ</span> at its most fundamental level, and if you die doing it, you died in jihad — because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَمْرُ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالنَّهْيُ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ</span> falls within the chapter of jihad. It is a type of jihad.</p>
<p>The second type is also common to human beings, but it relates to position — to what the scholars call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُؤْيَةُ النَّفْسِ</span>, seeing oneself. This is the anger of dignity: <em>I am a human being; you cannot treat me this way.</em> It arises from a sense of being belittled, being treated with disdain, being made to feel lesser than one deserves. Sometimes that sense is justified; sometimes it is not. Some people harbor a complete delusion about their own standing — a grandiosity of soul — and their anger flows from that distortion.</p>
<p>The third type is specific to certain individuals and their particular attachments. A scholar, for instance, will become angry if he sees a book mistreated. An illiterate farmer will not — he doesn''t grasp what the fuss is about. But that same farmer will become furious if you throw his pitchfork carelessly, because it is his tool. So this third category of anger is tied to what a person values, what is particular to them.</p>
<p>Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span> says the first type is healthy, unless taken to an extreme where a person begins to oppress others — taking their food, seizing their shelter. That becomes wrongdoing. The second type can be healthy up to a point, but the higher path is to cease seeing oneself so centrally and not to let these things provoke anger at all. The danger lies in the opposite extreme, which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَكَاة</span> — a disease he will address separately — where excessive self-effacement tips into humiliation. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُؤْمِنُ لَا يُذِلُّ نَفْسَهُ</span>: the believer does not humiliate himself. And Allah says of the believers that they possess <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِزَّة</span> before the disbelievers — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِلَّهِ ٱلْعِزَّةُ وَلِرَسُولِهِۦ وَلِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ</span> — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُحَمَآءُ بَيْنَهُمْ</span>. You do not present yourself before a non-Muslim in a posture of meek, Uriah Heep–style obsequiousness. You carry yourself with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِزَّة</span>. It is with fellow believers that you practice softness and yielding.</p>
<p>And according to Sidi Ahmad Zarruq, if a non-Muslim says something to you that does not touch your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> — not mocking your faith or your practice — you simply let it pass. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذَا خَاطَبَهُمُ ٱلْجَهِلُونَ قَالُوا۟ سَلَمًۭا</span>: when the ignorant address them, they say &quot;peace.&quot; Don''t get angry over things that bring no benefit. All they are doing is pushing buttons. This is why Allah says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاصْبِرْ عَلَىٰ مَا يَقُولُونَ وَاهْجُرْهُمْ هَجْرًا جَمِيلًا</span> — be patient with what they say and leave them gracefully. Turn away. Don''t let it consume you. Let it go. There is no benefit in it — none at all.</p>
<p>The fourth type involves <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْرَهُ</span>. When the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> spoke about this, Sa''d ibn Abi Waqqas <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span> declared his own jealous protectiveness, and the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> responded that he himself has more <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْرَهُ</span> than Sa''d, and that Allah has more <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْرَهُ</span> than he does. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْرَهُ</span> — often translated as jealousy, though it is better understood as a protective ardor for what is one''s own — is a good sign. It is the impulse that moves a Muslim when he sees his religion being threatened: he becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيُور</span> for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>; he wants to defend it. That is a positive anger. And Allah has made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْرَهُ</span> part of human nature in order to protect lineage — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّسَبُ</span>. Without it, men would lose their protective care for their families, and society would unravel through adultery and confusion of bloodlines. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْرَة</span> in a man toward his wife is healthy and natural, as long as it does not tip into oppression or groundless suspicion. And the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْرَهُ</span> a woman feels is equally natural — Allah has made it so, and indeed it is permitted. Very few women are entirely free from it, so it is not conside' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'anger';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'red a fault.</p>
<p>Now the author says this disease has two cures. The first prevents anger from arising in the first place. The second suppresses it once it has come. Allah praises in the Quran <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلْكَظِمِينَ ٱلْغَيْظَ وَٱلْعَافِينَ عَنِ ٱلنَّاسِ</span> — those who restrain their anger and pardon people. The famous story of Jafar al-Tayyar illustrates this: when a servant accidentally spilled water on him and he became angry, the servant immediately recited that verse, and Jafar said, &quot;I have suppressed it.&quot; The servant continued with the next part — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلْعَافِينَ عَنِ ٱلنَّاسِ</span> — and Jafar said, &quot;I forgive you.&quot; Then the servant recited, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱللَّهُ يُحِبُّ ٱلْمُحْسِنِينَ</span>, and Jafar said, &quot;You are free, for the sake of Allah.&quot; That is what a believer should be: someone who, on hearing the Quran, responds to it immediately.</p>
<p>Similarly, if someone is engaged in backbiting and another reminds him with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span>, he should stop at once and not resent the reminder. I was once told a story about Sheikh Ibn al-Habib, a Moroccan sheikh, and one of his most remarkable qualities: he never spoke ill of anyone. This was reported by people who had lived with him for over seventy years, seeing him every single day — and not once in all that time did they hear him say anything bad about a person. Ever.</p>
<p>Not long ago, someone wrote me a letter — a man I believe to have genuine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> — saying he had mentioned me in a letter to someone else and feared there may have been some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِيبَة</span> in it. So he sent me the original letter and asked me to read it and tell him if I found any <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِيبَة</span>, so that he could seek forgiveness. He was questioning something he''d written. As it happened, it was simply a disagreement between his understanding and mine, which caused me no difficulty at all. But that level of scrupulousness — even when it is not strictly required — reflects something called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِهْلَال</span>. Strictly speaking in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, you don''t have to go back to the person you spoke about and confess it; in fact it is often better not to, because many people will simply become angry at you. Others, though, won''t mind — it depends entirely on the person.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> gave us another way. He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَيَعْجِزُ أَحَدُكُمْ أَنْ يَكُونَ مِثْلَ أَبِي ضَمْضَمٍ</span> — is any of you unable to be like Abu Damdam? Every morning Abu Damdam would give away his honor as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span> for people: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللَّه</span>, if anyone has spoken ill of me, I give back to them whatever good deeds of theirs may have come to me as a result.&quot; So the Prophet said: just do that. Who cares, in the end? If you truly contemplate the grave, the questioning in the grave, the Day of Judgment — if you really sit with those realities — none of this will matter. Once you die, you could not care less that someone once remarked you get angry too easily, or said something that stung you one afternoon. It will all be completely irrelevant.</p>
<p>So the author presents two remedies. Then he says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاذْكُرْ لِتَزْدَادَنَّ بِهَا عَلَى رَاهِرِ كَثْرَةَ مَدْحِ الْحِلْمِ وَالتَّوَاضُعِ وَالصَّبْرِ</span> — remember, in order to adorn yourself with the quality that removes anger, reflect on how greatly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِلْم</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَاضُعٌ</span> are praised. Because a truly humble person does not get angry for himself. He may become angry for Allah''s sake, but not for his own sake — because the only reason people get angry is that they are seeing themselves. If the self is not there to be seen, what is there to get angry over?</p>
<p>Consider the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>: when the Quraysh called him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُذَمَّمٌ</span> — the opposite of his name, the condemned one — he simply said, &quot;It is remarkable how Allah turned the tongues of Quraysh, who intended to condemn me, against some man named <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُذَمَّمٌ</span>. But I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَمَّدٌ</span>.&quot; He did not get angry. He just observed: they are not talking about me; they are talking about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُذَمَّمٌ</span>. That is the art of letting go. When there is no self to defend, there is no anger. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَاضُعٌ</span>.</p>
<p>What the author is saying is this: to acquire this quality, meditate on how universally humility and forbearance are praised. Every culture has honored these virtues in its traditions. This one largely no longer does — though it once did. There are still people who value them, but a certain cultural shift introduced what might be called the ethos of chutzpah — an overweening pride, a &quot;if you''ve got it, flaunt it&quot; sensibility — which is in fact alien to traditional American culture. Historically, Americans did appreciate humility. And humility is praised by all the sages, across every religious tradition.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: whoever humbles himself for Allah''s sake, Allah raises him up; and whoever raises himself up, Allah brings him low. And the Prophet''s camel, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القَصْوَاء</span>, used to outrun every camel among the Arabs, and the Companions took great pride in this. But one day she lost a race to a Bedouin, and they were all dismayed. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> laughed and said, &quot;Don''t you know that nothing is ever elevated but that Allah must bring it back down?&quot; That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> of Allah in His creation. If you understand the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>, you can laugh at things like that rather than be troubled by them.</p>
<p>So the author says: if you truly reflect on how praiseworthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِلْم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَاضُعٌ</span>, and forbearance are — and how the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was the most forbearing of all people, forgiving those who wronged him, never given to anger, patient through everything — and if you consider that this praise is found in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِعَار</span>, in the sayings of all the sages, and that every prophet without exception was characterized by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِلْم</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَاضُعٌ</span> — none of them were known for arrogance or quick temper — then you are on your way.</p>
<p>And then he said the second remedy — suppressing anger when it comes — lies in recognizing that nothing happens except by Allah''s will. When something arises that provokes you, take a breath and say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. This is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاءٌ</span> from Allah, a trial, to see <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَيُّكُمْ أَحْسَنُ عَمَلًۭا</span> — which of you is finest in deed. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْسَ الشَّدِيدُ الَّذِي يَصْرَعُ، وَإِنَّمَا الشَّدِيدُ مَنْ يَغْلِبُ نَفْسَهُ عِنْدَ الْغَضَبِ</span> — the strong man is not the one who can wrestle others to the ground; the truly strong man is the one who overcomes himself when anger comes. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَدِيد</span>. That is real strength.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 142">page 142</div>
<p>Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span> also observes that some people, in their foolishness, equate explosive anger with virility and courage. They see macho posturing as a virtue of character. You will even find a man who brags about his criminal exploits — how he robbed someone, fought them, dominated them — as though this were bravery. It is not. There is nothing virtuous in it whatsoever.</p>
<p>As for the practical means of suppressing anger, the hadith gives a sequence: if you are standing, sit down; if you are sitting, recline; if neither helps, go and make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُضُوءٌ</span>; and if that is still not enough, begin to pray. Performing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُضُوءٌ</span> actually dissipates the heat of anger — for anger is fire, and anger comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. It is the quality of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. One day the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> observed a man whose face had gone red and whose veins were bulging with rage, and he said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span> — do you not see how, when one of you becomes angry, his face begins to resemble <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>?&quot; And he said that had the man uttered the remembrance of Allah, it would have been lifted from him entirely.</p>
<p>And I was once in a conflict-resolution situation, and it was really interesting — things started getting heated and both parties stood up at the same time. They had been sitting on couches, and I had to make them sit back down. But that is the nature of anger: you start rising, and the way to overcome it is to sit down.</p>
<p>If you are in a room and you begin to feel anger, leave the room. If someone is making you angry, just leave. Go to another place, relax, sit down, and let your anger subside — then you can return. This is a practice that, if you actually do it, works. If you do not, you will either end up in a physical confrontation — and if the other person is bigger than you, he beats you, and if he is smaller, everyone sees you as a bully. So the best thing is simply to avoid it. There is no benefit in it, none whatsoever. It is empty and meaningless, and it is simply best to walk away.</p>
<p>That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>: cold water, silence, sitting down, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُضُوء</span>. All of these came in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>. Imam al-Razi mentioned that one man with a fierce temper actually hired someone to insult him and paid him to do so, just so he could practice listening without reacting until he could master his self-control. That may sound extreme, but the point is that people took these matters very seriously, because anger is a terrible quality and no one wants to be possessed by it.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 106">page 106</div>
<p>There is a hadith in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that anger will even afflict the best of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>. No one illustrates this better than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا عُمَر</span>. He was a man of fierce anger — yet if you trace his growth as a human being and as a Muslim, by the end of his life <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمَر</span> was not someone whose anger ever got the better of him. In fact, it was quite the opposite: he became known for his forgiveness. You can see this clearly in his later years. Even when he was mortally stabbed, the first thing he asked was whether his assailant was a Muslim. When he was told no, he praised Allah that no Muslim had done this to him — he simply did not want that on a Muslim''s account. He said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, and that was his nature. For all his ferocity — he was truly a lion of Allah — his anger never consumed him.</p>
<p>On one occasion the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was sitting with some women of the Quraysh in Madinah — this was before the verse of hijab was revealed — and when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمَر</span> entered, they all immediately fled into another room and hid behind a curtain. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was laughing when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمَر</span> came in. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمَر</span> asked, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا أَضْحَكَكَ يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span> — &quot;What makes you laugh, O Messenger of Allah?&quot; The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said he was laughing at these Qurayshi women, who had fled the moment they saw him. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمَر</span> went over to the curtain and called them back. He asked them: &quot;How is it that you flee from me, yet you are not shy before the Messenger of Allah, who is far more worthy of your awe?&quot; They replied, &quot;Because you are harsher and sterner than he is — he does not get angry.&quot; At this the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> laughed and said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عُمَرُ، لَوْ سَرَكْتَ فِي جَادٍّ لَسَرَخَ الشَّيْطَانُ فِي جَادٍّ آخَرَ</span> — &quot;O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمَر</span>, if you went down one path, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> would take another path entirely.&quot;</p>
<p>That example shows what it means to master the quality of anger. He truly mastered it. Yet at the same time, his anger was rooted in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْرَهُ</span> and in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span> — in righteous indignation and the demand for justice. He became angry at wrongdoing because he wanted to right it. Even his coming to the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> and accepting Islam was driven by that impulse — he felt something was wrong and he wanted to correct it. Everything in him was rooted in his convictions. He had an extraordinarily powerful sense of justice, and the Khattab family were traditionally known for that quality, even in pre-Islamic Arab culture.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 107">page 107</div>
<p>Now let me address the question that came in. As for children under seven, one must be very careful about expressing anger toward them. For children over seven, however, discipline is necessary — they have to be disciplined. And here it is worth noting something relevant to the three souls and the question about a teacher needing to establish discipline. The classical scholars described three stages corresponding to the first three sets of seven years. The first seven years are governed by the appetitive soul — which is why young children are constantly eating, constantly wanting things; it is all desire and the fulfillment of appetite. The second seven years are governed by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span>, and the third by the rational faculty, by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْم</span>. This is precisely why the hadith says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَاعِبُوهُمْ سَبْعًا</span> — play with them for seven years — because play is the fulfillment of the appetitive soul. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> noted that there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَعِبٌ</span> — no play — in Islam except in certain contexts, one of which is with one''s spouse. That is a wholesome and praiseworthy kind of play: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاعَبَة</span>, the playful affection between husband and wife, and the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> himself engaged in this with his wives. So the hadith instructs us: play with your children for seven years — during this period of the appetitive soul, allow them to explore and indulge their nature.</p>
<p>It is part of what children are meant to do. They are discovering the world, and that discovery is entirely sensory — they have come from a spiritual realm into the realm of sensation, and so they must inhabit their bodies and experience them. This is why things that are quite traumatic often happen to children in those first seven years: they get burnt, they fall from high places. All of this is the process of learning to live inside a body and discovering that the body has limits. It is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span> of Allah that these things happen to children, because they literally teach the lesson that the body is bounded — unlike the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُوحٌ</span>, which is a more limitless kind of entity. The body is limited, and the Sharia is a protection for the body: touch fire and you learn to stay away from it; stay away from heights; do not endanger yourself. So you allow children to discover the world. Those first seven years really are a time of discovery, and that is why the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُدًى</span>, whose root means to lust — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَلُمَ</span>, to lust — captures it well. It is a lusty experience: children are very sensuous, they love to be touched, and that is part of how they develop and discover themselves.</p>
<p>Then comes the second stage. He said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدِّبُوهُمْ لِسَبْعٍ</span> — discipline them for seven years, because now their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبَبٌ</span> needs to be disciplined. They are moving into a new stage and require firm guidance. Discipline here means that when you see them becoming angry, you do not allow it. You do not let children rage at their parents — that is a bad sign. You have to teach them to control their anger. And if you yourself cannot control your anger, it will be a disaster: you cannot give what you do not have. If children see you losing your temper constantly, they will simply imitate what they see, and then you will wonder where they got that temper. It came from you, because you are their model. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدَب</span> of those second seven years is precisely this.</p>
<p>Then he said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُصَاحِبُهُمْ لِسَبْعٍ</span> — befriend them for the next seven years, because this last stage is the stage of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْم</span>. They are now moving into a phase where they need your guidance, your counsel — your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَوَرَ</span> — because you will help direct their education, their vocation, and the shape of their lives. That is the traditional model.</p>
<p>Any questions? — It is worth noting that the word for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْم</span>: what is its root in Arabic? You know the answer, but what about the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَمَ</span>? When they said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَفَّاهُ أَخْلَمَانَا</span> — the intellect — forbearance is actually a sign of intellect. Why? Because you have your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّةُ الْغَضَبِيَّةِ</span> under control; it is directed by the rational soul. Your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقْل</span> is working, because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقْل</span> is an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِكَالٌ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقِيلُهُ</span> — it is what puts the irascible and the concupiscent soul in check. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقْل</span> means to prevent, to restrain, and that is precisely what the rational mind is supposed to do: keep the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَبٌ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span> in check. If it fails to do that, you are not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَاقِل</span>, you are not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلِيمٌ</span> — you are, as they say, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِمَارٌ</span>. If someone becomes truly enraged, people say he is like a donkey: he is acting like a beast, not like a human being. And usually he is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِمَارٌ</span> because his anger has taken over.</p>
<p>But the crucial distinction is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحَلُّم</span> — and this is a very good point that was just raised, and I am genuinely glad it came up because I had forgotten to mention it. There is a hadith that is important here, because some of us will say: &quot;My God, I cannot do any of this — it is all too hard. I am a mess. I get angry. I overindulge.&quot; The hadith says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّمَا الْعِلْمُ بِالتَّعَلُّمِ</span> — knowledge is acquired; you are not born an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِيمٌ</span>, you become one. How? Through effort, through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهَدَة</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِيمٌ</span> is the one who struggles to learn: he rises at night, he studies, he reads, he attends lessons, he listens, he takes notes, he reviews. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهَدَة</span>. You were not born with that knowledge; it is something you acquire. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِلْم</span> is exactly the same. The hadith says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِنَّمَا الْحِلْمُ بِالتَّحَلُّمِ</span> — forbearance too is acquired. And what happens when someone becomes a true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِيمٌ</span>? When you encounter such a person, you do not see the student he once was. It seems as though he always had that knowledge — you did not witness all those years of study and struggle. The same is true of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلِيمٌ</span>: you do not see when he was wrestling with himself; you assume he was simply born that way. But it is something acquired, something that took real effort. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأَلُّم</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَرُّد</span>, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَلُّدٌ</span> — you have to work for it, and it is hard.</p>
<p>Now, here is a very important point. Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span> uses the metaphor of a calligrapher. He asks: what do you do when your handwriting is poor? And this is the metaphor for dealing with anger. You go to someone with an excellent hand, you watch him, you ask him to form the letters for you, you go home and you practice those letters again and again. At first they will be poor — but after a month or two, you begin to notice improvement. It is not mastery, it is far from it, but it is noticeably better. And someone who has never started will look at your work and say, &quot;You have beautiful handwriting,&quot; while the master will look at the same page and say, &quot;This is terrible&quot; — because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَنَاتُ الْأَبْرَارِ سَيِّئَاتُ الْمُقَرَّبِينَ</span>: you are advancing through ranks, and the standard rises with every rank. When you reach mastery, what happens? I once watched <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَمَّدٌ</span> Zakariyya — I took a calligraphy lesson from him, a single lesson — and I watched him work, and I thought: &quot;I can do that. That looks easy.&quot; Why did I think that? Because he is a master. He is an American master of calligraphy who wins awards in Turkey. But I did not see the years of nights he had put into it. Now he just flows across the paper as if he were born writing perfect calligraphy. Then I picked up the pen and started, and it was like watching something jump up and down — it was very hard. He simply flowed. That is mastery.</p>
<p>So what do you do when you want good character? Three things. First, you learn what it is. You have to learn what bad character is and what good character is — and that is precisely what we are doing here in these sessions. The first stage is learning. Second: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلَّذِينَ جَهَدُوا۟ فِينَا لَنَهْدِيَنَّهُمْ سُبُلَنَا</span> — those who struggle for Our sake, We will guide them to Our paths. You have to find people of good character and spend time with them. There is also a benefit in being around people who get angry easily, because it allows you to see clearly how unpleasant it is. This is why Imam al-Shafii, when someone asked him how he had developed such excellent character, said: &quot;I listened to what my detractors said about me, and then I looked into my own soul to see whether it was true. When it was, I struggled with myself until I removed it.&quot; Sayyidna Isa was once asked the same question — how did you develop such excellent character? — and what the tradition records is this: &quot;I looked at the bad character of people and I avoided it.&quot; So part of it is simply <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهَدَة</span>. The third stage is to be with someone who has good character — and although that is genuinely difficult to find, we should never despair. At least we should be trying.</p>
<p>I will tell you this: it really works. I am serious. I am nowhere near where I would like to be, but it helped me enormously to learn these things and to struggle with myself as much as I could. You do change. You do improve. Anger — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَطَّاب</span> — was a serious difficulty for me for a very long time, and it is much less so now. Part of that, I will admit, is simply aging. Imam Sayyidna Ali said that youth is a kind of madness, and the cure is to grow old. So there is that.</p>
<p>On anger toward parents — the principle is that you should be angry at the act, not at the actor. What disturbs you should be the action itself, but if it is your father or your mother, you must be especially careful. If you are in a position to act, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَمْرُ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ وَالنَّهْيُ عَنِ الْمُنْكَرِ</span> applies, provided it will not lead to a worse <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِتْنَة</span>. But with parents you must be very careful. A man came to the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> and said: &quot;My father takes my wealth and it really angers me.&quot; The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: &quot;You and your wealth belong to your father&quot; — meaning that he was the instrument by which you were given existence, and that carries an immense weight. Parents hold a very high <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span> in Islam. The best approach is to recognize that they are human beings with their limitations. I had a teacher once — not a Muslim, but a very wise person — who told me that he thanked his father because his father had taught him how not to be a father. His father had been so harsh with him that he did the precise opposite with his own children. In the end he genuinely felt that it was a gift. He was not being sarcastic — he meant it. Obviously it would be better if your father showed you how to be a father rather than how not to be one, but the idea is taking the worst situation and making the best of it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَن يُؤْتَ ٱلْحِكْمَةَ فَقَدْ أُوتِىَ خَيْرًۭا كَثِيرًۭا</span> — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَة</span>. And that is the beauty of Islam: in any situation, it can be turned to benefit.</p>
<p>So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, we are actually coming — believe it or not — to the end. There are only four sections remaining after today, and I think we will finish all the diseases of the heart next week and then move on. He has a few excellent sections ahead: one is called the cure for all the diseases of the heart together — a single, overarching remedy. The Gordian knot, you might say. Everyone knows about the Gordian knot: a massive knot in Turkey that no one could untie, and the legend was that whoever untied it would rule the land. When Alexander the Great arrived, they showed him the knot — and he drew his sword and cut straight through it. The diseases of the heart are like that, and sometimes the sword is the best answer. Then we will do the section on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوَاجِسُ</span> — thoughts — because one of the most obvious manifestations of the diseases of the heart is diseased thinking: impure thoughts that afflict the mind, but also thoughts that a person might believe are genuinely good yet are in fact from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> is described in the hadith as being like a wolf among a flock: if he finds the shepherd watching from one side, he comes from another. One of the key differences between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسْوَاس</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span> of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>''s whisperings are compulsive, while the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> has its own habitual pull. We know from Ramadan that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيَاطِين</span> are locked up — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُفِّدَتِ الشَّيَاطِينُ</span> — and yet people still struggle. That is where you begin to distinguish between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, because those are the four enemies of the human being: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'anger';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next section is on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَغَفْلَةٌ عَنْ رُوحِ الدُّنْيَا نَهْيًا وَنَهًى وَحُكْمٍ وَهَيْئَتِهَا</span> — and this is a truly important concept. Imam al-Junayd said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is the root disease. And even though we have heard different candidates for the root disease proposed — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادٌ</span>, and then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكِبْرُ</span>, and then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصْوِيفٌ</span>, which is procrastination — procrastination is itself a form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, so I think those scholars would likely agree. The reason you procrastinate is that you are not truly attending to the matter at hand; if you genuinely saw the urgency of it, you would act. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is a serious illness of the heart.</p>
<p>He calls it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>: inattentiveness, heedlessness, unawareness. The Arabic word for a simpleton — someone you can easily fool — is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُغَفَّل</span>, a passive form: one who has been caught unaware. And so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, in his definition, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحْو</span>. As you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحْو</span> is a vital concept in the prayer itself — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُجُودُ السَّهْوِ</span>. The very fact that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُجُودُ السَّهْوِ</span> exists, and is an essential element of knowing how to pray, tells us something: the nature of the human being is forgetfulness. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is a kind of forgetting.</p>
<p>The word in Arabic for the human being — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span> — is revealing. Some say it derives from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُنْس</span>, meaning intimacy, because the human being is a creature that needs companionship, that needs <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُنْس</span>; he is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَشْيٌ</span>, not a creature that can exist in isolation. Others say it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِسْيَانٌ</span>, which means to forget. If you examine the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span> — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span> applies to both male and female, though some linguists note that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَانَةٌ</span> is a valid form, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَانَةٌ فِي تَنَاتُنْ</span>, appearing in a line of Jahili poetry, though rare — the human being is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span>, male and female alike:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِنْسَانُ الْإِنْسَانُ الذَّكَرُ وَالْأُنْثَى</span> ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ أَتَىٰ عَلَى ٱلْإِنسَنِ حِينٌۭ مِّنَ ٱلدَّهْرِ لَمْ يَكُن شَيْـًۭٔا مَّذْكُورًا</span> ‎</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span> is the human being. And in the Quran, when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span> is used, it sometimes refers to Adam, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, and the first slip of our father Adam was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِسْيَانٌ</span> — he was caught unaware by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. That is why the human being must be vigilant; he must have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَاصِم</span>, a guard. And so the disease of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحْو</span> — a forgetting — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَحْتَرِيَاتُ الْإِنْسَانِ مِنْ قِلَّةِ التَّحَفُّظِ وَالتَّيَقُّظِ</span>: it befalls the human being as a result of an absence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحَفُّظ</span> — vigilance — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَيَقُّظٌ</span> — wakefulness.</p>
<p>When Sayyidna Ali, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>, said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّاسُ نِيَامٌ، فَإِذَا مَاتُوا انْتَبَهُوا</span> — &quot;people are asleep, and when they die they wake up&quot; — what he meant is that people are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِلٌ</span>, sunk in heedlessness about their destiny: where they are going, and what they will be called to account for when they get there. Because if they truly knew, with real wakefulness — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقَظَة</span> — they could not do the things they do. That is why Imam al-Junayd said this is the core disease: if the human being truly knew what was coming, there is no way he could act as he does.</p>
<p>This idea is not entirely unlike the Socratic understanding, which belongs to a parallel tradition in the West — the idea that people do wrong because they are genuinely unaware; if they really knew what they were doing, they would not do it. The metaphor given in that tradition is the metaphor of the cave: human beings are like people sitting inside a cave with a lamp behind them, unable to turn around, watching shadows on the wall and taking those shadows for reality. Then one of them escapes, goes out into the real world, sees the sun and the stars and the trees, returns to the cave, and tells the others: &quot;This is all an illusion — what we thought was real is not real; the real thing is somewhere else.&quot; And they kill him. That is the role of the prophets: they come and tell people, &quot;You are all asleep — wake up, do you not realize?&quot; So the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is profound, and that is why Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> uses the word in many Quranic verses.</p>
<p>Another word the Quran uses is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَمَدٌ</span> — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَمَدٌ</span> is similar to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, but there is a greater degree of awareness in it; the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَمَدٌ</span> knows, in some sense, but the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِل</span> does not. Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْقَمَرِ</span>: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَّقَدْ كُنتَ فِى غَفْلَةٍۢ مِّنْ هَذَا فَكَشَفْنَا عَنكَ غِطَآءَكَ فَبَصَرُكَ ٱلْيَوْمَ حَدِيدٌۭ</span> ‎ — &quot;You were in heedlessness about this; We have removed the veil from you, and your sight today is sharp.&quot; There is a veil of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> over the human being, screening him from reality. And Allah says in the Quran that there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِشَا</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَعَلَىٰ قُلُوبِهِمْ أَكِنَّةٌ</span> — and this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِشْوَة</span> prevents them from seeing things as they truly are. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِل</span> is not seeing things as they truly are, while the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقِظٌ</span> — the one who is truly awake — sees things as they truly are.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> prayed: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ أَرِنِي الْأَشْيَاءَ كَمَا هِيَ</span> — &quot;O Allah, show me things as they really are.&quot; And in another hadith: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ أَرِنِي الْحَقَّ حَقًّا وَارْزُقْنِي اتِّبَاعَهُ، وَأَرِنِي الْبَاطِلَ بَاطِلًا وَارْزُقْنِي اجْتِنَابَهُ</span> — &quot;O Allah, show me truth as truth and grant me the ability to follow it; and show me falsehood as falsehood and grant me the ability to avoid it.&quot; The one who is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِل</span> has precisely that state: they see truth as truth and falsehood as falsehood, they follow the truth and avoid the falsehood. And that is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَّقٍ</span> — that is him, the person of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>: he does what Allah commanded and avoids what Allah prohibited.</p>
<p>So he is saying that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلُ الْمَرْءِ عَمَّا أَمَرَهُ اللَّهُ بِهِ وَنَهَاهُ عَنْهُ</span> — heedlessness about what Allah has commanded and what He has forbidden. That is why people do wrong: not out of pure malice in most cases, but out of heedlessness about what they have been commanded and what they have been prohibited from doing. And this is why, if you see a believer doing something wrong and you remind him, and he responds with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرُ اللَّهَ</span> — that is a good person, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَّقٍ</span>. You have brought him out of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّر</span>, by contrast — because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> is what follows after all that we have been discussing — when reminded of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, will not accept the reminder. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخَذَتْهُ ٱلْعِزَّةُ بِٱلْإِثْمِ</span>: he takes refuge in his own pride and refuses. You say to him gently, and he gets angry at you: &quot;Who do you think you are?&quot; That is why the cure for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> in a believer is good company — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُحْبَة</span>. Be with people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدْق</span>, of sincerity; be with people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>; be with truthful, sincere people who will tell you plainly when you are doing something wrong, and encourage you when you are doing something right. Those are the people who will pull you out of your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>.</p>
<p>He then says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَهِيَ لَدَيْهِمْ أَصْلُ كُلِّ ذَنْبٍ</span> — those of this science consider <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> the source of all wrong actions, as Imam al-Junayd al-Baghdadi held. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَدَوَاؤُهَا بِأَرْبَعِ دَوَاءٍ</span> — and the cure consists of four things that carry rectifying qualities: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَاسْتَغْفِرِ اللهَ وَزُرْ وَصَلِّ عَلَى النَّبِيِّ وَاكْتُبْ وَاذْكُرْ</span>. So he gives the four things.</p>
<p>The first is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِغْفَار</span>, because once you realize you have been <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِل</span>, you begin to see what you have done wrong as a consequence of that heedlessness, and you want to ask forgiveness. Many of the later scholars said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِغْفَار</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةً عَلَى النَّبِيِّ</span> are the two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرٌ</span> of the final era — the two pillars of devotion for this last period of time. Istighfar is asking forgiveness of Allah, and it is a form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that you should ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِغْفَار</span> at least seventy times, up to a hundred times a day — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرُ اللَّهَ</span> — so it is always a good practice. A complementary practice that most of the early Muslims observed was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَاسَبَة</span>. The idea comes from what merchants do at the end of the trading day: they count the money, see what was gained and what was lost, and balance the scales. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَاسَبَة</span> for the soul is the same: at the end of the day, you look at your day and ask — was there loss with Allah, or gain? If there was loss, you ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِغْفَار</span>. If there was gain, you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span> and are grateful. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَىِٕن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ</span> — if you are grateful, Allah increases you. So that is one way. He goes into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> as well, since that is one of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامَاتٌ</span> we will reach <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>The second remedy is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِيَارَة</span> — visiting righteous people. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحِينَ</span> occupy the lowest of four ranks: the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْبِيَاء</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُسُل</span> are first, then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيقٌ</span>, then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُهَدَاء</span>, then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحِين</span>. And yet they are all, in a sense, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحٌ</span> — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَاهِد</span> must be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلِيح</span>, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدِّيق</span> must be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلِيح</span>, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْبِيَاء</span> are all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحِينَ</span> by their very nature. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاحٌ</span> in Arabic means to be sound, and this is related to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامَة</span> — soundness, wholeness, the person who possesses a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَلْبٌ سَلِيمٌ</span>. The definition of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلِيح</span> is: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُؤَدِّي حَقَّ اللهِ وَيُؤَدِّي حَقَّ الْعِبَادِ</span> — he gives what is due to Allah and he gives what is due to the servants of Allah. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقُّ اللَّهِ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>: doing what He commanded and avoiding what He prohibited. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقُّ الْعِبَادِ</span> are all the responsibilities you carry in the world toward other people. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلِيح</span> cannot cheat — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِسْقٌ</span>, the very opposite of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاح</span>. He does not lie, because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقٌّ</span> of people upon you is that you be truthful to them. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلِيح</span> is a person worth visiting.</p>
<p>Most scholars not only encourage visiting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحِينَ</span> who are alive, but also those who have died. If you are in Medina, you go to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَقِيعْ</span> and visit the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحِينَ</span> there — you give them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ</span>, you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُمُ اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> used to visit Sayyidna Hamza, and he visited the graveyard and said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُنْتُ نَهَيْتُكُمْ عَنْ زِيَارَةِ الْقُبُورِ فَزُورُوهَا</span> — &quot;I used to forbid you from visiting graves,&quot; because of what the pre-Islamic Arabs used to do at them, &quot;but now that they have been purified of that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاهِلِيَّة</span>, visit them.&quot; There is a benefit in visiting graves — it is a reminder of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> — and there is a greater benefit in visiting the graves of the people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاح</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 141">page 141</div>
<p>On the question of women and graves: there is a hadith — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَعَنَ اللَّهُ زَوَّارَاتِ الْقُبُورِ</span>, and in one narration <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَائِرَاتُ الْقُبُورِ</span> — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَوَّرَ</span> is an intensive form, referring to someone who visits graves excessively and repeatedly. There is a hadith that is considered <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَوَاتِر</span> in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> passed by the graveyard in Medina and found a woman there weeping. He said it would be better to be patient. She replied: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا أَصَابَكَ مَا أَصَابَنِي</span> — &quot;You have not been struck by the calamity I have been struck by&quot; — and she did not recognize him. He left her, and then someone told her that had been the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. She immediately went and knocked at his door and said: &quot;I did not know it was you.&quot; And the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّبْرُ عِنْدَ الصَّدْمَةِ الْأُولَى</span> — &quot;Patience is with the first blow.&quot; Patience is when the affliction first strikes — that is when Allah expects it from you. After a year, if the grief has passed and you have moved forward, that is not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span>; that is simply time doing its work. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span> is at the moment the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُصِيبَة</span> occurs, and that is where Allah wants to see <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثَبَاتٌ</span> — steadfastness — and not a shaking that would cause one to do something displeasing to Him. But the point of this hadith for our discussion is that the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> did not tell her to leave the graveyard — and scholars take from that the permissibility of women visiting graves, though there is indeed a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافٌ</span> on the matter.</p>
<p>As for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدَب</span> of visiting graves: what happens at some graves nowadays has nothing to do with what is being discussed here. At some shrines — and this applies in many parts of the Muslim world — people tie knots, women who cannot conceive go to ask the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ</span> to give them a son, people make vows to the buried saint. All of that has nothing to do with a proper <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِيَارَة</span>. He is talking about visiting with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدَب</span>, for the blessing of the visit itself — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِيَارَة</span>. That is something quite different. And this confusion is part of what has given <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span> a bad name: much of what is done under its label is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَة</span> — in fact, it really is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَة</span>. Women sitting around in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلِد</span> without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِجَابٌ</span>, chatting and gossiping all day — those are all problems. In such cases, the scholars'' strategy is sometimes simply to prohibit the practice altogether, because the corruption has become so pervasive. But the best, without question, is to visit the living <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحِينَ</span>.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, in our time, this is quite rare. To find a truly righteous person, you often have to travel far and go to difficult places, because many of them have simply retreated to places that are hard to reach. Someone like Murabbit al-Hajj — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللهُ</span> — whom we visited in Shinqit, the father of Shaykh Abdallah — I consider him, in my estimation, from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحِينَ</span>. When Allah says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَكُونُوا مَعَ الصَّادِقِينَ</span> — &quot;be with the truthful ones&quot; — He would not command us to do something if there were no such people and no way to recognize them. It would be foolish to think we cannot recognize people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِدْق</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاح</span>. There are obviously degrees and levels, but the basic criterion is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>: if someone is outwardly upright in their observance of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, that is our standard. We judge by the outward, and we judge by truth.</p>
<p>One of the dangers I have noticed in modern <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span> — in many Muslim countries — is that there are people who go by the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِرّ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> or whatever, and many of them inherited the title from their father, who inherited it from his father, and many of them have very little knowledge, sometimes almost none. And they sometimes use their positions for material gain — extracting money from followers, arranging advantageous marriages for their daughters, and so on. There are a great many charlatans. And nothing is worse than a religious charlatan — nothing. There is a man in Fez, Sidi Maib, whom some here know, and he once said to me: &quot;Allah has made the world vast, and He has made the means of earning <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span> provision without number — there are untold ways of earning <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلَالٌ</span>. So whenever you see someone using the religion to gain <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, know that it is a bad thing.&quot; If they are using it to accumulate wealth and exploit their position, that is a serious problem — and unfortunately there are many who claim to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُيُوخٌ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُوفِي شُيُوخٌ</span> who do exactly that.</p>
<p>That said, having a livelihood is a different matter entirely. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَيْرُ مَا أَكَلَ الرَّجُلُ مِنْ كَسْبِ يَدِهِ</span> — the best thing a man can earn from is the work of his own hands. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> have taken from the hadith on teaching the Quran that it is permissible to earn from teaching. And even though the early <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَفٌ</span> were generally opposed to taking payment for teaching, the later scholars recognized that people must have a livelihood and that the world has become more complicated. That is not the issue. The issue is amassing and accumulating wealth — using religion as a vehicle for that. Imam Malik, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>, was given enormous amounts of wealth because of who he was, but what he did was spend it all out. Murabit al-Hajj is a fine example of this: money comes from all over, and it all goes straight back out. You can see it — a few cows, a tent, an adobe house, a few robes, some books, and constant giving. People give to righteous people because they trust them, and practicing Muslims are generous, so they want to give. The question is not receiving — the question is hoarding, accumulating beyond one''s needs, refusing to let it flow out. When your needs are taken care of and you begin to hoard, that is where the problem lies.</p>
<p>You have to be very careful, and it has been a problem for a long time. The scholars complained about it centuries ago. One scholar — and this man himself was a genuine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span>, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبٍّ</span>, a person of real <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> and outward knowledge both — said that in his time, the ninth century, most people who called themselves <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُوفِي شُيُوخٌ</span> were complete charlatans. So it is an old problem. And that is a condition, by the way: anyone who does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> must have outward knowledge. They cannot be without it.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, in the Muslim world today there is a profound split between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span> and exoteric Islam — between the inward and outward dimensions of the tradition — and they have become two camps pitched against each other. Nowhere is this more visible than on the Arabian Peninsula, where you have the Najdis and the Hijazis: most of the Najdis are staunchly anti-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُوفِي</span>, and many of the Hijazis are enthusiastically pro-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلِد</span> and pro-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span>, and they are at loggerheads, with real hostility between them. We are out of balance as an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, and the truth lies somewhere between the two positions. Make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> for them, and ask to benefit from their respective knowledges.</p>
<p>Ahmad Baba al-Timbukti — the great scholar from Timbuktu who was taken as a slave to Morocco — when he arrived in Marrakech, he was so much more learned than any of the scholars there that they quickly realized they had made a grave mistake. He mentions in his book — Dibaj al-Mudhahhab, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَشْيَة</span> on Ibn Farhun''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَبَقَات</span> — visiting several of the graves of the righteous in Marrakech and asking Allah at one of them to grant him understanding of two books he was working on. He says that by Allah, he returned to those books and could read them without any difficulty. He was a great scholar, and that was the traditional practice: to ask for the blessing of their knowledges, since most of those buried in such places were scholars, and to ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللهُ</span> — that Allah give them light in their graves and bless them.</p>
<p>Qadi Abu Bakr ibn al-Arabi — whose grave is just outside of Fez — gave a fatwa permitting the visiting of the graves of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحِينَ</span>, as did Ibn al-Hajj. For those who say it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَة</span>: that is simply not consistent with the position of the vast majority of our scholars, who have not only permitted it but recommended it. And these are outward scholars, jurists — well known in the tradition. Qadi Abu Bakr ibn al-Arabi''s grave is well known outside of Fez; he was poisoned by his enemies and died a martyr there, having also fought jihad multiple times. He wrote two commentaries on the Quran. He brought the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْيَاءُ عُلُومِ الدِّينِ</span> from Imam al-Ghazali back to Morocco and Andalusia — he had studied it with al-Ghazali directly and transmitted it west. He is a great scholar, and anyone who has visited that place knows there is a great deal of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَةٌ</span> there, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>But the living — if you have the opportunity to visit them, do so. And you should have high <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِمَّة</span>: desire to be one of them, so that our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> has people worth visiting. We need these people. Make the intention to be one yourself, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, and ask Allah to give you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> — and give all of us <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> for that. One of the poets said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا الْمَرْءُ لَمْ يَرْتَدِ الْعُلَا</span> — if a man does not reach the heights, at least he as<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِرّ</span>es to them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَنْ قَنِعَ بِالدُّونِ كَانَ دَنِيًّا</span> — whoever is content with less than heights is himself less than heights. The greatest as<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِرّ</span>ation is to be close to Allah. Have high <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِمَّة</span>. And Sidi Ibn Ajiba said: if you do not believe that Allah can take you in this very moment and make you one of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوْلِيَاء</span>, then you know nothing of His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُدْرَة</span>. Never despair. As<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِرّ</span>e to be from the people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِلَايَة</span>, the people who are close to Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِيَارَة</span> is important. When you visit these people — those I have visited from the people of this path — you will notice that they do not engage in idle chatter. You do not go for a social call. You go for the benefit of learning something, and for the benefit of their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>. Ask them for their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, because it may well be accepted, and if it is, you receive the blessing of it. Visit them, ask for their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, and show humility in their presence.</p>
<p>I will share a story about Mulla Ramadan, who was from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَالِحِينَ</span> and was the father of Shaykh Said Ramadan al-Buti. Shaykh Said wrote a book called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هٰذَا وَالِدِي</span> — &quot;This Is My Father&quot; — describing what a great man his father was. I once asked Dr. Bukar — a Libyan scholar, I believe he is in New Jersey now — about Mulla Ramadan, because it is always strengthening to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> to hear stories of these people. He told me — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span> — that the most remarkable thing about visiting that man was this: he said he had always found the practice of kissing hands rather unnecessary, something he had never been comfortable with. He said he went in, Mulla Ramadan extended his hand, and he felt a kind of pressure on his head — and found himself going down and kissing his hand. When he looked back, there was nothing there. He was</p>
<p>They would pray on the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. Imam Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said he never spent a single night without seeing the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> in a dream. There is a great blessing in it, and people bear witness to that.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 133">page 133</div>
<p>The fourth practice is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تِلَاوَةُ الْقُرْآنِ</span> — recitation of the Quran, preferably with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span>, which means reflection and pondering the meanings rather than merely reading the words. Reciting with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span> awakens the heart; it is one of the things that genuinely rouses it from slumber. Even reciting without deep reflection carries its own benefit. You should have a daily portion of Quran — ideally at least a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِزْبٌ</span> or a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَزْء</span> each day. If that is not possible, then at minimum recite the surahs the Messenger of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> recommended: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَاسِين</span>, generally after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>; and the surahs after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُلْك</span> after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَاء</span>.</p>
<p>For those who have not yet learned Arabic, reading the meanings in English is something, but it is limited — and this is the honest truth — because part of what the Quran does operates through the actual sounds of the language. Think of it like medicine. From an energetic perspective, every substance in the world resonates at a specific wavelength. Diseases resonate, and a medicine that cures a disease resonates in a way that counteracts it. The recitation of the Quran works through the sounds themselves, the sounds that emanate from you as you speak. We now know from material science that sound waves resonate through every cell in the body. When you recite the Quran, you are producing divine words that Allah revealed — and those words carry a sound.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 134">page 134</div>
<p>I was once with a man at a gas station. He was asking about Islam. A Muslim mechanic was there, and I was present wearing a robe, so he began asking me about it. He mentioned that he had read the Quran. I said, &quot;You have never read the Quran unless you read it in Arabic.&quot; He said he couldn''t believe that. I said it was a fact. He asked why, and I said, &quot;Just listen.&quot; Then I recited the opening of Surat al-Rahman to him — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهِ</span>, by the One besides whom there is no god — when I finished, he simply said, &quot;I understand now.&quot; He was not joking. Non-Arab people, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَجَمِي</span> people, are often stunned when they hear the Quran, even if they are not Muslim. They are amazed by the sounds.</p>
<p>You have to recognize that the Quran is not only its meanings — it is also the sounds, which transcend and participate in the meaning in a realm we do not fully comprehend, because everything in reality is meaning. Allah has taken meaning all the way down to the phonemes of the Quran. In modern linguistics, phonemes are considered meaningless in isolation; they acquire meaning only when joined into polysyllabic forms. Someone once wrote a nasty critique of Dr. Hamid Algar, complaining that his speech was filled with polysyllabic sentences. Dr. Algar pointed out in his rebuttal that a sentence that is not polysyllabic is, by definition, meaningless. And yet even individual phonemes carry meaning in Arabic. We may not know what that meaning is, but the meaning goes all the way down. Those are the four practices he identifies.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 135">page 135</div>
<p>Now, Allah warns the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> about sitting with those whose hearts He has placed in a state of heedlessness. Allah clarifies elsewhere in the Quran that when people deviated, He caused their deviation to increase. Those who turn away from the truth, Allah places in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, then, is itself a punishment. A Muslim who is in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is experiencing a consequence of his own disobedience toward Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷻ</span>. It is a serious condition — you do not want to be among the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَافِلِينَ</span>.</p>
<p>And yet, in all honesty, all of us will move in and out of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>. That is simply the nature of existence in this world. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> ends the moment you die. What matters is that you spend more of your life in a state of wakefulness than in a state of heedlessness. You want to be someone who is not in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>.</p>
<p>This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> Umar said that the best remembrance of Allah is when you reach a limit He has set. In other words, when you are on the verge of committing a sin — none of you drink wine, but imagine you were about to — and in that moment you remember Allah and think, &quot;I am about to put fire into my stomach,&quot; and that remembrance becomes a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَجْرٌ</span>, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنِيع</span>, a deterrent that prevents you from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> — that is the best <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرٌ</span>. When the desire for disobedience rises and the remembrance of Allah holds you back: that is what he means. So there are degrees of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, and what he is saying is that the disease is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> that prevents you from fulfilling what Allah has commanded and avoiding what He has prohibited. Anything beyond that, remembering Allah is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَحَبّ</span>.</p>
<p>Allah has prescribed remembrance at certain fixed times of day. He did not make remembrance throughout the entire day an obligation, because not everyone is capable of that. He said: remember Me at appointed times, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوْقَاتٌ مُعَيَّنَةٌ</span>. But He also encourages us to make much remembrance of Allah — and that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْل</span>, recommended, not obligatory. The more you remember Allah, the less likely you are to fall into the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> you do not want. And again, the disease of the heart is specifically the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> that prevents you from fulfilling your obligations or refraining from what has been prohibited.</p>
<p>As for the opposite — those in whose hearts Allah has written <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> — one of the meanings here relates to writing itself: to inscribe without diacritical marks or voweling signs. That is one dimension of the meaning. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> that Allah writes into the heart is of that nature.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'heedlessness';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next disease is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span>. Ghil is a disease of the heart related to rancor — resentful malice. The root word is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثُلَاثِي</span>, a three-letter root that can go three ways: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلٌّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلٌّ</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلٌّ</span> or the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غُلّ</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيْد</span>, a shackle. The people of the Fire have a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلٌّ</span> that binds them. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was sent to relieve humanity of their burdens and the things that were binding them — that is what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلٌّ</span> is. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> is when the heart becomes bound, as though shackled, to treachery, malice, or the desire to betray or harm someone. There is a lock holding it in place, just as a handcuff has the mechanism that holds it closed.</p>
<p>What holds the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> together is resentful malice — something that results from being angry at a person and keeping that anger inside the heart until it festers and eventually becomes a desire to harm that person. This is why among the things <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> mentions in the Quran is the supplication: do not place in our hearts <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> toward the believers. You do not want <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> in your heart toward any believer, because it will kill your heart. Literally kill it.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 138">page 138</div>
<p>One thing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> does in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> is remove <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> entirely. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِنِيْن</span> may die still carrying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span>, but you do not want to bring it with you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says in the Quran: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَزَعۡنَا مَا فِی صُدُورِهِم مِّنۡ غِلٍّ</span> — &quot;And We will remove whatever <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> is in their hearts,&quot; and He makes them brothers, reclining together on couches.</p>
<p>In the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا عَلِيٍّ</span> had an experience of this. A man who had fought against him — it may have been at the Battle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَمَال</span> — came to him and apologized. And that is the verse <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا عَلِيٍّ</span> recited: We removed from their hearts. He was saying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will take that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> out of the heart. You can carry rancor toward a person and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> can remove it. So ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> to remove the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> from you.</p>
<p>He then tells us how to do it: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحْسِنْ إِلَيْهِ</span> — do good to that person. That is what the man did when he went to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا عَلِيٍّ</span>. He humbled himself and apologized, and that was his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>. If you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> toward a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span>, do good to him. The nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span> is captured in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُبِلَتِ الْقُلُوبُ عَلَى حُبِّ مَنْ أَحْسَنَ إِلَيْهَا</span> — by their nature, hearts are inclined to love those who do good to them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has built this inclination into the heart regardless of a person''s will. If you do good to someone, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has made his heart naturally drawn toward you.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 139">page 139</div>
<p>Now some of the Arabs noted — and an Arab poet said — that when you do good to a noble person you win him over, but if you do good to a lowly person it can make him more rebellious, more agitated. So there are people like that. But you have done your part. If you do good to someone in order to cure your own heart, that is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is asking of you. You do your best. If the other person does not respond, you have done what was in your power. You are then left to make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> for them and to remember that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> can forgive them.</p>
<p>He also says this will cause your enemies — meaning <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> and his minions who love to sow <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> among the believers — to despair. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> loves to do this. He loves to set the believers at each other''s throats. I once asked a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> why it is that so many disbelievers seem to live peacefully together without much conflict, while even sincere Muslims often fight one another. He said: because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> is not occupied with the disbelievers anymore. They are already in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْرٌ</span> — he can leave them alone. But he is obsessed with the people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>. He works on them constantly, trying to make them stray. That is why, as has been transmitted, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> has despaired of being worshipped on the Arabian Peninsula, but he has not despaired of causing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِتْنَةٌ</span> and dissension among the people. We have to be vigilant against him.</p>
<p>And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تُقْنِتُهُ</span> — the way you cause <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> to despair when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> begins to arise in the heart is to reject it and to do good to that person at precisely the moment you want to harm them.</p>
<p>Then remember what came in the authentic <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> about Mondays and Thursdays. According to that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span>, the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> forgives people on those two days, and when the angels bring the records, they say: there are two people here who are in conflict, both believers. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says: leave them — leave them until they reconcile with one another. What this means is that if you carry <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِلّ</span> toward a fellow believer, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is not forgiving you. If you are at war with another Muslim, that forgiveness is withheld. All you have to do is extend the greeting with sincerity. That is all. Do not turn your back. If you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ</span> and they do not reply, you have fulfilled your obligation. It is then in their hands. Do what you can — that is all you are responsible for. But this is a frightening <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span>. You do not want animosity toward another believer, because it will prevent you from receiving <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s forgiveness.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'rancor';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="lecture 2">lecture 2</div>
<p>Now, to — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ</span> — this is the significant one: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَخْر</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَخْر</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 141">page 141</div>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَخْر</span> is, at its core, boasting. Boasting is a disease of the heart. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَخْر</span> differs from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبَاحَة</span> and similar things in that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَخْر</span>, as understood here, is boasting about something you have not yourself earned. It is boasting about lineage — saying, for instance, &quot;I am a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيف</span>,&quot; or trading on what your father or ancestors did.</p>
<p>One of the most striking moments illustrating this is from the Battle of Uhud. The Arabs would shout out, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا ابْنُ فُلَانٍ</span>&quot; — &quot;I am the son of so-and-so&quot; — as a form of war cry, a display of lineage. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> would respond: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا ابْنُ الْعَوَاتِكِ</span>&quot; — &quot;I am the son of the women named <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَاتِكَة</span>.&quot; The Arabs did not boast through their mothers, so he was doing the opposite of what they intended. Many of his grandmothers bore the name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَاتِكَة</span>, and that was his response to their boasting of fathers.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 142">page 142</div>
<p>On the matter of boasting about parents, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخُدَّام</span> mentions — and the saying goes — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَئِنْ فَخَرْتُ بِآبَاءٍ ذَوِي شَرَفٍ، لَقَدْ صَدَقْتَ وَلَكِنْ بِئْسَ مَا وَلَدُوا</span>: if you boast about ancestors who truly had honor, what you say may even be true — if they were from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَنِي هَاشِمٍ</span>, for instance, then yes, it is factually true — but what a wretched child they left behind. If you are boasting about your parents and have yourself accomplished nothing, then as the Arabs say: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَيْسَ الْفَتَى مَنْ قَالَ كَانَ أَبِي، إِنَّمَا الْفَتَى مَنْ قَالَ هَا أَنَا</span> — the man is not the one who says &quot;my father was.&quot; The man is the one who says &quot;here I am.&quot; You do something yourself.</p>
<p>Jalal al-Din al-Rumi has a poem in which he names all the great figures who came before, and then says: but do not be content with the stories of those who went before you — go out and create your own story. Be one of those people that those who come after you will speak about. This is part of our teaching: the idea of being genuinely significant — not for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, but for the sake of being someone who actually does something.</p>
<p>This is boasting. Saying &quot;my father did this&quot; — it is what small children do. Children say, &quot;My dad is a fireman.&quot; &quot;Well, my dad is an astronaut.&quot; It is childish. Mentioning a parent''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>, something good they did for you — that is a different matter entirely, and there is nothing wrong with it. But boasting is something we all recognize.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 143">page 143</div>
<p>Now he says — the mountain, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَوْدُهَا شَامِخٌ</span>, this high mountain — deem that mountain insignificant if you desire to bring it down to the ground. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>: arrogance. In Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> with a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَتْحًا</span> gives you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبَرٌ</span>, meaning old age. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> with a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُكُونٌ</span> over the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span> gives you arrogance. The root is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَبُرَ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَبِيرَة</span> — two related roots. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَبُرَ</span> means to grow, to be great; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَبِيرَة</span> means to grow old. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَبِيرٌ</span> means great — Ibrahim, peace be upon him, said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلْ فَعَلَهُۥ كَبِيرُهُمْ</span>&quot; — &quot;the great one did it.&quot; So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَبِير</span> is greatness. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> is something that lives in the heart. When it manifests in outward behavior, you call it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> are the same disease — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> is simply the action of it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبَّرَ عَلَيَّ</span>: he was arrogant toward me, meaning he did something that made it visible.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 144">page 144</div>
<p>There are many signs of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>. And I will tell you honestly — of all these diseases, this is the one I find most frightening. Because you have some degree of it. All of us do. It exists on a continuum: it can be severe, it can be mild, and you will rarely encounter someone who has none of it at all. When you do meet such a person, you feel it — their absence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> is palpable. But nearly everyone carries some. And the most frightening thing about it is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا يَدۡخُلُ الۡجَنَّةَ مَنۡ كَانَ فِي قَلۡبِهِ مِثۡقَالُ ذَرَّةٍ مِنۡ كِبۡرٍ</span></p>
<p>No one will enter Paradise — or, as the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> puts it, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَمَا قَدْ</span> — no one will enter Paradise who carries even the weight of an atom of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>, of arrogance, in his heart. Arrogance, then, is literally a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>rrier to Paradise.</p>
<p>This is grounded in the Quranic verse: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَأَصْرِفُ عَنْ آيَتِىَ ٱلَّذِينَ يَتَكَبَّرُونَ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ بِغَيْرِ ٱلْحَقِّ</span> — &quot;I will divert My signs from those who show arrogance on the earth without right.&quot; The scholars note that the phrase <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِغَيْرِ الْحَقِّ</span> implies that there are situations in which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> is actually appropriate. This was precisely the case with Abu Dujanah''s famous <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبَخْتُرٌ</span> — his swaggering gait on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ttlefield — which carried a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقٌّ</span>, a legitimate justification. In that context, what would ordinarily be reprehensible became praiseworthy.</p>
<p>So Allah says He will divert His signs from those who are arrogant on the earth without justification, without any legitimate right. And He also says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَذَلِكَ يَطْبَعُ ٱللَّهُ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ قَلْبِ مُتَكَبِّرٍۢ جَبَّارٍۢ</span> — Allah will seal the heart of every arrogant tyrant. And: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّهُۥ لَا يُحِبُّ ٱلْمُسْتَكْبِرِينَ</span> — He does not love those who harbor <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِكْبَار</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِكْبَار</span> is essentially the same as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>, but with an additional nuance: the person actively considers himself great. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَسْتَكْبِرُ</span> — he thinks he is great. And at its root lies <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span>, that self-admiration he discussed earlier — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُعْجَبٌ بِنَفْسِهِ</span>, thoroughly impressed with himself.</p>
<p>There is also the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which Allah says, as reported by the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقُولَ اللَّهُ تَعَالَى الْكِبْرِيَاءُ رِدَائِ وَالْعَظَمَةُ إِذَارِ فَمَن نَازَعْنِي فِيهِمَا قَصَمْتُهُ كِبْرِيَاءُ</span> — Greatness is My cloak, and majesty is My lower garment. Whoever tries to compete with Me over either of them, I will break him. These are two attributes Allah has reserved exclusively for Himself, and He does not permit any created being to lay claim to them.</p>
<p>This brings to mind something relevant to the subcontinent: there are certain names commonly given there that would pro<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>bly be better avoided. Ibn al-Hajj observed that Shaytan entered this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> through two different doors. With the eastern Muslims, he entered through names of grandiosity. With the western Muslims, he entered by causing them to mispronounce good names — dropping the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span> — so that, for example, in parts of West Africa, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ الْكَبِيرِ</span> becomes simply <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الكَبِيرُ</span>, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ الْقَادِرِ</span> becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَادِرُ</span>. And in Sudan, the pronunciation shifts similarly — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَادِرُ</span>, which sounds as though it derives from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَدَرَ</span>, meaning the treacherous one, when of course Allah''s name is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَادِرُ</span>. Ibn al-Hajj identified this as one of Shaytan''s stratagems.</p>
<p>As for the eastern Arabs, Shaytan led them toward names such as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَخْرُ الدِّين شَمْسُ الدِّين جَمَالُ الدِّين</span>. This is why Imam Yahya al-Nawawi, whose father named him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحْيِي الدِّينِ</span>, flatly refused that name and insisted on being called Yahya. He would not accept it at all. Names in this category — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَكْبَرُ، عَظِيمٌ، عُزْمَةٌ</span> and the like — carry that same problem. The issue is that such names constitute a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَزْكِيَة</span>, a self-endorsement. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> had a wife named <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِرَّ</span> and renamed her <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَيْنَب</span>, and said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تُزَكُّوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ</span> — do not declare yourselves pure. He also said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَصْدَقُ الْأَسْمَاءِ حَارِثٌ وَهَمَّامٌ</span> — the truest names are Harith and Humam, because every person <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَحْرِثُ</span> — he toils — and every person carries concern. Those, then, are the most honest names. And the best names are those that include <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدَ اللَّهِ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدَ الرَّحْمَنِ</span>, or names like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحْمَد</span>. If your name happens to be one of the others, that is simply what your parents gave you — so live up to it as best you can. As was pointed out with some humor, the trouble is when someone bears a lofty name yet turns out to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِلْعَقَةُ الدِّينِ</span> — a spoon of the religion, just eating all the time.</p>
<p>Now there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> — and it needs verification, though the transmission appears to be there — that is simply astonishing: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَحْشَرَ الْمُتَكَبِّرٌُونَ يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ فِي مِثْرِ صُورَ الْذَرَّ تَطَّأُهُمَ النَّاسُ ذَرًّا فِي مِثْرِ صُورَ الرِّجَالِ يَعْلُوهُمْ كُلُّ شَيْءٍ مِنَ الصَّغَارِ ثُمَّ يُسَاقُونَ إِلَى السِّسْنِ فِي جَهَنَمٍ يُقَالُوا لَهُ بَوْلَسٍ</span>. It is reported in al-Tirmidhi: the people of arrogance will be raised on the Day of Judgment transformed into atoms, and people will walk over them. Then they will be driven to a prison in Jahannam called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاصْ</span>. SubhanAllah. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاصْ</span> is strikingly close to what Arabic speakers call the police — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُذُوا إِلَى الْبَوْلَسِ، جَبَّارُونَ خُذُوا إِلَى الْبَوْلِسِ</span>. Whether that detail is precisely established in al-Tirmidhi needs to be confirmed, but if it is, it is extraordinary. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَرّ</span> can mean either an atom or an ant — both readings exist.</p>
<p>Moving on to the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِكْبَار</span> itself: there are two types. The worst form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> is refusing to accept the truth and refusing to submit to the worship of Allah — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعْظَم التَكَبُّر امتنع قبول الحق وإذعان للعبادة</span>.. Everything else falls beneath that in degrees of severity.</p>
<p>A companion once came to the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> and said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يا رسول الله إني امرأ أحب الجمال</span> — O Messenger of Allah, I am a man who loves beautiful things. I wear fine shoes and a nice garment. He then asked: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَهَذَا مِنَ الْكِبْرِ</span> — Is this from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>? The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> replied: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا إن الله جميل يحب الجمال وإنما الكبر بطر الحق وغمص الناس</span> — No. Allah is beautiful and loves beauty. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> is, rather, rejecting the truth and looking down on people.</p>
<p>This is an important <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, because the man first explained his motivation: he loved beautiful things. The scholars clarify that the problem arises when a person dresses or adorns himself in order to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبَّرَ عَلَى النَّاسِ</span> — to demonstrate superiority over others. This is painfully familiar in contemporary Muslim culture. Go to some Muslim weddings and you will see people consumed with outdoing one another — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span> and Khadija are going to show everyone what a real wedding looks like.&quot; That competitive one-upmanship is a form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>, because the underlying drive is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَتَكَبَّرُ عَلَيْهِمْ</span> — to assert superiority. It springs from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِكْبَار</span> has two faces. On one side, a person wants to achieve greatness and be great. Imam Raghib al-Isfahani says that if this drive is oriented toward something genuinely great, exercised at the right time, and for the right reasons, then it is praiseworthy. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِكْبَار</span> in this sense is not inherently negative — and this returns to the broader principle of transforming these diseases into virtues. A desire to be great for the sake of Allah is a good thing. A desire to accomplish great things is a good thing. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> had towering <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِمَّةٌ</span> — tremendous aspiration. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِمَّةٌ</span> was the highest of all, and he lifted them all toward it. They set their sights on al-Thuraya, the Pleiades. They reached for the heights in everything they did, and Allah made them great people. That is praiseworthy <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِمَّةٌ</span>.</p>
<p>The blameworthy kind, by contrast, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يظهر من نفسه أنه أفضل من غيره</span>. — when a person tries to project the image that he is better than others. That is what is censured. The one exception is demonstrating to non-Muslims that Muslims are better — because Allah actually commands this. Allah commands us to show them. Someone raised this very point the previous night regarding the Christian response to human nature. The Christian answer, in simplified terms, is that human beings are irredeemably corrupt by original sin — there is nothing to be done about it. Allah''s answer is: show them this is not true. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَذَٰرِكَ جَعَلْنَاكُمْ أُمَّةً وَسَطًى</span> — We made you a just, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>lanced community — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لِّتَكُونُوا۟ شُهَدَآءَ عَلَى ٱلنَّاسِ</span> — so that you may be witnesses to mankind, demonstrating that all other ways fall short. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَيَكُونَ ٱلرَّسُولُ عَلَيْكُمْ شَهِيدًۭا</span> — and the Messenger is a witness over you.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was a witness to us — he proved it was true. His Companions were witnesses to us — they proved it was true. Their followers were witnesses in turn. But each generation becomes more diluted, until eventually there are no more <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُهَدَاء</span>, no more living witnesses. This is one reason why so many people in the end of times fall into disbelief: they no longer see the truth embodied anywhere, so they lose it.</p>
<p>One of the signs of the end of times, according to Imam al-Biqai, is that Allah will hand the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> over to the disbelievers, and Muslims will a<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ndon Islam in pursuit of what the disbelievers have. He read this in the verses of Surah al-Zukhruf: had Allah not wished to prevent everyone from becoming disbelievers, He would have made their houses of silver and given them staircases to climb. Look around today — that is almost precisely what you see. People in Mali and Niger watch television and see those gleaming houses and want them. It is a real and serious problem.</p>
<p>Can the sealed heart be penetrated? Yes. Allah says that even rock can be split and water can flow from it — seals can be broken. The scholars say they are broken by the power of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَارِد</span>, the spiritual influx. A prophet, through the sheer spiritual force he carries — by the permission of Allah — can penetrate even the sealed heart. Sidi Ahmad Zarruq says that when someone with a sealed heart hears the truth, several factors determine whether it gets through: the stature of the one delivering it, the intrinsic strength of the truth itself, and the force with which it reaches the heart. If these converge with sufficient power, it can shake the heart and crack the seal. But if nothing follows — no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span>, no action taken — the crack can close over again. This is what happens when someone attends a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُطْبَة</span>, feels something stir in the heart, then steps outside and returns to the same life: it seals <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ck up.</p>
<p>Now, before concluding, here is a brief survey of the different types of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> and their distinguishing qualities.</p>
<p>The first type: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَقَرٌّ</span> — fixed and settled in the heart. The person genuinely believes he is better than others. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which a man was being praised in the Prophet''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> presence as a person of virtue. When the man entered the gathering, the Companions said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يا رسول الله هذا الذي ذكرناه لك</span> — O Messenger of Allah, this is the man we mentioned to you. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إني أرى في وجهه صفعة من الشيطان</span>. — I see in his face a trace of Shaytan. The man gave <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ</span> and sat down. Then the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> turned to him and said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أسألك بالله حدثتك نفسك أليس في القوم أفضل منك</span>. — I ask you by Allah: did your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> tell you, when you walked into this gathering, that no one here is better than you? The man said: Yes. So outwardly he appeared to be a righteous man, but the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> saw the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> embedded in his heart — and wanted the Companions to understand how subtle and hidden <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> can be.</p>
<p>Imam al-Ghazali says that among all people, scholars are in the greatest danger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>. And that includes students of sacred knowledge — people in exactly the situation under discussion. This is one of the gravest spiritual hazards of pursuing religious learning. If a person fights it, however — if he sees the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> within but forces himself to resist it — he is at least preventing the bitter fruits of that tree from ripening. The tree is there, the roots are there, but the fruit does not emerge.</p>
<p>The second type is where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> moves from the interior to the exterior: the person begins to manifest it in behavior, looking down on others, turning his face away, frowning in people''s faces. Frowning at someone is a sign of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>. Refusing to give someone the time of day is a sign of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> never did this. Adi ibn Hatim said he knew the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was not a king precisely because an old woman stopped him on the street and he simply stood there and listened to her — that was how he was. This second type includes the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَابِد</span>, the devout worshipper, who looks down on others because of his worship — he becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّرٌ</span> through his religiosity.</p>
<p>The third type is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> on account of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَبٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَسَبٌ</span> — one''s personal qualities and one''s lineage. These two traditionally went together. Western culture has the concept of <em>noblesse oblige</em>: those born into nobility are obliged to behave nobly. Most traditional cultures hold something similar. Among the Arabs, if you were a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيف</span> of the Tai clan, you were expected to be generous — generosity was the hallmark of Tai. If you were Qurayshi, you were expected to carry dignity, to be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَعِيمٌ</span>. If you were Yemeni, you upheld the qualities associated with Yemen. Each group had its defining virtues, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَسَبٌ</span> carried an obligation to embody them.</p>
<p>But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَسَبٌ</span> also becomes a source of arrogance. People with noble lineage begin to see themselves as superior to others. Some Arabs, simply because they are Arab, look down on Indians — this is common. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَقُّ يُقَالُ</span> — to tell the truth — there are many Arabs who think this way. One Arab once said plainly: I never thought a non-Arab could truly be a real Muslim. He said that directly. Similarly, in Pakistan, Indians who did not make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِجْرَة</span> may be seen as lesser. If you are Hydera<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>di, certain Hydera<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>dis consider themselves automatically superior to all other Indians by virtue of being from Hydera<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>d. Americans carry their own version — &quot;we''re number one.&quot; In Hausaland, many Hausa people are convinced of their superiority over the Yoru<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span> and Igbo, and pro<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>bly over the Fulani — though the Fulani, for their part, see themselves as superior to the Hausa. In Mauritania, the Kunta see themselves as better than the Massouma. In Greater Syria, the Damascenes consider themselves superior to the people of Aleppo — they even guard the name <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَامِي</span> jealously, reluctant to let anyone else claim it. This is a human disease, widespread and indiscriminate.</p>
<p>This is a disease of the heart — thinking yourself better simply because of some quality you happen to possess, like being from Damascus. Or being a Nejdi. The Nejdis deem themselves superior to the Hijazis; they call them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَافُ الْحُجَّاجِ</span>. You find this kind of arrogance embedded in the language, in the terminology of every culture. It is all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>.</p>
<p>Allah says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ أَكْرَمَكُمْ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ أَتْقَىٰكُمْ</span> — the most honored of you before Allah are those with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. Yet this disease deludes a person into assuming that someone else couldn''t possibly have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> — how could he? He''s a Hindi. He''s a Nejdi. He''s whatever.</p>
<p>This is not a modern phenomenon. It goes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ck a long way. Even among the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَأ</span> — those who trace their lineage to the family of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> — you find <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>. And of all things to feel superior about in terms of lineage, that would have to rank first. I''d put it at number one in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَسَبٌ</span>. And that is precisely the danger, because sometimes these distinctions do carry genuine weight.</p>
<p>There is something real and honorable about descending from the family of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> — it is a tremendous distinction. But there are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> that should put fear in your heart. One <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا أَبْطَأَكُمُ الْعَمَلُ لَمْ يُسْرِعْ بِكُمُ النَّسَبُ</span> — if your deeds hold you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ck, your lineage will not carry you forward. In other words, if you have the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَسَبٌ</span> but no deeds to go with it, it will get you nowhere. A fine car with no fuel. If you''re a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيف</span>, it''s a Mercedes — but deeds are still the petrol. You are not going anywhere without them. There are many <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> warning of this danger.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 144">page 144</div>
<p>Now, as for the cure — one of them is to reflect on where you actually came from. Allah says that you are from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَاءٍ مَهِينٍ</span>, a vile fluid. Your very origin is a vile fluid. One of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَفٌ</span>, speaking of someone who was being arrogant, said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ يَحْمِلُ بَيْنَ جَنْبَيْهِ الْعَذِرَةِ</span> — glory be to God, he carries between his two sides nothing but filth. Where does the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> come from? He is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>g of blood like everyone else. Every culture has its own crude ways of making this point, which I won''t go into — but people everywhere understand it. Nobody likes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>.</p>
<p>The problem is that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّرٌ</span> never sees his own arrogance. That is why, when someone is arrogant with him, he loathes it — even though he himself is guilty of the same thing. This is the meaning of the saying: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التَّكَبُّرُ فِي وَجْهِ الْمُتَكَبِّرٌِ عِبَادَةٌ</span> — to show arrogance in the face of an arrogant person is itself a form of worship. It is throwing it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ck at them. You give them a taste of their own medicine — but it should be understood as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَوَاءٌ</span>, a remedy, just enough for them to feel what it is like.</p>
<p>That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>. It is a serious problem. And if you feel that you are a righteous person — that itself is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>, right from the start. That is why, when you look at the early generations, they were people who genuinely saw themselves differently. One of the companions of Abu Hanifa was sitting with him when a man moved his foot aside out of deference. The companion asked why he had done so. The man said, out of respect for someone like you. He replied: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهِ لَا أَرَى أَحَدًا هُنَا أَشَرَّ مِنِّي</span> — by God, I see no one in this gathering worse than me.</p>
<p>That is how those people were. It was not performance. They genuinely saw themselves that way.</p>
<p>Look at Sayyidina Ali. There is a sound <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which a man asked him: who is better, you or Abu Bakr? He said, Abu Bakr. The man asked, then what about you and Omar? He said, Omar. Then the man asked about himself, and Ali said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ما أنا إلا رجل من المسلمين</span>. — I am just a man among the Muslims. No claim, no station.</p>
<p>For us, looking at this from the outside, it reads as humility. But for the Imam himself, he was not calculating: <em>I should say this in order to appear humble.</em> No — that way of thinking is itself a disease. Ibn Ataillah says: if you are aware of your humility, you are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّر</span>.</p>
<p>This is why the character of Uriah Heep in Dickens'' <em>David Copperfield</em> is so remarkable. He is a complete hypocrite who is forever saying, &quot;In my very umble opinion, sir&quot; — always performing lowliness, always deferring — while simultaneously stealing money and scheming behind everyone''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ck. The point is: if you think of yourself as humble, you are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّر</span>, because you are observing your own lowliness from a position of height. Humility means to be low. If you can see how low you are, you must be looking down from somewhere above. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> by definition.</p>
<p>We are diseased. We simply have to accept that and work with it. Our scholars have said: if you cannot yet be like the true people of virtue, at least imitate them — because there is benefit even in the imitation. The Imam himself acknowledges this: pretending at humility is better than being an outright <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّر</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 145">page 145</div>
<p>One of the things Imam al-Ghazali, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللهُ</span>, says is this: if you want to master calligraphy, you go to the master calligrapher and you repeat what he does, over and over again. It feels completely unnatural at first, but eventually you become a master calligrapher. Interestingly, a neurosurgeon at Stanford said something that maps onto this perfectly: the brain learns through repetition. Whatever you do most consistently becomes embedded in the brain — like driving. Most people drive in a nearly subconscious state; they are not aware of their own driving. That is the ideal of humility — it becomes so woven into your nature that the brain is no longer conscious of it.</p>
<p>When you first start learning to drive, you are actually more careful. Those poor learners with the dual-control car on top, checking the mirror, checking the blinker, anxious and alert — they are often safer than the experienced driver who is singing along to music, talking on the phone, and thinking about other things. The experienced driver is the one who gets into the accident, precisely because it has all become unconscious. And what the neurologist pointed out was striking: stroke patients and those with dementia often retain the things they did most repetitiously. They may forget almost everything else, but they can still drive — because those neural pathways are so deeply fixed that they remain.</p>
<p>This is the idea behind repetition in spiritual practice: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّمَا الْعِلْمُ بِالتَّعَلُّمِ</span> — you acquire it through learning, through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكْرَار</span>, doing it over and over again. You must force yourself to act with humility until it genuinely becomes your nature. Most of us do not have these qualities naturally — if we did, we would not need to study any of this. That is why we are studying it: we all recognize these diseases in ourselves.</p>
<p>Now the fourth type is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التَّفَاخُرُ بِالْجَمَالِ</span> — arrogance on account of physical beauty. One of the cures for this is the old adage: beauty is only skin deep. Beauty is an illusion. First, no one retains their beauty forever — everyone loses it in the end. Second, Allah gave it to you; it is not something you earned. So taking arrogant pride in beauty is a particularly shallow and foolish form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>.</p>
<p>What I find genuinely fascinating is a study that examined what makes people beautiful. Researchers looked at large numbers of faces and found that what people consistently identified as beautiful was, in fact, average — statistically. An average nose, average eyes, average mouth — all placed together on one face. Beautiful people are, in a real sense, mean-looking. They represent the median. I thought that was remarkable: beauty is the average. And there is a kind of truth in it — the median holds something. It is a foolish thing to take pride in.</p>
<p>There is also a deeply sobering <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> involving Aisha, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهَا</span>, in which a woman entered and the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> asked who it was. She indicated the short one. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اغْتَبْتِهَا</span> — you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ckbitten her. The scholars explain that the reason it constituted <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ckbiting was that it arose from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>: she had, in that moment, a sense of her own greater height, an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِعْجَابٌ</span> with her own stature — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُعْجَبَةٌ بِقَامَتِهَا</span> — and looking at the other woman from that place of self-regard made the remark an act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> rebuked her for it.</p>
<p>The fifth type is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ بِالْمَالِ</span> — pride in wealth. This afflicts kings with their treasuries, merchants with their goods, and people who own vast tracts of land, fine clothes, horses, or — in our time — expensive cars. They look down on the poor, and even on other wealthy people who are less wealthy than themselves. Think of Leona Helmsley, who famously said that only the little people pay taxes. I once read a remarkable interview — I cannot recall exactly who it was — one of those figures this culture calls &quot;beautiful people.&quot; They were being airlifted by private helicopter to a ski slope while a lodge for less-wealthy skiers sat below, and someone looked down and said, &quot;Look at all the little people down there.&quot; At that very moment, the helicopter tipped and they crashed. The point is: she got the message. Some people would not have seen it that way. So be very careful about how you regard others, because we are all little people. The homeless man on the street looks up at the CEO and sees a little person; the CEO looks down at the homeless man and sees a little person. It is all perspective. That is why the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّرٌٌ</span> are transformed into ants on the Day of Judgment — because they looked at everyone else as ants to be crushed. It is a very dangerous thing.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 194">page 194</div>
<p>The sixth is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ بِالْقُوَّةِ</span> — pride in physical strength. An example is Rukana al-Muttalibi, who challenged the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> to a wrestling match. The Prophet threw him twice, and still he did not accept Islam. There is a narration suggesting he did eventually embrace Islam — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> he did, though I cannot say with certainty. The point is that he had never been thrown by anyone, and that fed his arrogance. Such a person will inevitably grow old. The strength will leave him. He will no longer be able to do what he once could.</p>
<p>The seventh is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّرٌ بِالْأَتْبَاعِ</span> — pride derived from one''s followers, supporters, students, and servants. A teacher who has many students may look down on a teacher with fewer. Someone with many powerful friends, influential relatives, or a connection to royalty — even if he is not the king himself — may feel superior because of that association. This is a very dangerous form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> rooted in social standing and prestige.</p>
<p>And then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span> through knowledge, which the imam identifies as among the most insidious forms of all. The reason is precisely that knowledge is so honored in the tradition. People naturally revere and defer to the learned. So when someone genuinely possesses knowledge — especially a significant amount of it — people show him respect, and that deference becomes dangerous for the heart. He begins to think, &quot;I am somebody. I matter.&quot; This is what Shaytan does to him.</p>
<p>Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says in the Quran: how has man been destroyed — what makes him such an ingrate? And then Allah says: let man think about what We created him from. He came from a fluid. He did not even exist before that fluid. He was not even a thought on anyone''s tongue fifty years ago. He was nothing, nonexistent. Then he came into existence, and when he first arrived in this world, he needed his diaper changed seven or eight times a day — all he did was defecate, urinate, sleep, cry, and nurse. That is the human being. Then he grows, reaches the peak of his strength, and becomes arrogant. And then it reverses — because the trajectory is curvilinear. If you live long enough, you end up where you started. People reach that forty-year peak and think everything is settled, everything is fine. Then Allah says:</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِن نُّطْفَةٍ خَلَقَهُۥ فَقَدَّرَهُۥ</span></p>
<p>He created him from a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُطْفَة</span> — an ejaculated fluid — and then measured out everything for him. Man did not arrange any of that himself. And then Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says:</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 195">page 195</div>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوَلَمْ يَرَى الْإِنسَانِ أَنَّا خَلَقْنَاهُ مِنْ نُطْفَةٍ فَإِذَا هُوَ خَصِيمٌ مُبِينٌ</span></p>
<p>Has man not considered that We created him from a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُطْفَة</span>, and now he has become an open adversary against Us? It is astonishing. Allah created the human being, and then that human being rises up in rebellion against his Creator — and he is nothing. Allah can stir up a hurricane — like Hurricane Mitch — and it is just a small wind in one corner of one planet in one solar system within a vast universe. It is nothing. And yet for those struck by it, entire homes, wealth, everything — gone. From a wind that Allah sent. And Allah says in the Quran that when people see such a wind approaching, they think it is a good wind bringing rain — and it turns out to be a torment.</p>
<p>The point of all of this is that we are simply foolish to be in rebellion against Allah. He gave us a tongue, two eyes, the capacity to think — all of these gifts. He guided us and showed us two roads, telling us plainly: at the end of this road is a painful punishment, and at the end of that road is a reward. Choose. And yet people choose the road of ruin, and at the root of that choice is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>.</p>
<p>Look at the Sira — I guarantee you, every single person who refused to submit, the reason was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>. It is unmistakable. The reason people cannot submit is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكَبُّر</span>.</p>
<p>Consider the prayer. There is a kind of servitude embedded in it that some people find deeply offensive. Many Western people struggle profoundly with the concept of being a slave of God — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span>. They recoil from it: &quot;I am free.&quot; And yet that same person is a slave to his desires, a slave to his appetite, a slave to his petty ambitions — completely unable to control them. He is enslaved to all of these things. But a slave to Allah? No. He will gladly be a slave to his own passions but not to Allah. It is a thoroughly delusional state.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 196">page 196</div>
<p>So the imam is saying that the way to uproot this disease is: first, to know your Lord, and second, to know yourself. That is, admittedly, an enormous undertaking. Some scholars say the only way to know your Lord is to know yourself — and Allah points us in that direction: <em>in yourselves, do you not see?</em> If you reflect deeply on the self, what you come to realize is that you are completely bound. Even <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِيسَى ٱبْنَ مَرْيَمَ</span> was not too proud to acknowledge that he was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْدُ اللهِ</span> — he accepted it. And the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا سَيِّدُ وَلَدِ آدَمَ وَلَا فَخْرَ</span> — I am the best of the children of Adam, and I say this without boasting. The ulama explain that he said this not out of pride, but because he was commanded by Allah to inform the Companions of his station — and precisely because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاهِلِي</span> Arabs used boasting as an act of arrogance, he was careful to add <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا فَخْرَ</span>, making clear this was not that. The ulama draw a further <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِشَارَة</span> from this: not only was there no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَخْر</span> in it, but his true source of honor was his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span> to Allah — his being the most perfect slave of Allah — not his being the master of creation. That is where his glory lay. This is why there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> — some scholars question its chain, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُلَّا عَلِي الْقَارِي</span> affirmed that its meaning is sound — that his empowerment before Allah was his glory.</p>
<p>So: know yourself, know your Lord — and whoever truly grasps both is humbled, feels insignificant, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَتَوَضَّأُ وَيَهُونُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ تَوَاضَعَ لِلَّهِ رَفَعَهُ اللَّهُ</span> — whoever humbles himself for the sake of Allah, Allah raises him up.</p>
<p>He also makes another point: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامُهُ يَنْفِي مَقَامَ الشُّكْرِ</span> — the station of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> makes genuine gratitude to Allah impossible. The way you show gratitude to Allah is through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُبُودِيَّة</span>, and if you are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّرٌ</span>, you are not an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span> — because an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span> by his very nature cannot be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّرٌ</span>. That is what makes him an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span>. And if you are not a true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span>, you cannot be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكُورٌ</span>. It is categorically impossible. Conversely, the nature of humility is that it leads you to gratitude, because when you are genuinely humbled before Allah, you see everything in its true proportion — you see how generous Allah has been, how much He has given you that you do not deserve. And that sense of being undeserving is precisely what generates gratitude. If you feel you deserve something, gratitude does not arise. As the Moroccans say: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا شُكْرَ عَلَى الْوَاجِبِ</span> — there is no thanks for what is owed. If you feel entitled, you simply think: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا، أَعْطَانِي مَا هُوَ حَقِّي</span> — he only gave me what was mine. But once you recognize you are undeserving, everything changes.</p>
<p>That is why he says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقِّرْهُ</span> — see this towering mountain of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> for what it actually is: insignificant and meaningless. When you see it that way, you will bring it down. This is also why we say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span> — and traditionally, whenever Muslims saw something impressive in themselves or in others, they would refer it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ck to its source: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. It is a constant reminder. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ مِنِّي، اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ مِنْكُمْ، اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ مِنَ الدُّنْيَا وَمَا فِيهَا</span> — Allah is greater than me, greater than you, greater than this world and all it contains. He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَبِيرُ الْمُتَعَالِي</span>. And it is to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَعَالٍ</span> that He says: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُقْ إِنَّكَ أَنتَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْكَرِيمُ</span> ‎ — now taste it; you are the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَزِيزٌ</span> now. So taste the punishment. This is the <span class="ar-inl' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'boasting & arrogance';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'ine" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحَكُّم</span> of the Quran.</p>
<p>So the last topic remaining is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُلّ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَاءٌ</span> — but we will need to prepare for that, so we will take it up next week, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَبَارِكْ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ كَمَا بَارَكْتَ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ سَيِّدِنَا إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَفِي الْعَالَمِينَ إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْحَكِيمُ الْعَلِيمُ</span>.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا مَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا وَزِدْنَا عِلْمًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ انْشُرْ عَلَيْنَا رَحْمَتَكَ وَانْشُرْ عَلَيْنَا حِكْمَتَكَ يَا ذَا الْجَلَالِ وَالْإِكْرَامِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَصَلَّى اللَّهُمَّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَصَحْبِهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span></p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا مَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا وَزِدْنَا عِلْمًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ انْشُرْ عَلَيْنَا رَحْمَتَكَ وَانْشُرْ عَلَيْنَا حِكْمَتَكَ يَا ذَا الْجَلَالِ وَالْإِكْرَامِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَصَلَّى اللَّهُمَّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَصَحْبِهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span></p>
<hr />
<p>Now, this section just covered brings us to the end of that class. We are nearly done with the first half of the book. The second half — which I mentioned to some of you last night — deals with the opposite: after treating the diseases of the heart, it moves to adorning the heart with the states and stations of certainty. We will <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> begin that in February.</p>
<p>We have been looking at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَخْر</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span> — serious illnesses of the heart. One of the things worth noting here is the concept of the mean, what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْوَسَطِيَّة</span> in Arabic. Virtues are means between two extremes — with one important exception: justice, which is simply the opposite of injustice. It has an opposite, but not two flanking extremes in the same way.</p>
<p>Now, the next disease the imam addresses is one that sits at the opposite extreme from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>. The virtue is humility. An excess of it — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِيَادَةٌ</span> — slides into arrogance. A deficiency — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَقْص</span> — slides into a<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>sement and self-humiliation, which is not a virtue in Islam. One might argue it is regarded as a virtue in certain strands of Christian thought, but in Islam it is not. The imam calls this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُلّ</span>.</p>
<p>In the Quran, Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> mentions that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُلّ</span> was among the afflictions visited upon Bani Israel as a consequence of their disobedience. So a<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>sement is often the result of disobedience — Allah afflicts people with it as a response to their arrogance. The sun never sets on the British Empire — and then it sets. The British lion becomes the tail of America. For Anglo-Saxons who always viewed the Americans as colonials — as the lesser people — those lesser people now dictate British foreign policy. That is what Allah does with peoples. And it will come to the Americans as well, just as it comes to everyone. Their turn is coming; there are already many signs of it.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُلّ</span> can be a social phenomenon afflicting an entire people. Many Muslim countries find themselves in this condition today. You will find Muslims who will do unbelievable things — lie, cheat, scheme — just to obtain a visa to a European country or to America. Inside their own countries, they conduct themselves in ways that are simply shocking in terms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>sic human dignity. All of it traces <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ck to this inner lowliness, this humiliation. And yet — despite that — you can also find a people crushed by great tribulations who nevertheless maintain their human dignity entirely. That is something else altogether.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْمُؤْمِنُ لَا يُذِلُّ نَفْسَهُ</span> — the believer never humiliates himself, never a<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>ses himself. This is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. So when we speak of humility, we do not mean humiliation. You humble yourself; you do not humiliate yourself.</p>
<p>He then mentions: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذَلُّ وَبَاعَ</span> — to be lowly, to be brought low. And the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: nothing raises itself except that Allah will a<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>se it. That is the sunna of Allah in His creation. If you elevate yourself, Allah will a<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>se you. These are wisdoms to be treasured.</p>
<p>Before Allah, you should have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُلّ</span>. Before Allah you should be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَلِيل</span> — you should carry a sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُلِّ وَالْهَوَانِ وَبَاءَ</span>. That is the proper orientation before Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. This is why Sidi Abd al-Qadir al-Jilani said that he looked upon all the doors leading to Allah and found them crowded — except one: the door of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذُلٌّ وَتَوَاضُعٌ</span>, humility and lowliness. And so he called out to people: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلُمَّ ارْعَ رَبَّكُمْ</span> — come, you want to reach your Lord? This is the door. No one is there. It is hard on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, which is precisely why the path is empty.</p>
<p>He then warns against the opposite failure: do not a<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَا</span>se yourself before creation. In fact, he says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاكْبُرْ عَلَى الْغَنِيِّ الْمُسْتَكْبِرِ</span> — hold yourself with dignity before the rich and arrogant. It has been said that among the noble things of this world is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَنِيٌّ مُتَوَاضِعٌ</span> — a wealthy man who is humble. That is genuine nobility. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيفٌ سُنِّيٌّ</span> — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيف</span> who follows the Sunna. These are among the things mentioned as truly distinguished. But the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَنِيُّ الْمُسْتَكْبِرِ</span> — the arrogant rich man — is unfortunately the norm.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱلْإِنسَنَ لَيَطْغَىٰٓ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَن رَّآهُ ٱسْتَغْنَىٰٓ</span> — Haven''t you seen? Man transgresses when he deems himself self-sufficient. When he becomes wealthy, he imagines he has no need of anyone — and that imagined independence causes him to overstep the boundaries of Allah.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 246">page 246</div>
<p>And that is why, like Nimrod — because Allah gave him dominion over the earth, he thought he was God. He argued with Ibrahim. And this is what wealthy people often do: they think that because they have wealth, it gives them the right to hold opinions on everything, to be an expert on everything. It is a serious matter.</p>
<p>So he said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التَّكَبُّرُ فِي وَجْهِ الْمُتَكَبِّرٌِ عِبَادَةٌ</span> — it is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, and in one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِوَايَة</span> the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> appears. That is the second person.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'boasting & arrogance';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next disease is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَاهَةُ الذَّمِّ</span> — dislike of blame. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِمّ</span> means to blame someone. By human nature, we do not like to be blamed; it is something inherent in us. We teach this to our children early on: we praise them for certain things and blame them for others, and they quickly learn that praise is pleasant — it comes with smiles, gifts, warmth, hugs — while blame is painful, accompanied by frowns, anger, raised voices, and sometimes physical punishment. So from very early on, children learn to want praise and to dislike blame. The problem is that this natural aversion becomes a disease: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَاهِيَةُ ذَمٍّ</span>.</p>
<p>He says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَاهَةُ ذَمِّ بَنَانٍ مَأْلُوفٍ</span> — it is a well-known disease. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالنَّظَرُ إِلَى الْعِبَادِ وَالْوُقُوفُ مَعَهُمْ حِجَابٌ عَنْ مَقَامِ الْإِحْسَانِ</span> What he is saying is that displeasure with blame is a well-known disease of the heart. Preoccupation with what people think of you — craving their praise and resenting their criticism — becomes a barrier between you and the station of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>, because your actions begin to be driven by the desire to be praised or the fear of being blamed.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 248">page 248</div>
<p>One example from the seerah class: Sa''d ibn Muadh <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>. When the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> asked the Aus to accept his judgment regarding Bani Quraydah — since Bani Quraydah were confederates of the Aus, and the Khazraj had secured the release of Bani Nadir through the intercession of Abdullah ibn Ubayy, the Aus wanted the same outcome for their confederates. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> put the matter to Sa''d. Now, what Bani Quraydah had done was actually far graver than what Bani Nadir had done — they had joined the enemy army during the siege. Bani Nadir had attempted to assassinate the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, which is a more serious crime in the larger picture, but the treachery of Bani Quraydah during battle was of a different order. In any case, when Sa''d came forward, people were urging him: please intercede with the Prophet, get them off just as Bani Nadir were spared. And he said — and this is the important point — that he had reached a place in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْقِف</span> where he had no fear of the blame of any blamer. He was near death. He said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْآنَ وَجَدْتُ نَفْسِي فِي مَقَامٍ لَا أَخَافُ فِيهِ لَوْمَةَ لَائِمٍ</span> — I have found myself in a position where I am not concerned with what people will say.</p>
<p>What the Aus were worried about was public perception: our confederates were all killed while the Khazraj got theirs off. Sa''d ibn Muadh said: I am about to meet my Lord. I am not concerned with what people will say.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 249">page 249</div>
<p>The point is that, in reality, you are in that very moment at every instant of your life. Death is simply veiled from you — that veil is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طُولُ الْأَمَلِ</span>, which is another disease in itself. It makes you think you will be around. But that is a complete illusion; you have no guarantees whatsoever. So what the example of Sa''d illustrates is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامُ الْإِحْسَانِ</span> — all he could see was Allah. Creation did not enter into it.</p>
<p>That is the whole point of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَاهِيَةُ الذَّمِّ</span>: when you start worrying about what others will think, it often prevents you from doing the right thing — because very often, the right thing is precisely what everyone else will oppose. That is the nature of this world. Taking a courageous stance usually means taking a stance you will be blamed for. And courageous positions are often only recognized much later.</p>
<p>Geronimo is a good example. When he was fighting the US cavalry, he was portrayed as an evil, horrific barbarian. Now he is seen as a hero by both whites and Native Americans alike — a great freedom fighter defending his land. At the time, even his own people turned against him; most had already surrendered, and he kept fighting until he was finally betrayed by those around him. Malcolm X is another example. Once enough time passes and a person is no longer perceived as a threat, the very forces that opposed him will co-opt his legacy — that is what they do. But at the time, he was taking positions that virtually the whole of society was against. Had he been worried about their opinion, he could never have spoken the truth. That is what happens when people are consumed by concern for what others think: they lose the ability to take the courageous positions that are unpopular precisely because they are true, and that attract attack for that very reason.</p>
<p>Then he says that overcoming this barrier comes through the realization that there is no benefit or harm except from Allah. This is, as you will notice, a recurring cure for many of these diseases — genuinely recognizing two names of Allah: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّافِعُ وَالضَّارُّ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّافِع</span> is the One who benefits, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الضَّرُّ</span> is the One who harms. These are attributes of Allah alone; no human being possesses them. Only Allah can truly benefit, and only Allah can truly harm. So if concern about what people think is preventing you from doing what you should do for the sake of Allah, you are not seeing reality. You are in a delusional state — one that is not only harmful to your iman but is simply out of accord with how things actually are.</p>
<p>This is why the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said to Ibn Abbas <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span>, giving him this counsel: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إذا استعنت فاستعن بالله</span> — when you seek help, seek it only from Allah. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وإذا سألت فاسأل الله</span> — and when you ask, ask only from Allah. And then he said: know that even if all of creation were to gather together to harm you, they could not harm you except with what Allah has decreed for you. And if they gathered to benefit you, they could not benefit you except with what Allah has decreed. Creation can do nothing to you — they can neither harm you nor benefit you.</p>
<p>Now, this obviously does not mean ignoring the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْبَاب</span> — the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> wore two coats of chain armor at the Battle of Uhud. Those are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْبَاب</span>. He knew better than anyone that no harm and no benefit come except from Allah, yet that did not negate using one''s intellect and engaging with the world of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْبَاب</span>. It also does not negate diplomacy, because as the poet said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَقَدْ خُطَّهَا حَقٌّ وَلَٰكِنْ مَا كُلُّ حَقٍّ يُقَالُ</span> — I said the truth, but not every truth needs to be said. There are times when discretion is the wiser course.</p>
<p>I know of a Libyan man — an Azhari graduate — who was part of an official delegation in Iraq at a large gathering. Someone was making a toast to the Baath Party, and this man stood up and said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَعَنَ اللَّهُ حِزْبَكَ وَمَنْ دَعَا لَهُ</span> — may Allah curse your party and those who pray for it — and he went on cursing. The rest of the delegation were horrified and tried to cover for him, telling the hosts he was not quite right in the mind and not to pay him any attention. In the end, nothing happened to them. But the point is: he probably should not have been there supporting those people in the first place, and the outburst, however sincere, was not the most sagacious move.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَلِمَةُ حَقٍّ أَمَامَ سُلْطَانٍ جَائِرٍ</span> — speaking the truth in the face of an oppressive tyrant — the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> called that the greatest jihad, and if you die for it, you are a shaheed without question. But that does not mean every moment calls for it. If there is long-term benefit to be weighed, if this is a situation where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَرْبُ خُدْعَةٌ</span> — war is strategy — then you have to measure things carefully.</p>
<p>Then he says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَسْتَمِدُّ الْحَرَامُ مِنْهُمَا جَرَّاءَ إِرَاءٍ مُحَرَّمٍ كَمَا الْغَزَّالِيُّ فَصَّلَ الْغَزَّالُ</span> — the impermissible dimension of this disease is what leads in turn to the prohibited, as Imam al-Ghazali has clarified. In other words, while not liking to be blamed is natural, what becomes haram is whatever that aversion leads you to do that is itself haram. For instance: if you are obliged to enjoin the right and forbid the wrong, but you hold back out of worry about what people will say — out of fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَوْمَةُ لَائِمٍ</span>, the blame of the blamer — and that fear causes you to abandon an obligation, that is where the disease becomes sinful. Or, say you are at work and it is time to pray, but you start thinking about what your co-workers will think if you ask to step out — because praying at work is not exactly popular in America. If that concern stops you from praying, that is clearly <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَاهِيَةُ الذَّمِّ</span> in a form that is not acceptable. Being concerned about people''s opinion when doing something genuinely wrong is natural and appropriate. The disease — the haram — is when that concern prevents you from fulfilling your obligations.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 253">page 253</div>
<p>Another important point about this disease: real iman does not become established in the heart until, as the tradition of Ibn Masud states, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا يُؤْمِنُ أَحَدُكُمْ حَتَّى يَسْتَوِيَ عِنْدَهُ الذَّمُّ وَالْمَدْحُ</span> — blame and praise become equal to him. That is, you are acting for Allah, and you genuinely do not care what people think. If someone praises you — fine. If someone blames you — fine. You did it for the sake of Allah, and Allah knows that. That is what you are concerned with: not the eyes of others. That is the perfection of sincerity.</p>
<p>On a related note: one of the things I observed in West Africa that struck me as genuinely wise was that in the desert tradition, they never have young children perform displays of their Quran memorization — no &quot;come on, let''s hear you recite this&quot; followed by everyone applauding. They do not put children on display like that. I think that practice, however well-intentioned it may seem, is actually harmful, because it plants the early roots of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> — the desire for praise, for showing off. Parents naturally want to show their children off, and that desire is understandable.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 254">page 254</div>
<p>Even Sayyidina Umar <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span> — when Ibn Umar was in a gathering and the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> asked which tree resembles the believer, standing firm in the desert, and everyone started guessing — the Qalha, the Zainab, all sorts of trees — Abdullah ibn Umar, who was a young boy sitting among the great <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span>, immediately thought: it is the palm tree. But he was too shy to speak up in front of Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, and the senior companions. So he stayed quiet. When no one got the answer, the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> told them it was the palm tree, and afterward Ibn Umar told his father that he had known. Umar said: I would have loved for you to have said it — he would have been proud for his son to have spoken up in that gathering.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'displeasure with blame';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next disease is antipathy towards death: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَاهِيَةُ الْمَوْتِ</span>. He says about it: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَاهَةُ الْمَوْتِ بِحَيْثُ يَنْفِرُ مِنْهُ وَيَأْنَفُ إِذَا مَا يُذْكَرْ، حَتَّى كَأَنَّهُ بِذَوْقِ كُلِّ نَفْسٍ لَهُ الَّذِي أَتَى ذُو الْجَهْلِ</span> — antipathy towards death, this dislike of it, is when one flees from it and becomes annoyed whenever it is mentioned, as if he is entirely ignorant of Allah''s words: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُلُّ نَفْسٍۢ ذَآئِقَةُ ٱلْمَوْتِ</span> — every soul shall taste death.</p>
<p>Death is our inevitable end in this world. Each of us will taste it. This dislike of death he is describing is the kind where one runs from it.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 255">page 255</div>
<p>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ إِنَّ ٱلْمَوْتَ ٱلَّذِى تَفِرُّونَ مِنْهُ فَإِنَّهُۥ مُلَقِيكُمْ ۖ ثُمَّ تُرَدُّونَ إِلَىٰ عَلِمِ ٱلْغَيْبِ وَٱلشَّهَدَةِ فَيُنَبِّئُكُم بِمَا كُنتُمْ تَعْمَلُونَ</span> ‎ — say: this death you are fleeing from will certainly meet you, and then you will be returned to the Knower of the seen and the unseen, who will inform you of what you used to do.</p>
<p>Death is inevitable, and fleeing from it is futile. Some people observe that this culture is a death-denial culture. Muslims are less afflicted by this than non-Muslims — non-Muslims actively avoid mentioning death, find it uncomfortable in conversation, consider it almost bad manners to bring it up. I was once in a federal building getting a passport, riding up in an elevator with two men who looked like bureaucrats. One said to the other: I''m just waiting for that guy to die — he''s about to drop dead any minute, and I''m really looking forward to it. The other asked why. He said: it''ll just be nice to be in a world without him. Then the second one said: maybe we''ll die before him. And the first said: no, no, he''s almost gone.</p>
<p>That exchange is a revealing example of death denial in action — death is only something that happens to sick people, to old people, to the man on his deathbed. The healthy, the young — they operate as though death simply does not apply to them.</p>
<p>You know, what if we''re in Los Angeles and an earthquake strikes? There is a deep <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> that people fall into regarding death — a profound heedlessness. They don''t truly believe they are going to die.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page N">page N</div>
<p>And if they do acknowledge it, it''s mere lip service: <em>Oh, that''s a long way off.</em> So when someone hears death mentioned and it genuinely disturbs them — <em>I don''t want to talk about that. Let''s change the subject. This is depressing. This is morbid</em> — that attitude is foreign to our tradition.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> once passed by a group of people laughing in a gathering and said, &quot;Mix your gatherings with remembrance of the destroyer of pleasures.&quot; In other words, the remembrance of death brings a kind of sobriety and gravity to a gathering that has become too frivolous — not that people cannot enjoy themselves, but gatherings can drift into pure levity with no substance and no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَطْهِير</span>. That is precisely what the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was warning against: sitting in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَجْلِسُ الْغَفْلَةِ</span>.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> also said that there is no hour in this life you will regret in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> except one spent without remembering Allah. And in another narration, he said that anyone who sits in a gathering and then rises from it without having remembered Allah is like one who rises from a dead carcass — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِفَاءً</span>, carrion. We were created to worship Allah, and we are naturally drawn to intimacy and companionship, so our gatherings should not be purely for entertainment. They should carry some purpose: discussing matters of importance, making <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> for one another, and so forth.</p>
<p>Then the author states that this — hating death — is counted among the diseases of the heart, and he says: be content with what Allah the Exalted has decreed for you. If you are in a state of genuine acceptance of death, it will not disturb you — and accepting death is accepting the decree of Allah. One shaykh once told me in a gathering: &quot;We are all on death row. We are simply waiting for the executioner.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. We have all been condemned to death; we are just waiting. But once you realize that the One who condemned you to death is the very One who gave you life in the first place, you can be content — because He has done it for a wisdom.</p>
<p>From another perspective, death is a celebration for the believer. Shaykh Ibn al-Habib said that in death, for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span>, there is a thousand-fold repose. He said that as long as you are in this world, there is not a single cell in your body that cannot suffer pain or disease. Once you leave this world, all of that ends. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَهَا</span> in death. He also said on another occasion that death for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span> is like being moved from the third-class compartment to first class — the conductor comes and says, &quot;No, no, you shouldn''t be here. Come up to first class.&quot; And do you say, &quot;No, no, I want to stay in third class&quot;? Of course not. It is like being bumped up to first class on an airplane. You''re not upset. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. For the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span>, death is not a bad thing.</p>
<p>That is also why our mourning period is only three days. I had a professor who taught a course called Death and Dying, and he said that one of the worst things about this country is the mourning industry — that it does people a disservice by prolonging and commercializing grief. Many people go into mourning for years and spend years in therapy trying to recover. All of that is love of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> and attachment to it. The people of Allah, when they accept Allah''s decree — if Allah takes a beloved person, a child, anyone — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَاصٌ</span>. It is painful; the Prophet felt pain and wept. But it was Allah who did it, and we must be pleased with what Allah does. That is why the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span> is able to pass through such trials.</p>
<p>And there is something remarkable about Muslims in this regard. No people — as far as I know, certainly not the Europeans — can face death the way Muslims can. When calamities befall Muslims, you see their true character. When the same calamities befall non-Muslims, many fall apart, some take their own lives. Muslims, because of their strength with Allah and their divine connection, are able to do what others cannot — including face death.</p>
<p>Maurice Bucaille, the French physician, said that one of the things that first drew him toward Islam was watching how Algerians died in France compared to the French. He was profoundly impressed by the way North Africans faced death. Having worked on hospital wards myself where people were dying, I can say: you see people in this culture who simply do not know how to die — they have no idea. But watch a devout Muslim, and you will see someone who has been preparing for death his entire life. When it comes, he is ready.</p>
<p>Shaykh Uthman Bashir Uthman, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَ اللهُ</span>, was a close friend of mine. He was in his early eighties, had grown up in Eritrea, and eventually came to the United States. I told him about Darul Islam and he said we had to go there. We went together — I came along because he couldn''t speak Arabic — and afterward he returned to Medina, where he died shortly after. He told me that he had always known he would die in Medina, because when he was a young man in Eritrea, his shaykh had told him: &quot;There will be great <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِتْنَةٌ</span> in this country. The communists will take over and you will have to flee. But Allah, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, will open a place for you in Medina. You will live there, die there, and be buried in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَقِيعٌ</span>.&quot; I heard that from his own lips.</p>
<p>This is what they call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَشْف</span> — something that happens for certain people of Allah. It is not revelation; rather, Allah grants them a kind of opening through which they perceive things and can sometimes convey them to others. At the time, he had always assumed his shaykh was simply saying kind words. But sure enough, the communists took over. He was a wealthy businessman and had to flee. A place was opened for him in Medina; he received citizenship there and lived his remaining years traveling in and out. He told me, &quot;I truly feel, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, that I will die in Medina.&quot;</p>
<p>When the time came, they had him in a hospital in Jeddah. He had cancer. He was very weak, hooked up to equipment, barely able to move. Then one day he told his son, &quot;We have to go to Medina today.&quot; The doctor said he absolutely could not leave. He said, &quot;I am leaving today. My son will drive me.&quot; The doctor was adamant — he was too ill, he needed to stay. He said no. They took him that night, drove all the way to Medina. He arrived, spent the entire night saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. Just before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>, he told his son a few things to convey to certain people, then said the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَة</span> and died. He was buried in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَقِيعٌ</span>, in the old section next to Imam Malik and Nafi, in a spot where people are no longer usually buried — but when they arrived, they found a grave already dug, and they placed him in it. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَازَة</span> prayer was held on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُمُعَة</span> at the Prophet''s Mosque <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, and he was the only person that day with a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَازَة</span> there.</p>
<p>I tell that story because it is an example of how Muslims who know how to die, die.</p>
<p>There is also a story — and Allah knows its full truth — told about Farid al-Din al-Attar, who was a perfumer. The account of how he made his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> goes like this: a man once came begging for alms, and al-Attar told him to go get a job. The man asked what he did for a living. Al-Attar said he sold perfumes. The man replied, &quot;The most important thing you will ever do is die. And I know how to do that.&quot; Then he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللهُ، أَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> — and died. Whether or not the story is literally true, there is a truth within it. There really are people who know how to die. When the moment comes, they know exactly what to do. And there are others who do not — when the time comes, they fall into complete confusion.</p>
<p>This is one of the blessings of remembering death often. Our scholars, following the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> based on his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> on the remembrance of death, have also recommended passing through the states of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> in one''s mind: visualizing the moment of dying, being washed, being wrapped, being placed in the grave, having the earth cover you, being questioned by the angels, the resurrection, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِيزَان</span>. To reflect on those stages as a spiritual practice. It is a deeply sobering exercise — one that can rouse you from the sleep we are all walking through in this life.</p>
<p>The author then adds: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَّا إِذَا قَلَاهُ لَا لِذَاتِهِ وَلَا لَلْإِنْسَرَامِ عَنْ لَذَّاتِهِ بَالْخَوْفَ قَطْعِهِ عَنْ إِسْتِعْدَادِهِ بِطَاعَةِ اللَّهِ إِلَىٰ مَعَادِهِ</span> — if someone dislikes death not merely because it is death, and not because it will sever him from the pleasures of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, but out of fear that he has not had enough time to prepare for what lies ahead, then that is a legitimate reason. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> encouraged people to desire a long life on that basis: if you are a person who has done wrong, you have time to make amends; if you are a person doing good, you have more time to increase your good deeds and strengthen your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحِيفَة</span> — your scroll — for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ</span>. He mentioned that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طُولُ الْأَمَلِ</span>, desiring a long life, is a good thing when one is engaged in righteous deeds.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> also said to Aisha <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهَا</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رضي الله عنه من أحب الله أحب لقاءه</span> — whoever loves Allah loves to meet Allah. She responded, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يا رسول الله الموت نكره الموت</span> — O Messenger of Allah, we dislike death. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> indicated that this was natural — but said that what truly mattered was the dislike of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> itself, of meeting Allah.</p>
<p>The point is that disliking death is obviously natural. Allah has placed it in our bodies at a cellular level. That is why, when life is threatened, you enter a heightened state of functioning and do things not consciously chosen in order to protect yourself or others. Allah has placed that in us and it is a good thing. That is not the dislike being addressed here. The Quran describes Ibrahim <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>: when he offered the angels food and they did not eat, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَوْجَسَ مِنْهُمْ خِيفَةًۭ</span> — he felt a twinge of fear, a natural human response. That kind of instinctive fear is not what is being criticized.</p>
<p>Then the author says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوْ فَوَّضَ الْأَمْرَ إِلَى مَوْلَاهُ فَمَا يَشَاءُ أَرْضَهُ أَوَبْقَاهُ</span> — or, if he fully entrusts his affair to his Master, and whatever Allah wills — whether to take him or to grant him more time — contents him. In other words, you are simply at peace with whatever Allah chooses to do with you. Both of these stances are good stances.</p>
<p>That is why the author says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَذَانِ مَمْدُوحَانِ مَحْمُودَانِ وَالْكُرْهُ لَا يُبَعِّضُ مِنْ كَدَّانِ</span> — both attitudes toward death are commendable and praiseworthy. A dislike of death in no way distances you from its proximity. Even if you dislike it, that dislike changes nothing — it will not push death any further away. It is far better, for the sake of your own inner state, to accept it.</p>
<p>Then the author says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَاكِرُهُ يُكْرَمُ بِالْقَنَاعَةٌِ وَبِنَشَاطِ قَلْبِهِ لِلطَّاعَةِ</span> — the one who remembers death often is honored with certain qualities, among them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَنَاعَةٌ</span>. Because when you genuinely believe you could die at any moment, there is a natural inclination to be present with what you have rather than perpetually deferring enjoyment to some imagined future when you''ll &quot;finally get it together&quot; — which, as they say, never actually happens.</p>
<p>Contentment is vital. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القَنَاعَةٌُ كَنزٌ لَا يَفْنَى</span> — contentment is a treasure that is never exhausted. And he prayed: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ ارْزُقْ آلَ مُحَمَّدٍ الْقَنَاعَةٌَ وَالْكَفَافَ</span> — O Allah, grant the family of Muhammad contentment and sufficiency. Just give them enough to live on and make them content with it.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُنُوعٌ</span> — the person in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَنَاعَةٌ</span> — is the only truly wealthy person. A poor man who is content is in reality a rich man. A rich man who is discontented is in reality a poor man. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَقْرٌ</span> is want — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَنَاعَةٌ</span> is the absence of want. If you have no want, you are wealthy, because wealth is defined by the absence of need. If you have no need, you are rich. That is why the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغِنَى غِنَى النَّفْسِ</span> — real wealth is the wealth of the soul. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْسَ الْغِنَى عَنْ كَثْرَةِ الْعَرَضِ</span> — it is not having an abundance of things. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِنَّمَا الْغِنَى غِنَى النَّفْسِ</span> — real wealth is the contentment of the soul.</p>
<p>And so when you remember death, Allah grants you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَنَاعَ</span> — contentment — as one of its blessings. This is all the more significant in an age when so many false needs and wants are being constantly planted in people''s hearts. That is precisely what media does: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَغْرِسُ</span> — it sows the seeds of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span>, of desire, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَةٌ</span>, embedding all of these things deep within.</p>
<p>He also says that remembering death gives you energy — it directs the heart''s activity toward obedience. Thinking about death makes you want to be more obedient to Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, producing a kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَشَاط</span>, an energized readiness to do good deeds. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَبِبِدَارِي تَوْبَةٌٍ وَيُبْتَرَى</span>.</p>
<p>Beyond that, remembering death moves you to hurry toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span>. If a slip occurs and you do something wrong, the awareness of death prompts you to make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span> quickly — to rectify the wrong. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span> corrects bad actions, and that is itself a gift that comes from remembering death. When you do something wrong and then reflect, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, I am accountable for this — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْتَغْفِرُ اللَّهَ</span> — that awareness drives you to seek Allah''s forgiveness <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>The opposite holds equally true. The one who forgets death receives the three opposites of these gifts. He will always want more and will never attain <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَنَاعَةٌ</span>. He will <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُرْسِلُ جَوَارِحَهُ فِي المَعْصِيَة</span>. — give free rein to his limbs in disobedience — because death and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِسَابٌ</span> are not on his mind. And when he does wrong, he feels no remorse, because he is not concerned with what awaits him in the Akhirah.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'antipathy toward death';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next disease is one of the most important in the entire book, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكَر</span> — gratitude — is truly essential to the deen of Islam. One of the meanings of the Arabic word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَافِرٌ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْرَانُ النِّعْمَةِ</span>: the denial or concealment of blessing. This is precisely why a Muslim can fall into a form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَفَّرَ</span>, for there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْرٌ دُونَ الْكُفْرِ</span> — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَفَّرَ</span> that falls short of leaving Islam altogether. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> warned women, for instance, against <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْرَانُ الْمُعَاشَرَةِ</span> — being ungrateful for the companionship of marriage, failing to recognize the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> of a husband who provides protection and care.</p>
<p>The next disease, then, is called oblivion to blessings. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِن عُيُوبِ النَّفْسِ نِسْيَانُ النِّعَم</span> — and the root of it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَةٌ</span>, heedlessness toward the blessings one has been given. The Quran states: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا بِكُم مِّن نِّعْمَةٍۢ فَمِنَ ٱللَّهِ</span> — every blessing you possess is from Allah. People often say, count your blessings — but those who actually do so are rare. And Allah Himself declares: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِن تَعُدُّوا۟ نِعْمَةَ ٱللَّهِ لَا تُحْصُوهَآ</span> — if you try to enumerate the blessings of Allah, you will never encompass them.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 208">page 208</div>
<p>In light of this, he says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَبِدَوَامِ ذِكْرِهَا</span> — keep this verse ever before you: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا بِكُم مِّن نِّعْمَةٍۢ فَمِنَ ٱللَّهِ</span>. The Quran opens with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, and some of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفَسِّرُونَ</span> explain that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰنُ</span> refers to the giver of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَلَائِلَ النِّعَمِ</span> — the great, obvious blessings — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُنْعِمِ بِجَلَائِلِ النِّعَمِ</span> — while <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحِيمِ</span> refers to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُنْعِمِ بِدَقَائِقِ النِّعَمِ</span> — the giver of subtle, hidden blessings.</p>
<p>The blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرَّحْمَٰنُ</span> are easy to recognize: health, for instance, is plain enough. Tell someone, &quot;Thank God you''re healthy,&quot; and they immediately agree — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحمد لله على الصحة والعافية الحمد لله على الإيمان</span>. But then there are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَقَائِقُ النِّعَمِ</span> — the subtle, intricate blessings. Consider the eye: it is constantly lubricated. Some people must use artificial drops because they suffer from dry eye, and when the eyes are dry, every blink — which normally cleans and protects the eye — becomes painful. Those eyelashes, that rapid blinking reflex that keeps even a stray hair from causing harm, the sockets that set the eye slightly back within the skull to shield it from blunt injury — all of these are hidden blessings most people never consider. People who cannot produce tears must use drops to relieve the pain. People who cannot close their eyelids, or who lose their eyelids entirely, exist — and only then does one grasp what had been taken for granted.</p>
<p>Or consider the eye''s capacity for color — the full range of the visible world it opens to you. Then consider balance: people who sustain damage to the eighth cranial nerve in the ear begin to lose their balance and cannot remain upright. Balance itself is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> from Allah. The way you eat — the digestive juices working silently within you, breaking down food and separating its components — these are hidden <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعَمْ</span> you are entirely unaware of. The cerebellum, and the whole unconscious operation of the brain. Breathing: you do not have to remember to breathe. These are all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعَمْ</span> Allah has granted you, and most of the time you are not even conscious of them.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 209">page 209</div>
<p>Take even the thumb. Other animals lack the opposable thumb. Allah says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَدْ كَرَّمْنَا بَنِي آدَمَ</span> — We have ennobled the children of Adam — and some scholars say one dimension of that ennobling was the thumb. Tellingly, the Arabic word for thumb is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْهَام</span>, from the same root as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبْهَم</span> — something enigmatic, something strange — because no other creature has it. The thumb is a tremendous <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> from Allah. Conquerors used to cut off their enemies'' thumbs so they could never fight again — lose your thumb, and you lose most of your capability.</p>
<p>Then consider all the pleasures Allah has given you. Taste buds are a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>. Allah could have made your food like rocks and your water brackish. He says exactly that in the Quran — He could have made your water unpleasant to drink and you would still have needed it. But He made it sweet. He could have made all food taste the same, yet He gave lemons to those who love sour, and honey to those who love sweet, and then gave you the taste buds capable of savoring every one of these different things.</p>
<p>All of this points to the importance of reflecting on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعَمْ</span>. Imam al-Ghazali says: look at your food. Allah says that in the Quran — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلْيَنظُرِ ٱلْإِنسَنُ إِلَىٰ طَعَامِهِۦٓ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّا صَبَبْنَا الْمَاءَ صَبًّا ثُمَّ شَقَقْنَا الْأَرْضَ شَقًّا</span> — We poured down rain, then split the earth open. You can see it happen: when the rain reaches the seed, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اخْتَلَطَ بِهِ نَبَاتُ الْأَرْضِ</span> — it mingles with what is in the earth, and then the earth breaks open. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَالِقُ ٱلْحَبِّ وَٱلنَّوَىٰ</span> — Allah is the Splitter; He causes the earth to burst forth. So Allah says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلْيَنظُرِ ٱلْإِنسَنُ إِلَىٰ طَعَامِهِۦٓ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّا صَبَبْنَا الْمَاءَ صَبًّا شَقَقْنَا الْأَرْضَ شَقًّا فَأَنبَتْنَا فِيهَا حَبًّا وَعِنَبًا وَقَبًّا وَزَيْتُونًا</span> — the grapes, the olives, the dates, the grain, the fodder for animals from which we benefit in turn. And there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبًّا</span> in there too — the pasture that animals eat, from which we benefit indirectly: the animals consume it, and we eat the meat, receiving that nourishment at one remove. Allah is telling us: look at these things, reflect on them, and recognize that all of it is from Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. The more you do that, the less inclined you will be to forget the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> of Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, and the more you will honor it.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 210">page 210</div>
<p>The importance of this cannot be overstated. He says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَذِكْرَ الْآيَاتِ الْمُرْجِفَةِ غَيْرِهَا</span> — keep before you the other verses that should put fear in the heart. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَا يُغَيِّرُ مَا بِقَوْمٍ حَتَّىٰ يُغَيِّرُوا۟ مَا بِأَنفُسِهِمْ</span> — Allah does not change the blessings of a people and turn them into their opposites until the people themselves change, by failing to show gratitude for them. That is how the scholars interpret this verse. Allah gives you a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> and will not take it away until you show ingratitude for it. Allah says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَلِكَ بِأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَمْ يَكُ مُغَيِّرًۭا نِّعْمَةً أَنْعَمَهَا عَلَىٰ قَوْمٍ حَتَّىٰ يُغَيِّرُوا۟ مَا بِأَنفُسِهِمْ</span> — that is because Allah will not change the blessing He has bestowed upon a people until they change themselves through ingratitude.</p>
<p>A poet captured this perfectly: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن كُنتَ فِي نِعْمَةٍ فَارْعَهَا</span> / <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ الْمَعَاصِيَ تُزِيلُ النِّعَمَ</span> — if you are in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>, guard it, for disobedience strips <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> away. Disobedience to Allah costs you your blessings. This is why Waki, when he was old and still remarkably agile, was asked the secret of it, and he replied: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثِرْكَ الْجَوَارِحْ حَفِظْنَاهَا لِلَّهِ فِي صِغَرِنَا فَحَافَظَهَا لَنَا فِي كِبَرِنَا</span> — these are limbs we guarded for Allah''s sake when we were young, so Allah has guarded them for our sake now that we are old. If you protect the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> Allah has given you, that is how you maintain it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَيْدُ النِّعَمِ الشُّكْرُ</span> — the thing that binds <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> to you is gratitude. And Allah has announced: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَىِٕن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ</span> — if you are grateful for My blessings, I will increase you in them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأَذَّنَ رَبُّكُمْ</span> — your Lord has declared it.</p>
<p>So oblivion to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> is a disaster — a genuine disaster. And related to this is what is known as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِدْرَاج</span>: Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> allows someone to continue in disobedience while continuing to increase their blessings, so that the person concludes that Allah must be pleased with them. This is the error the Quran describes: a man given abundance says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّي أَكْرَمَنِي</span> — my Lord has honored me. But when things become tight, he says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّي أَهَانَنِي</span> — my Lord has humiliated me, has demeaned me. People read ease as a sign that Allah loves them, and hardship as a sign that Allah hates them. This is a profound mistake.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 211">page 211</div>
<p>This brings us to an important distinction: what actually is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>? How do we identify one? Consider the following categories. There are things that benefit you both in the short term and the long term — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْمٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُسْنُ الْخُلُقِ</span>, good character, are examples. Something that benefits you in your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> and your akhirah is a pure, unadulterated <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>. Then there are things that harm you in both the short term and the long term — ignorance and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُوءُ الخُلُقِ</span>, bad character, are examples: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَرَرٌ فِي الحَالِ وَالمَآلِ</span>.. Then there are things beneficial in the short term but harmful in the long term — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَوَاتٌ</span> often fall into this category. Overeating, for instance: enjoyable while it is happening, but harmful in the long run — plain and simple. Beneficial short term, harmful long term. And then there is the opposite: things that are difficult in the short term but beneficial in the long term, like restraining the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَوَاتٌ</span>. Stopping before you are full is hard — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَضُرُّكَ فِي الحَالِ</span> — you do not enjoy it in the moment, but the long-term benefit is real.</p>
<p>The ignorant person mistakes the third category — short-term benefit with long-term harm — for a genuine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>, and is drawn to it. This is, at its root, a failure of intelligence. The difference between an intelligent person and an ignorant one is that the intelligent person always looks at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَاقِبَةٌ</span> — the long-term consequence. Children cannot do this naturally. There is a well-known study that illustrates this beautifully: a child is brought into a room with a cookie on the table. The researcher says, &quot;You can have one cookie now, or if you wait, I will give you two when I return.&quot; Consistently, the more intelligent children chose to wait — willing to forgo the short-term reward for the greater long-term gain. That is intelligence. And that is precisely what Islam teaches: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَاقِبَةُ لِلْمُتَّقِينَ</span> — the ultimate outcome belongs to the God-fearing. The disbelievers think in terms of the short term, the present quarter. The Muslims are meant to think long term.</p>
<p>There is a worldly example of this too. The Jews, for instance, have historically been skilled at long-term thinking — at planning for future benefit. This stands in painful contrast to where many Muslims find themselves today. Many Muslims are dangerously short-sighted, which is a sign of a loss of the intelligence we once had — intelligence that came from guidance. Because Allah does not grant us <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> through anything other than Islam. Sayyidina Umar said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَحْنُ قَوْمٌ أَعَزَّنَا اللَّهُ بِالإِسْلَامِ فَإِذَا طَلَبْنَا الْعِزَّةَ فِي غَيْرِهِ أَذَلَّنَا اللَّهُ</span> — we are a people whom Allah gave dignity through Islam, and if we seek dignity from anything other than Islam, Allah will humiliate us. The rules are simply different for Muslims than for others. There are universal <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَنٌ</span> — patterns Allah has embedded in creation for everyone — but Allah will not give Muslims <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> except through Islam. It is impossible otherwise. The disbelievers may enjoy temporary successes through their disbelief and disobedience, but in the long term they must ultimately fail. They have no other option.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 212">page 212</div>
<p>Another point he raises — there are two categories of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُصُولٌُ النِّعَمِ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُرُوعُ النِّعَمِ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُصُولٌ</span> are the roots — the foundational blessings — and they are the most important. They include things like health, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, Islam, safety of body, security in one''s home and community, and general well-being. These are the core <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَعَمْ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُرُوع</span> are the branches — the embellishments that Allah adds on top.</p>
<p>Allah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says of Talut in the Quran: when the Bani Israel objected to his kingship on the grounds that he had no wealth, Allah replied that He had chosen him and given him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَسْطَةًۭ فِى ٱلْعِلْمِ وَٱلْجِسْمِ</span> — an abundance in knowledge and physical stature. This is something Allah uses to embellish certain people with. This is why Sayyidina Umar used to say: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا بَعَثْتُمْ رَسُولًا فَابْعَثُوا حَسَنَ الْوَجْهِ</span> — if you send a messenger, send someone with a good face. The reason is practical wisdom: there is an innate human inclination toward people who are attractive and personable, and Allah has placed that inclination in hearts for a purpose. This is why the Prophets were all, without exception, striking to look at. Leadership has long been associated with height as well — tall stature tends to draw followers, as history repeatedly shows. Napoleon was a conspicuous exception to that pattern, compensating with cleverness where he lacked height. But even Napoleon, for all his brilliance in certain respects, ultimately proved to be deeply short-sighted in the end.</p>
<p>But there is wisdom in all of this — it continues to unfold. I think everyone understands the point, so let''s move on.</p>
<p>A man once said, in the Prophet''s presence, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ إِنِّي أَسْأَلُكَ تَمَامَ النِّعْمَةِ</span>&quot; — &quot;O Allah, I ask You for the completion of blessing.&quot; The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> replied, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَتَدْرِي مَا هُوَ تَمَامُ النِّعْمَةِ؟</span>&quot; — &quot;Do you know what the completion of blessing is?&quot; The man said he did not. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُخُولُ الْجَنَّةِ</span>&quot; — to enter Paradise.</p>
<p>That is the completion of blessing. A good life in this world is a blessing from Allah, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> is the greatest blessing of the worldly life. But the completion of that blessing is to enter Paradise with Allah, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَادْخُلِي فِي عِبَادِي، وَادْخُلِي جَنَّتِي</span>: &quot;Enter among My servants, enter My Paradise.&quot; That is the ultimate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span>.</p>
<p>Islam itself is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span> Allah gave us in this world — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَتْمَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ نِعْمَتِى</span>: &quot;I have completed My blessing upon you&quot; — the blessing of Islam.</p>
<p>There is also something Sheikh Muhammad al-Hajjar shared with me. I met him in Medina, and as we walked together toward his car, right beside <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَابُ السَّلَامِ</span>, he said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ أَصْبَحَ مُعَافًى فِي بَدَنِهِ، آمِنًا فِي سِرْبِهِ، عِنْدَهُ قُوتُ يَوْمِهِ، فَكَأَنَّمَا حِيزَتْ لَهُ الدُّنْيَا بِحَذَافِيرِهَا</span>&quot; — whoever wakes up in good health, safe in what he possesses, and with enough to see him through the day, it is as though the whole world has been handed to him. That is from the Tirmidhi. Reflect on it: if you woke up this morning with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَافِيَة</span> — good health — free from fear, and with enough provision just for today, you effectively have everything in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. That is a blessing every one of us should be grateful for.</p>
<p>Now, there are branches of blessing — what we might call ornamental <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ</span>. A house, for example, is a need. But extra furnishings and comforts beyond what you need are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُرُوعُ النِّعْمَةِ</span> — accessory blessings. You can live without them. And the more blessings you have, the greater the accounting you face. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> once visited a house where there was meat, dates, and cool water, and Allah said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثُمَّ لَتُسْأَلُونَ يَوْمَئِذٍ عَنِ النَّعِيمِ</span> — &quot;Then, on that Day, you will surely be asked about the blessings.&quot; The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَذِهِ هُوَ النَّعِيمُ</span>&quot; — &quot;This is the blessing we will be asked about.&quot;</p>
<p>When some of the Companions heard this, they came to the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> and said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, we have no blessings to be asked about&quot; — they were not eating meat and dates. Jibril, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, then came with a message from Allah: Does he wear sandals? Does he drink cool water? The point is that you can walk without shoes, so having them is a blessing. You can drink water that is not cool, so the coolness itself is a blessing you will be asked about. Follow that thread, and there is no end to it.</p>
<p>Sayyidina Ali said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعْقَلُ النَّاسِ الزُّهَّادُ</span>&quot; — the most intelligent people are those who practice <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span>, doing without. &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَحْمَقُ النَّاسِ الْبَخِيلُ</span>&quot; — and the most foolish person is the miser. Why? Because, as he put it: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُرِمَ مِنْهُ فِي الدُّنْيَا وَعُقِبَ عَلَيْهِ فِي الْآخِرَةِ</span>&quot; — the miser is denied enjoyment of his wealth in this life, and then punished for it in the next. He hoards without enjoying, and the hoard becomes a burden in the hereafter. That is the definition of foolishness.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span>, by contrast, occupies a genuine spiritual station — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span>. And the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> himself embodied a kind of balance in this: by modern standards, he would be considered living well below the poverty line. What he had was so little it is almost unimaginable. Yet he was not impoverished in any meaningful sense — his needs were met, and he worried about nothing. Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said that two months would sometimes pass without smoke rising in the house of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. Someone asked her what they were eating, and she said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَسْوَدَيْنِ</span>&quot; — dates and water.</p>
<p>Someone asked Marad al-Tarhaaj whether a person could be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> while possessing great wealth. He replied: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كان عثمان أزهد الناس وكان عبد الرحمن بن عوف زاهدا</span>&quot; — Uthman was among the most ascetic of people, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf was also a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span>, yet both were enormously wealthy. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> is a condition of the heart, not the pocket. If you have wealth and could lose it all tomorrow and remain the same person — that is a sign of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span>. But if losing it breaks you, and people say, &quot;He used to be wealthy and now look at him&quot; — that means the wealth had taken root in his heart. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> is the one who, if it is there, says &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>,&quot; and if it is gone, says &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.&quot; That is why the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> chose to go hungry one day and be full the next — to ensure he never grew attached to either state. It is all about non-attachment.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 199">page 199</div>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'obliviousness to blessings';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The next disease is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحُزْء</span> — derision, mockery, making fun of people. When Musa, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, commanded the Bani Israel to sacrifice a cow, they responded: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَتَتَّخِذُنَا هُزُوًۭا</span>&quot; — &quot;Are you mocking us?&quot; He replied: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِٱللَّهِ أَنْ أَكُونَ مِنَ ٱلْجَهِلِينَ</span>&quot; — &quot;I seek refuge in Allah from being among the ignorant.&quot; From this exchange, the scholars derived that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِهْزَاءٌ</span> — mockery — is rooted in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَهْل</span>, ignorance. To ridicule people is an act of ignorance. This includes lampooning, caricature, comedic ridicule — any form of targeting someone to make others laugh at their expense.</p>
<p>What the author says about its cure is that it is the same as the cure for arrogance, because mockery is a form of arrogance. To mock someone, you must first look down on them. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>. This is why the Moroccans have a saying — though it traces back to Sayyidina Ali — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا تحقر عبدنا عسى يكون لله وليا</span>&quot; — do not belittle anyone, for he may be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ</span> of Allah.</p>
<p>Even if you see a man drunk and vomiting in the street, you do not know what his seal will be. Imam al-Qurtubi made a striking point on this: when Umar was still bowing to idols in Mecca, he was already beloved to Allah, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, who knew what the seal would be. The Muslims watching Umar at that stage might have mocked him — &quot;What a fool, a polytheist&quot; — but they did not know Umar. They were seeing one moment in a story still unfolding. That is why it is profoundly dangerous to judge someone by where they stand right now, or to mock them for it.</p>
<p>This is why Allah, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, says in the Quran:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لَا يَسْخَرْ قَوْمٌ مِّن قَوْمٍ عَسَىٰ أَن يَكُونُوا خَيْرًا مِّنْهُمْ</span></p>
<p>&quot;O you who believe, let not one people mock another people — perhaps they are better than them.&quot;</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا نِسَآءٌۭ مِّن نِّسَآءٍ عَسَىٰٓ أَن يَكُنَّ خَيْرًۭا مِّنْهُنَّ</span></p>
<p>&quot;And let not women mock other women — perhaps they are better than them.&quot;</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 200">page 200</div>
<p>It is worth recalling here that the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was commanded not to curse the idols of the polytheists:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَسُبُّوا الَّذِينَ يَدْعُونَ مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ فَيَسُبُّوا اللَّهِ عَدْوًا بِغَيْرِ عِلْمٍ</span></p>
<p>&quot;Do not revile those they call upon besides Allah, lest they revile Allah out of ignorance and hostility.&quot;</p>
<p>This is the principle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَدُّ الذَّرَائِعِ</span> — blocking the means to harm. Muslims burning Israeli flags or American flags in public — what does that provoke? They go and burn Qurans, or they stomp on a Saudi flag that bears <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا إله إلا الله محمد رسول الله</span>. You incite them into an act that is not only harmful to them but sacrilegious to us. We do not want people cursing Allah. There is no good reason to do it.</p>
<p>This is also why the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, even in his greatest victories, was never boastful. When he entered Mecca, his head was bowed low — not the posture of a conquering hero, but of a man in total humility before his Lord. That is the truly great leader. The other kind — the triumphant swagger, the chest-thumping — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاهِلِيَّة</span>. That is Abu Jahl. We are meant to be above it, not in a superficial way, but at a deep level.</p>
<p>You can, of course, make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> against the oppressors. The Quran contains supplications against the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَالِمِينَ</span>. But there is a difference between that and public spectacles of contempt. And making <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> for people''s guidance is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَحَبّ</span> — recommended, though not obligatory.</p>
<p>Some scholars even cautioned against cursing &quot;Israel&quot; by name, because it is the name of a prophet — the Prophet Yaqub, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>. Beyond that, many Jews have embraced Islam throughout history and continue to do so. They are human beings, not demons. The Serbs who carried out atrocities are human beings too. Even Wahshi, who killed Hamza — even Hind, who mutilated his body — they both became Muslim. People can make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span>. People change. And those who have committed terrible acts often carry those acts with them for the rest of their lives. More veterans of the Vietnam War have taken their own lives than were killed in the war itself — many people do not know that. They paid, and keep paying. They have hearts. As Allah says:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن يَمْسَسْكُمْ قَرْحٌۭ فَقَدْ مَسَّ ٱلْقَوْمَ قَرْحٌۭ مِّثْلُهُۥ</span></p>
<p>&quot;If you are wounded, they are wounded just as you are.&quot; We all bleed red.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 201">page 201</div>
<p>As for reading the Quran while being among the oppressors it condemns — the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُبَّ قَرِئَنَّ الْقُرَانَ وَالْقَرَانَ يَلْعَنَهُ</span>&quot; — perhaps a man recites the Quran while the Quran is cursing him. Because Allah says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَا إِنَّ لَعْنَةَ اللَّهِ عَلَى الْكَاذِبِينَ</span> — &quot;Verily, the curse of Allah is upon those who lie.&quot; If you are reading the Quran and you are a liar, you are reading a curse upon yourself.</p>
<p>And that brings us to the final point on this disease. The author says its treatment follows the same path as the treatment of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>, which we have already covered. But he adds one more element of self-knowledge: recognize that the purpose of mockery is to humiliate someone else. That is what drives <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِهْزَاءٌ</span>. Yet in doing so, a person humiliates himself before Allah and earns in return only ignorance. Allah says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَنَابَزُوا بِالْأَلْقَابِ</span> — &quot;Do not insult one another with offensive labels&quot; — and the consequence is that evil comes back upon you.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 202">page 202</div>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'derision';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>Now we move to the final and comprehensive section: a treatment plan for the diseases of the heart as a whole. The author addresses the root causes of those diseases, the source of good and bad character, and treatment through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. That will be the last section, and as I mentioned, we have essentially covered the full ground of the preceding material.</p>
<p>One more thing before we close — and I am glad I remembered it. Does anyone know the seven deadly sins? Any former Catholics in the room?</p>
<p>Pride. Anger. Envy. Sloth. Greed. Gluttony. Lust.</p>
<p>Now, of all those seven, which ones did the author cover? Pride — yes. Anger — yes. Envy — yes. Sloth — essentially, in its spiritual dimension. Greed — yes. That is five. But gluttony and lust — did he cover those?</p>
<p>He mentions <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span> in relation to causes and contributing factors, but he does not treat it as a standalone disease. And that is deliberate. Both gluttony and lust are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِطْرَة</span> inclinations — built into human nature by design. This is why even in the Catholic tradition they were placed in a different category; the other five were considered graver spiritual failings than these two.</p>
<p>Imam al-Ghazali addresses gluttony and lust in their own dedicated book — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَسْرُ الشَّهْوَتَيْنِ</span>, &quot;Breaking the Two Desires.&quot; Part of that work has been translated beautifully by Abd al-Hakim Winter — T.J. Winter — with excellent notes, and I would strongly recommend it as a companion to what we have been studying here.</p>
<p>The connection between the two is important: gluttony tends to fuel lust. Feed the appetite excessively, and the other desire grows alongside it. The link is not absolute — people who fast rigorously can still struggle with lust — but the general pattern holds. This is why, when the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> spoke to young men who could not afford to marry, he said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَعَلَيْكُمْ بِالصِّيَامِ</span>&quot; — &quot;Then you must fast&quot; — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَإِنَّهُ لَكُمْ وِجَاءٌ</span>&quot; — &quot;for it will act as a shield.&quot; The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِجَاءٌ</span> was a small pouch placed on a goat to prevent it from impregnating others — a form of restraint. Fasting functions the same way: it dampens and subdues that desire.</p>
<p>Both hunger and sexual desire are part of human nature. They are not meant to be eliminated — you do not want to eradicate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهْوَة</span> any more than you want to eradicate <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهِيَّة</span>. They share the same Arabic root: desire for food and desire for intimacy. The goal is not suppression but mastery — balance, not obliteration. And the book is worth reading for anyone who wants to work through these two more deeply.</p>
<p>I mention all this because, looking at the seven deadly sins, the author covers five of them in considerable depth. Those two he leaves largely untouched here, though they are not absent from his broader work. In any case — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> — we are approaching closure on the diseases of the heart. What remains now is the comprehensive treatment plan: the general remedies, the root causes of corrupt character, the sources of virtue and vice, and the central role of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرٌ</span> in healing the heart.</p>
<p>So he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقَبُّوا أَمْرَاضِ الْقُلُوبِ الْجَامِعُ فَهُوَ نَهْيُ النَّفْسِ عَمَّا تَتَّبِعُ</span> — that the comprehensive and general treatment plan for the diseases of the heart is to prohibit the self from its own desires, coupled with — and then he gives us: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالصَّغَبُ وَالسَّهْرُ اللَّيَالِ وَالصَّمْتُ وَالفِكْرَةُ وَهُوَ خَالِي</span> — hunger, nighttime vigil, silence, and meditation in private.</p>
<p>He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللَّهُ</span>, that in order to treat the self — the heart itself — you must prohibit the self from what it desires. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا يؤمن أحدكم حتى يكون هواه تبعاً لما يأتوا به</span> — that none of you truly believes until his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span>, his caprice or desire, conforms to what I have brought.</p>
<p>The way you purify the heart, then, is this: all those diseases he just enumerated are forbidden — they run contrary to the guidance <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and His Messenger have given us. So the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ ﷺ</span> tells us that faith is incomplete, that the heart is not sound, until your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span> accords with what he brought — which is guidance. And the way to achieve that is by withholding the self from precisely those things that conflict with guidance, until you no longer desire them.</p>
<p>This is the meaning of the verse: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَفْسُ كَطِفْرِي إِن تَهْمِلْهُ شَبَّعَ لَحُبَّ الرَّضَاعِ وَإِن تَفْتَمْهُ يَنْفَطِمِي</span> — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> is like an infant: if you neglect it, it grows up craving to suckle; but if you wean it, the craving fades. Think about it — there are people in this culture, like followers of La Leche League, who breastfeed until the child is four or five years old. The child just keeps wanting it, and they keep going. But if you wean a child at two years, or one year, or six months — whatever age you choose — the desire passes. The Sharia actually prefers weaning at two years, and there is wisdom in that.</p>
<p>There was a study done in Israel — this is a digression — that performed a statistical analysis comparing breastfed children with children who were not breastfed. Correlations in studies like that are always the tricky part, but the finding was an increase in IQ of three points for every month of breastfeeding. There are obviously many variables in a study of that kind, but it is interesting, because the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكُمْ وَالْحَمْقَىٰ فَإِنَّهَا تُغَذِّي</span> — beware of the wet nurse who lacks intelligence, for she is nourishing the brain. Traditionally, people sought out women of sound mind and good character to serve as wet nurses. And Halima — Halima Sadiyah, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُ</span> — is a remarkable example of this. Read her <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>. If you do not know Arabic, read it in translation. In every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> collection that includes it, the scholars have to annotate nearly every word because her Arabic is so elevated. She was extraordinarily eloquent. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهَا السَّلَامُ</span> — she is the nursing mother of the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ ﷺ</span>.</p>
<p>This is what he is pointing us toward. And it is the same teaching found in the Quran: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَمَّا مَنْ خَافَ مَقَامَ رَبِّهِ وَنَهَى النَّفْسَ عَنِ الْهَوَى فَإِنَّ الْجَنَّةَ هِيَ الْمَأْوَى</span> — the one who fears standing before his Lord, and then actually restrains the self from what it desires — for such a person, Paradise is the abode.</p>
<p>So this is the work of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَهَّدُ قَمْعِ النَّفْسِ</span> — literally suppressing the desires of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>. It is self-discipline. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ دَانَ نَفْسَهُ</span> — you must discipline the soul. Discipline the soul and you will find happiness. Fail to discipline it and you will be miserable. Look at all the self-help literature in this culture — it is overwhelmingly about people who cannot control themselves.</p>
<p>This is precisely why Islam gives us prayer: to train us in discipline over our time and our days. It gives us ritual washing: to discipline us in cleanliness. It gives us fasting — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَللَّهُمَّا صِرِعَتَ سَيْنِ مُحَمَّدٍ</span> — which is entirely about learning discipline: disciplining the tongue, the stomach, the genitals. Everything Islam brings relates, at its core, to learning how to discipline yourself.</p>
<p>Because if you have no discipline over time, you will not pray on time. Especially in this culture, where there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذًى</span> ringing out and no mosques on every corner, you truly have to stay vigilant. Whenever you travel to a Muslim-majority country from here, it is striking how effortless prayer becomes. The difference is enormous. Here, maintaining that discipline requires active effort — and that effort itself is part of the training.</p>
<p>So you discipline the soul, discipline the tongue. You learn to catch yourself when you feel the urge to backbite — stopping and saying inwardly, I don''t want to do this. You have to learn to do that. It is a process. But if you do it enough, it reaches a point where you simply are not doing it anymore. And that is where the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوًى</span> finally comes into alignment with what the Messenger brought. That is what he is telling us.</p>
<p>Now he gives us the means. What are the core things the soul craves? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَقْلٌ</span> — hunger. The stomach. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَسْرُ الشَّهْوَتَيْنِ</span> — control the stomach and you gain control over the genitals as well. The stomach is the primary driving impulse. If you can learn to master your eating, that is why one of the early masters, as quoted by Imam al-Qushayri, said: for me to lift my hand from the plate while I am still hungry is better than spending an entire night in prayer. Because if a person can discipline that one thing, everything else becomes easier.</p>
<p>People get very agitated when food is delayed. They blame it on hypoglycemia — and there are people who genuinely have that condition, I do not want to dismiss that — but there are also people who simply become angry if food is not on time. You see it in restaurants; waitstaff deal with it constantly. Where is my food? And that is America: fast food, drive-throughs, get it now. The infantile impulse — because the very first thing a newborn does is cry for food. The mother hears the cry and her milk flows. The baby gets the breast and immediately relaxes into a state of complete contentment. That is the infantile pattern, and it is what we must all work to discipline within ourselves: the ability to do without food, to not become grumpy over it, to recognize that missing a single meal will not kill you. People say, &quot;I''m starving.&quot; You are not starving. There are people in the world who are genuinely starving — truly dropping from hunger — but missing a meal is not that.</p>
<p>This is why the early shuyukh focused on hunger above almost everything else in their teaching. This does not mean becoming anorexic. The point is simply: learn discipline. Two meals a day is enough — I can say that with confidence. Morning and late afternoon. If you want a third, make it something light in the evening. But two genuine meals are sufficient.</p>
<p>There is a man mentioned — he was from Yemen, from the tribe of Bani Hanifa, actually of the Hanafi persuasion, and I cannot recall his name at this moment — who came to the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, and the story is in al-Bukhari. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> gave him a bowl of milk. He drank it. The Prophet asked if he wanted more. He said yes. The Prophet kept giving, bowl after bowl, until the man had drunk seven bowls. The next day he embraced Islam, and that evening when he returned, he could only manage one bowl. The Companions who had watched him drink seven the night before were astonished. They asked the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> about it. He said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْمُؤْمِنُ يَأْكُلُ فِي مِعًى وَاحِدٍ وَالْكَافِرُ يَأْكُلُ فِي سَبْعَةِ أَمْعَاءٍ</span> — the disbeliever eats from seven intestines, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْمُؤْمِنُ يَأْكُرُوا۟ مِنْ مَعِينٍ وَاحِدٍ</span> — but the believer eats from one. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> actually reduces the appetite.</p>
<p>Now, people today eat far more than people have eaten at virtually any other point in human history. There is a massive surplus of food, driven by agribusiness. Meat consumption in particular has risen dramatically. Ask your grandparents — if they are still alive — how often they ate meat. If they were not from a wealthy family, the answer in most Muslim-majority countries was probably once a week, if they lived in a city; far less if they lived in a village. Many Yemenis, for instance, ate meat only on Eid. This is within living memory.</p>
<p>And in this country too, people who are older remember a time when you did not have snacks available around the clock. Children did not have constant access to food between meals. You ate when it was mealtime, and that was that. There were no convenience stores on every block selling cold drinks and packaged food at any hour. All of this is part of a modern culture that in this regard is making people behave more like animals — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> mentions in the Quran that such people are like cattle, simply grazing. Some of those co-op-style bulk food stores actually post signs near the open bins that say &quot;No Grazing.&quot; That sign says something. Sociologists call it &quot;food contact,&quot; and research suggests the average American has around twenty food contacts per day — twenty separate occasions during the day when food is consumed or handled. Some nutritionists even recommend this pattern of small, frequent eating as healthier, but if you look at traditional cultures worldwide, people ate at set times and did not eat between meals. The absence of refrigeration alone enforced much of that. A Bedouin from Mauritania can go days on very little food, because that kind of discipline has been built into his life.</p>
<p>As for the American military: they now have self-heating field rations — you pour in water, a chemical reaction activates, and the food is hot. They have Indian curries for Sikh soldiers, and various options for different ethnic groups, with special requests accommodated. All of this erodes fortitude. And for every one soldier in the field, seventeen support personnel are required. The plans at the time called for every soldier to eventually carry a personal PC. It is difficult to imagine an army of that kind maintaining its edge — but that is a whole other discussion.</p>
<p>The point is that fasting instills discipline. Muslims, from the practice of Ramadan alone, have the experience of going extended periods without food — they are used to it. The tragedy is that Ramadan itself has been compromised: Muslims now often eat more during Ramadan than in any other month. I have seen it — the night is not given to worship but to feasting, massive spreads lasting until dawn. Walimas and dinner invitations have become extravagant productions, to the point where a person feels unable to invite anyone over without preparing something elaborate. The Prophets of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, peace be upon them, warned against all of this. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> would sometimes host guests with dates and water. You do not have to go that far, but when you are a guest, be content with whatever is served and do not take it as an insult if the spread is modest. If someone keeps things simple, assume the best — perhaps they are following the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُسْنُ الظَّنِّ</span>, a good opinion of people, is always the wiser path. You never lose by assuming the best.</p>
<p>So, regarding hunger — there is much more that could be said, but practically speaking: if food is something you struggle to discipline yourself around, begin by reducing your portions. Try to eat with others rather than alone — eating alone leads to eating more, and there is less <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَةٌ</span> in solitary meals. The more people sharing a plate, the more <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَةٌ</span> descends, and the less food each person needs. Sheikh Khadir, when he returned to Mauritania, was once asked what had struck him most about life there. Among the things that shocked him most, he said, was that everyone ate from separate plates. When he described this to people back home, they were genuinely baffled — they simply could not imagine it. He said he embarrassed himself repeatedly, because a large communal plate would be set down and he would start eating from it, not realizing that people were waiting to serve themselves small portions onto their own dishes.</p>
<p>So eat together. There is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَةٌ</span> in it. Aim for two meals a day if you can manage it. If you cannot, at the very least avoid heavy meals — eat what is enough, and stop. And keep in mind: the higher the quality of the food, the less of it you need. Part of the reason people here eat such enormous quantities is that the food is nutritionally poor. I am confident that in a typical American restaurant, the single serving placed in front of one person would feed ten people in West Africa. Ten people. And most diners here clean their plates.</p>
<p>So, you know, if the food is good, you''ll need less of it. If the food is poor — empty calories, fast food, that sort of thing — you''ll eat more and still feel unsatisfied. By good food and bad food I mean genuinely nutritious food: wholesome grains, vegetables, things like that. I wouldn''t set foot in a fast food restaurant myself. Even if it''s a McFish burger or whatever it is, I''d rather skip it. I might even say the cardboard box it came in is more nutritious. Throw the hamburger out — at least cardboard has roughage.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ وَفِّقْنَا</span>. If you want to bring the heart to life, give some time to the night. They say even two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> is enough. Sheikh Muhammad Ali ibn Malik said: never abandon <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَام</span>, even if it is only two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span>.</p>
<p>Sheikh Abdul Khadim, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَفِظَهُ اللهُ</span>, adds that in this age, when so many people find these practices difficult, it is far worse to do it sporadically than to do it modestly but consistently. One night you get up and pray ten <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> with long <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُوَرٌ</span> and spend <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>f an hour or more — and then the next night nothing, and the night after that nothing. It is far better to get up just five minutes before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> and pray two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span>, every single night without fail. Make it a habit. Give some prayer to the night, do it consistently, and you will see results. A little here and a little there produces nothing. Ahmad Zarruq compared it to drilling a hole — a little bit here, a little bit there, and you will never strike water.</p>
<p>So give some portion to prayer. A few things about the prayer itself.</p>
<p>When the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> arrived in Medina, Abdullah ibn Salam — may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> be pleased with him, the rabbi who embraced Islam — narrated that the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> gave a khutbah after descending upon Quba. He said: O people, spread peace — greet one another with peace. Feed people. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَصَلُّوا بِاللَّيْلِ وَالنَّاسُ النِّيَّامِ</span> And pray in the night when others are sleeping. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدْخُلَ الْجَنَّةَ بِالسَّلَامِ</span> You will enter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> without any difficulty.</p>
<p>Just look at how straightforward that is. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفْشُوا السَّلَامَ</span> — spread peace. Do not be a cause of division. Be someone who unifies, who brings people together. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَطْعِمُوا الطَّعَامَ</span> — feed people. Be generous. When you feed someone, you are showing you care about them. So: spread peace, be concerned about others, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَصَلُّوا بِاللَّيْلِ وَالنَّاسُ النِّيَّامِ</span> — and then tend to your own soul. Correct yourself. If you do those things, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدْخُلُواا الْجَنَّةَ بِالسَّلَامِ</span> — and that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدْخُلُوا</span> is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَوَابُ شَرْطٍ</span>: if you do those things, that is the condition. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَاصٌ</span>. That is all. If every one of us took that single <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> and made it our practice, that is a guarantee from the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ ﷺ</span> that we will enter Paradise.</p>
<p>They said of one man who did some questionable things during the day but prayed at night: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَتَنْهَاهُ</span> — it will eventually stop him, if he keeps at it. On the other hand, there is another <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, quite frightening, about a woman who prayed at night and gave <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span> but was cruel to her neighbors. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَانَتْ تُؤْذِي جِيرَانَهَا</span> — she used to treat her neighbors badly. And the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِيَ فِي النَّارِ</span>. She is in the Fire. And it is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحِيحٌ حَدِيث</span>. Then of another person, they said: he does not pray at night, he does not give <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span>, he does not do these other things — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَيُحْسِنُوا إِلَى جِيرَانِ</span> — but he is good to his neighbors. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيَدْخُلَ الْجَنَّةَ</span>. He will enter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>.</p>
<p>So you can pray all night if you like — but without good character, it counts for very little. Keep things in perspective. Tahajjud is immensely important, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> has encouraged it for all believers. He says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَنِ اللَّيْلِ فَتَهَجَّدْ بِهِ نَافِرَةً لَكَ عَسَىٰ أَن يَبَعَثَكَ رَبُّكَ مَقَامًا مَحْمُودًا</span> — And from the night, spend a portion of it in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهَجُّد</span> as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَافِلَة</span>, a voluntary act. Perhaps your Lord will raise you to a praiseworthy station. So this practice helps purify the heart.</p>
<p>And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says of the believers: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِنُون تَتَجَافَتْ جُنُوبُهُمْ عَنِ الْمَضَاجِعِ</span> — they lift their sides from their beds at night when they would far rather sleep. Sleep is something the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> loves. We all love sleep. And that is precisely why restraining the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> from sleep — in moderation — is spiritually powerful. Not all night. You do not want to push yourself into a sleepless psychotic state. You do not deprive yourself entirely. Keep it within reasonable bounds.</p>
<p>In the same way, when they speak of hunger as a discipline, they do not mean the emaciated ascetic whose spine is visible — the image people call the starving Buddha, that famous statue. That is not our tradition. The point is to learn to govern the food-driven <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>. The same applies to night prayers: you do not spend the entire night in prayer. There was a taifa among the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> who spent a great deal of the night in prayer — not all of it, but a great deal. According to al-Raghib al-Isfahani, a taifa is fewer than a thousand, so it was a number of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> who sustained this, though not all of them.</p>
<p>Consider the well-known <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> about the man the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said was a man of Paradise. One of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> followed him for several days and observed nothing remarkable. So he asked him directly: what are you doing? The man replied: all I know is that every night before I sleep, I make sure there is no rancor in my heart toward any Muslim. He was not praying at night, was not fasting, was not doing anything outwardly exceptional. Not all the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> prayed through the night.</p>
<p>But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says of a group among them: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كانوا قليلا من الليل ما يهجعون</span> — in one reading, they would not sleep at night; in another reading, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كانوا قليلا من الليل ما يهجعون</span> — they slept only a small portion of the night. Both readings are valid. The point is that for some among them, this was their particular practice.</p>
<p>For us, I think what is rational and reasonable is a steady, consistent practice — something regular. Shaykh Abdullah al-Haddad advises that given the nature of this age, it is good to recite the shorter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُوَرٌ</span> that carry great reward. I mentioned last night the line: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إذا العشرون من شعبانا ولت فواصل شرب ليلك بالنهار</span> — when your life is drawing toward its end, start doing as much as you can. He actually recommends even just reciting <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ</span> ten times in short <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> to accumulate great reward.</p>
<p>So even short <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> count. If your practice is to pray some portion of the night and you happen to oversleep until just before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>, it is still permissible and acceptable to pray those <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهَجُّد</span> before your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> prayer — as long as you will not miss <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> itself and as long as there is still some darkness before the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْفَار</span> sets in. You can easily pray ten <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ</span> and proper <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طُمَأْنِينَةٌ</span> in seven or eight minutes — they will not be rushed, they will be sound <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span>, and you can complete them in ten minutes or so. A minute per rakaat is quite manageable.</p>
<p>For women who cannot pray at certain times, it is perfectly acceptable to get up in the night and read something beneficial, or to meditate, or to make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>. You can recite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ</span> in every rakaat. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُوَرٌ</span> that carry particular benefit — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا زُلْزِلَتِ ٱلْأَرْضُ زِلْزَالَهَا، قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ، الْمُعَوِّذَتَيْن، قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ الْفَلَقِ، قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ النَّاسِ، لِإِيلَافِ قُرَيْشٍ، أَلْهَاكُمُ التَّكَاثُرُ</span> — those are the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُوَرٌ</span> the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> used frequently. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلتِّينِ وَٱلزَّيْتُونِ</span> is another. He recited <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا زُلْزِلَتِ ٱلْأَرْضُ زِلْزَالَهَا</span> often. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالضُّحَى وَاللَّيْلِ إِذَا سَجَى</span> as well. And it is not a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَةٌ</span> to recite the same surah in every rakaat of a voluntary prayer.</p>
<p>You shouldn''t make it a regular practice, and there is some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافٌ</span> about this. The Shafiis, I believe, would permit it, while the Malikis would consider it a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَةٌ</span>. So be careful — for some imams it may be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَةٌ</span>, for others it may be acceptable. For Imam Malik, it is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَةٌ</span> to recite <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ</span> at every Maghrib prayer, whereas Shaykh Abdullah al-Haddad, in one of his treatises, mentioned that it was a good thing to do, and there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> indicating that one of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> did exactly that. So you have to be a little careful with these matters.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 238">page 238</div>
<p>There is also the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> about Sayyidina Abdullah ibn Umar, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> be pleased with him. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> once walked past him and said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نعم الرجل لو كان يقوم الليل</span> — &quot;What an excellent man — if only he would spend some time at night in prayer.&quot; The scholars have clarified that this is not simply a compliment; the statement contains the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْتَ</span>, a particle of wishing, as in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْتَ لَيْتُهُ كَانَ يَقُومَ اللَّيْل</span>. What the scholars took from this is that a person can still be a good person even without praying at night — like Abdullah ibn Umar — but how much better would he be if he did? The believer who hears that desires perfection. And so, when Abdullah ibn Umar heard those words — and he himself is the narrator of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, which he related to his son — he never abandoned <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامُ اللَّيْلِ</span> again after that.</p>
<p>The scholars also drew from this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> that it is permissible to praise someone who will not be made conceited by that praise. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> knew that Abdullah ibn Umar would not hear those words and think, &quot;I''m already excellent, so I don''t need to pray at night.&quot; Rather, it spurred him on to do more — which is exactly what happened.</p>
<p>Then there is the well-known <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> of Aisha, which is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحِيح حَدِيث</span>, in which she describes how the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> would pray so long at night that his feet would crack and become slightly swollen. What the physicians call venous stasis — when you stand for extended periods, blood can pool in the feet and cause mild edema. This is why people who are on their feet all day, such as nurses, often wear compression stockings to help the blood return upward. It happens to many people.</p>
<p>So Aisha said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, why do you do this when your Lord has forgiven you everything — past and future?&quot; And he replied, &quot;Should I not be a grateful servant?&quot; In other words, that very fact should make me want to show even more gratitude. Worship, then, is a way of expressing gratitude to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. The scholars say this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> shows that one of the greatest ways to give thanks for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is to do some night work — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَهَرُ اللَّيَالِي</span>. And they say: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">من قرب المعالي سهر الليالي</span> — whoever seeks high things stays awake at night.</p>
<p>For students especially, the night is the best time to study. If <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> falls at a particular hour, divide the night from Maghrib onward into six equal parts. Each part, in a twelve-hour night, would be roughly two hours. The last sixth of the night — the final two hours before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> — is the best time for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامُ اللَّيْلِ</span>. If we take, for simplicity, Maghrib at six p.m. and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> at six a.m., then four a.m. to six a.m. would be that optimal period. That said, any prayer after Isha counts as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامُ اللَّيْلِ</span>, and some scholars say even after Maghrib.</p>
<p>For people who find it genuinely difficult to rise in the early morning — those with what the tradition calls a &quot;heavy liver,&quot; meaning a slow constitution — it is perfectly valid to pray some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقَائِق</span> before going to bed. You should do something in the night hours. Interestingly, many people find it easier to wake up around three or four in the morning than right before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>, because the sleep immediately before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> is recognizably the heaviest sleep of the night. This is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span> from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, and it also corresponds to what science tells us: cortisol levels in the brain are at their peak at that time. Ibn Ataillah said the wisdom in this is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> wants you to be able to pull yourself out of sleep precisely when it is most difficult to do so.</p>
<p>As for the number of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقَائِق</span>: eight and ten are both established by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحِيح حَدِيث</span>, and I was told ten is the best. You pray two by two — two, four, six, eight, ten — and it is better done after Isha, unless you are someone who by nature rises every night without fail, in which case you may wait. Sayyidina Abu Bakr, who was very strong in his spiritual practice and certainly prayed the night prayer, used to pray before he went to bed.</p>
<p>What if you are staying up late — do you have to sleep first and then get up? No, you don''t have to. Obviously it is better to sleep and then rise, because the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that was the best way — the way of Dawud, peace be upon him, who would sleep for a portion, rise for a portion, sleep again, and rise again. That is harder on the self than simply staying up, and he maintained that practice just as he maintained his fast of every other day. It is true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهَدَة</span> — real mastery over the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> — because it is genuinely difficult to fall asleep and then get up again.</p>
<p>And never forget — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ</span> — that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> is always nearby. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَانٌ يَعْقِدُ عُقَدًا ثَلَاثَةً</span> — he ties three knots on your head while you sleep, in the unseen world, and whispers, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَامَكَ لَيْلٌ طَوِيلٌ</span> — &quot;You have a long night ahead of you, just relax.&quot; In winter especially, this is all too convincing. Those three knots are heavy. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that if you wake up and recite the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> of waking — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَحْيَانِي بَعْدَ مَا أَمَاتَنِي وَإِلَيْهِ النُّشُورُ</span> — the first knot is loosened. If you get up and make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُضُوء</span>, the second knot is loosened. And if you then pray, the third knot is loosened and you are freed entirely.</p>
<p>The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> was also asked about a man who slept straight through until morning without praying — and the fact that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> asked about it shows this was not their norm. He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَاكَ رَجُلٌ بَالَ الشَّيْطَانُ فِي أُذُنِهِ</span> — &quot;That is a man in whose ears <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> urinated.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. Anyone who does not pray the night prayer has <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> using his ear as a toilet. We should not want that. The way out is simply to do something — get up in the night, pray even a few <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقَائِق</span> for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 243">page 243</div>
<p>Some of the scholars said this should be understood <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقِيقَةً</span> — literally — that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> literally urinates in the ear. And the principle in Islamic jurisprudence is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحقيقة لا تُصرف إلى المجاز إلا لضرورة</span> — the literal meaning is not set aside for a metaphorical one unless there is a necessity to do so. Some scholars found no such necessity and took it literally. Others interpreted it metaphorically, saying it means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> deprives that person of all the good that comes from spending part of the night in worship. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهُ تَعَالَى أَعْلَمُ</span>.</p>
<p>Can you remind us of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَحْيَانِي بَعْدَ مَا أَمَاتَنِي وَإِلَيْهِ النُّشُورُ</span> — Praise be to the One who brought me back to life after He caused me to die, and to Him is my return.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 244">page 244</div>
<p>There is also the practice of reciting the closing verses of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ آلِ عِمْرَانَ</span> upon waking. I spent many nights in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبِّي حَجٌّ</span>s tent, and every single night he would get up — usually four, sometimes five hours before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> — and he would wake you with those verses: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ فِي خَلْقِ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ وَاخْتِلَافِ اللَّيْلِ وَالنَّهَارِ لَآيَاتٍ لِأُولِي الْأَلْبَابِ الَّذِينَ يَذْكُرُونَ اللَّهَ قِيَامًا وَقُعُودًا وَعَلَى جُنُوبِهِمْ وَيَتَفَكَّرُونَ فِي خَلْقِ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ رَبَّنَا مَا خَلَقْتَ هَذَا بَاطِلًا</span> — and I can still hear his voice reciting them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> give us <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span>. It is all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span> from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> — the ability to do these things is itself a gift from Him.</p>
<p>What prevents us from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامُ اللَّيْلِ</span> — what blocks it — is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِيبَة</span>, eating what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, and similar violations. These are what close the door. This is a gift from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, and those who have experienced it and then lost it know that when they had it, it truly felt like a gift, and when it was taken away, it felt like a punishment. So make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span>, ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> to restore it, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> He will.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 245">page 245</div>
<p>The people of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَوْم</span> used to hold themselves to such rigorous <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَاسَبَة</span> that whenever something afflicted them they could trace the cause. One scholar said, &quot;I once said something about someone that I should not have said, and I was deprived of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهَجُّد</span> for forty days because of it.&quot; They could see the direct connection between cause and consequence. Another scholar said that near the end of his life he went bankrupt, and he realized it was the result of something that had happened forty years earlier — he had once said to a man, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا مُفْلِسُ</span>,&quot; meaning &quot;you bankrupt one,&quot; and he understood that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> had given him exactly what he had called that other man. As the saying goes, the good actions of the righteous are the bad actions of those who are near to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> — meaning what is an ordinary slip for most people carries far greater weight for those whose hearts are refined.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 246">page 246</div>
<p>On the question of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span>: do not pray the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span> twice. Witr should be the last prayer of the night — that is the correct practice. It is perfectly valid to pray additional prayers after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span>, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span> should come last. So if you go to bed early, it is better to pray your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span> before you sleep, since <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span> should be completed before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>. If you wake up and forgot to pray it, you can still make it up after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>, but it is better done before. In the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَذْهَبُ الْحَنَفِيُّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاجِب</span>; in the other <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَذَاهِب</span>, it is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّةٌ مُؤَكَّدَةٌ</span> — a strongly emphasized <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِفَاءٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span> should be prayed before you go to bed. Then if you rise during the night, do not repeat the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span>; simply pray <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهَجُّد</span> in pairs — two by two, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَتْنًا مَتْنًا</span>, as the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> says. You do not pray four together; you pray two, then two.</p>
<p>If you are asking whether you can do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهَجُّد</span> after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span> — yes, you can. The preferable practice is to delay the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span> so that it is the last prayer of the night. That is the ideal. But put it this way: if you are as certain as one can reasonably be that you will wake up before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>, then it is better to delay your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span>. If your consistent, reliable practice is to wake up <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>f an hour or an hour before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> and pray — and you are confident in that pattern — then it is better to delay. If, on the other hand, you are not consistent in waking up, then it is better to pray your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span> before you go to sleep.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 247">page 247</div>
<p>And if you do wake up during the night before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>, you pray your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهَجُّد</span> without ending with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span>, since you have already prayed it and there is only one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْرٌ</span> in the night.</p>
<p>— Ingrizi nahi malum hai. That''s all the Urdu I know. There is an Afghani joke along these lines. An American traveled to Afghanistan having heard that the country was home to great spiritual teachers and saints. He wanted to find the greatest of them. So he approached an Afghani man and asked, the way Americans do — assuming everyone speaks English — &quot;Who is the greatest spiritual teacher in Afghanistan?&quot; The man replied, &quot;Nami fahman.&quot; So the American went around asking after Nami Fahman, treating it as a name.</p>
<p>At some point a funeral procession passed by, and he saw a crowd watching solemnly. He went up to one of the Afghans and asked, &quot;Who just died?&quot; The man replied, &quot;Nami fahman.&quot; Nami fahman, of course, means &quot;I don''t understand&quot; in Persian.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 248">page 248</div>
<p>On another point — about taking things easy and not driving yourself into the ground — Shaykh Habib said in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَصِيدَة</span> that you should travel the path with ease toward yourself. You need yourself to complete the journey. Your horse has to carry you to where you are going, so you treat your horse well. Do not wear it out to the point where it cannot carry you any further. He is not saying be lazy — he is saying do not exhaust yourself so completely that you lose the capacity to continue.</p>
<p>The goal is to move along the path with yearning. If you are not too hard on yourself, your self will actually begin to do these things. If you try to stay up the whole night, it is too much for the self. If you try to stay up <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>f the night, it is still too much. The self loses energy and gives up entirely. But if you start small, something shifts — you begin to taste the sweetness of it, you feel the benefit, and you start to want it. The self begins to look forward to it. That is the point he is making.</p>
<p>And then he says: two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقَائِق</span> prayed by a lover are better than a thousand prayed without love. If you pray two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَقَائِق</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَوْق</span> — with genuine longing, with a real desire for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s pleasure — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will love it. But if you are going through the motions thinking, &quot;I have to do this, it is important, I must,&quot; with no love behind it — there is no love there. The action is driven by an idea in your head, not by yo' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'comprehensive treatment for the heart';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'ur heart. The whole point is that all of this should arise from a yearning for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَحَبَّة</span> from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 249">page 249</div>
<p>And then he says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالصَّمْتُ</span> — silence. One of the diseases he addresses is lust for speech — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْكَلَامُ</span> — which is, for most people, a deeply ingrained drive. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ أُوتِيَ صَمْتًا فَقَدْ أُوتِيَ الْحِكْمَةَ</span> — whoever is given silence as part of his nature has been given wisdom. There is wisdom in silence itself, simply in being silent. Even an ignorant person, if he is given the gift of silence, possesses a kind of wisdom — because at the very least, if you are ignorant, silence keeps you from making a fool of yourself.</p>
<p>If you have something to say, speech is better than silence. If you have nothing to say, it is permissible — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبَاحٌ</span> — but there is no benefit in it. And if that permissible speech amounts to casual pleasantries — how are you, where have you been — that is fine.</p>
<p>But if it turns to &quot;did you hear about so-and-so,&quot; it slides toward either <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكْرُوهٌ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. Learning to govern the tongue is a serious discipline.</p>
<p>Imam al-Shafii said that whenever he was in a gathering and felt moved to speak, he would first look inward and ask himself whether he was speaking out of a desire of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> — to prove a point or display his knowledge. He would check whether he was content for someone else to say the very same thing. If he could honestly say yes, he would speak. If not, he would stay silent, as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span> for his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>. He also said that whenever he entered a debate, he would make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> cause the truth to appear on his opponent''s tongue, so that he himself could submit to it. He had no wish to win — he would have preferred to lose, because losing would make him humble. That is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجُلٌ</span>, that is a man.</p>
<p>The Prophet, peace be upon him, was the most reserved of people. When he spoke, he spoke the truth. He was not what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْفَدْ</span>. He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ أَبْعَدَكُمْ مِنِّي وَأَبْعَدَكُمْ إِلَيَّ يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ، الْفَرْفَارِينَ، الْمُتَفَيْهِقِينَ</span> — the most distant of you from me, and the most distant to me on the Day of Judgment, are the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْفَرٍ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُتَفَيْهِقِينَ</span>. The companions recognized the other terms, but did not know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُتَفَيْهِقِينَ</span>, and he explained it as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَدِّر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْفَدْ</span> is someone who speaks incessantly — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْفَرَ، يُفَرْفِرُ</span> — they simply never stop talking. He disliked the chatterbox. If you are teaching or saying something of use, that is another matter entirely. But speech for speech''s sake is a waste.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 243">page 243</div>
<p>Imam Malik, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللَّهُ</span>, said of one of his students: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ الرَّجُلُ، إِلَّا أَنَّهُ يَتَكَلَّمُ كَلَامَ الشَّهْرِ فِي يَوْمٍ وَاحِدٍ</span> — he is a fine man, except that he speaks a whole month''s worth of words in a single day.</p>
<p>Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفِكْرَةُ وَهُوَ خَالٍ</span> — reflection in solitude. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَالِي</span> means to be in a place with no one around, engaged in reflection. In English we call this meditation, and it is a genuine practice — not yogic, but simply sitting and meditating. Within the Islamic tradition there are various forms. One is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التَّفَكُّرُ فِي الْمَوْتِ</span>, or contemplating the states of the hereafter: you sit and walk through the raising from the grave and the scenes of the Day of Judgment. Another is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَكُّر</span> in the divine names: you move through them — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللهُ، يَا رَحْمَٰنُ، يَا رَحِيمُ</span> — and reflect on each attribute. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِكْرَة</span> in solitude benefits the heart.</p>
<p>Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُحْبَةُ الْأَخْيَارِ</span> — keeping company with the righteous. This practice is rooted in a Quranic injunction: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ وَكُونُوا مَعَ الصَّادِقِينَ</span> — O you who believe, have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> and be with the truthful ones. This is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُحْبَة</span> in the Persian tradition: keeping good company with righteous people in order to purify the heart.</p>
<p>Imam Ibn Ataillah says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكَم</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَصْحَبْ مَن لَا يُنْهِضُكَ حَالُهُ وَلَا يَدُلُّكَ عَلَى اللَّهِ مَقَالُهُ</span> — Do not take as a companion someone whose state does not lift you, and whose speech does not direct you toward <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>Sidi Ahmad Zarruq notes in his commentary that this is not referring to spiritual masters — it is referring to the friends you spend ordinary time with. You should seek people who will elevate your state, not drag it down. And when they elevate yours, their own state rises too.</p>
<p>What this means in practice is that such people are in a good state with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> — a state of gratitude. When you are around people who are genuinely grateful, it becomes difficult to complain. They are saying, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّٰهِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, I feel truly grateful, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has given us so much — and you find yourself sitting there unable to say, sorry, I can''t agree, life is hard. They are in a different <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span> from you. So if you are the one in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, do not keep company with the one in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكْوَى</span>. And if you are the one in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَكْوَى</span>, seek out the one in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span>, so that you may become like him. Do not drag him down — let him lift you up. That is the teaching.</p>
<p>And as for their speech directing you to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>: there are people who, if you did complain to them, would gently say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِن تَعُدُّوا۟ نِعْمَةَ ٱللَّهِ لَا تُحْصُوهَآ</span> — if you try to enumerate the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, you cannot count them; that applies to everyone. So perhaps instead of cataloguing your troubles, you might try counting his blessings. They help you shift your focus. And it is remarkable how a person''s entire state can be transformed simply by sitting with someone who offers sincere and good counsel. A heart that was utterly dark and negative can be turned around entirely, because the effect was real and valuable — the person''s perspective changes, and that is guidance.</p>
<p>That is what you want. And on this, Sidi Abu al-Hasan said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَن دَلَّكَ عَلَى الدُّنْيَا فَقَدْ غَشَّكَ</span> — whoever directs you toward this world has cheated you. If someone''s entire counsel to you is about financial planning, acquiring property, securing your future in this life — that is a sign that he has cheated you, because you could die at any moment.</p>
<p>The example I like to use is this: imagine two salesmen come to you. The first says, &quot;I have a house for sale. The only drawback is that it could disappear at any time — I cannot tell you exactly when, maybe fifty years from now, but it will be gone within seventy at the most, and it could vanish at any point before that.&quot; The second says, &quot;I have another house. It will last forever and is guaranteed — but it is more expensive.&quot; Which one do you buy? Seriously — which one? Any person of sound intellect is going to buy the second one.</p>
<p>That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>. And you purchase it with your soul. The other house, you also purchase with your soul — but you buy it by destroying the soul, not by preserving it.</p>
<p>So: keeping good company. Then Sidi Abu al-Hasan also said that the one who tells you to rush and pile up deeds has merely exhausted you — if you believe you will reach <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span> through your deeds alone, you will wear yourself out. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَن دَلَّكَ عَلَى اللَّهِ فَقَدْ نَصَحَكَ</span> — but the one who shows you the path to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> has given you sincere counsel. That path is the contentment of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. If you are content with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>, a small amount of action will be enough. If you are not, no amount of action will ever suffice.</p>
<p>And finally, he speaks of taking refuge — of returning to the One to whom all affairs return, since <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> says that to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> all affairs return. Seeking refuge in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is, above all else, the truest remedy.</p>
<p>This is why a scholar of the path says that the only real cure for all these diseases of the heart is to go before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> in a state of complete neediness — please, I need to change, and I need You to help me change — with a genuine sense of urgency. If that is the state a person is in, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will help them change.</p>
<p>For my own part, I truly believe that my coming to Islam was the result of a state I entered before I was Muslim — a state of complete <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِفْتِقَارٌ</span>. I called on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> by the name I knew, which was God, and I simply asked: give me guidance, show it to me. I was in a state of utter neediness. Most of the people I have met who became Muslim will confirm something similar: that there was a moment of absolute — just show me what to do. And then the guidance opened up, and it became very clear. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَاصٌ</span>. After that it becomes easy, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>I believe <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> is merciful, and that where there is sincerity, even within a person''s limited and imperfect understanding, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> that is enough. There is an apocryp<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span> story — and because it is apocryp<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>, no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقِيدَة</span> is based on it, I want to be clear about that — but it carries a wisdom that has been told: about the Prophet Musa, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, encountering a man who is speaking to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> about wanting to wash His feet, give Him a massage, and tend to Him — a man who is an anthropomorphist without knowing it.</p>
<p>And Musa, moved by righteousness, rebuked the man and explained the true meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>. Yet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> reminded Musa that he had been harsh, for the man had spoken those words out of love — he was simply ignorant. The right response to ignorance is to teach. So the point is that everyone is at the level where they are. Even the one bowing before an idol — that is his understanding; that is where he stands. This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> says:</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا تَجَعَلُوا لِلَّهِ أَنْدَادًا</span></p>
<p>Do not set up rivals to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> —</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ</span></p>
<p>— while you know it is wrong. Once knowledge reaches a person, that is when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> destroys him. If someone has no knowledge, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>s messenger has not yet reached him, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span> will not punish him:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا كُنَّا مُعَذِّبِينَ حَتَّىٰ نَبْعَثَ رَسُولًۭا</span></p>
<p>So if someone does not know Islam and is genuinely seeking guidance, that guidance comes, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهُ تَعَالَى عَنْهُمْ</span></p>
<p>Then the author says:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِأَنْ يَكُونَ كَغَرِيقًا أَوْ كَمَنْ ضَلَّ بِتِيهِنْ لَا يَرَى الْغِيَاثَةِ مِنْ سِوَى الْمُهَيْمَنَ الْعَظِيمِ الْقَدْرِ وَهُوَ الْمُجِيبُ دَعْوَةٍ مُقَرِّي</span></p>
<p>He is saying that you must be like someone who is drowning, or like someone lost in a barren desert who sees no source of help except the Almighty God. This is precisely what happened to Yusuf Islam. I heard him describe it in a lecture: he was caught in the undertow at Malibu, being dragged out to sea — genuinely terrifying — and in that moment he called on God completely and promised: give me guidance and I will follow it, whatever you ask of me. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرِيقٌ</span> — the drowning man — with nowhere left to turn.</p>
<p>And then there is Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, whose guidance came on a boat carrying Abyssinian Christians. When the waves began to overwhelm the vessel and everyone feared they would drown, Ikrimah started calling on Hubal. The Christians on board laughed at him. They said: you are calling on an idol made of wood, sitting in Mecca, to save you out here? And in that moment it struck him — how absurd it was. He realized he could only call on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ</span>. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> entered his heart right there. That is the state of the drowning man. And the author says this is in fact the most beneficial state — the one that will truly help us. It must reach the point where you are like a man going under.</p>
<p>So that is that.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'comprehensive treatment for the heart';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>Now, on the actions that purify the heart, he says:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَابِهِ لَلْقَلْبِ صَفْوً مِنْ عَمَلٍ أَنْفَعَهُ هُوَ الْمُدَامُ وَلَوْ يَقِلْ</span></p>
<p>As for the action most beneficial for purifying the heart, none surpasses the one that is consistent — even if it is small. This comes from the hadith:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَيْرُ الْأَعْمَالِ أَدْوَمُهَا وَإِنْ قَلَّ</span></p>
<p>The best actions are the ones maintained continuously, even if they are slight.</p>
<p>Think of it this way: if you take a coin and polish it hard with a few vigorous strokes, then set it down, versus taking the same coin and polishing it gently but without stopping — the second coin becomes bright and shiny in a way the first never will. One burst of effort cannot do what quiet consistency achieves over time. The heart works the same way. What encrusts it through accumulated wrong actions is not undone by one intense push. A little effort, maintained steadily, is what gradually breaks that crust away. That is why small deeds done consistently far outweigh large deeds done once.</p>
<p>The proof is in how the Companions conducted themselves. They were always consistent in their practices. The man who asked the Prophet about fasting every other day — the fast of Dawud — came to regret it in his old age. But the scholars took that very regret as evidence: the Companions held even voluntary practices so seriously that they would not abandon what they had taken upon themselves. It was not that the practice was obligatory — it was that they had committed to it.</p>
<p>And if you read the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحَادِيث</span>, you find that the Prophet used to fast so much that the Companions thought he never broke his fast — and then there were times when they thought he never fasted at all. He used to spend so much of the night in prayer that they thought he never slept — and then there were times when they thought he barely did any <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span>.</p>
<p>There is something very important to understand about the Messenger of Allah. He is the universal prophet — his being encompasses the possibilities of every good believer. So in him, every good believer has a model. And different believers are opened to different things. There are people for whom <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span> is not the door Allah opens for them. There are people for whom recitation of the Quran is not their particular opening — their opening may be in hadith. Others are opened through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span>, not fasting. Others through fasting, not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span>. Some through wisdom. It is very rare for anyone to be opened through all of them simultaneously.</p>
<p>This is why, when the Prophet described the different gates of paradise — the gate for the fasters, the gate for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهِدِينَ</span> — Abu Bakr asked: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللَّهِ، أَيَدْخُلُ أَحَدٌ هَذِهِ الْأَبْوَابَ كُلَّهَا؟</span> Can anyone enter through all of them? And the Prophet said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَرْجُو أَنْ تَكُونَ مِنْهُمْ</span> — yes, and I hope you are one of them. As they say, I hope he was. The point is: keep working. That applies to all of us.</p>
<p>So some people are given broad and wide-ranging <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق</span>. Imam Malik, for instance, wore fine clothes, used the best scents, burned <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُودٌ</span> — and to an outsider, especially among the ascetics, this looked extravagant. One of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْدٌ</span> wrote him a letter saying: I cannot believe your state. You eat the finest flour, you wear expensive clothes, the perfume alone must cost a fortune — you would do better giving all of that up and spending less time with people. He was, after all, teaching extensively in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span>.</p>
<p>So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> was essentially telling Imam Malik: compared to me, you are in a bad state. This is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَرْجَبٌ</span> — essentially a kind of spiritual boasting. And Imam Malik wrote back an extraordinary letter. He said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عَبْدَ اللَّهِ</span> — servant of Allah — Allah has opened for His servants many doors of goodness.</p>
<p>He then says: include action done in the absence of witnesses, and action done out of love for Allah or in awe of Him. So one of the things that benefits the heart is performing acts that no one else can see. Giving charity privately. Praying in a way that no one observes. This protects you from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُجْب</span> and everything that flows from them.</p>
<p>There is a story — it was actually Fudayl ibn Ayyad, not Bishr al-Hafi — who was once walking down a street and overheard someone say: there goes a man who spends the entire night in prayer. When Fudayl heard it, he burst into tears and said: I cannot remember ever spending a single complete night in prayer. And then he said: how great is the honor Allah has bestowed on me by veiling me and making people think I am better than I am. That idea — of concealing one''s state, of hiding — is the better path.</p>
<p>And then action done out of love: that is the highest. The scholars say there are two principal motivations. One is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَوْف</span> — fear — which, when it reaches the level of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَوْفُ مَقَامِهِ</span>, becomes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَلَاءٌ</span>: awe of Allah''s greatness, a profound sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَيْبَة</span> in the divine presence. The other is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَحَبَّة</span> — love — born of Allah''s mercy, yearning for Allah, doing things out of that longing. That is the highest motive, and the author tells you that the final <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَحَبَّة</span>.</p>
<p>He also says that the purest actions are those of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> — the one who is free of worldly wants, who does what he does seeking nothing in return, no thanks, no gratitude, not even reward in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>. He does it solely for Allah''s sake. That is the highest and best action for the heart. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> is not an outward condition — it is the absence of attachment within the heart. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> is not like the Christian ascetic who wears rough clothing and practices flagellation. In Islam, the ascetic is the one for whom the world has no grip on the heart. That is why Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf was a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span>, even though he was one of the wealthiest men in Mecca.</p>
<p>Then the author says that the actions of those who strive out of hope are higher and more exalted than those driven by fear alone. If fear is your only motivation, that is still valid — Allah tells us to have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَوْف</span> — but hope lifts the action higher. And the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبِيبُ اللهِ</span> — the beloved of Allah — and Allah loved him. That is the pinnacle: mutual love between the servant and his Lord.</p>
<p>He then says: particularly, action whose benefits extend to others. An action that purifies the heart is made greater when it reaches beyond the self. Charity does not only benefit your own soul — it benefits another human being. And in every creature that possesses a moist liver — that is, every living thing — there is reward. Water an animal and there is reward. Plant a tree and every creature that eats from it, for as long as that tree stands, earns you reward. All of these things are elevated precisely because they extend outward.</p>
<p>Then he speaks of fasting in relation to one''s character. If you are someone who has poor control over his appetite — if gluttony is your weakness — then fasting is particularly powerful for you. It strikes at the root of that disorder. But for someone who already has reasonable control over his relationship with food, fasting is still good, yet it will not work the same transformation as it does on the one who genuinely needs to discipline that aspect of himself.</p>
<p>Similarly with the miser: for a generous person, giving in charity is natural. For someone who is stingy, giving out is a far greater struggle — and for that very reason, it carries more weight. The hadith on this is direct: the best <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span> is what you give when you are healthy, when you want to hold on to it, and when you fear poverty. That is when giving out really costs you something. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span> that truly rubs <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>''s nose in it.</p>
<p>Then he says: you earn reward in proportion to your avoidance of wrong. And this brings up a related point. There are people in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> who spent the better part of their lives in disobedience, then in old age made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span> and began lecturing the young. But that person does not carry the same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span> as the young person who had every opportunity and every impulse toward disobedience — and chose otherwise. The older person no longer has the energy for most of those wrongs anyway. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> comes more easily when the temptation is largely gone.</p>
<p>There is also a real danger in the opposite direction: the heart that has been in disobedience for decades sometimes becomes so encrusted that a genuine return becomes very difficult. And yet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, many Muslims do make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span> as they grow older. They reach forty, forty-five, and something shifts. In Tunisia, they say the year of responsibility is forty — because that is when the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَحْي</span> came to the Prophet. Which has given some people the mistaken impression that you can do whatever you like before forty. This is ignorance, pure and simple. And yet it has become a kind of cultural artifact in parts of the Muslim world — people who say: when I go to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span>, I will grow my beard and become a proper Muslim. Some of them do come back changed. But these attitudes are a marker of how far we have drifted from our tradition.</p>
<p>What Allah loves is the young person who is raised up in obedience. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَبَابٌ</span> — youth — the Arabs generally put between fifteen and thirty-five, some say up to forty. After forty you are no longer a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَقْلٌ</span>; you are a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجُلٌ</span>, a man.</p>
<p>Then he says: and also the contribution of a miser — giving out of the best of his wealth, while he is in sound health, seeking the pleasure of Allah — all of these things purify the heart. Moreover, anything done with discretion and concealment is also purifying. And the best of endeavors is the one that purifies the heart. So the best thing you can do with your life is what is genuinely good for your heart.</p>
<p>Likewise — and this is the other side — the worst things are those that harden the heart, especially when a person persists in them or takes pleasure in them. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> that enjoyed them: those are the things most destructive to the heart.</p>
<p>One thing that hardens the heart is speaking at length without mentioning Allah. This is recorded in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَطَّأ</span> of Imam Malik. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا عِيسَى</span> is reported to have said: do not sit in gatherings without remembering Allah, for a gathering in which Allah is not mentioned will harden the heart — and you will not even notice it happening. The hardening comes gradually, spiritually and even, one might say, physically — and you are unaware. But the more the tongue is occupied with remembrance of Allah, the softer the heart becomes. That is simply the reality. The more <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرٌ</span> someone does, the more compassion flows from them, and the more they benefit.</p>
<p>Now this brings us to a truly important point. Allah says that a wrong action that produces in its wake a sense of shame and brokenness before Allah is better than an act of obedience that produces pride and arrogance in the one who performs it. And this is the secret of wrong actions — the wisdom concealed within them. Allah permitted us to stumble so that through the stumbling we might arrive at this state. The Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, said: if you did not commit wrong actions, I would fear for you something worse than wrong actions — namely, being pleased with yourself, admiring yourself, vanity, the feeling that you are something wonderful. That is worse.</p>
<p>Now, Sidi Ahmad Zarruq clarifies this so there is no confusion. He says the good of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَهَارَة</span> is in the very essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَهَارَة</span>, and the evil he is describing is what obedience results in when it goes wrong. So do not misread this as saying that there is such a thing as good <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعَاصِيَهْ</span> or bad <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَهَارَة</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَهَارَة</span> is good. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعَاصِيَه</span> is bad. Full stop. What is being said is about the secondary results. If the secondary result of an act of obedience was arrogance — and the secondary result of a disobedience was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span> — then in terms of what was ultimately produced, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span> was the better outcome. The obedience generated poison; the disobedience generated a return to Allah.</p>
<p>Think of people in America who commit terrible crimes, go to prison, and find their way to Allah there. That happens often. The disobedience was genuinely wrong. But it became part of the causal chain through which Allah guided them. It was not a good thing — but it resulted in a good thing. And that is what he is saying: a wrong action that results in humility is preferable to an obedience that results in arrogance, in terms of what they produce in the heart.</p>
<p>Then he says:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَذَرَّةٌ مِنْ عَمِلَ الْقَلْبِ الْعَلِي مِثْرُ الرِّضَى وَالزُهْدِ وَالتَوَكْرِ أَغْضَرُوا عَنْدَ اللَّهِ مِنْ جِبَالِ شَمَخْنَ مِنْ ظَوَاهِرَ الْعَمَالِ</span></p>
<p>An atom''s weight of a lofty inward action — such as contentment, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span>, or trust in Allah — is greater with Allah than mountains of outward deeds performed without any heart. And this is the same principle as:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَإِنَّ رَكَعَتَيْنِ مِنْ مُحِبٍ خَيْرَ مِنْ أَلْثٍ مِنْ غَيْرِ حُبِّ</span></p>
<p>Two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> prayed with love are better than a thousand prayed without it. Immeasurably better.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 200">page 200</div>
<p>Then he mentions what Imam Sahnun, may Allah be pleased with him, said:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَتَرْكُوا دِرْهَمٍ لِكَوْنِهِ حَظَرَ أَظْدَرُ مِنْ تَصَدُقَاتٍ وَعُمَرَ</span></p>
<p>Leaving a single <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَانِق</span> of what Allah has made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> is better than performing seventy thousand <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمْرَة</span>. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَانِق</span> is the smallest unit of currency in the Arabic tradition — a farthing, a penny.</p>
<p>And they also relate that Imam Jafar al-Sadiq said there are four things whose very best still carries a kind of diminishment:</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْبَعَةٌ عِزُّهَا وَظُلُّ</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدَّيْنُ وَلَوْ دَانِقٌ</span></p>
<p>A debt, even if it is only a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَانِق</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبِنْتُ وَلَوْ مَرْيَمُ</span></p>
<p>A daughter, even if she is Maryam — not because daughters are lesser than sons in worth, but because in that social context she lacks the physical strength and social protection a man possesses. And the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَزِيزٌ</span> in Arabic, let us not forget, also means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَوِيٌّ</span> — physically strong, or strong in position. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَزِيزُ النَّصْرِ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَزِيزُ النَّصْرِ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسُؤَادْ وَلَوْ كَيفَ الطَّرِيق</span></p>
<p>Asking a question, even if it is only: which way do I go? There is a humility inherent in asking that is itself a small diminishment of the self — not a wrong thing, just an honest reality.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْوَحْشَةَ وَلَوْ لَيْلًا</span></p>
<p>And being alone — even for a single night.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 201">page 201</div>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'beneficial actions for purifying the heart';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>Now we come to the root causes of the diseases. And what I will do is cover the treatment of diseases through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> next week — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخ خَتْرِي</span> will be here, and I will ask him to teach that class. He knows this book deeply. He loves it. He has spent a great deal of time with it. I have read sections of it with him — and I read much of it with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> Abdullah as well. We actually completed a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَتْمٌ</span> of it on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span>, on the day of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرَفَات</span>. We went through every disease, named them all, and made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> to purify us of all of them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, for all of us.</p>
<p>Now he says: the comprehensive root — in the view of both Imam al-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِلَال</span>i and Imam Ibn Askar — of all these diseases is love of the temporal world, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الدُّنْيَا</span>. There is some difference of opinion on this, as we saw: Ibn Abbad considered the root to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span> — desire. But you can see how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَمَعٌ</span> flows from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الدُّنْيَا</span>. And when Ibn Askar says the root is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الرِّيَاسَةِ</span> — love of status and power — even that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الدُّنْيَا</span>, because why do you want status? You want power, you want the world. So in the end it really does come back to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الدُّنْيَا</span>. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> on this — though it is classified as weak — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَأْسُ كُلِّ خَطِيئَةٍ حُبُّ الْعَاجِلَةِ</span>: the love of this world is at the root of every wrong action.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَطَاءِ اللهِ</span>, on the other hand, considered the root to be a man''s self-satisfaction with himself. That is a distinct and fascinating take. He was a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِي</span> jurist who died in 709 AH, corresponding to 1309 CE, and he is considered one of the greatest masters of this science. He wrote the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكَم</span>. Imam al-Banna said that had it been permissible to recite something other than the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> in prayer, it would have been permissible to recite the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكَم</span> — a clear hyperbole, but an indication of the profound esteem in which the work was held.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 202">page 202</div>
<p>In the thirty-fifth aphorism, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَطَاءِ اللهِ</span> says that the source of every disobedience, every indifference, and every passion is self-satisfaction with the self. And the source of every obedience, every vigilance, and every virtue is dissatisfaction with the self. He says: it is better for you to keep company with an ignorant man who is dissatisfied with himself than with a learned man who is satisfied with himself. And then: what knowledge is there in a self-satisfied scholar? And what ignorance is there in an unlearned man who is dissatisfied with himself — and who therefore keeps asking, keeps wanting to learn?</p>
<p>This connects directly to something strange about modern culture. Think of the whole industry of self-help books aimed at getting rid of shame, building confidence, feeling good about yourself. But why is the person standing in that self-help aisle in the first place? Because he is dissatisfied with himself. And if he were completely self-satisfied, he would never pick up a book. The author of the book, incidentally, has no real interest in your becoming self-satisfied — because then you would stop buying books. Most of that industry is built on keeping you just dissatisfied enough to keep purchasing. But the point stands: dissatisfaction with the self is what drives a person to want to change and improve.</p>
<p>If anyone here were truly satisfied with themselves, you would not be in this class studying the diseases of the heart. The very fact that people have these struggles is what moves them to seek help. If you feel you lack guidance, you seek guidance. The one who submits to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> begins seeking because he knows he needs <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> direction. But if you are walking through life perfectly content — I am already guided, I have no need — you will never reach out.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 203">page 203</div>
<p>Now, Sidi Ahmad Zarruq identifies three signs of contentment with the soul.</p>
<p>The first is seeing that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقٌّ</span> belongs to oneself. This is interesting to consider in the modern context: we have a bill of rights but no bill of responsibilities. Islam is far more focused on your responsibilities than on your rights. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, in a real sense, is a bill of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> rights over us. And the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an rights, as understood in the secular modern framework, carries a very different set of assumptions than the Islamic one. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَمٌّ</span>an beings do have rights — but in the Islamic understanding, those rights originate with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. They are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> rights embedded in His servants. If someone is protected from oppression, it is because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقٌّ</span> that His creation not be oppressed. If someone takes a life in accordance with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, it is because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> demands His right be fulfilled. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an being has a right — but it was given by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, and the other person fulfills it because he fears <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.</p>
<p>This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> — fear of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — is the very foundation of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span> society. The first sign of self-satisfaction, then, is always seeing the rights as belonging to the self: they wronged me, it is all their fault, I am always the injured party. This is an extremely common psychological pattern.</p>
<p>There is a Moroccan story about a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> and a self-satisfied scholar who disliked him. This scholar came to visit one day and said: the least of my students is better than you. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> said: show me. Come to my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْرَسَة</span> tomorrow. The next day, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> secretly instructed all his students: when the visiting scholar enters, do not return his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ</span>. And when my student comes in — who had been set up to visit — do not say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ عَلَيْكُمْ</span> to him either.</p>
<p>The self-satisfied scholar walked in, no one returned his greeting, and his reaction was immediate outrage: are these your students? Have they not he<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> commands? Have they not he<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> His words — return a greeting with what is better, or at least return it in kind?</p>
<p>Then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span>s own student came in. No one returned his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ</span>. He said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ عَلَيْكُمْ</span> — again. Still nothing. Then he said: O brothers, please forgive me. I must have done something wrong. What did I do? Why have you not responded? Whatever I have done, please excuse me.</p>
<p>Those two responses reveal everything. The self-satisfied man arrived saying: I have a right, and you have violated it. The student arrived thinking: what right of yours did I fail to fulfill? One looked outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> in demand; the other looked inw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> in concern. That is why, even as the least of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span>s students, the young man showed something the scholar — for all his learning — did not possess. This is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكْمَةٌ</span> behind <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَطَاءِ اللهِ</span>s words: what knowledge does a self-satisfied scholar truly have? And what real ignorance remains in an unlearned man who is genuinely dissatisfied with himself and wants to be taught?</p>
<p>The second sign of contentment with the self is ignoring one''s own faults by interpreting them away — convincing yourself that you are already in a state of purity, even if you have to construct a justification to get there. For instance, a person gets angry and tells himself: well, the Prophet said anger can be from the best of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, and I am from the best of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, so this is fine. He sees the fault but reframes it so that it no longer registers as a fault. This is what the poet meant when he said: the eye of contentment does not see faults. Just as the eye of one in love cannot see the beloved''s flaws — and one of the signs of falling out of love is that you begin noticing them all: I never noticed before that one ear is larger than the other.</p>
<p>The third sign of self-satisfaction is excessive self-pity — poor me, I am having such a h<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> time — chronic complaining and self-indulgent lamentation.</p>
<p>Those are the three signs of a person who is satisfied with himself. The three signs of the opposite — of a person genuinely dissatisfied with himself — are these:</p>
<p>The first is constant self-scrutiny, a persistent suspicion of one''s own intentions. And this is modeled even by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> Yusuf, peace be upon him, who is a prophet — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْصُوم</span>. There is a scholarly disagreement about the precise meaning of his statement, which Sayyid Ahmad Zarruq and others discuss. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> Yusuf said: I will not declare my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> innocent — for by its nature, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> commands to evil. So always ex<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمِين</span>e your intentions. Ask yourself: am I doing this for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاءٌ</span>? Hazrat Abu Bakr said: the only one who feels safe from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِفَاق</span> is the hypocrite. The true believer is always watchful over himself, always alert to the blemishes of the soul. This is reflected in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> the Prophet made: do not leave me to my own soul even for the blink of an eye, or less than that.</p>
<p>The second sign is forcing the self to do difficult things — things it dislikes. Eating less. Giving out money. Going out in the path of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. These are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكْرُوهٌ</span> to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> — the self naturally recoils from them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> is genuinely h<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>. Charity is genuinely h<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> wants to hold on to what it has. Compelling it to do otherwise is one of the marks of a person who has not surrendered to self-satisfaction.</p>
<p>Abu Uthman said: whoever sees anything good about himself has not truly seen the faults of his soul.</p>
<p>Now there is an obvious question here — and it is an important one, because the whole Western discourse around self-help, psychology, and modern wellness is built on the opposite premise. Is this not just self-loathing? Is this not low self-esteem? Is this not exactly what the self-esteem movement told us is harmful?</p>
<p>And the answer is: look at the actual people shaped by this tradition. Look at the real <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُيُوخ</span>. They are not miserable. They are not broken. They carry genuine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>ility alongside genuine dignity.</p>
<p>Consider Aisha, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be pleased with her. She was the wife of the best of creation. She was physically beautiful. She was among the most brilliant minds in Islamic history — and in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an history. She came from a noble family, the aristocracy of Quraysh. And yet, when she yearned for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> to declare her innocence in the incident of the slander, she said:</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَكُنتُ أَرَى نَفْسِي حَقِيرًۭا</span></p>
<p>I considered myself so insignificant that I would never have imagined the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> would be revealed on my account.</p>
<p>Despite all of that — beauty, lineage, brilliance, position — she did not reg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> herself as someone for whom <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> would descend. And yet Aisha was not a woman without self-confidence. She was a woman of enormous strength. She was one of the very few people who questioned the Prophet directly — she c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>lenged him on a point of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> when he said that a person given an easy reckoning would enter paradise:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَدْخُلُ الْجَنَّةِ بِلَا حِسَابٍ وَلَمْ يَخُلُ اللَّهُ فَسَوْفْ يُحَاسِبُ حِسَابٍ يَسِيرًۭا</span></p>
<p>She pressed him: but how can you say that, given what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says? Most of the Companions would not have done that. It takes a person of extraordinary inner confidence to question the Prophet of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> on a matter of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>ic interpretation.</p>
<p>She also led an army out. She later regretted it, but in the moment she said: he is wrong and we are right — let us go resolve this. She rode out in her hawdaj to the Battle of the Camel. And when one of the men addressed her as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أُمَّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ</span>, she replied:</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا أُمِّنُكَ</span></p>
<p>Which in the Arabic of the time carried the force of: do not call me your mother — you are no son of mine. That is a sharp, strong response. She was a strong woman.</p>
<p>And yet, with all of that, her sense of her own standing before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> was one of profound smallness. That is what this teaching is about. It is not about collapsing into self-loathing. It is not about erasing confidence. It is about never becoming content with where you are — always knowing there is more, always wanting to improve. Be confident, yes. But do not be complacent.</p>
<p>No matter how accomplished you become, there is always someone better. No matter how much <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> you have mastered, there is always more mastery to pursue. The master calligrapher looks at his work and sees room to improve. If he stops seeing it, he stops improving. And every one of these sciences — every field of knowledge, every spiritual state, every skill — is endless. True mastery belongs not to the one who has arrived, but to the one who never stops. The one committed to constant improvement is the real master, because they are always moving forw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>, always refining their technique. But the moment a person is self-satisfied, the improvement stops — and in a very real sense, so does the life of the heart.</p>
<p>This is what he means when he says that self-dissatisfaction acts as an impetus to seek virtuous character and to remain vigilant about what is unbecoming. So you remain watchful, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>We will stop there. Next week we will finish this section, and then the week after that I will go through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْكَارُ الصَّبَاحِ وَالْمَسَاءِ</span> — the morning and evening remembrances — which everyone should be doing. I will get you a copy of those. And then we will do the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَوَاطِر</span>. That is how the remaining sessions will be structured.</p>
<p>Now there was a question from the previous class about women and male slaves — specifically whether a woman may own a bondsman. Yes, that is permissible in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْه</span>. But they are not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَحْرَم</span>, and all the normal rules reg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ing seclusion and mixing apply. There can be no sexual relationship between the two. That was the question.</p>
<p>Now before we close, let me do the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَفَاتِيح</span> — the keys — because I did not cover that last week.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَفَاتِيح</span> is the plural of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِفْتَاح</span>, which means key in Arabic. The root, if you look at the word, is the same root as the verb to open. And the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَزْن</span> — the morphological pattern — it sits on is that of a tool: the thing you use to do the action. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِكْنَسَة</span> is the thing you use to sweep. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِفْتَاح</span> is the thing you use to open.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 209">page 209</div>
<p>In Arabic, the word for key does not just mean an object that unlocks something — it means the thing that causes an opening. The metaphor is richer than the English word &quot;key&quot; carries. And it connects directly to one of the names of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَتَّاحُ</span> — the One who opens. People who carry spiritual keys have the ability to open things that are otherwise closed. And there are many <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحَادِيث</span> on this: &quot;such and such is the key to such and such.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span> is the key to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَرَجُ</span> — to the opening, to relief. If you want <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرَجٌ</span> from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, the key is patience.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, then, is what tells us what is good and what is bad or ugly — the intellect has no independent access to that. It can sometimes arrive at the right answer, but not always, which makes it a relative and limited source of guidance rather than an absolute one. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> indicates what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَنَةٌ</span> and what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَيَّاح</span>. And when a person recognizes that his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> will not save him or help him, he turns to what actually will help him — namely, doing what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has commanded — and that recognition leads him to act in accordance with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and His message.</p>
<p>The poem''s point, the sheikh explains, is that the origin of both heart disease and spiritual well-being is the company one keeps, for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> states that a man is upon the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> of his companion. If you keep company with someone who delays his prayer, who neglects <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاةٌ</span>, or who commits wrong actions, you will inevitably drift tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> those same things. Conversely, good company will pull you in a good direction. This is precisely why people must ex<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمِين</span>e who they spend time with, because one''s companions are a reliable indicator of one''s own spiritual state. The sheikh offers a vivid illustration: if you sit at the door of a bar, you will smell the foulness of alcohol; if you sit at the door of a perfumer, you will breathe in the fragrance of perfume. Whatever you are near, you are affected by. Proximity to goodness brings goodness; proximity to corruption brings corruption.</p>
<p>For this reason, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لُقْمَانُ الْحَكِيمُ</span> — mentioned in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> — is recorded in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَطَّأ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَام مَالِك</span> as having told his son: sit with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span>. Keep their company. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> are people who possess knowledge and live by it, and sitting with them is like rain falling on dry earth and bringing it back to life. That is what their company does to the earth of your heart — it revives it, and it does so in a way you may not even notice. Simply being in the presence of righteous, knowledgeable people allows the heart to stir back to life, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> begins to grow and increase within it.</p>
<p>He says that sitting with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> — the people of true knowledge, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَارِفِينَ</span> — will take a person from six things to six things: from doubt to certainty; from doing things for other than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> to doing them with sincerity, with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصٌ</span>, for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> alone; from heedlessness to the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>; from desiring this world to desiring the next; from arrogance to repentance; and from corrupt inner character and bad intentions tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> people to sincere, wholesome character and genuine goodwill. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَطَاءِ اللهِ</span> makes a related point in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِكَم</span>: do not keep company with someone whose state — his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>, his condition — does not elevate you, and whose speech does not direct you tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ اللهُ</span>. If a person''s presence neither lifts your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِمَّة</span> nor points you tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ اللهُ</span>, that company is harmful.</p>
<p>Of all the diseases ex<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمِين</span>ed in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَطْهَرَة</span>, the comprehensive cure — the one that addresses all of them — is the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. More than anything else, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> is what heals the heart. Dhikr includes recitation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاح</span>, and all the formulas of praise: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ الْعَظِيمِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللَّهِ</span> — as well as the supplications the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> has transmitted to us. But the highest and best form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِرَاءَةُ الْقُرْآنِ</span>. A person who is in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> will have a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَهَارَة</span> heart — a pure heart.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِي</span> Ahmad ibn Hanbal is reported to have seen <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> ninety-nine times in his dreams. In one of them he was asked: of all the things that draw a slave near to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, which is the greatest? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> answered him: recitation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. He then asked: Ya <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, with understanding or without understanding? And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> answered, according to the Imam, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِفَهْمٍ أَوْ بِغَيْرِ فَهْمٍ</span> — with understanding or without. This means that even someone who does not understand the words he is reading is still drawing close to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>.</p>
<p>In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحِيحُ الْبُخَارِيِّ</span>, the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span> said that the difference between one who remembers <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and one who does not is like the difference between the living and the dead. Whoever remembers <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَلْبُهُ حَيٌّ</span> — his heart is alive, and so he is alive. Whoever does not remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَلْبُهُ مَيِّتٌ</span> — his heart is dead, and so he is considered dead. A sound <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> also says: make remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> until people say you are mad. This is because one thing that marks people who are troubled in their minds is that they talk to themselves constantly. Someone who remembers <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> all the time may appear, outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ly, very similar — a non-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span> who sees you walking down the street doing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> may well think you are talking to yourself. That is what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> points to.</p>
<p>There is also the sound <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, transmitted in various narrations, in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> asks: s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>l I not tell you what the best of all your actions is — the purest in the sight of your Lord and King, the highest in rank among all the deeds that carry degrees, better for you than spending gold and silver, and better even than going out to meet your enemy in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span>, striking them and being struck, killing them and being killed? They said: of course, tell us, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>. He said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرُ اللهِ تَعَالَى</span> — the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Some people wonder how remembrance could be better than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> — may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> have mercy on them — explain that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرُ اللَّهِ</span> is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَايَة</span>, the end itself, the very purpose for which we were created. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> and everything else are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَائِل</span> — means. And no means, however noble, is greater than the end it serves. We were created to remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, which is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> is the highest thing in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اذْكُرُونِي أَذْكُرْكُمْ</span> — remember Me, and I will remember you. Whoever remembers his Lord, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> remembers him. And whoever <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> remembers has been enriched in both this world and the next, and stands in need of nothing. Another distinguishing feature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> — unlike <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span>, fasting, or even formal prayer — is that it is not bound to a specific time or place. It can accompany every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an act. There are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْكَار</span> related to going to the toilet, to eating, to marital relations — every moment of life has a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> belonging to it. And it is said that whoever is in a state of remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is never struck by calamity in a way that harms him, because whatever befalls someone who was remembering <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> only raises him in rank. If he dies while remembering <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, he dies as a martyr. This is the treatment for the diseased and foul soul — remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>When you wish to engage in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>, you begin with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِغْفَار</span> — seeking forgiveness — because the purpose of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> is purification, and the reason purification is needed is the wrong actions one has accumulated. So the process begins by asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> to remove those wrong actions. Once He removes them, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> that follows will then remove their lingering effects, for wrong actions leave traces and effects on the heart.</p>
<p>You then send prayers upon the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاح</span> upon the Prophet acts as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَبٍّ</span> — a nurturer — for the heart and as a means of its purification. This is why most of the later <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span> specifically encouraged these two practices: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِغْفَار</span> for the wrong actions most of us are engaged in, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاح</span> upon the Prophet because by the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِجْمَاع</span> of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, it is the only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> that is absolutely guaranteed to be accepted by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Anyone who says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَآلِهِ وَسَلِّمْ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقْبُول</span> — accepted. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> commanded this with a command He Himself initiated, which is unique among all of His commands — He does not begin with the action Himself except in the case of prayer upon the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span>. He said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ وَمَلَٓئِكَتَهُۥ يُصَلُّونَ عَلَى ٱلنَّبِىِّۚ</span> — and then enjoined the believers: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا صَلُّوا عَلَيْهِ وَسَلِّمُوا تَسْلِيمًا</span> — pray upon the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and greet him with peace. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَآلِهِ وَسَلِّمْ</span>. Whoever prays upon the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> once, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> prays upon him ten times — this is established in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحِيح حَدِيث</span>. And whoever <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has prayed upon ten times, what else could he possibly need?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلصَّلَاةُ عَلَيْهِ، عَلَيْهِ نُورُهُ فِي قَدْرِهِ، وَنُورُهُ فِي قَدْرِهِ، وَنُورٌ عَلَى الصِّرَاطِ</span> — the Prophet of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span> said that prayer upon him is light in this world, light in the grave, and light on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاط</span>. It gives you guidance — and that guidance — when you need it most. It illuminates the grave so that it is light rather than dark. And it illuminates the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِرَاط</span> so that you can see your way across it. Light in the heart, as the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said.</p>
<p>Because we have been commanded to pray upon the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, it is generally good to begin by reciting the actual <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span>, for in doing so you are fulfilling the command of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — this is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">امْتِثَالُ أَمْرِ اللهِ</span>. That command is obligatory at least once in a lifetime, but you can fulfill it anew every single time you send prayers upon the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. The one who prays upon the Prophet should observe the same <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدَب</span> he would observe when remembering <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>: it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْدُوب</span> for him to be in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُضُوء</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْدُوب</span> to face the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِبْلَة</span>, and so on — the same courtesies that are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْدُوب</span> for prayer apply here as well.</p>
<p>He also mentions <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِحْضَار</span> — that when you pray upon the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span>, you should do so as if you were in his presence. In the same way that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span> in prayer means worshipping <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> as though you see Him, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِحْضَار</span> in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاح</span> upon the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> means bringing his presence before you as though he were there — calling to mind the guidance he gave us, all the things we owe him as our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسِيلَة</span> for teaching us <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> and for conveying to us the knowledge of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. And you remember that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> Himself and His angels send prayer upon this Messenger, so in doing so you are participating in something that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> Himself declared He does.</p>
<p>Another thing to be mindful of in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَحْن</span> — incorrect pronunciation. You should avoid it to the best of your ability. Some people simply cannot pronounce every Arabic sound correctly, but the effort must still be made. He notes that some mispronunciations constitute a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْصِيَة</span> or a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَطَأٌ</span>, a sin or an error, while others can reach the level of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِدَّة</span> — potentially taking a person out of Islam. As for those who cannot distinguish between sounds like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَادٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَكَاءٌ</span> — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعْجَمِيَّة</span> — if there is a genuine heaviness in the tongue, a physical impediment that persists despite every effort to correct it, then that person is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْذُور</span>: excused by the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>. He should still engage in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> even if the pronunciation falls short, because the incapacity is real and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> does not burden a soul beyond what it can bear.</p>
<p>He receives the rew<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>, and no wrongdoing is entailed by performing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> in that way. Among the greatest acts of worship is also meditating on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> creation. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> mentions in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> those who remember Him standing, sitting, and reclining on their sides, and who reflect on the creation of the heavens and the earth. Dhikr and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَكُّر</span> — remembrance and reflection — are thus joined together.</p>
<p>It is said that the action of the heart, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَكُّر</span>, is better than all the actions of the limbs. This is why one narration states that a single moment of reflection is better than a year of worship. The signs of genuine reflection fall into four directions.</p>
<p>The first is reflecting on the signs of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. The second is reflecting on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> creation and what those signs point to — and this leads to love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, because one sees everything <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has done for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an being. The third is reflecting on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> promised rew<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>s and the signs connected to them; each sign kindles a desire for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>, so that the mark of this reflection is a yearning, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَغْبَة</span>, for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>. The fourth is reflecting on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> promised punishment; the sign of this reflection is that fear takes root in the heart and the person turns away from disobedience.</p>
<p>There is also reflection on the weakness of the self — the harshness of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ اللهُ</span> despite His unceasing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>. When one contemplates all that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has given and then honestly weighs how little one has offered in return, this reflection produces <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَيَاء</span>, a deep sense of shame before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — shame that He has given so much, while one has given so little in return.</p>
<p>The heart is what distinguishes good from evil, and the source of all these matters lies in the inrushings that come to the heart. These inrushings arrive like a guest descending upon the heart, and they take different forms.</p>
<p>The question Qais raised concerned what people today call the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَصَوِّفَة</span>. Imam al-Maqari distinguished between the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُوفِيَّة</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَصَوِّفَة</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَصَوِّفَة</span> are those who merely claim to be Sufis. A common sign of such people is that they engage in abundant <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> while neglecting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَجِبَتْ</span> — the obligatory duties.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span>s answer was direct: the first and foremost obligation on every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an being is to acquire knowledge — before anything else. A person who has not learned the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرَائِدُ الْعَيْنِ</span>, the indivi<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>lly obligatory matters, has no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span> before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. It is as though he stands on the same footing as an animal, or worse, because an animal is not morally responsible, whereas this person is astray and is making no effort to correct himself.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> Abd al-Khadim cited many scholars on this point — Imam al-Sharani, Imam al-Suyuti, and all the great imams who mastered both the outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> sciences and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span> — all of them affirm that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْه</span> must precede <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span>. If a person pursues <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span> without first grounding himself in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْه</span>, he will go astray. There is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصَوُّف</span> without the knowledge of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرَائِضُ</span>. Tasawwuf comes after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْه</span>.</p>
<p>Even <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِرُّ الْوَالِدَيْنِ</span> — dutiful care of one''s parents — though it is the highest virtue <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> mentions in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, still comes after learning what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has made obligatory. If caring for one''s parents is preventing one from acquiring that obligatory knowledge, then one is committing a wrong by abandoning what is more right.</p>
<p>Reg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ing the question about women: a woman in a state of menstrual impurity may perform all forms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> that men perform. She may not recite from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> extensively, though there are nuances — if she has memorized the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, she may recite what is needed to retain her memorization, or she may recite short phrases such as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَوُّذ</span> or verses like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْمُلْكِ</span> in that capacity. She may not touch the physical <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> during that period. Beyond these restrictions, she may freely recite the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْكَار</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>, and the prayers upon the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>.</p>
<p>Reg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ing the question about non-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s in America who are drawn to Islam through its spiritual teachings — those who hear about the inner meanings of the tradition and feel themselves drawn closer — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span>s answer was that the best path for all people is submission to the will of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and acceptance of His commands as He has made them binding. When the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> called people to Islam, he began with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> and then told them that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> had made five prayers obligatory. He called them first to the unity of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and then to prayer. That is the methodology of the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span>, and that is what we should be calling people to.</p>
<p>In contemporary culture there is a pervasive false or pseudo-spirituality built on the notion that one can access <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقِيقَة</span> without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>. This is a Dajjalic phenomenon — it misleads people and draws them away from the truth. It is, in effect, anti-spirituality, because genuine spirituality is protected by the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>. The fruit is protected by its peel; if you strip the peel before the fruit has ripened, you ruin it. Likewise, if you reach for spirituality before you have shielded yourself with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, you will destroy your soul and become one of those false figures — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَمَلِّئٍ</span> — people who project an outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> appearance of spiritual attainment while their hearts remain filled with every disease.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> also addressed the importance of concealing one''s faults. If a person has committed wrong, he should veil it and make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span>; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> will forgive him. But if he publicizes it openly, the harm multiplies, because the sin spreads as a tribulation beyond himself.</p>
<p>He cited the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> of the man who came to the mosque and told the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> that he had committed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِينَة</span> and asked to be given the prescribed punishment. The Prophet turned away from him. The man followed him outside and repeated himself. Finally the Prophet looked at him and said: &quot;Did you not just perform <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُضُوء</span> and pray with us?&quot; — meaning: that is what Islam is for, to purify one''s wrongs. The man, however, persisted until the punishment was carried out. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> also said that whoever commits such offenses should cover himself with <span class="a' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'the root of all diseases of the heart';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'r-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> covering and make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span>, but whoever comes forw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> and openly admits his sin, the prescribed punishment will be applied.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> then turned to the question of companionship. For worldly matters, one should keep company with those who have less, not those who have more — because constantly comparing oneself to those who have bigger houses and better cars feeds love of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. But for matters of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>, one should always seek the company of those who are better. If one only keeps company with people whose <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> is weaker, one begins to think one''s own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> is sufficient and grows complacent.</p>
<p>The people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, he noted, tend to see themselves as lesser than everyone around them, so the danger of pride does not really apply to them in the same way. Keeping their company benefits you precisely because their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>ility makes you feel how much further you still have to go.</p>
<p>This is also why one should never look down on anyone. A person who appears to be in a worse state today might make a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span> tomorrow that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> accepts and rise to become a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — while the one who despised him might later stumble. No one can know another person''s standing with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. So one should never feel superior to anyone in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, and should always seek out the company of those who are better in their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>.</p>
<p>Those are rightly guided, if they truly follow what those imams brought — because those four imams <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ أَرْضَعَهَا وَقَفُوا غَيْرِهُمْ جَمِعُوا مَنَعَهَا</span> — it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>; those four imams are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ</span>, meaning it is an absolute consensus that they are rightly guided. So those who call to something other than that are on shaky ground. In matters of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَقِيدَة</span>, take a simple, sound creed from a straightforw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> source — Ibn Ashir, Imam al-Tahawi, any of those — and do not wade into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُرُوعٌ، فَرْعِيَّاتٌ</span>: where is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, what is the hand like, all of that. Someone came to me and asked what I say about the verse <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلرَّحْمَنُ عَلَى ٱلْعَرْشِ ٱسْتَوَىٰ</span>, and I said that the very fact they are asking that question is, by the testimony of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> itself, a sign of deviation in the heart — because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> states that anyone who pursues those verses has deviation in their heart. So whenever you hear people arguing over the verses of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> that belong to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَشَابِهَاتٌ</span>, the ambiguous ones, just leave them alone. That is guidance.</p>
<p>The question had been: what do we do in a time when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُيُوخ</span> have disappeared and people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَةٌ</span> are scarce? What <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> Hafri — may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> bless him — said was that the very asking of that question is a sign that the person cares about their soul, so it is a good question, and it reflects a real condition. He said that if you are in this country, then things like the classes you are attending now are good — this is the right kind of thing to do. He said to make sure the source of your knowledge is sound. The things we teach, like these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتُونٌ</span> — these are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتُونٌ</span> that are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثَابِتٌ</span>, rooted in our tradition, and they come through a chain of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُيُوخ</span>. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> and my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَابِطُ الْحَاجِّ</span>, and whoever has seen <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَابِطُ الْحَاجِّ</span> knows who he is. His knowledge is pure. The source of the knowledge is pure. No one who has lived with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَابِطُ الْحَاجِّ</span> for over seventy years can say they ever saw him do something even <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكْرُوهٌ</span>. He has never been he<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> to speak an ill word about anyone by name. His knowledge is pure <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, and he teaches people the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, because he is in a state that one of the people described this way: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجُلُوسُ عِنْدَهُ كَالْجُلُوسِ عِنْدَ حَبْرِ الْغَيْبِ</span> — sitting with him is like sitting with someone who is simultaneously present and absent. He is present in that he will teach you and answer your questions; he is absent in that his heart has moved away from this world — it is no longer in this world.</p>
<p>People like that are rare to find. There is a question here — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, he is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَابِط</span>; we love him. A woman asked: is it possible for a woman to come and learn from you, to study with you? He said that anyone who wants to go to Mauritania may go. He said the hidden aspiration of a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَقْطَعُ مِنَ السَّيْفِ</span> — more cutting than the sword. If the hidden intention is strong, people can do remarkable things.</p>
<p>He gave the example of Yahya, an American man who became <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span> here. He was young — nineteen or twenty — when he took his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَة</span>. He was, as the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> put it, a spoiled American, from a wealthy family, with older brothers. He became <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span> and wanted to seek knowledge. He had an expense-paid place at Berkeley, had already completed two years of college. He came to me, and I told him initially to just finish university for his parents'' sake, and then go.</p>
<p>My sister Nabila — and I told <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخُ حَدَّامِين</span> this — said to me: you did him an injustice. I said: why? It is only two years; it will pass quickly, and then he can go do what he wants. She said: how do you know what will happen to him in those two years? Maybe he will encounter a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِتْنَةٌ</span> at Berkeley. Maybe he will lose the desire for knowledge right now, while the fuel is still burning in his heart. When she said that, I realized she was right. I went to him and said: that advice I gave you — I was wrong. He said: I am really glad you said that, because I was not going to take it anyway. That is what he told me.</p>
<p>When I told <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخُ حَدَّامِين</span> — who is the cousin of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخ خَتْرِي</span> and the cousin of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَابِطُ الْحَاجِّ</span>, having learned all his knowledge from him — when I told him what I had said, and what my sister had said, he replied: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, I had wanted to tell you that you were wrong, but my respect for you held me back. I am glad your sister said it. And he said he loves her for the good counsel she gave.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخُ حَدَّامِين</span> said that Yahya — who is the son of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَابِطُ الْحَاجِّ</span> — now lives in a small hut made of tree branches with rags tied together. He said: learn for one year and you can learn four times over. It is not very much. And once you have learned those four times, go wherever you want, do whatever you want. Once you have your four times, practice it in your home, do your work, live your life. But if you have only four times, do not squander your time on other things, because it is all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يض تجويد تجويد تجويد تجويد</span>. Hadith should be transmitted with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَجْوِيد</span>, and when you recite the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْكَار</span> of the morning and evening, you should observe the proper <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَخَارِجُ الْحُرُوفِ</span> — where each letter originates — along with all the rules that apply to them.</p>
<p>The best of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَكُّر</span>, and the highest station of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَكُّر</span> is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَنَاءُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَنَاءُ</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ الْجُنَيْدِ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ الْجُنَيْدِ</span> called it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَنَاءُ</span>, which in Arabic literally means extinction. This concept arises from the fact that each of us experiences a sense of separation from other people. One of the things you notice about a newborn is that this sense of separation does not yet exist in them. There is no &quot;I,&quot; no fixed focal point of self. You can see it in the unfocused way their eyes move — there is no centralization. As the child grows into its identity, into its sense of &quot;I-ness,&quot; it begins to perceive itself as separate from everything else.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ الْجُنَيْدِ</span> said that in deep spiritual practice, certain experiences can arise. One type is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>, which occurs when someone is overcome by a spiritual state that is often beyond their control — it simply is uncontrollable. The scholars of this science distinguish between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span> is actually higher than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span> is where you are fixed in a condition. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span>, for instance, is where you cannot commit disobedience, because you are established in the station of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span> — you are in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span>, and so you cannot knowingly do wrong. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span>, by contrast, is when someone who has been doing wrong actions is suddenly seized by an intense state of remorse and turns to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> for forgiveness. That is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>, not a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span> is better because it endures. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> is when someone is settled in a station of detachment; the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> is a state that comes upon them and does not last. A person may experience a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> or a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span>, but it passes. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span> is above the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 213">page 213</div>
<p>Then there is what is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوَارِدُ</span> — an inrush that enters the heart, very powerful, flooding it with light and bringing with it an extraordinary expansion. That too is a temporary state. What <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ الْجُنَيْدِ</span> is saying is that the highest is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَنَاءُ</span>, and beyond <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاءٌ</span> there is something called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَقَاءٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَنَاءُ</span> is when the self is no longer aware of itself — there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِيبَة</span>, a disappearance, a loss of the experience of the self. This does not last permanently, because if it did, you would call it insanity. And this is precisely why spiritual psychosis is real. There are people who go into experiences they cannot control or understand, and they become ill. It happens regularly in this culture — many people who take up meditation, who go into Zen Buddhism or similar paths, and practice intensively, can literally lose themselves completely. This is why it is so dangerous to pursue these things without the protection of prayer, without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُضُوء</span>, without the grounding that Islam provides. Islam is all about grounding. We are not chasing spiritual experiences — we are meant to be grounded. That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has commanded. But if you do what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> commands, and you do it properly, then certain things will often happen inw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ly, in the interior self. And there is a science for understanding those things — part of which is the science of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَوَاطِر</span>, knowing whether what arises comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> has many games he can play on those pursuing this path, or whether it is angelic, or whether it comes from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 214">page 214</div>
<p>This is one of the problems with New Age practitioners: most of what happens to them — and they do have genuine experiences, people who go into New Age religion, or Zen, or Kundalini with its fire yoga and breathing practices — they cannot distinguish between the satanic, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْسَانِيّ</span>, the angelic, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَانِيّ</span>. They have no tools to tell them apart. And most of what is happening to them is from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>. Possession? Yes, absolutely — possession is very real. People open themselves up to a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِنٌّ</span> to enter them. That is absolutely the case.</p>
<p>As for group <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> — I will address that in a moment. So this idea, what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَنَاءُ</span> is, is simply that the person engaged in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> is no longer aware of their own self. And that can genuinely happen. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامُ الْجُنَيْدِ</span> says this is the highest form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَكُّر</span>. This is why when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> Ali''s son — not al-Hasan or al-Husayn, but one of his other sons — was struck by an arrow, those who wanted to remove it waited until he entered prayer. When he went into prayer, they pulled it out, and he felt nothing. That is someone truly in that state where the self is absent — completely absent.</p>
<p>There are people in India, yogis, who can do very radical things through meditation — endure extraordinary pain and the like. So these experiences are not exclusive to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s in terms of the underlying psychology. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an psyche has remarkable capacities. What <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s recognize, however, is that if a person is sincere and is genuinely remembering <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> can bring that person into a state of absolute witnessing with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, where they are no longer aware of their surroundings. Many of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> were in that state — clearly, many of them were.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 215">page 215</div>
<p>This is one of the interpretations of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>: &quot;My slave does not draw near to Me by anything more beloved to Me than the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرَائِضُ</span>, and he will continue to draw near to Me through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَوَافِل</span> until I love him. And when I love him, I become the eye with which he sees, the ear with which he hears, the tongue with which he speaks, the hand with which he grasps. And if he seeks refuge in Me, I give him refuge. If he asks Me, I give him.&quot; That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> never means that God becomes man — which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُلُولٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُلُول</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> to believe, and whoever believes it is outside of Islam. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُلُول</span> is the doctrine of divine incarnation, and that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> does not mean that. Anyone who interprets it that way is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِنْدِيق</span> — a heretic, outside of Islam. We do not believe in divine incarnation. We do not believe that God becomes creation. That is the position of Christianity, Hinduism, and similar traditions, not ours.</p>
<p>What we do believe is that a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an being can reach such a state of awareness of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِعْل</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — the acts of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — that it transforms their perception entirely. The whole world is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِعْل</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>; we do not say the world is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, but we say the world is His act. And there are people who enter a state of absolute witnessing in which they see everything as the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِعْل</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — they see <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> behind all of creation. They no longer perceive otherness the way other people do in this world of separation. They recognize that everything comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — the good and the bad — and so they see it all as good. For the moment, everything is good. That is what that experience means. Even though good and bad exist, it all comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, and because it all comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, it is all, for the moment, good — because even the bad holds wisdom: it causes growth, purification, and drawing closer to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. So even the bad is seen as good. This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَعَسَىٰ أَن تُحِبُّوا شَيْئًا وَهُوَ شَرٌّ لَّكُمْ</span></p>
<p>Maybe you love a thing and it is bad for you.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَعَسَىٰ أَن تَكْرَهُوا شَيْئًا وَهُوَ خَيْرٌ لَّكُمْ</span></p>
<p>And maybe you hate a thing, but it is good for you. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> knows, and you do not know. That is the reality of being in a state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُشَاهَدَة</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَاقَبَةٌ</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 216">page 216</div>
<p>Now, what you need to do is go through the sheet in front of you. You will find many different versions of these, which are called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْكَارُ الصَّبَاحِ وَالْمَسَاءِ</span> — the morning and evening remembrances. My recommendation, for myself and for all of you, is to do these every day to the best of your ability. If you cannot do all of them, there are certain ones you should absolutely not miss, and I will point those out. Now, this particular collection does not have a specific narration from the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span> specifying that he did this, then this, then this in sequence. So whenever you see these compiled collections, the formulations will vary. Sidi Ahmad Zarruq has one — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَفِينَةُ النَّجَاءِ مِنَ الِالْتِجَاءِ إِلَى اللَّهِ</span> — which is a very fine one, drawn entirely from the Book and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>.</p>
<p>One of the things Sidi Ahmad Zarruq says is that you should observe the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوْرَاد</span> that came down from the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span> before turning to any <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءٌ</span> written by other people. It is permissible to read <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءٌ</span> composed by others, provided those people knew what they were saying — meaning they were known to be true scholars and people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءٌ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَبْعٌ</span> had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءٌ</span>, and these have come down to us. There are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَوَات</span> that come from other than the Prophet as well. But what Sidi Ahmad Zarruq said is this: if you are neglecting the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْكَار</span> that the Prophet told us to observe every day and preferring other people''s compositions over them, then something is wrong. That is his point.</p>
<p>And Qadi Abu Bakr Ibn Arabi said, at the end of his book <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَانُونُ التَّأْوِيلِ</span>, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> primary task with any <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span> is to pull them away from the remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> altogether. But if he finds them persisting in much remembrance, he will then try to steer them away from the remembrance drawn from the Book and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>, tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> remembrance composed by other people.</p>
<p>That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> strategy. And so the beautiful thing here — and this is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِرْد</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> Abdullah Al-Had<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَادٌ</span>, who is from the noble lineage, one of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُرَفَا</span> of Yemen — is that he is considered a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَدِّد</span> of the twelfth century as well. His grandson was one of my teachers, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> Ahmad Mashhur Al-Had<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَادٌ</span>, who wrote <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِفْتَاحُ الْجَنَّةِ</span>, which has been translated into English. I received this from him, and he took it from his father, who took it from his grandfather, who took it from the author himself.</p>
<p>He begins it with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>. This follows the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> that anything worth doing, if it does not begin with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span>, is cut off from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَةٌ</span>. So anything that is truly worth doing should be done in the name of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, in the name of compassion.</p>
<p>He then begins with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ</span>, recited three times. This comes from a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَيَعْجِزُ أَحَدُكُمْ أَنْ يَخْتِمَ الْقُرْآنَ كُلَّ يَوْمٍ</span> — is one of you incapable of reading the Quran once every day? The Companions asked, &quot;Who could do that, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>?&quot; He replied that Surah al-Ikhlas — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاصُ عَدِيلُ فِرَاثِ الْقُرْآنِ</span> — is equivalent to one third of the Quran in its weight and meaning. By reading it three times, we hope for the rew<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> of having read the entire Quran. It should therefore be read every day — ideally after every prayer, and at the very least in the morning and evening.</p>
<p>The next two are called the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤَوِّلَتَيْنِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤَوِّل</span> means that which gives you refuge — from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعْوِيذَة</span>, meaning protection. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤَوِّلَتَيْنِ</span> are ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ</span> ‎ and ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ</span> ‎, and these should be read every day. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> indicating they should be recited before sleep, after which you cover yourself — a protection from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِنٌّ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيَاطِين</span>.</p>
<p>He then prescribes reciting, three times, the supplication of seeking refuge in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَمَزَاتِ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَمَزَاتٌ</span> means to goad someone on — &quot;come on, do it, do it.&quot; The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَمَزَاتِ</span> of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيَاطِين</span> are those whisperings within the soul urging you tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> what you should not do. So in this supplication you seek refuge in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَمَزَاتِ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>.</p>
<p>One of the signs of the end of time is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْذِيرُ الْحَوْصَاتِ</span> — that people''s minds will be overwhelmed by constant whispering, until that is all they hear. When you consider this now, it is striking: so many people have music, films, and television running constantly around them, and all of that is from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> — all of it, without exception. By exposing yourself to it, you are allowing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> not merely to whisper but to send visual images stre<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمِين</span>g into you as well. All of it goes into the heart. In doing so, you are slowly destroying your heart.</p>
<p>People walk around carrying all these images that surface in their minds — songs, jingles, advertising music — and everywhere you go now, in stores and shopping centers, it is all there, constantly bomb<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ing people. This supplication is a shield against the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَمَزَاتِ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> carries its own impulses as well.</p>
<p>Recited three times:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ أَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ هَمَزَاتِ الشَّيَاطِينِ وَأَعُوذُ بِكَ رَبِّهُمْ يَحْضَرُونَ</span></p>
<p><em>&quot;My Lord, I seek refuge in You from the insinuations of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيَاطِين</span>, and I seek refuge in You, my Lord, lest they be present.&quot;</em></p>
<p>You say it all together, three times separately — recite it once, then return to the beginning and recite it again.</p>
<p>As for sports: most sports involve the exposure of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوْرَة</span>. People''s flesh that one should not be looking at is on display — shorts that reveal the thighs, and so on. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> said: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَكُمْ دِينُكُمْ وَلِىَ دِينِ</span> ‎ — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says, do not keep company with those who take their lives as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَعِبٌ وَلَهْوٌ</span>, play and pastime. In fact, they call baseball in this country the national pastime. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَلَاءُ الدِّينِ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَلَاءُ الدِّينِ ها الوَطَنِ</span> — the national pastime.</p>
<p>Now, if you want to go out and actually play some sport, at least you get some physical exercise from it. But to sit and watch — I will tell you honestly: when I was growing up, baseball was my life through high school. It was all I cared about. And I simply cannot watch a game now. You could not pay me to watch a game, and I am completely serious. I loved baseball. Football too. You could not pay me. I was once at someone''s house and they were watching the Iranian-American soccer match, and I did unfortunately indulge a little — but it was at someone else''s house; I would not do that on my own. I would not waste my time that way. Those things are simply a waste of your life.</p>
<p>You need to ask yourself: how much is an hour of your life worth? How much are two hours worth? People are selling their kidneys now. If someone could buy a year of your life from you, what would you sell it for? Once you begin to realize that you will be accountable not only for your life but for every minute of it — and that life itself, along with health, is the greatest gift <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has given you after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> — you start to ask: what are you doing with what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has provided you?</p>
<p>We were created to use our time well. That does not mean you cannot enjoy yourself — there are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَاهَاة</span> things, permissible forms of enjoyment. That is precisely why the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, when attending a wedding, asked whether they had brought some entertainment. They said yes. He said that the Ansar love entertainment, and he wanted to show the Jews and the Christians that there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُسْحَة</span> in our religion — that we are not joyless, not prudish, not people who refuse to enjoy themselves or mark occasions. But what is happening now — this entire society has become one vast entertainment machine. People work solely in order to be entertained; they earn money so they can go out and spend it on entertainment. We all need to disengage from that. Wean ourselves away from this society. Just wean yourself — cut the cord. Stop nursing from the breast of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> and the breast of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَاهُوت</span>.</p>
<p>And it is pleasant, no doubt about it — it is pleasant. You sit back and watch a good film for a couple of hours. And then what? There is always an empty feeling afterw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>, the sense that you have just wasted two hours of your life. Everyone knows this feeling at the end — you watch it, and then what? Why would you do that? It is all a trick. They just trick you into it.</p>
<p>So: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفَحَسِبْتُمْ</span> — or as it appears in the next passage — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفَحَسِبْتُمْ أَنَّمَا خَلَقْنَكُمْ عَبَثًۭا</span> — do you think We created you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَذَثًا</span>, just for nothing? Did We create you in vain?</p>
<p class="ar-block">‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفَحَسِبْتُمْ أَنَّمَا خَلَقْنَكُمْ عَبَثًۭا وَأَنَّكُمْ إِلَيْنَا لَا تُرْجَعُونَ</span> ‎</p>
<p>And you will not be returned to Us? You are not going to come back to Us? Exalted is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, the King, the Truth. There is no god but He. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ ٱلْعَرْشِ ٱلْكَرِيمِ</span> — the Lord of the Noble Throne.</p>
<p class="ar-block">‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَن يَدْعُ مَعَ ٱللَّهِ إِلَهًا آخَرَ لَا بُرْهَنَ لَهُۥ بِهِۦ فَإِنَّمَا حِسَابُهُۥ عِندَ رَبِّهِۦٓ ۚ إِنَّهُۥ لَا يُفْلِحُ ٱلْكَفِرُونَ</span> ‎</p>
<p>This is a verse of the Quran that the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> would recite every day.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقِرَّبَكُ فِي الْوَرَحَمُ وَإِنتَ خَيْرُ الرَّاحِمِينَ</span> Then comes another verse — a supplication: &quot;O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, forgive, have compassion, for You are the best of those who show mercy.&quot; And then yet another verse the Prophet would recite every day: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَسُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ حِينَ تُمْسُونَ</span> — &quot;Glory be to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> when you enter the evening&quot; — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَحِينَ تُسْبِحُونَ</span> — &quot;and when you enter the morning.&quot; This is the foundation of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْكَار</span> said at those two times — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسَاءً وَالصَّبَاحُ</span> — morning and evening are the times to say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهُ ٱلْحَمْدُ فِى ٱلسَّمَوَتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَعَشِيًّۭا وَحِينَ تُظْهِرُونَ</span> ‎ — &quot;And His is all praise in the heavens and the earth, in the evening and at midday.&quot; Scholars have also cited this verse as a proof for the five prayers: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِينَ تُمْسُونَ</span> corresponds to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِينَ تُسْبِحُونَ</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَشِيًّا</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَحِينَ تُظْهِرُونَ</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظُهْر</span>.</p>
<p>As for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ الْخَيْرُ الرَّحْمِينَ</span> — &quot;Forgive, show mercy, for You are the best of those who show mercy&quot; — is this for oneself alone or for all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s? In principle it is general, but when you are making this supplication the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِغْفِرْ لِي</span> is specifically for yourself.</p>
<p>Then comes: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَخْرِجْ الْحَيَّ مَنَ الْمَيْتِينَ وَيَخْرِجْ الْمَيْتِ مَنَ الْحَيِّ</span> — &quot;He brings the living from the dead and the dead from the living.&quot; The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفَسِّرٌ</span> mention several meanings here: one is the literal example of a chick emerging from an egg, or the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an being arising from a dead, inanimate source. But there is also a spiritual meaning — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> brings <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> out of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْرٌ</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْرٌ</span> can extinguish <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>. Dead hearts come alive through faith, and living hearts can die by falling into disbelief. The Prophet also said that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> brings the earth back to life after its death, and that in the same way He will raise us up — and this too points to the heart''s capacity for revival. In the Quran, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> is used <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَجَازٍ بِالْقَلْبِ</span> as a traditional <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفْسِير</span> — so <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> revives the heart through His remembrance after it has died through heedlessness and forgetfulness of Him.</p>
<p>The Prophet also used to recite this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعْوِيذَة</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَمِيرْ عَلِيمٍ عَلِيمُ بِاللَّهِ سَمِيرْ عَلِيمٍ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ</span> — three times. That was among his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعْوِيذَات</span>.</p>
<p>Then there is the verse that the Prophet would recite in both the morning and the evening: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَوْ أَنزَلْنَا هَذَا ٱلْقُرْآنَ عَلَىٰ جَبَلٍۢ لَّرَأَيْتَهُۥ خَشِعًۭا مُّتَصَدِّعًۭا مِّنْ خَشْيَةِ ٱللَّهِ ۚ وَتِلْكَ ٱلْأَمْثَلُ نَضْرِبُهَا لِلنَّاسِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَتَفَكَّرُونَ</span> ‎ — &quot;Had We revealed this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> to a mountain, you would have seen it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>bled, torn apart by the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَشْيَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.&quot; Khashya is fear grounded in knowledge — it is different from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَوْف</span>. A rat can experience <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَوْف</span>, but a rat cannot have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَشْيَة</span>. That is why the scholars are described as those who have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَشْيَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, and why the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا أَعْلَمُكُمْ دُنْيَا</span> — &quot;I am the most knowledgeable of you concerning <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>&quot; — and therefore <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْشَاكُمْ</span> — the most filled with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَشْيَة</span>. What <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is conveying here is that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> carries such a weight of knowledge that even a mountain — an inanimate object — would be shattered and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>bled by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَشْيَة</span> if the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> were revealed to it, because of the meanings and knowledge contained within it. This is precisely why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَطْهِيرُ الْقَلْبِ</span> — deep reflection on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> — leads to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُشُوعٌ</span>, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>bled and awed awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> then says that these are similitudes He strikes for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>anity so that they might reflect. Does this mean the mountain has knowledge? Some scholars say that the mountain here is a symbol of a scholar, because in Arabic a great scholar is compared to a mountain — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هو جبل من الجبال</span> — &quot;he is a mountain among mountains.&quot; They would say of someone like Imam al-Shafii: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">امام شافع كان جبلا من الجبال</span>. So the meaning is that if this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> were revealed to anyone of immense knowledge and understanding, it would shatter them. Yet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> also tells us in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> that He offered the trust to the heavens, the earth, and the mountains. Scholars note that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> begins with the heavens because they are His most awe-inspiring creation in terms of sheer magnitude — the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span> is the greatest in terms of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامٌ</span>, but in terms of awesomeness the heavens surpass everything, and only the astronomer can begin to grasp that. Then comes the earth, then the mountains. And that refusal, scholars say, was not a refusal of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> then says He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — there is no doubt in it — the Knower of the seen and the unseen. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَة</span> means &quot;the seen,&quot; but it also carries the meaning of testimony, because everything visible is testifying to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. This is why the Prophet said: how extraordinary that a man could disobey <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, how strange that anyone could disbelieve in Him — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَفِي كُلِّ تَحْرِيكَةٍ وَفِي كُلِّ تَحْرِيكَةٍ وَتَسْكِينَةٍ</span> — in every movement and every stillness there is always a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَاهِدٌ</span>, a witness. Everything in creation is testifying that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is One. So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَة</span> — this visible world — is, in its very meaning, saying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. That is the message the entire world is sending to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an being, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an being is the one capable of decoding it.</p>
<p>This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> describes Himself as the King, the Holy, the Sanctified, the Complete. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ</span> here does not mean peace in the ordinary sense — it is wrong to render <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> name as simply &quot;peace&quot; in that casual way. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُؤْمِن</span> is the one who makes others safe, who gives <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمِين</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُهَيْمِنُ</span> is the one who oversees everything and holds all things in His control. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَزِيزُ</span> is the Great, the Powerful. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْجَبَّار</span> is the Compeller — the One who compels all things and all beings to do what they do. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُتَكَبِّرُ</span> is the Exalted, the Magnificent, the Supremely Great. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span> — &quot;Glory be to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> above all that they associate with Him.&quot; In Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ</span> means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَمْزِيح</span>: when you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span> you are declaring that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is utterly unlike anything. You are asserting His incomparability.</p>
<p>As for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَالِقُ</span>: I prefer not to use certain terms because of their complexity in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَالِقُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَارِئُ</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُصَوِّر</span> represent three aspects of the creative process — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْق</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَاءٌ</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَصْوِير</span>. Al-K<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>iq is when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> decides to create: He possesses the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُدْرَة</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْمٌ</span>, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِرَادَة</span>, and when these three converge, creation comes into being. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> ultimate reality this capacity is always present — His power to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَالِقُ</span> is eternally there. When He decides to create, He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَارِئُ</span> — the One who actually brings it into existence. Creation is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرِيئَةٌ</span>, which is why the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَيْرُ الْبَرِيَّةِ</span> — the finest of all that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرِيءٌ</span> has brought into being. Al-Musawwir is the Fashioner: He shapes and gives form. In the context of a fetus, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl"' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'the root of all diseases of the heart';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || ' lang="ar">الْخَالِقُ</span> is the One who determined there would be a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an life; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْبَارِئُ</span> is the One who brought about the seed in the man and the ovum in the woman and united them; and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُصَوِّر</span> is the One who fashions the form — the cells differentiate, the liver becomes a liver, the brain becomes a brain, the spine takes its shape. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُصَوِّر</span>.</p>
<p>In reality, however, there is no linearity in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span> says these are only facets — and because we are limited, we can only perceive one aspect at a time. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is describing for us what is in reality a multi-faceted process, but it is only multi-faceted from our limited vantage point. In truth it is all one; it is all <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Attributes are approximations for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an understanding, because we exist in time and our intellects are bounded. These are simply different facets of a single creative reality. Is that clear?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَهُ ٱلْأَسْمَآءُ ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has the most beautiful names. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُسَبِّحُ لَهُۥ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَوَتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ</span> — everything in the heavens and the earth glorifies Him, and beyond that, without exception, everything — rocks, atoms, all things — is praising <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَهُوَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْحَكِيمُ</span> — and He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَزِيزُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَكِيمُ</span>.</p>
<p>Then: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَمٌ عَلَىٰ نُوحٍۢ فِى ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّا كَذَلِكَ نَجْزِى ٱلْمُحْسِنِينَ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّهُۥ مِنْ عِبَادِنَا ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ</span>. Whether this verse was included based on a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> or on the compiler''s own <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِجْتِهَادٌ</span> is unclear. But one of its wisdoms, I think, is this: in a certain sense we are living in an age of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُوحٌ</span>. A great flood is coming — and all of us know it. When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُوحٌ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span> was building his ark, people laughed at him. Building an ark in this age — preparing for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> — invites the same ridicule. People think you are a fool. If you are genuinely concerned about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>, people assume something is wrong with you. Even <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s — they would not say outright that they do not believe in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>, because they do not want to make that admission, but all of their actions tell you otherwise. Everything they care about is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. And when they see <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s who are focused on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>, they say: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَخِي</span>, why don''t you do something for yourself? For your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْمَلْ لِدُنْيَاكَ كَأَنَّكَ تَعِيشُ أَبَدًا</span> — live for your world as if you will live forever.&quot;</p>
<p>Next comes: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَللَّهُمَّ إِنِّي أَصْبَحْتُ مِنْكَ بِنِعْمَةٍ وَعَافِيَةٍ</span> — &quot;O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, I have entered this morning in Your blessing, with good health and with my wrongs veiled by You — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَتِمَّ نِعْمَتَكَ عَلَيَّ وَعَافِيَتَكَ وَسِتْرَكَ فِي الدُّنْيَا وَالْآخِرَةِ</span> — so complete Your blessing upon me, Your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَافِيَةٌ</span>, and Your veiling, in this world and the next.&quot; This is said three times. (There are two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِوَايَة</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَتِمَّنَا</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَتْمِمْ</span> — but both are transmitted.)</p>
<p>The next supplication: the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that whoever recites it four times receives a rew<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> equivalent to that of freeing a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an being from bondage. It goes: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَللَّهُمَّ إِنِّي أُشْهِدُكَ</span> — &quot;O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, I enter this morning calling You to bear witness&quot; — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأُشْهِدُ حَمَلَةَ عَرْشِكَ</span> — &quot;and calling the angels who carry Your throne to bear witness&quot; — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَلَائِكَتَكَ وَجَمِيعَ خَلْقِكَ</span> — &quot;and all of Your angels and all of Your creation to bear witness&quot; — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَّكَ أَنتَ اللَّهُ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا أَنتَ وَحْدَكَ لَا شَرِيكَ لَكَ وَأَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُكَ وَرَسُولُكَ</span> — &quot;that You are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, there is no god but You, alone, without partner, and that Muhammad <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> is Your servant and Your messenger.&quot; This is said four times.</p>
<p>Then: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ حَمْدًا يُوَافِي نِعَمَهُ وَيُكَافِئُ مَزِيدَهُ</span> — &quot;All praise belongs to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, Lord of all the worlds, a praise commensurate with His blessings and reciprocating all the increase He bestows.&quot; This <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> encompasses the complete fulfillment of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْدٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْر</span> for all the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعَمٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. When we asked Habibullah al-Hajj for a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَصِيَّة</span> — a piece of counsel — to pass on to people, this is the supplication he gave. He would say it frequently: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ حَمْدًا يُوَافِي نِعَمَهُ وَيُكَافِئُ مَزِيدَهُ</span>.</p>
<p>Then comes a supplication the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said removes all traces of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>: &quot;I believe in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> the Magnificent, and I reject everything magical and every object worshipped alongside <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. I have taken hold of the firmest handle&quot; — which is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>, and the way of Islam — &quot;and this handle will never break as long as you hold onto it. There is no separation, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is...&quot; This is a protective supplication.</p>
<p>The following supplication: the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that whoever recites it three times receives the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِضْوَانٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, because you are saying: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَضِيتُ بِاللَّهِ رَبًّا</span> — &quot;I am content with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> as my Lord&quot; — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَبِالْإِسْلَامِ دِينًا</span> — &quot;and with Islam as my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span>&quot; — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَبِمُحَمَّدٍ</span> — or, in some recitations, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسَيِّدِنَا</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَبِيًّا وَرَسُولًا</span> — &quot;and with Muhammad <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> as my Prophet and Messenger.&quot; As for adding <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span>: it is not found in the transmitted wording, but adding it out of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأَدُّب</span> — out of reverence — is permissible, and this is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخَارَفَةً تَأَدُّبًا</span>, as has been discussed. Just as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> Ali refused to remove the words &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُّحَمَّدٌۭ رَّسُولُ ٱللَّهِ</span>&quot; from the treaty of Hudaybiyyah, one may add <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا</span> out of the same spirit of reverence. Both forms exist, but the actual wording transmitted in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَارِد</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِمُحَمَّدٍ صلى الله عليه وسلم نَبِيًّا وَرَسُولًا</span>..</p>
<p>Then the next supplication: the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said to recite it seven times in the morning and seven times in the evening — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسْبِىَ ٱللَّهُ لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا هُوَ عَلَيْهِ تَوَكَّلْتُ وَهُوَ رَبُّ ٱلْعَرْشِ ٱلْعَظِيمِ</span> — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is enough for me; there is no god but Him. I have placed my trust in Him, and He is the Lord of the magnificent throne.&quot; Seven times.</p>
<p>Then: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَآلِهِ وَسَلِّمْ</span> — a concise prayer upon the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>. We know from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> that Jibril brought the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> the glad tidings that whoever sends one prayer upon him receives ten times the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> in return. So the instruction here is to do it ten times — which equals a hundred instances of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ رَحْمَة</span> upon you. It is preferable to send prayers upon the Prophet at least a hundred times in the morning and a hundred times in the evening. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> Muhammad, for an extended period of his life, would do it five thousand times a day as a spiritual practice. For fourteen years he maintained a practice of supplication twice daily, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَتْمٌ</span> of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> once daily, and one hundred and fourteen thousand recitations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span> every single day. Those who were present said that when he emerged from his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْوَة</span>, people would make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَة</span> simply by seeing him.</p>
<p>He lived in Fes at a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْرَسَة</span>, and he had a room there — a room he occupied so long and so deeply that he memorized one of the most demanding books of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِي فِقْه</span>, a multi-volume work, entirely by heart. He was a great <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِي</span> scholar and a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَقِيهٌ</span>. But people talked — they wondered why he never left that room. They assumed he must be hiding treasure up there, some gold he did not want anyone to find. So they went to his father to persuade him to go up, because he would not allow anyone to enter, and there was a ladder to reach it. His father came up, and he let him in — and when his father entered, he found the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْتٌ</span> — &quot;death&quot; — written across every wall. There was nothing else in the room. His father understood: he realized that some people are in a world entirely their own, and others simply cannot comprehend it. That man was in a completely different realm — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>.</p>
<p>As for the form of the prayer on the Prophet: if you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span> then the verb is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَّمَ</span> with a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَتْحًا</span>. But if you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَآلِهِ وَسَلِّمْ</span> — like that — then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلِّمْ</span> carries a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَسْرَة</span>, because this is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِيغَةُ الدُّعَاءِ</span>, the form of supplication. Even though it uses what looks like a past-tense verb, it is actually a supplication — like when you say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللَّهُ</span>, you are saying &quot;may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> have mercy on him,&quot; but you use the past tense <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَاؤُلًا</span> — as if it has already been accepted. Similarly, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَّضِىَ ٱللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ</span> is said of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> in the past tense, though you could equally say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَللَّهُمَّ ارْضَ عَنْ أَبِي بَكْرٍ وَعُمَرَ وَعُثْمَانَ وَعَلِيٍّ</span> — using the direct form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>. So with the direct form — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِيغَةُ الدُّعَاءِ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِيغَةُ الْأَمْرِ</span> — you would say <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَسَلِّمْ</span> with a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَسْرَة</span>. There are various forms one may use; this particular one is shorter and so lends itself to higher volume of repetition. I believe this is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِيغَة</span> that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> used.</p>
<p>After that comes a supplication in which the Prophet asked <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> for good surprises and sought refuge from bad ones — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُجَاءً</span> being something that happens suddenly and unexpectedly. So you are asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> to let only good surprises reach you in your day, and to shield you from the harmful ones.</p>
<p>Now we come to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدُ الاِسْتِغْفَارِ</span>. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said — and it is in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحِيح حَدِيث</span> — that this is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدُ الاِسْتِغْفَارِ</span>, the foremost of all ways of seeking forgiveness, the best form in which a person can ask for it. You say: &quot;O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, You are my Lord; there is no god but You. You created me and I am Your servant. I am upon Your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْد</span> — the covenant of Islam — and Your promise to forgive me and admit me into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّة</span>, to the best of my ability.&quot; In other words: as best I am able, I am doing what You have commanded me to do. &quot;I seek refuge in You from the evil I have done. I return to You, acknowledging that all my blessings are from You — I am returning them to You, admitting they are Yours entirely. And I take full responsibility for my wrong actions: they are mine, I committed them, I admit them. So forgive me, for none can forgive wrong actions but You.&quot; That is this supplication.</p>
<p>Then: &quot;Whatever <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> willed came to be, and whatever He did not will did not come to be&quot; — this is sometimes said in times of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِصْبَة</span>, calamity, as an acknowledgement and surrender. &quot;And I know that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is powerful over all things, and that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> encompasses all things in His knowledge.&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;I seek refuge from the evil of my own soul and from the evil of every creature that You hold by its forelock.&quot; This is a reminder of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>: every creature is in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> grip. Their evil reaches you only because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — who holds them by the forelock — has permitted it. So when you seek refuge in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> from the evil of others, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> will protect you from that evil. There are many <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> that speak to this, and it is a powerful supplication. There is also a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> of reciting it forty-one times before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>. These two names invoked in it are very powerful names.</p>
<p>&quot;I am seeking through Your mercy, and I am seeking safety from Your punishment — rectify all of my affairs and do not leave me to my own soul, and do not leave me to anyone from Your creation, even for the blink of an eye. Be my sole reliance — do not make me dependent on anyone other than You.&quot; This does not mean you will never receive help from others — but you recognize that all help comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> alone. That is the meaning: do not let me think the help is coming from other than You.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَعْفَر الصَّادِق</span>, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be pleased with him, was once c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>lenged by an atheist who told him he did not believe in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَعْفَر الصَّادِق</span> said to him: &quot;Have you ever been at sea?&quot; The man said yes — and then told him what had happened: he had been on a boat when a violent storm struck, so severe that the vessel was torn to pieces. All the sailors drowned. He alone survived, clinging to a plank of wood. Then the ocean took even the plank from his hands, and he had nothing. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَعْفَر الصَّادِق</span> said: &quot;What did you do?&quot; He said: &quot;The sea flung me to the shore and I survived.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَعْفَر الصَّادِق</span> said to him: &quot;When you bo<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ed that boat, you put your trust in the boat and in the sailors. When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> took those away from you, you put your trust in that little piece of wood. And when you lost the wood — where did you put your trust then? Did you hope to survive?&quot; He said: &quot;Yes, I hoped that I would.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَعْفَر الصَّادِق</span> said: &quot;Who did you hope in?&quot; Because in Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَاءٌ</span> is a transitive verb — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَجَوْتُ</span> requires an object. You cannot hope in the abstract; hope must have an object. So he pressed him: &quot;From whom were you hoping for safety?&quot; Then he said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا عَبْدَ اللَّهِ</span> — O servant of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — the One who took away every means you had, and then saved you despite the absence of every means — that was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.&quot; That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَكِلْنِي إِلَى نَفْسِي وَلَا إِلَى أَحَدٍ مِنْ خَلْقِكَ طَرْفَةَ عَيْنٍ</span> — &quot;do not leave me to myself or to any of Your creation for even the blink of an eye.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا أَقَلَّ مِنْ ذَلِكَ</span> — not even less than that.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَللَّهُمَّ إِنِّي أَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنَ الْهَمِّ وَالْحَزَنِ</span> — Sheikh al-Khatri told me that someone once came to him without a job, and he gave him this supplication. Within a few days, that person had found work. It comes from a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which a man came to the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> and said: &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, I am crushed by debts and overwhelmed by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُزْن</span>.&quot; The Prophet gave him this supplication, and the man said he began reciting it and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> resolved all his debts and lifted his grief. So: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَللَّهُمَّ إِنِّي أَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنَ الْهَمِّ وَالْحَزَنِ</span> — &quot;O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, I seek refuge in You from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُزْن</span>&quot; — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنَ الْعَجْزِ وَالْكَسَلِ</span> — &quot;and I seek refuge in You from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَجْز</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَسَلٌ</span>.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَمٌّ</span> is anxiety about the future — worry over what has not yet come. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُزْن</span> is grief over the past — sorrow over what has already gone. One concerns the future, the other the past. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَآ إِنَّ أَوْلِيَآءَ ٱللَّهِ لَا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ</span> — the friends of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> have no fear about the future nor grief about the past. This is about entering the present moment, because there is no past or future for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. When you are in a state of awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, the past holds no anxiety and the future holds no dread — you cannot change the past, and the future is entirely in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> hands. All you have is the present. So there is no point in carrying grief about the past or anxiety about the future.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَجْز</span> is the condition of someone who has the desire but lacks the ability — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِرَادَة</span> without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُدْرَة</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَسَلٌ</span> is the opposite: having the ability but lacking the will — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُدْرَة</span> without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِرَادَة</span>. Both are states to seek refuge from. You want to be someone who has both: the ability to do what needs to be done, and the will to do what should be done.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنَ الْجُبْنِ وَالْبُخْلِ</span> — &quot;I seek refuge in You from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُبْنٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُخْل</span>.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُبْنٌ</span> is cow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ice — the Arabic word comes from the image of curdled cheese: a cow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>, when firmness is required, collapses. They lack <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثَبَاتٌ</span>, steadiness. You are seeking refuge from the inability to give yourself with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَجَاعَة</span> when it is called for. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُخْل</span> is miserliness — the cow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ice of wealth. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُبْنٌ</span> is the cow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ice of the self and body; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُخْل</span> is the cow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ice of wealth and property. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ ٱشْتَرَىٰ مِنَ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَنفُسَهُمْ وَأَمْوَلَهُم بِأَنَّ لَهُمُ ٱلْجَنَّةَ</span> — &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has purchased from the believers their selves and their wealth, and in exchange is Paradise.&quot; What He purchased requires <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> with both <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِأَمْوَالِهِمْ وَأَنْفُسِهِمْ</span> — their wealth and their selves. If you are a cow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> you cannot give yourself in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> with your body. If you are a miser you cannot give in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> with your wealth. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُبْنٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُخْل</span> are the two things that prevent you from fulfilling what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has purchased from you — which is why seeking refuge from them is so important.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ غَلَبَةِ الدَّيْنِ وَقَهْرِ الرِّجَالِ</span> — &quot;I seek refuge in You from overwhelming debt and from being overpowered by men.&quot; These two are paired deliberately. If you study history, you will find that one of the primary means by which the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَفَّرَ</span> gained control over the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s was through debt. They would lend <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s money — on interest — and when the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s could not repay, the debt had compounded to a point where they could no longer meet their obligations. That is when the demands for concessions began. Debt is a mechanism of control.</p>
<p>In this country, the entire economic system is designed to put people into debt. The tax laws are structured that way. The banking system was built that way — because those who hold your debt hold power over you. They make people into economic slaves. People will resist physical enslavement — they will rebel against it. But economic slavery is different: it comes with guilt. In German, the word for debt — Schuld — is the same word for guilt. When you are in debt, you feel you owe something; you feel diminished. And through that sense of obligation, people become enslaved. This is a deep wisdom in seeking refuge from debt and from being overpowered by others — because the two go together.</p>
<p>This is also why the scholars said: if there were anything better to ask for than <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَافِيَةٌ</span> — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَافِيَةٌ</span> in its full meaning encompasses Islam itself, as well as spiritual, physical, and economic well-being — the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> would have told his uncle <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبَّاس</span> to ask for it. So asking <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَافِيَةٌ</span> is among the most comprehensive of supplications.</p>
<p>And this is why, when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا عُمَر</span> went to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَمٌّ</span> and found <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا مُعَاوِيَة</span> wearing a magnificent <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَمِّي</span> robe, richly ornamented, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُمَر</span> was troubled by it.</p>
<p>And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا عُثْمَان</span> said to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا عُمَر</span>: these are people — if you come to them dressed the way we dress in the desert, they will think nothing of you. That was the wisdom behind it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا عُمَر</span> did remove his robe and put on a finer one, then mounted a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرْزُونْ</span> — but then he dismounted, put his own robes back on, and refused. He simply said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> gave us dignity through our Islam, not through outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> trappings. And if we seek honor in anything other than that, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> will <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>iliate us.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 302">page 302</div>
<p>Remarkably, in Jerusalem, their own books had foretold that the man who would open the city would have — I believe — seventy-three patches on his robe. When they counted the patches on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدِنَا عُمَرَ</span> robe, the number matched what was written in their books. Seventy-two? Seventeen? I am not entirely certain of the exact figure.</p>
<p>Now, reg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ing the last point someone raised — about group <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>: there is a scholarly disagreement on this. Some scholars considered it a blameworthy innovation — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَةٌ مَكْرُوهَةٌ</span> — meaning not forbidden outright, but disapproved. What troubled them was the absence of a clear narration showing that the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span> or the Companions practiced it in that specific form. Imam al-Shafii, on the other hand, considered it permissible and saw nothing wrong with it. He drew on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> indicating that there was a teacher with a group of learners, and that the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> sat among those learning and said, &quot;I was sent as a teacher&quot; — though he did not rebuke the other group either. However, because of the weakness of that particular narration, it is difficult to derive a firm ruling from it alone.</p>
<p>There is, however, a completely sound <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> stating that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has traveling angels — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلَائِكَةٌ سَيَّارَةٌ</span> — who roam the earth searching for circles of remembrance, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِلَقَاتُ الذِّكْرِ</span>. When they come upon one, they descend upon it. Then, when they return to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, He asks them — and this is to teach us; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, of course, knows — &quot;How did you find My servants?&quot; And they reply: &quot;We found them saying...&quot; The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> indicates they were gathered in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَلْقَة</span>.</p>
<p>Some scholars derived from this that it is permissible to gather for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>, but not necessarily to recite in unison, since the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> does not specify a single voice. Other scholars held that there is no prohibition on reciting in unison, since <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> itself is permissible and no clear prohibition of that form exists. They noted that there are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> showing the Companions did say certain things together — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَكْبِير</span> and the like — so collective vocalization is not without precedent.</p>
<p>These are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافِي</span> matters. The majority of scholars, particularly the later ones, permitted group <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَحِّدٌ</span> actively encouraged it in Morocco, including the communal recitation of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> in a single voice. They established a practice in Morocco that continues to this day: completing the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> every month in the mosques. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَحِّدٌ</span> initiated this because they observed that people had stopped reading the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> — and by setting a collective recitation in the mosque, people would join in simply because it was there and it was a good thing to do. Their reasoning was that it was better for people to read and complete the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> than not to read it at all.</p>
<p>Ibn al-Hajj disagreed with this in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْخَل</span>. On the question of poetry, he noted that the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> loved to hear it — there are many sound <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> on this — and that he would listen and say, in effect, &quot;Yes, let us hear more.&quot; The Arabs used to sing their poetry — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلْحَانُ الْعَرَبِ</span> — in their gatherings. So in the tradition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَشِيدٌ</span>, such as one finds in Palestine, Syria, and Morocco, there are sung poems in praise of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and of the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span>. That is permissible, and it is in fact a wholesome form of entertainment — beautiful to listen to when done well, with good voices. People would be far better served listening to that than to music and the like.</p>
<p>As for a translation — I do not have one written down, but I just gave you one, more or less, so it is on tape. It would need to be polished, since I rendered it on the spot. But even without the meanings, these things carry benefit.</p>
<p>On the question of what Abu Hanifa said about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> — as far as I know, the Hanafi <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَذْهَب</span> accepts group <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>. And historically, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s did practice it. If you study the early books, you will find that group <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> was indeed practiced.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرَابِطُ الْحَاجِّ</span> does not do it in that form — he is like Ibn al-Hajj in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَذْهَب</span>, cautious about anything that even carries a scent of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَة</span>. But he does not make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْكَار</span> of others, because he is a scholar and he knows the ruling. He himself simply exercises caution — that is all. And yet if you spend time with him, all he does is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>. Being in his company is itself a form of group <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>, because everyone around him is in a state of remembrance. So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>The essential point is: remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Those who remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> standing, sitting, and reclining on their sides — that is the stand<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>. And there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> that Sheikh Khatri mentioned in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said: &quot;S<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ</span>l I not tell you what the best of your actions is, the purest in the sight of your Lord, the highest in your ranks, better for you than spending gold and silver, and better for you than that you should go out and strike the necks of the enemy and they strike yours?&quot; They said: &quot;Tell us, O Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.&quot; He said: &quot;The remembrance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرُ اللَّهِ</span>.&quot;</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 305">page 305</div>
<p>The scholars stipulate, of course, that if a city is under attack, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> takes precedence over everything. So these matters must always be understood in context. If defenders are needed at the walls and a group instead sits saying &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> is better&quot; — that is ignorance and foolishness. Everything has its context. But in its proper place, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> is the highest act. This is why the scholars have said that performing voluntary <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span> — when the communal obligation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> is already being fulfilled — is superior to going out on voluntary <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span>. The reason is fundamental: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> is a means to an end, and an end always ranks above the means. We were not created to kill people. We were created to remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. But if people are preventing us from remembering <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, killing us for it, then we must go out and stop them — and that is the point.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> holds the immense rank that it holds. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>:</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اسْتَجِيبُوا لِلَّهِ وَلِلرَّسُولِ إِذَا دَعَاكُمْ لِمَا يُحْيِيكُمْ</span></p>
<p>&quot;O you who believe, answer <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and His Messenger when they call you to what brings you to life.&quot; The scholars have said, nearly by consensus, that this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span> is interpreted as referring to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> — for that is what brings this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> back to life. So let no one belittle <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> or the station of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهِدٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهِدٌ</span> is honored in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, and all of us should carry within ourselves a longing for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَة</span>. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> said that whoever dies and never even entertained in his heart the thought &quot;I wish I could go on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span>&quot; dies a form of spiritual death — a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِفَاق</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 306">page 306</div>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِهَادٌ</span> is immense. I am not belittling it in any way. But what people have forgotten is this: what will give <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s the certainty — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقِين</span> — they need when they go out to fight, and what will make them capable of fighting solely for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> sake, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Because there are many <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s today who want to go out and fight simply because they are angry. Truly. They are angry at the world, angry at the Americans, angry at one thing or another. And there is much in the world that is legitimately angering. But anger alone is not spirituality. It is not a purified state of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>. Dhikr is what rectifies the heart and elevates its motivation from the self to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. That is the work.</p>
<p>As for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلِد</span> — that is another <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافِي</span> matter. Personally, I believe the scholars settled these questions centuries ago, and it is genuinely saddening that we are still debating them. The formal celebration of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلِد</span> on a fixed date was not something the first community practiced in that organized form. But there is no doubt that they were celebrating him every single day of their lives. There is a sound <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which a man asked the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> whether he could fast on Mondays, and the Prophet replied: &quot;That is the day I was born&quot; — indicating it was an excellent day to observe the fast. That is a sound <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>.</p>
<p>There is also a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> mentioned that Abu Lahab — the bitter enemy of Islam — is given a small relief from punishment every Monday in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَهَنَّم</span>, amounting to</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 307">page 307</div>
<p>water between his t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>b and forefinger, because he freed a slave girl on the day the Prophet was born. In other words, even Abu Lahab''s spontaneous joy at the Prophet''s birth earned him a weekly reprieve from torment. So one should never say &quot;we do not celebrate the Prophet''s birthday.&quot; And I have he<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> people say that. There is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> that a man will utter a word without thinking, and it will carry him further than he imagines. You should never say that. If you wish to frame it in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْه</span> terms, you may say: &quot;To celebrate his birthday on a specific fixed date is something the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَفٌ</span> did not formalize.&quot; But to say &quot;we do not celebrate his birthday&quot; — no. Never. We celebrate his birth. We should be happy every day of our lives that he was born, because it is only through him that we came to know <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, and it is only through his sacrifice that we have the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> today. We should celebrate every single day that he came into this world.</p>
<p>Holding a gathering on the twelfth of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِيعُ الْأَوَّلِ</span> is a later innovation, yes. Most scholars called it a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَةٌ حَسَنَةٌ</span> — a praiseworthy innovation. Some scholars were against it; that is true. But the majority were not. Imam al-Suyuti wrote a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَتْوَى</span> saying it was a good thing and that people should do it. You can find that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَتْوَى</span> in his collected <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَتْوًى</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَاوِي لِلْفَتَاوِي</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 308">page 308</div>
<p>So if you choose not to observe it in that form, that is fine. But consider the person whose priorities are entirely disordered: he attends the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلِد</span> celebration once a year and does nothing the Prophet commanded for the rest of the year. That is truly shameful. If you want to celebrate the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, do what he told you to do — that is what will genuinely honor him. Coming together once a year for a celebration and then ignoring his guidance for the remaining three hundred and sixty-four days is a contradiction. What is genuinely good is to hear the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيرَة</span>, and traditionally that is what these gatherings consisted of: reciting the account of his birth and his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنَاقِب</span> — the noble qualities and virtues of the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ ﷺ</span>. That is a sound and beneficial practice.</p>
<p>On the question of which date the Prophet was actually born — that is a separate dispute between Eastern and Western scholarship. The argument over the precise date is one of the classical objections raised against fixing the celebration to a single day. Time magazine even ran an article about this once, with a photograph of someone celebrating the Prophet''s birthday as though it were literally a birthday party — candles and all.</p>
<p class="ar-block">There you go. We should pray <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْحَكِيمُ الْعَلِيمُ، اللَّهُمَّ افْتَحْ عَلَيْنَا حِكْمَتَكَ وَانْشُرْ عَلَيْنَا رَحْمَتَكَ يَا ذَا الْجَلَالِ وَالْإِكْرَامِ، اللَّهُمَّ افْتَحْ عَلَيْنَا حِكْمَتَكَ وَانْشُرْ عَلَيْنَا رَحْمَتَكَ يَا ذَا الْجَلَالِ وَالْإِكْرَامِ، اللَّهُمَّ افْتَحْ عَلَيْنَا حِكْمَتَكَ وَانْشُرْ عَلَيْنَا رَحْمَتَكَ يَا ذَا الْجَلَالِ وَالْإِكْرَامِ، وَصَلِّ اللَّهُمَّ عَلَىٰ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَآلِهِ وَصَحْبِهِ وَسَلِّمْ تَسْلِيمًا، وَلَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللَّهِ الْعَلِيِّ الْعَظِيمِ، وَلَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللَّهِ الْعَلِيِّ الْعَظِيمِ، وَلَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللَّهِ الْعَلِيِّ الْعَظِيمِ، بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ، بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. I think it is a blessing that we have come to the end of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُظَاهَرَات</span>, and for most of us I believe this is the first day of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> — which is truly the month of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُظَاهَرَة</span>. It feels entirely fitting that we should conclude the study of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُظَاهَرَا' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'the root of all diseases of the heart';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'تُ الْقُلُوبِ</span> at the opening of this month, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is the greatest practical school for implementing everything we have learned over the past twelve weeks about the heart. The purpose of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is to purify the heart with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> — that is its purpose, that is its blessing, and that is its secret: it exists to purify the heart.</p>
<p>I want to say something about — I''m not sure how many of you were here last night — how many people saw the moon last night? How many went out to look for it? About the same number. For those who were here, I think we all had a truly wonderful experience. Were you here with us? Who was here? Wasn''t that a remarkable experience?</p>
<p>I was also thinking this morning about yesterday and seeing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِلَال</span>, and something struck me that I find to be a genuinely interesting perspective. When we saw the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِلَال</span> last night for the first time, my initial reaction was uncertainty — I didn''t know whether it was really there. I actually wondered whether my eyes were playing tricks on me, because it seemed to come into existence and then vanish again, flickering in and out. I realized that when we first went out, there was nothing there but the light of the sun. We had to wait. Then Venus appeared, and then, after some time, we were finally able to see that small sliver.</p>
<p>What I came to understand is this: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says that they ask you about the new moons, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> directed the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> to tell them that these moons are simply markers of time. But the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفَسِّرُونَ</span> explain that the reason people asked about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِلَال</span> was that they were trying to understand the mechanism — how does this new moon come into being? The reason for that question is that people in antiquity actually believed that every moon was literally a new moon: that it disappeared completely and then began again from nothing. They wanted to understand how that was happening.</p>
<p>What I realized is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, in His response, did not negate their belief — He simply explained why the moon is there. It is their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوَاقِيت</span>, their appointed times: a way of marking time for people, for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span>, to determine the times of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span>. And one of the things that Qadi Abu Bakr ibn al-Arabi said in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفْسِير</span> is that the secret of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> using a lunar calendar rather than a solar calendar is that the sun governs worldly benefits while the moon governs otherworldly benefits. So the orientation of time for this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> is tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> the next world, not tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> this one.</p>
<p>What I also realized is this: if you look at it from a purely direct, non-abstract perspective, what you witness when you watch the new moon is what philosophers call emergence — and this is the real problem for those who deny the Creator. How do things come into existence out of nothing? Why is there something rather than nothing? That is their problem. And there you are, sitting, looking at the sky, and suddenly this thing emerges out of nothing right before your eyes — because you cannot see it initially. The reason you cannot see it is the sun: the sun''s light is too strong. The reason you are then able to see it is that the sun''s light has dimmed. And what emerges is reflected light. What you are seeing is reflected light — and really, the entire creation is reflected light. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is the source; the entire creation is reflected light from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. The only reason we can see it is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> allows it to exist. He is the one who brings it into existence and the one who takes it out of existence. What you are witnessing is this extraordinary event: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is letting you see reflected light come into being. That is the new moon. That is why it is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِهْلَال</span> — the birth. Just as a child must be born, this is the birth of reflected light, which is creation itself, because all of it reflects <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. The whole thing is a reflection of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كيف يجحد الاله يا عجب كيف يعصى الاله عن كيف يجحده الجاهد ولله في كل تحريكة تسكينة أبدا شاهد وفي كل شهر آية تدل على أنه واحد</span></p>
<p>How strange that we can disobey <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — and stranger still that anyone can disbelieve in Him — when in every movement and every stillness there is a clear witness for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, and in everything itself there is a sign.</p>
<p>So the creation sets up these meanings in these images, and what we are doing is trying to decode what those meanings are. It is a genuine gift to witness the birth of the new moon — a real gift. I think people felt that last night, because instead of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْمُ الْيَقِينِ</span> you had <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَيْنُ الْيَقِينِ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> speaks of three levels: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْمُ الْيَقِينِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَيْنُ الْيَقِينِ</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقُّ الْيَقِينِ</span>. Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span>s example is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْمُ الْيَقِينِ</span> is like hearing a trustworthy person tell you there is a fire in the forest — you believe them, but that is not the same as seeing the fire yourself. When you go and actually see it, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَيْنُ الْيَقِينِ</span>. And if you go and touch it, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقُّ الْيَقِينِ</span>. The new moon is an amazing thing in that reg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>. Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَكِيمُ</span> recorded a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> in which the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَيْرُ عِبَادِ اللهِ الَّذِينَ يُرَاعُونَ الْأَذِينَ وَالْأَحِنَّةَ ذِكْرًا لِلهِ</span> — the best of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهِ</span> servants are those who are vigilant about new moons and prayer times as a means of remembering <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. And I think for all of us last night, the natural impulse when we saw it was precisely to remember <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لِتُكَبِّرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ عَلَىٰ مَا هَدَىٰكُمْ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. And for me personally, there was a real <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَشَبٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> that entered my heart — a feeling, a surge of energy — because it was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَيْنُ الْيَقِينِ</span>. That was the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> moon. It is a real gift.</p>
<p>Anyway, I just wanted to share that with you. So let us review what we covered. We went through the diseases of the heart: we began by looking at the importance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدَب</span>, and then we ex<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمِين</span>ed the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُكْم</span> of knowing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمْرَاضُ الْقُلُوبِ وَأَذِيَّتُهَا</span> and their treatments — which, as Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span> stated, constitutes a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْضُ عَيْنٍ</span> — and then he went through the diseases of the heart alphabetically. He obviously gives the most attention to those that matter most. The scholars who have narrowed it down say that if you can control roughly four or five of these diseases, the rest become relatively easy to remove. But he began with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُخْل</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is a month that is truly suited to curing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُخْل</span>. They said that the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, was the most generous of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an beings and was even more generous during <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> — described as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرِيءُ الْمُرْسَلَةِ</span>, like a rain-bearing wind that brings gifts and causes things to flourish. So there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُخْل</span>. Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَطَر</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَطِرَتْ مَعِيشَتَهَا</span>...</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَطَرَ مَعِيشَتَهُمْ</span>: being delighted by the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, by one''s livelihood. The believer''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرَآهُ</span>, his joy, is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِينَمَا يُفْطِرُ وَحِينَمَا يَلْقَى رَبَّهُ</span> — when he breaks his fast and when he meets his Lord. So that stands in direct contrast to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَطَر</span>.</p>
<p>Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُغْضٌ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is a time when you are supposed to address <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُغْضٌ</span> — enmity between hearts. The Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفْضَلُ الصَّدَقَةِ فِي رَمَضَانَ</span> — the best <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span> is in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> — and in another <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفْضَلُ صَدَقَةٍ يُصْلَحُ بَيْنَ اثْنَيْنِ</span> — the best <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span> is rectifying relations between people. So the best <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span> of all would be reconciling between people during <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>. This is the time to remove <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُغْضٌ</span> from your heart tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> others.</p>
<p>Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَغْي</span> — oppression and transgression against others. You are fasting, and the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, told us: if someone provokes you or fights with you — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَنْتَ صَائِمٌ، فَقُلْ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ إِنِّي صَائِمٌ</span> — while you are fasting, say: I am fasting. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَبْغِ</span>: do not fight back. Tell them you don''t want to go there. Say: I''m fasting.</p>
<p>Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الْجَاهِ</span> — love of position and status. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is about gaining a position with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. The secret of fasting is that it is the only act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> that no one can tell you are performing while you are performing it. If you give charity, that is a visible act. If you pray, that is a visible act. If you make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجٌّ</span>, that is visible. All the acts of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span> that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has given us can be seen outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ly. Fasting alone is invisible — you can be standing next to someone who is not fasting and someone who is fasting, and only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> knows which is which. You can be among all these non-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s; they see you and have no idea you are fasting. You look like everyone else. The point is that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْزِلَة</span> you are seeking is with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, not with people. That is the cure for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الْجَاهِ</span>.</p>
<p>Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الدُّنْيَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is also about weaning yourself from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> is like a child: if you neglect to wean it, it grows up clinging to what it loves — love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> — but if you wean it, it becomes weaned. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is the time to wean the soul from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> and from the love of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>.</p>
<p>Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الْمَدْحِ بِلَا فِعْلٍ</span> — loving to be praised for things you have not done. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is the time to witness the shortcomings of your own actions, to genuinely recognize that you have wasted the whole year, and that now it is time to make up for it. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span> you didn''t do in the previous year — make it up now. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقَةٌ</span> you neglected — give it now. What you are trying to do is perform actions that actually warrant praise. So this disease too has its cure in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>.</p>
<p>Then there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَد</span> — envy. To purify the heart from envy. But if you can conquer desire — because envy arises from desire — then you can remove envy from your heart. And desire, according to Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span>, is rooted in the two appetites of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرَحٌ</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَطْن</span>. If you can master those two — not destroy them, but bring them under control — then all the other desires become manageable.</p>
<p>Then he mentions that what we are really doing in this month is defeating the enmity of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> by taking control of all the things <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> uses to wage war against the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِرَادَة</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an beings — His will for them, which is that they believe in Him and follow Him. That is why he mentions that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says: if you give victory to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> through fasting and conquering <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> will give you victory and make you firm — and that is what you want. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span> is part of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثَبَاتٌ</span> — steadfastness. We are learning both. And by doing that, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says He is with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحْسِنِينَ</span>. He also mentions the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span> that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> will not take away His blessings from a people until they become preoccupied with their <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَوَات</span>. And you can see that in this society — it is the best example of a pleasure culture. Everything is oriented tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> gratification. The idea of fasting is completely alien to most people now because they eat all day long. Not that long ago, people did not graze all day — there were no snack machines everywhere. This grazing culture is something new.</p>
<p>Now, reg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ing the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيَاطِين</span> being chained in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> — it is only the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيَاطِين</span>, meaning the bad <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِنٌّ</span>, as the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> indicates. And some of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span>, like Imam al-Qadisi al-Farsi who was a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِي</span> scholar, was asked why people still experience bad thoughts in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>. His explanation was that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>s indicate only the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَافِرٌ جِنٌّ</span> are locked up, while the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُسَّاق</span> from among the believing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِنٌّ</span> are not. He admitted, however, that he had no textual basis for that, and said that it seems a high probability — and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> knows best.</p>
<p>On a different note, it would genuinely be interesting to conduct serious statistical studies on whether crime rates go down in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>. That would require the kind of resources and stability you have when there is a functioning <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافَة</span> and peace in the land — but someone should attempt a PhD thesis on it and convince a university to fund the research.</p>
<p>Now, a couple of important points. Imam <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَزَالِيّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللهُ</span>, goes into the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْهِي</span> issues of fasting, which we covered last night. There are two levels of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْه</span>: the outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ فِقْه</span>, which is the concern of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُقَهَاء</span>, and the inw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ فِقْه</span>, which is the concern of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَوْمٌ</span> — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُفِيَ</span>. The outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ فِقْه</span> ensures that your outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> fasting is legally valid. The inw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ فِقْه</span> ensures that your inw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> fasting is sound. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> makes this very clear, in which the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, said that many people who fast gain nothing from their fast except hunger and thirst. Their outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> fast may be correct, but inw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ly they are breaking their fast constantly — because if you are backbiting, and doing all these things, you are breaking the fast of the heart.</p>
<p>He describes three types of fasting: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَوْمُ الْعُمُومِ</span> — the fast of the general public; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَوْمُ الْخُصُوصِ</span> — the fast of the elect; and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَوْمُ خُصُوصِ الْخُصُوصِ</span> — the fast of the elect of the elect.</p>
<p>The fast of the common people is simply restraining the stomach and the genitals from pleasure during the daylight hours. That is the fast any <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span> is capable of observing.</p>
<p>The fast of the elect is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَفُّ السَّمْعِ وَالْبَصَرِ وَاللِّسَانِ وَالْيَدِ وَالرِّجْلِ</span> — gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ing the ears, the eyes, the tongue, the hands, and the feet. Remember that Ibn Ashir, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللهُ</span>, speaks of the seven <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَوَارِح</span> — the seven limbs — for which we are responsible: the eyes, the ears, the tongue, the hand, the stomach, the genitals, and the feet. Two of those seven are addressed by outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> fasting — the stomach and the genitals — and so the person who abstains from food and sexual relations has gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ed two of the seven. These are called the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدَاخِلُ الشَّيْطَانِ</span>, or the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُرُوقُ الشَّيْطَانِ</span>: the entrances of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span>. Ibn al-Qayyim, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللهُ</span>, noted that the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an body is like a country: the capital is the heart, and the borders are the seven limbs. You must gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> those seven limbs with vigilance. The heart is the one that sends out the command to gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> the limbs — it dispatches the armies to the borders. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> enters the heart through one of those seven things. So the point of fasting is to protect the borders during <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> and gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> them so that the heart has a chance to draw near to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.</p>
<p class="ar-block">‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ آمَنُوا۟ كُتِبَ عَلَيْكُمُ ٱلصِّيَامُ كَمَا كُتِبَ عَلَى ٱلَّذِينَ مِن قَبْلِكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَتَّقُونَ</span> ‎</p>
<p>Fasting is prescribed so that you may learn <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> —</p>
<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نحن التقوى ها هنا وأشار إلى قلبه</span></p>
<p>— the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نحن التقوى ها هنا وأشار إلى قلبه ثلاث مرات</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is here, pointing to his heart, three times. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> resides in the heart. What you are doing through fasting is protecting the heart — by gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ing the entrances through which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْطَان</span> would enter it. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has helped you: He has placed constraints on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيَاطِين</span>, giving you tremendous assistance this month. He has also helped by making it obligatory to abstain from food and relations — the two most fundamental sources of temptation. But He left the rest to us, because there must be some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهَدَة</span>. It is up to us to gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> the eyes, the tongue, the ears, the hands, and the feet. That is the fasting of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَوَاصّ</span>: they fast with all seven limbs. You gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> your tongue, gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> your eyes, gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> your hands, gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> your feet. Do not look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. Do not speak the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. Do not listen to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. If someone starts to backbite in your presence, protect yourself — don''t let it reach your ears. Gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> your fast.</p>
<p>The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> of the Prophet about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> was: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ سَلِّمْنَا رَمَضَانَ وَسَلِّمْ رَمَضَانَ مِنَّا</span> — O <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, give us <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> safely, and protect <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> from our evil. You do not want <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> to receive any portion of your evil. You want to receive <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>''s portion of good. This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> carries what is known as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُرْمَةٌ زَمَانِيَّةٌ</span> — the sanctity of time. Wrong action committed in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is graver than wrong action at other times, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has given this month a sanctity that must be honored. And you honor it by refraining from what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has forbidden.</p>
<p>One of the things <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has told the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an being is that we are to deliver the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَانَة</span> to those who are entitled to it — that is one of the signs of the believer. The believer gives back what has been entrusted to him. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤْمِن</span> comes from the same root as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَانَة</span>. A person who has no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَانَة</span> has no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has given us a trust: our sight, our hearing, our tongue, and above all the heart itself, which generates all the actions of the other limbs. You are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْؤُول</span> — responsible — for all of them. They are trusts that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has placed in your care, and you must return the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَانَة</span> on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الْقِيَامَةِ</span>, returning it intact, not having corrupted it. Fasting is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> teaching us how to honor the trust and return it to Him the way He wants it returned: the tongue free of slander, the ears free of what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, the eyes free of what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, the hands free of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span> acts, the feet free of walking tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, the genitals gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ed, and the stomach free of consuming what is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>. That is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> wants. And the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْرَسَة</span> through which He is teaching us to achieve this — is fasting.</p>
<p>Then there is the highest level: the fast of the elect of the elect. He defines it as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَوْمُ الْقَلْبِ عَنِ الْهِمَمِ الدَّنِيَّةِ وَالْأَفْكَارِ الدَّنِيَّةِ</span> — the fasting of the heart from base aspirations and lowly thoughts. In other words, the fast of the elect of the elect is that the heart''s aspiration is not directed tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> at all. That is why in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>, giving out is the mode of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>, whereas in other months we are often busy acquiring wealth. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>, you should be busy giving wealth away for the sake of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. You are not in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span> mode — you are in an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span> mode. You have to shift this month. That is the shift.</p>
<p>And reg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَفْكَارُ الدَّنِيَّةُ</span>: one of the scholars of Andalusia said that the first degree of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِلَايَة</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَاسَبَةُ الْخَوَاطِرِ</span> — beginning to hold one''s thoughts accountable. That is where it begins: the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ</span> measures each thought against the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ميزَانٌ</span> of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>. That is what makes him a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيٌّ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is the time to do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَاسَبَة</span> of the heart.</p>
<p>And understand that an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَمِّي</span> — a common person — in Arabic does not mean someone who is ignorant of the sacred sciences. There are scholars who consider themselves above the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوَامّ</span> because they are learned, yet they are still <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوَامّ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَمِّي</span> can be the street sweeper who knows only a few <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span>s but whose heart is entirely with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. That is why the saying goes: how many people are not fasting yet with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> they are fasting, and how many people are fasting yet with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> they are not fasting? There are people throughout the year who gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> their eyes, their ears, their tongues, their feet, their genitals, and their stomachs — in reality they are living the purpose of fasting, because fasting is an exercise designed to teach us how to do that properly. Others fast physically but with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> they are not fasting. That is the point. So when he speaks of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُشُوعٌ</span>, do not imagine that means someone who has mastered Imam Sibawayhi''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكِتَاب</span> or memorized all the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفَاسِير</span>. These people can be nearly illiterate — if the heart is with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, that is what counts.</p>
<p>Consider Imam Sahnun — Muhammad ibn Sahnun, son of the Sahnun who wrote the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُدَوَّنَة</span>. He had a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَجْلِس</span> in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَيْرَوَان</span>. Every day during his teaching sessions, a man would come in, walk over people''s shoulders to reach the sheikh who was seated in a chair, and whisper in his ear for a while — then leave. This happened every time the sheikh taught. The sheikh would actually ask people to move aside so the man would not have to step over them. One day the man stopped coming. Imam Muhammad ibn Sahnun asked his students about him, sent someone to find him, and the man came. The sheikh asked: what happened, you stopped visiting me? The man said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span> — I have to explain myself. People who envy you came to me — I am a very poor man with many daughters I need to marry off. They said: go and disturb him every day in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَجْلِس</span>, and if you can make him angry and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>iliate him in front of his students, we will give you money to marry your daughters. So every day I would come and I would curse you in your ear and say the most horrible things. And I realized it was having no effect on you whatsoever. So I gave up. Muhammad ibn Sahnun said: why didn''t you simply come and ask me for money? I would have given it to you.</p>
<p>That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span> you want to learn. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span> of the people of the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْر</span> of his family. And Salman is from our family — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلْمَانُ الْفَارِسِيُّ</span>. He was patient from the very beginning of his search. He was a Zoroastrian, and he used to see the sacred fire go out and be quietly relit, while the worshippers were told it never goes out. His father was in charge of tending it, so Salman knew it was a lie. He set out to find the truth. He went to the Christians, served a monk for years, and witnessed that monk stealing the people''s charity and burying it in his backy<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>. He then went to another monk who was genuinely devout, and that monk told him: there is a prophet who will appear in the land of Arabia in your time.</p>
<p>In terms of this month, here is my recommendation to myself first and foremost, and to all of you. Don''t do a lot of unnecessary talking. Utilize the time. Every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span> should be conscious that this month is unlike any other — don''t waste it. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَفٌ</span> used to spend six months before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> begging <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> to let them reach this month. This is not an ordinary time. And none of us knows whether this is our last <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>; none of us even knows whether we will make it to the end of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>. So seize the moment. Utilize this time. Don''t let the month slip by and find yourself in the last few days full of regret about what you didn''t do. We will all feel that to some degree reg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>less — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>an beings are such that we never do anything without realizing afterw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> we could have done it better. But hopefully the regret will be mild because we did a great deal, not because we did very little.</p>
<p>Utilize the night. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَتْمٌ</span> of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرَاوِيح</span> is the tradition, but even praying one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span> is effective. Even if you prayed your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرَاوِيح</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ</span> in every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكْعَةٌ</span> — if that is all you know — and you completed your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span>, that counts as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span>. So do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span> every night, even if it is twenty <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ</span>. Do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span> at night. In fact, one scholar said that tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> the end of time it is good to pray the night prayer with the shorter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span>s that carry great rew<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>, simply because there is so little time and most people no longer have the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِمَّة</span> for long recitation. So utilize the time. Try to pray the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضُحَى</span> prayer. Try to rise before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>, do some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span> before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span>, and then utilize the time after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَجْر</span> — try not to sleep during that period, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكْرُوهٌ</span> in any case, and in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> that time is even more precious than at other times. Then try to do the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضُحَى</span>. The Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, instructed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو هُرَيْرَةَ</span> never to abandon the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضُحَى</span>, so you should try to pray it — it is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْدُوب</span> and has a very strong basis in practice, since the Prophet did it consistently: two, four, six, or eight <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> before the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَوَالٌ</span>, all the way up to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَوَالٌ</span>. For the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِكِي</span>s and the Hanafis, there is the period during which the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَافِلَة</span> cannot be prayed, but otherwise pray <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَافِلَة</span> freely.</p>
<p>And then: prayer on time. Prayer on time. We he<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> earlier that the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, on his deathbed — his final <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَصِيَّةٌ</span> to his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span> — his last words were: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّلَاةَ، الصَّلَاةَ</span>. Prayer is above everything. Gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> your prayer. Use this month to discipline yourself to pray on time. If you were not praying on time before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> — and you should have been — then use this time to build that discipline. Pray on time.</p>
<p>If you''re working, just stop — your prayer matters more than your job. I guarantee it. Prayer is more important than your job.</p>
<p>Pray first. And pay particular attention to the time between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْر</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span>, which is a powerful time throughout the year — a time of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>. During <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْر</span> especially, it carries a particular weight. It is, as the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> tells us, a time for the people who believe — a time of love for one another.</p>
<p>And that is why in this culture you see people at the end of the day completely worn out, needing to go out and drink. They call it happy hour. No, seriously — that is what they call it. They need oblivion so they don''t have to think about how empty their lives are. For us, that time is about preparing for sunset, which is a metaphor for death. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْر</span> to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span> represents the later years of a person''s life. That entire period carries symbolic weight. And breaking the fast is like what the Messenger described — there are two joys in it. The analogy being made is that breaking the fast resembles meeting your Lord: when the day is over, when death is over, you return to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>After you break your fast, the best thing is to break it on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُطَبٌ</span>, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَمْرٌ</span>, or a few sips of water. If you have none of that, you can break it on whatever is available. If there is no food at all, then go ahead and pray. Delaying the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِفْطَار</span> is only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكْرُوهٌ</span> when the delay comes from believing it is somehow better to delay — at that point it becomes a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِدْعَة</span>. But if there is a valid reason to delay, it is perfectly fine. Just do not eat before praying <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span>.</p>
<p>When you visit <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span> households, they will sometimes ask: do you want to eat first or pray? Tell them — let''s pray first. Do not be shy about that. Because if people sit down to eat, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span> prayer can slip out of its time. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span> has a very narrow window, and you do not want to delay it. The preferred time is right at the beginning. After the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذًى</span> comes in, make your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, find a clean place, and pray. During the fast you are given a small window — break the fast with something to drink, make the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, and then when you finish, move to prayer. These are good habits to build: learning the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءٌ</span>, practicing them, carrying them with you.</p>
<p>As for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span> prayer — if you have a strong resolve, it is better to pray your night prayers at home. But if you know yourself well enough to know that your resolve is not strong, or if there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُقْرِئ</span> doing a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَتْمٌ</span> of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> and you want to be part of that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَتْمٌ</span>, then going to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> for it is also a good thing. It is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْدُوب</span>.</p>
<p>What you should avoid — and unfortunately many of our brothers have fallen into this — is the idea that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرَاوِيح</span> is only eight <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span>, and so once the eight are done, they say, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, we''re following the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>,&quot; and then spend the next hour standing outside the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> talking. Just be honest with yourself: it is fine to say you find twenty <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> long. But do not dress your tiredness up as following the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span>. Because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> is certainly not to loiter at the door of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> and make idle conversation — I guarantee you that. The Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, did not waste his time. And empty talk at the door of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> is — that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَقْل</span>-level behavior. That is what the marketplaces are for. We do not want to be part of that.</p>
<p>And do not belittle people, do not look down on them. I am not trying to be judgmental — everyone is where they are for their own reasons. The point is simply this: do not see yourself as better than someone else. Instead, thank <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> that He has not afflicted you with that affliction — because it is an affliction. When the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صلى الله عليه وسلم</span>, saw fifty people doing such things, he said what he said. Recognizing a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَةٌ</span> that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has given you is not arrogance. Arrogance is thinking you deserve it more than someone else does. It is simply a favor <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> has extended to you, for reasons known only to Him — and He could take it from you tomorrow, give your state to that person, and leave you standing at the door of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span> talking.</p>
<p>There is a story about one man who accused Muadh ibn Jabal of doing something <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَامٌ</span>, and Muadh — who was known as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْتَجَابُ الدُّعَاءِ</span>, someone whose supplications were answered — made a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span> against him. In his old age, that man was seen on the streets of Medina, pinching young women as he walked, because his eyelids had literally fallen over his eyes. He could not see. That was the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْتِلَاء اللهُ</span> gave him in his old age, for falsely condemning Muadh ibn Jabal.</p>
<p>So I am not asking anyone to be self-righteous — myself included. But the point stands: do not go down that road. If people are gathered and talking, greet them. It is a joyous time and there is a place for that. But keep it in its proper place.</p>
<p>And remember our brothers and sisters who are in great tribulation right now — the people of Palestine, the people of Iraq, the people of Algeria, the people of Bosnia, the people of India. I could go on and on, and I would rather not. Those people are genuinely suffering. So temper your joy with some gravity as well.</p>
<p>One thing I learned a long time ago about spiritual light is that sometimes, when a great deal of it descends on people, their reaction is to become giddy — to laugh, to get frivolous. You can see it happen. And it is a way of spilling the light, of wasting it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> can do that to people. Because there is so much light pouring in, and they have not learned how to contain it, they end up laughing through the whole month, socializing, having a good time — and by the end they have spilled it all out. So try to hold on to what you are receiving. Try to contain the benefits of this month.</p>
<p>I think we ' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'the root of all diseases of the heart';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'are about to break. Is the bus here? Yes. You are supposed to do the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span> of the evening. I will come back after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>. Are they leaving now? Okay. Then before we pray, since there is still a little time, I want to read from the section on fasting in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوَطَّأ</span> — for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَةٌ</span> of it.</p>
<p>What is that? I am glad you mentioned that. The other thing: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَام مَالِك</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللهُ</span>, during <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> did not like storytelling, did not like idle talk. He said: this is a time of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِرَاءَة</span>. So the best <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> during <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. You should still be doing your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوْرَاد</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِرْد</span> — those are important in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> as at any other time. But you need to have your reading of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>.</p>
<p>Imam Abu Hanifa, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللهُ</span>, said that if you read the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> at least twice a year, you are not considered to have abandoned it — meaning you will not fall under the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span> in which the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> complained that his people had left the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> is the time to spend with the Book of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Ideally, if you can complete more than one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَتَمَ</span>, that would be excellent. I know some of you personally — and one person I know completed twenty-six <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَتْمٌ</span> in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>. But that was essentially all he was doing. For people who work, that is clearly difficult.</p>
<p>If you are not proficient in recitation, there is also the option of listening to a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُقْرِئ</span> — if you have a recording. When you are driving and you do not have much memorized, it is better to recite what you do know from memory, but if you do not have much to draw from, then simply listen to recitation. And also do some <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَدَبُّر</span> of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> during <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> — not only the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَعَبُّد</span> of it, but genuine reflection on its meaning.</p>
<p>If you want a structured approach, there is the seven-day <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِرْد</span> of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. The pattern goes: three, five, seven, nine, eleven, thirteen, and then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفَصَّل</span>. On the first day, you read the first three <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَة</span>s. On the second day, the next five. Then seven, then nine, then eleven, then thirteen, and then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفَصَّل</span> — which runs from Surah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَاف</span> to the end of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, or according to some, from Surah <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجُرَات</span>. In seven days you can complete the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. This is how many of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَفٌ</span> approached it: breaking it into those odd-numbered portions — three, five, seven, nine, eleven, thirteen, and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُفَصَّل</span>.</p>
<p>Question. If you owe <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span> prayers, I would not pray <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرَاوِيح</span>. Use <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> as the time to make up what you owe. Do the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span>. Instead of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قِيَامٌ</span>, use that time to make up your missed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span> prayers. The only <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْل</span> prayers you should prioritize if you owe <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span> prayers are the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْر</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَغِبَ</span> — the pre-dawn prayer. Everything else should go tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> making up what is owed.</p>
<p>According to the reckoning of the scholars: if you owe prayers and you are performing fewer than five make-up prayers at each prayer time throughout the year, you are not doing enough. If you owe a great deal — years'' worth — then do the minimal valid form of the prayer, just the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْكَان</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤَكَّدُ سُنَنٍ</span>. That is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاجِب</span> you owe. Do that consistently, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> will accept it.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 306">page 306</div>
<p>As for estimating, the scholars say: do your best to figure out when you stopped praying. Most people remember roughly when they went seriously astray in their lives — that tends to be a recognizable turning point. Start from there.</p>
<p>By minimal prayer, I mean: perform the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْكَان</span> — the sixteen pillars — and the eight <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤَكَّدُ سُنَنٍ</span>. That is it. There are sixteen <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْكَان</span> and eight <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُؤَكَّدُ سُنَنٍ</span>. That is the form you focus on when making up a large number of prayers.</p>
<p>If you are still learning and you have doubts about whether you are making mistakes in the prayers you are currently practicing — I think you just have to move forw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>. There are scholars who would say you must make those up, but my view is: if you are learning <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْه</span> now and your errors were from ignorance, then make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span> and ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. If there was an obligation you were not even aware of — because your parents never taught you, you genuinely did not know — then in a real sense, that responsibility traces back further. A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span> child is an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَانَة</span>, and one of the obligations of that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمَانَة</span> is to teach them the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرَائِضُ</span>. Many <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسْلِم</span>s were simply never told any of this. They thought they were doing right.</p>
<p>For those people — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span> — I would say: make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span>, ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> to excuse you for your ignorance, and ask Him to accept those prayers. I would not burden them beyond that. I think that is the appropriate position.</p>
<p>Question. Can a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَغِبَ</span> include the two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span>? No — the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَغِبَ</span> is not that. Some scholars say you should pray at least two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَكَعَاتٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْل</span> after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span>, and there is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خِلَافٌ</span> about that. But the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وِتْر</span> and the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَغِبَ</span> should definitely be maintained even when one owes <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span> prayers.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 307">page 307</div>
<p>Question. If you owe <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span> prayers, what about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> recitation in the meantime? If you have time that you would use to make up prayers — that time should go to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span> takes precedence. As the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> states: &quot;My servant does not draw near to Me with anything more beloved to Me than the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span>.&quot; So the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span> is above everything. Truly. If you are performing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْل</span> and not the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْدٌ</span>, you are wasting your time.</p>
<p>If the number owed is fewer than five, do them in order. If more than five, you do not need to do them in order. And some scholars say: if you owe a large number, simply commit to a set amount at each prayer time — for example, five prayers at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظُهْر</span> time, five at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْر</span> time, five at <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَغْرِب</span> time, and so on. You do not have to go in sequence.</p>
<p>Is the bus here?</p>
<p>Alright. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, a few closing words — this is the last class for those going on the intensive. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَزَاكُمُ اللهُ خَيْرًا</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْفِيق إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>. I want to thank all of you for your attendance, your respect, and the wonderful spirit that has characterized this entire course. Please excuse me for any shortcomings or breaches of courtesy on my part. And please, make <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>.</p>
<div class="md-comment" data-md-comment="page 308">page 308</div>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span></p>
<p>By the blessing of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, we were able to go through this text and complete it. We ask <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> to grant us, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, the ability to implement what we have learned.</p>
<p>In closing this text, I would like to share some counsel written by one of our great scholars, Sidi Ahmad Zarruq, who died in 1492. He was a great scholar from Morocco who died in Misrata in Libya. He said that the fragrance of his teaching would spread throughout the world long after his bones were laid to rest — and this has proven to be one of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَامَات</span>, his saintly miracles, because his teaching is still reaching us and shaping us today.</p>
<p>He said — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَهُ اللهُ</span>:</p>
<p>Know — may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> grant you and us success, rectify our worldly and otherworldly lives, and grant us adherence to the way of truth in our journeys and in our sojourns — that repentance is a key, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> is vast, and uprightness is the source of all rectification. A servant is never free of blunders, or shortcomings, or lassitude. Therefore, never be neglectful of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْبَةٌ</span>. Never turn away from returning to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. Never neglect the acts that bring you closer to Him. Every time one of these three occurs — repent and return. Every time you make a mistake — listen and obey. Every time you display shortcomings or lack of enthusiasm — do not give up. Let your primary concern be to remove from your outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> state anything that is displeasing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, and then to maintain that outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> state through continuous self-counsel. Keep doing this until you find that you naturally flee from anything outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ly displeasing, and that your avoidance of the boundaries of the prohibited has become like a protective net placed before you.</p>
<p>At this point, the time has come to turn inw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> — tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> your heart''s presence and its reality — through both reflection and remembrance. Do not hasten the end result before you have completed the beginning, but likewise, do not begin without keeping the end in view. The one who seeks what belongs to the outset at the end loses providential care, and the one who seeks what belongs to the end at the outset loses providential guidance.</p>
<p>Act in accordance with principles and the appropriate legal rulings, not in accordance with stories and fantasies. Do not consider accounts of how things went with others except as a tonic to strengthen your resolve — certainly not as a reference to imitate based on their outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> forms or what they appear to be saying.</p>
<p>In all of this, rely on a clear path you can refer to and a foundation you can depend upon in every state. The best of these is the path of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ابْنُ عَطَاءِ اللهِ</span>, for it contains a clear direction tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ اللهُ</span>. Do not take from others'' words unless what they say accords with your own path, but submit to their implications if you desire realization.</p>
<p>Avoid all forms of vain and foul speech to your absolute utmost. Set aside anything whose benefit you cannot discern immediately. Beware of being excessively h<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> on yourself before you have attained mastery over it, but equally beware of being too lax in anything that concerns sacred rulings.</p>
<p>This is because the self is constantly fleeing from moderation in everything, inclining tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> extremism in both deviance and guidance alike. To pause and reflect on this is extremely important, because what the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخٌ</span> is telling us is that the nature of the self is extremism — and he is warning us to be aware of this in both good deeds and foul ones, in both obedience to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> and disobedience to Him.</p>
<p>So he is telling us: beware of being excessively h<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> on yourself before you have attained mastery over it, but also beware of being too lax in anything that concerns sacred rulings. He continues: seek out a companion to help you in your affairs and take his counsel in matters that arise from both your inw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> states and your outw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> affairs. If you do take his companionship, then treat him in a manner commensurate with his state, and offer your counsel according to his limitations and capacities, for the perfected friend is no longer to be found.</p>
<p>Indeed, in these times even a suitable, agreeable companion rarely lasts. Beware of the majority of people in matters concerning your religious and worldly affairs, unless you have established that a person has a sound relationship with his Lord — grounded in knowledge free of caprice or love of leadership, and in a sound intellect free of hidden agendas. Do not be heedless of the machinations of others or their hidden states.</p>
<p>Consider people from the vantage point of both their origins and their actions. A person of character and distinguished lineage will rarely affect you with anything other than good, whereas a person of low origins typically lets those roots lead him to disreg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> you when times grow difficult. Be extremely vigilant of the dominant qualities of the people in any given land, do not be heedless of the divine wisdom in creation, and take notice of both gatheredness and separation.</p>
<p>Some of this we have already covered in our book <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَرَائِبُ</span>, so refer to it there. Engage with any matter only in a manner appropriate to what that moment specifically calls for, using gentleness and toleration, and be very wary of both harshness and laxity. Too much laxity in permissible matters pulls the heart backw<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> in its journey until even a person of firm resolve ends up resembling a foolish boy.</p>
<p>Work for this world as if you will live forever, but work for the next life as if you will die tomorrow. Do not neglect the externals of your worldly needs, and in the meantime do not be heedless of your end and final resting place.</p>
<p>Be extremely vigilant about avoiding positions of leadership, but should you be tried with such responsibilities, at least know your own limitations. Be utterly sincere — with the sincerity of one who knows full well who is placing demands upon him. Surrender completely to His decree with the submission of one who knows he can never overcome Him.</p>
<p>Have a firm foundation for all your affairs and you will be safe from their pitfalls. Organize your devotional practices and you will find your time extended through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَةٌ</span> — the blessings — within it. Never be fanatical about anything, whether it is truth or falsehood, and your heart will remain sound tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> others.</p>
<p>Never claim anything you are entitled to, let alone what you are not entitled to, and you will be safe from tricks and treachery. Anyone who claims a rank above his own will fall in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُمْ</span>iliation; those who claim a rank they do warrant will have it stripped from them; while those who claim a station less than their true rank will be elevated to levels even higher than they actually deserve. Never give your companion anything of your inner state beyond what his own state warrants. If you descend to his level, he will show you contempt, whereas if you attempt to raise him to your level, he will abandon you.</p>
<p>Never demand a right from anyone, whether an intimate or a stranger. A stranger in reality owes you nothing, and someone close to you is too precious to be the target of your blame. Never assume that anyone in this world can truly understand your circumstances except through the lens of their own. In reality, everyone sees things in accordance with their own frame of reference and personal path. Yet when aims, purposes, and aspirations are similar, people tend to work together tow<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> a common goal.</p>
<p>Never belittle any talk that involves people in their absence, even if it seems harmless, given the possibility that harm may enter into it. Gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> your secrets even when you feel safe with someone, for the person you confide in is not a safer vessel than your own heart from which the secret first arose. Never abandon even an atom''s weight of your regular devotional practice. Never be lenient with yourself, whether in times of laxity or times of high resolve. Should you miss some of your practice at a given time, make it up at another. If you are unable to perform your usual practice, at least occupy yourself with something comparable.</p>
<p>Never obey your ego, even for a moment. Never believe any of its claims, no matter what it tells you. Be vigilant about your resolve in all your affairs to your utmost. Should you resolve to do something, do it immediately before the resolve wanes. Ex<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمِين</span>e your soul constantly reg<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span>ing what you are obliged or need to do. Anything you have no need to do, leave it — even if it is recommended. That means not involving yourself in anything beyond what is absolutely necessary and what constitutes a real, discernible need.</p>
<p>Treat others exactly as you would wish to be treated, and fulfill what is due to them. All of this is beautifully captured in the words of the poet:</p>
<p><em>If you desire to live such that your religion is safe, your portion full, and your honor sound — gu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْدٌ</span> your tongue, and never mention another''s faults, remembering that you yourself have faults and others have tongues. Watch your eye; should it ever reveal to you the faults of others, say to it: O my eye, other people have eyes too. Live treating others well, and avoid aggression, and should others aggress against you, leave them — but in the best way.</em></p>
<p>The source of these words is nothing other than the traditions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be upon him, when he said: <em>Be vigilant of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> wherever you are, and follow a misdeed with a good deed — it will remove it — and treat others with the most excellent of character.</em> In another narration he said, peace and blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> be upon him: <em>Every child of Adam makes mistakes, and the best of those who make mistakes are those who seek to redress them.</em> And again: <em>The Holy Spirit inspired my heart''s core that no soul will die until it fulfills its decreed portion of this world and its appointed time here. So be conscious of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and make your requests with dignity.</em></p>
<p>In summation, repentance, awareness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, and uprightness are the foundations of all that is beneficial. The truth is clear, and its details are weighty and significant. The affair belongs only to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>; success is in His hands. Peace be upon you.</p>' WHERE slug = 'purification-of-the-heart' AND anchor_key = 'the root of all diseases of the heart';
